window.ECON={"chapters":[{"id":"1","title":"Introduction","intro":"Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole.","lessons":[{"title":"Meaning, origin and principal concerns","body":"Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole. The term Macro comes from Makros, meaning large.\n\nKeynes’ The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) established macroeconomics as a separate branch. Its principal concerns are employment, inflation, growth of output and depression.\n\nKeynes stressed government intervention when inadequate demand reduces output and employment.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.1 ABOUT MACROECONOMICS"},{"title":"Micro versus macro: seven bases of distinction","body":"Both study scarcity and choice, but at different levels. An industry aggregates firms, yet studying one industry is still microeconomics.\n\n**1 · Concept:** Microeconomics studies individuals, households, firms and industries; macroeconomics studies the economy as a whole.\n\n**2 · Central Issue:** Micro focuses on allocation of resources: consumers maximise satisfaction and producers maximise profit. Macro focuses on overall output and employment, and policies that maximise growth with stability.\n\n**3 · Economic Variables:** Micro uses variables such as an individual consumer’s demand and a producer’s supply. Macro uses aggregate demand, aggregate supply, national income, economy-wide consumption, investment and employment.\n\n**4 · Aggregation of Economic Units:** Micro involves limited aggregation, such as all firms in one industry. Macro involves the wider aggregation of economic units across the whole economy.\n\n**5 · Economic Agents:** Micro emphasises individual agents such as consumers and producers pursuing personal gains. Macro examines institutional decision makers such as the RBI, government and regulators, whose policies affect growth and stability.\n\n**6 · Method of Study:** Partial equilibrium studies one market assuming other markets unchanged. General equilibrium studies simultaneous equilibrium and interdependence across markets. The textbook associates the former with micro and the latter with macro.\n\n**7 · Assumptions of Study:** In the textbook’s simplified comparison, micro takes overall output and employment as given while studying allocation and distribution; macro varies overall output and employment while taking distribution as given. These are analytical assumptions, not a claim that macro can never study inequality.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.2 Microeconomics versus Macroeconomics","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Microeconomics","Macroeconomics"],"rows":[["Concept","Individual, household, firm or industry","Economy as a whole"],["Central issue","Resource allocation; satisfaction and profit","Overall output and employment; growth with stability"],["Variables","Individual demand and supply","AD, AS, national income and total employment"],["Aggregation","Limited, such as one industry","Economy-wide"],["Agents","Individual consumers and producers","Government, RBI and other institutions"],["Method","Partial equilibrium","General equilibrium"],["Simplified assumption","Overall output and employment given","Distribution given while output and employment vary"]]},"key":"restored-micro-versus-macro-seven-bases-of-distinction","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Both study scarcity and choice, but at different levels. An industry aggregates firms, yet studying one industry is still microeconomics.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Concept","text":"Microeconomics studies individuals, households, firms and industries; macroeconomics studies the economy as a whole.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Central Issue","text":"Micro focuses on allocation of resources: consumers maximise satisfaction and producers maximise profit. Macro focuses on overall output and employment, and policies that maximise growth with stability.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Economic Variables","text":"Micro uses variables such as an individual consumer’s demand and a producer’s supply. Macro uses aggregate demand, aggregate supply, national income, economy-wide consumption, investment and employment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Aggregation of Economic Units","text":"Micro involves limited aggregation, such as all firms in one industry. Macro involves the wider aggregation of economic units across the whole economy.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Economic Agents","text":"Micro emphasises individual agents such as consumers and producers pursuing personal gains. Macro examines institutional decision makers such as the RBI, government and regulators, whose policies affect growth and stability.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Method of Study","text":"Partial equilibrium studies one market assuming other markets unchanged. General equilibrium studies simultaneous equilibrium and interdependence across markets. The textbook associates the former with micro and the latter with macro.","tone":"point"},{"title":"7 · Assumptions of Study","text":"In the textbook’s simplified comparison, micro takes overall output and employment as given while studying allocation and distribution; macro varies overall output and employment while taking distribution as given. These are analytical assumptions, not a claim that macro can never study inequality.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Micro–macro paradox and interdependence","body":"What is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level. Saving may improve one person’s future prosperity; if everyone saves more by spending less, aggregate demand, investment, output and employment may fall.\n\nMacro affects micro when an RBI interest-rate change affects one producer’s borrowing. Micro affects macro when individual increases in spending together raise aggregate demand.","formula":"","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"Micro-Macro Paradox · Micro-Macro Interdependence"},{"title":"Scope of macroeconomics: all six areas","body":"Scope means the field of study. The chapter identifies six leading areas; each needs its own heading and explanation.\n\n**1 · Estimation of National Income and Related Aggregates:** Macroeconomics begins with national income and measures the economy’s overall economic activity. It estimates related aggregates such as Gross Domestic Product (GDP) and Net Domestic Product (NDP), allowing comparison of output and income over time.\n\n**2 · Theory of Employment:** It studies employment and unemployment in the economy. Keynesian theory explains why inadequate aggregate demand can leave willing workers unemployed, and identifies remedies for restoring output and employment.\n\n**3 · Theory of Money:** It studies money and the creation of credit by commercial banks. It also examines the central bank’s role—in India, the RBI—in regulating money supply and credit in the economy.\n\n**4 · Theory of General Price Level:** It explains movements in the general price level, including inflation and deflation. Inflation is a persistent increase in the general price level; deflation is a persistent decrease. A change in one product’s price is different from a change in the economy-wide price level.\n\n**5 · Government Budget:** It studies how government receipts and expenditure affect the level of economic activity. Through taxation, public spending and borrowing, budgetary policy influences aggregate demand, employment, growth and economic stability.\n\n**6 · Exchange Rate and Balance of Payments:** It studies the determination and management of the foreign exchange rate. Balance of payments records a country’s economic transactions with the rest of the world, connecting the domestic economy with international trade, income and financial flows.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.3.1 Area of Study (Scope) of Macroeconomics","key":"restored-scope-of-macroeconomics-all-six-areas","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Scope means the field of study. The chapter identifies six leading areas; each needs its own heading and explanation.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Estimation of National Income and Related Aggregates","text":"Macroeconomics begins with national income and measures the economy’s overall economic activity. It estimates related aggregates such as Gross Domestic Product (GDP) and Net Domestic Product (NDP), allowing comparison of output and income over time.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Theory of Employment","text":"It studies employment and unemployment in the economy. Keynesian theory explains why inadequate aggregate demand can leave willing workers unemployed, and identifies remedies for restoring output and employment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Theory of Money","text":"It studies money and the creation of credit by commercial banks. It also examines the central bank’s role—in India, the RBI—in regulating money supply and credit in the economy.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Theory of General Price Level","text":"It explains movements in the general price level, including inflation and deflation. Inflation is a persistent increase in the general price level; deflation is a persistent decrease. A change in one product’s price is different from a change in the economy-wide price level.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Government Budget","text":"It studies how government receipts and expenditure affect the level of economic activity. Through taxation, public spending and borrowing, budgetary policy influences aggregate demand, employment, growth and economic stability.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Exchange Rate and Balance of Payments","text":"It studies the determination and management of the foreign exchange rate. Balance of payments records a country’s economic transactions with the rest of the world, connecting the domestic economy with international trade, income and financial flows.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Significance of macroeconomics: all six points","textbookHeading":"1.3.2 Significance of Macroeconomics","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-significance-of-macroeconomics-all-six-points","body":"Significance explains why studying these economy-wide relationships is useful. Keep these six headings separate from the six areas of scope.\n\n**1 · Description of the Economy:** National-income estimates across sectors reveal the nature and level of economic activity. Unemployment data show the extent of the employment problem and help identify remedies. The government budget shows how the state regulates economic activity. Together these provide a systematic picture of the economy’s performance.\n\n**2 · Roadmap for Business Decisions:** The description of the economy helps businesses decide whether to expand investment and production. For example, rising national income and consumer expenditure suggest stronger demand; firms may invest more, expanding output and employment. Macroeconomic conditions therefore guide individual business decisions.\n\n**3 · Policy Formulation:** Macroeconomic data help governments design policies and programmes. For example, information about people below the poverty line helps target Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT): money is transferred directly to beneficiaries’ linked bank accounts. The policy should respond to the scale and nature of the problem identified.\n\n**4 · Global Economic Issues:** It brings international issues into view, including exchange rates and the movement of Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) between countries. These affect trade, financing, productive capacity and the relationship between the domestic economy and the rest of the world.\n\n**5 · Environmental Pollution and Sustainable Development:** Macroeconomic analysis helps assess environmental costs and formulate a strategy of sustainable development. Sustainable development meets development needs without depleting the resources available to future generations; output growth must therefore be assessed alongside its environmental consequences.\n\n**6 · Structural Changes and Growth Path of the Economy:** It helps identify changes in the economy’s structure and trace the nature and pace of growth. Changes in occupational structure—for example, workers moving from agriculture towards industry and services—show how development is changing the economy, beyond an increase in total GDP.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Significance explains why studying these economy-wide relationships is useful. Keep these six headings separate from the six areas of scope.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Description of the Economy","text":"National-income estimates across sectors reveal the nature and level of economic activity. Unemployment data show the extent of the employment problem and help identify remedies. The government budget shows how the state regulates economic activity. Together these provide a systematic picture of the economy’s performance.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Roadmap for Business Decisions","text":"The description of the economy helps businesses decide whether to expand investment and production. For example, rising national income and consumer expenditure suggest stronger demand; firms may invest more, expanding output and employment. Macroeconomic conditions therefore guide individual business decisions.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Policy Formulation","text":"Macroeconomic data help governments design policies and programmes. For example, information about people below the poverty line helps target Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT): money is transferred directly to beneficiaries’ linked bank accounts. The policy should respond to the scale and nature of the problem identified.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Global Economic Issues","text":"It brings international issues into view, including exchange rates and the movement of Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) between countries. These affect trade, financing, productive capacity and the relationship between the domestic economy and the rest of the world.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Environmental Pollution and Sustainable Development","text":"Macroeconomic analysis helps assess environmental costs and formulate a strategy of sustainable development. Sustainable development meets development needs without depleting the resources available to future generations; output growth must therefore be assessed alongside its environmental consequences.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Structural Changes and Growth Path of the Economy","text":"It helps identify changes in the economy’s structure and trace the nature and pace of growth. Changes in occupational structure—for example, workers moving from agriculture towards industry and services—show how development is changing the economy, beyond an increase in total GDP.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Why the Great Depression challenged classical economics","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · Emergence of Macroeconomics","kind":"Ability Zone","key":"restored-why-the-great-depression-challenged-classical-economics","body":"The Great Depression of the 1930s explains why macroeconomics emerged as a distinct field, rather than simply applying individual-market reasoning to the whole economy.\n\n**Classical belief: individual gains and social welfare:** Classical reasoning argued that individuals pursuing self-interest maximise their satisfaction; together this was expected to maximise social welfare and ensure optimal use of scarce resources.\n\n**Classical belief: automatic full employment:** Competitive markets were expected to move to equilibrium. If labour supply exceeded demand, falling wages were expected to reduce supply and raise demand until full employment returned. Full employment was therefore treated as the normal condition.\n\n**What the Depression showed:** The textbook’s historical example reports US unemployment rising from about 3% to 25% during 1929–33, alongside a roughly one-third fall in GDP. Persistent unemployment contradicted the expectation of rapid automatic recovery.\n\n**Low Level Equilibrium Trap:** Low AD → low planned output → low employment → low GDP/income → low AD again. Firms could settle at an equilibrium with widespread unused capacity; market adjustment did not necessarily break the cycle.\n\n**Keynes’s diagnosis and remedy:** Keynes identified inadequate aggregate demand as the central problem and advocated large-scale government expenditure to raise demand. His 1936 General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money established the modern macroeconomic approach.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"The Great Depression of the 1930s explains why macroeconomics emerged as a distinct field, rather than simply applying individual-market reasoning to the whole economy.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Classical belief: individual gains and social welfare","text":"Classical reasoning argued that individuals pursuing self-interest maximise their satisfaction; together this was expected to maximise social welfare and ensure optimal use of scarce resources.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Classical belief: automatic full employment","text":"Competitive markets were expected to move to equilibrium. If labour supply exceeded demand, falling wages were expected to reduce supply and raise demand until full employment returned. Full employment was therefore treated as the normal condition.","tone":"point"},{"title":"What the Depression showed","text":"The textbook’s historical example reports US unemployment rising from about 3% to 25% during 1929–33, alongside a roughly one-third fall in GDP. Persistent unemployment contradicted the expectation of rapid automatic recovery.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Low Level Equilibrium Trap","text":"Low AD → low planned output → low employment → low GDP/income → low AD again. Firms could settle at an equilibrium with widespread unused capacity; market adjustment did not necessarily break the cycle.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Keynes’s diagnosis and remedy","text":"Keynes identified inadequate aggregate demand as the central problem and advocated large-scale government expenditure to raise demand. His 1936 General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money established the modern macroeconomic approach.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"1","flow":[["Individual choice","Micro studies a consumer, firm or market."],["Economy-wide totals","Macro studies output, employment and prices."],["Interdependence","Individual decisions combine; aggregate conditions feed back."]]},{"id":"2","title":"Some Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics","intro":"Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users.","lessons":[{"title":"Final goods, intermediate goods and end use","body":"Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users. They lead to final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure.\n\nIntermediate goods are purchased by one firm from another for resale or for use as raw material in producing other goods. Classification depends on end use, not the physical nature of the good: milk bought by a household is final; milk bought by an ice-cream producer is intermediate.\n\nUnsold additions to inventory count as investment.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.1 MEANING OF END-USE CLASSIFICATION OF GOODS","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Final goods","Intermediate goods"],"rows":[["Position","Crossed the production boundary; ready for final use","Used up or transformed in further production"],["National income","Count their final value","Do not add separately to final output"],["Test","Use matters","The same good can change category with its use"]]}},{"title":"Consumption goods versus capital goods","body":"Consumption goods directly satisfy human wants. They include durable goods, non-durable goods, semi-durable goods and services.\n\nCapital goods are fixed assets of producers, repeatedly used in production: durable-use producer goods such as plant and machinery. A household car is a durable consumption good; a taxi used to produce transport services is a capital good.\n\nBoth are final goods. A capital good is not intermediate merely because it helps produce something.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.1.1 Final Goods · Consumption Goods · Capital Goods"},{"title":"Consumption expenditure and investment","body":"Consumption expenditure includes expenditure by households, government and non-profit private institutions. Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital.\n\nFixed investment adds to fixed assets; inventory investment changes stocks of raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods. New residential construction is investment.\n\nBuying existing shares transfers financial ownership; it does not itself create new capital goods. Inventory investment can be negative when closing stock is less than opening stock.","formula":"Inventory investment = Closing stock − Opening stock\nGross investment = Fixed investment + Inventory investment","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.2 CONSUMPTION EXPENDITURE · 2.3 INVESTMENT"},{"title":"Gross investment, net investment and depreciation","body":"Net investment refers to the increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year. Gross investment includes replacement of worn-out capital.\n\nDepreciation, also called consumption of fixed capital, refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use through normal wear and tear, normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence. Unexpected destruction is a capital loss rather than normal depreciation.\n\nA depreciation reserve fund is kept to cope with depreciation losses.","formula":"Net investment = Gross investment − Depreciation\nDepreciation per year = (Capital value − Scrap value) ÷ Estimated life","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.3.3 Gross Investment, Net Investment and the Concept of Depreciation"},{"title":"Stocks and flows","body":"Stocks are quantities measured at a point of time; flows are quantities measured over a specified period of time. Wealth, money supply, capital and bank balances are stocks.\n\nIncome, consumption, investment and saving are flows. Capital is a stock; investment is the flow adding to it. “Money supply on 31 March” is a stock even though money changes hands repeatedly.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.4 STOCKS AND FLOWS","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Stock","Flow"],"rows":[["Time","Measured at a point in time","Measured over a period"],["Examples","Wealth, capital, inventories","Income, investment, change in stocks"],["Link","An accumulated quantity","Adds to or subtracts from a stock"]]}},{"title":"Four sectors and circular flow of income","body":"The household sector supplies factor services and consumes goods. The producing sector produces goods and services.\n\nGovernment acts as a welfare agency and producer; the external sector links the domestic economy with the rest of the world. Real flows are flows of goods and factor services; money flows are payments and receipts in the opposite direction.\n\nCircular flow of income refers to the circularity of production, income and expenditure across sectors. In a simple two-sector model, production generates income and income generates expenditure.","formula":"Production → Income generation → Expenditure → Production\nValue of output = Factor income = Final expenditure","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.5 FOUR SECTORS · 2.6 INTERSECTORAL FLOWS · 2.7 CIRCULAR FLOW OF INCOME","blocks":[{"title":"Household sector","text":"The household sector supplies factor services and consumes goods.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Producer sector","text":"The producing sector produces goods and services.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Government sector","text":"Government acts as a welfare agency and producer.","tone":"point"},{"title":"External sector","text":"The external sector links the domestic economy with the rest of the world.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Real flows","text":"Real flows are flows of goods and factor services.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Money flows","text":"Money flows are payments and receipts in the opposite direction.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Circular flow of income refers to the circularity of production, income and expenditure across sectors. In a simple two-sector model, production generates income and income generates expenditure.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Types of consumption goods","textbookHeading":"2.1.1 (a) Consumption Goods or Consumer Goods","body":"Durable-use consumer goods serve consumers repeatedly over a long period, such as cars and refrigerators. Semi-durable goods serve over a shorter period, such as clothes.\n\nNon-durable goods are used up in a single act of consumption, such as food. Services are intangible acts satisfying wants, such as teaching or medical treatment.\n\nDurability alone does not make an item a capital good: capital goods are used by producers in production.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"Durable-use consumer goods","text":"Durable-use consumer goods serve consumers repeatedly over a long period, such as cars and refrigerators.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Semi-durable goods","text":"Semi-durable goods serve over a shorter period, such as clothes.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Non-durable goods","text":"Non-durable goods are used up in a single act of consumption, such as food.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Services","text":"Services are intangible acts satisfying wants, such as teaching or medical treatment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Durability alone does not make an item a capital good: capital goods are used by producers in production.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Why final goods are counted and intermediate goods are not","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Significance of the Distinction between Intermediate Goods and Final Goods","body":"Intermediate inputs are embodied in final output. Counting both adds the same value more than once.\n\nIf cloth worth ₹5,000 becomes shirts worth ₹7,000, final output is ₹7,000, not ₹12,000. Under the value-added method, the cloth producer’s value and the shirt producer’s additional ₹2,000 are each counted once.\n\nIt is incorrect to say intermediate goods contribute nothing: their contribution is included in final value.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""},{"formula":"","title":"Fixed investment and inventory investment: why each matters","textbookHeading":"2.3.2 Components of Investment · Significance","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-fixed-investment-and-inventory-investment-why-each-matters","body":"Investment adds to capital. Fixed investment concerns productive assets; inventory investment concerns changes in stocks of inputs and output.\n\n**Fixed Investment:** An increase in producers’ fixed assets, such as plant and machinery, adds productive capacity. For example, increasing a stock from eight to ten machines adds two machines. Higher capacity supports higher output and economic growth.\n\n**Inventory Investment:** Inventory includes raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods. Inventory investment is closing inventory minus opening inventory; it can be positive or negative. It is a flow even though inventory itself is a stock.\n\n**Why keep raw-material stocks?:** They ensure uninterrupted supplies of inputs and reduce the need for daily market purchases. This protects production against uncertainty about input prices and availability.\n\n**Why keep finished-goods stocks?:** They enable producers to meet expected future demand promptly. Desired inventory is planned; undesired inventory accumulates when sales fall short of expectations and can impose losses.\n\n**Significance of Net Investment:** Net investment adds to the existing capital stock, expands productive capacity and creates employment opportunities. Gross investment also includes replacement of worn-out assets, so a large gross figure does not necessarily imply a large net expansion.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Investment adds to capital. Fixed investment concerns productive assets; inventory investment concerns changes in stocks of inputs and output.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Fixed Investment","text":"An increase in producers’ fixed assets, such as plant and machinery, adds productive capacity. For example, increasing a stock from eight to ten machines adds two machines. Higher capacity supports higher output and economic growth.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Inventory Investment","text":"Inventory includes raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods. Inventory investment is closing inventory minus opening inventory; it can be positive or negative. It is a flow even though inventory itself is a stock.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Why keep raw-material stocks?","text":"They ensure uninterrupted supplies of inputs and reduce the need for daily market purchases. This protects production against uncertainty about input prices and availability.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Why keep finished-goods stocks?","text":"They enable producers to meet expected future demand promptly. Desired inventory is planned; undesired inventory accumulates when sales fall short of expectations and can impose losses.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Significance of Net Investment","text":"Net investment adds to the existing capital stock, expands productive capacity and creates employment opportunities. Gross investment also includes replacement of worn-out assets, so a large gross figure does not necessarily imply a large net expansion.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Circular flow: three phases, assumptions and significance","textbookHeading":"2.7.2–2.7.4 Circular Flow of Income","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-circular-flow-three-phases-assumptions-and-significance","body":"Production, income generation and expenditure form an unending circular flow. Each phase leads to the next, giving three ways of measuring the same economic activity.\n\n**1 · Phase of Production:** Producers hire land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship from households and use these with intermediate inputs to add value. Turning wood worth ₹5,000 into chairs worth ₹10,000 creates ₹5,000 of value added.\n\n**2 · Phase of Income Generation / Distribution:** Value added becomes factor income: rent for land, wages and salaries for labour, interest for capital and profit for entrepreneurship. What firms pay as factor payments, households receive as factor incomes.\n\n**3 · Phase of Disposition / Expenditure:** Income is spent on final goods: household consumption and producers’ investment. Expenditure creates demand, inducing further production. Thus production generates income, income finances expenditure, and expenditure supports production.\n\n**Assumptions of the Simple Two-Sector Model:** Only households and producers exist; households spend their entire income, so there is no saving; the economy is closed, so there are no exports or imports; and there is no government. State these when explaining the simplest circular-flow diagram.\n\n**Triple Identity:** Value of production = income generated = expenditure on final output. Money flows express the value of real flows and move in the opposite direction to goods and factor services.\n\n**Significance: Estimation of National Income:** Circular flow explains the production, income and expenditure approaches. Measure value added, sum factor incomes, or sum final expenditure; each views the same flow from a different angle.\n\n**Significance: Intersectoral Interdependence:** Households depend on producers for goods and income; producers depend on households for factor services and demand. The model makes these mutual dependencies visible.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Production, income generation and expenditure form an unending circular flow. Each phase leads to the next, giving three ways of measuring the same economic activity.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Phase of Production","text":"Producers hire land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship from households and use these with intermediate inputs to add value. Turning wood worth ₹5,000 into chairs worth ₹10,000 creates ₹5,000 of value added.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Phase of Income Generation / Distribution","text":"Value added becomes factor income: rent for land, wages and salaries for labour, interest for capital and profit for entrepreneurship. What firms pay as factor payments, households receive as factor incomes.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Phase of Disposition / Expenditure","text":"Income is spent on final goods: household consumption and producers’ investment. Expenditure creates demand, inducing further production. Thus production generates income, income finances expenditure, and expenditure supports production.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Assumptions of the Simple Two-Sector Model","text":"Only households and producers exist; households spend their entire income, so there is no saving; the economy is closed, so there are no exports or imports; and there is no government. State these when explaining the simplest circular-flow diagram.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Triple Identity","text":"Value of production = income generated = expenditure on final output. Money flows express the value of real flows and move in the opposite direction to goods and factor services.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Significance: Estimation of National Income","text":"Circular flow explains the production, income and expenditure approaches. Measure value added, sum factor incomes, or sum final expenditure; each views the same flow from a different angle.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Significance: Intersectoral Interdependence","text":"Households depend on producers for goods and income; producers depend on households for factor services and demand. The model makes these mutual dependencies visible.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Depreciation, replacement and the reserve fund","textbookHeading":"2.3.3 Depreciation Reserve Fund","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-depreciation-replacement-and-the-reserve-fund","body":"Depreciation is consumption of fixed capital: loss of value while fixed assets are used. Replacement maintains capacity; net investment expands it.\n\n**Normal Wear and Tear:** Repeated use wears out machines and other fixed assets. A part of gross investment must replace the capital consumed rather than expand the stock.\n\n**Accidental Damage and Expected Obsolescence:** The chapter includes normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence due to changes in technology or demand. Unexpected catastrophic destruction is a capital loss, distinguished from normal depreciation.\n\n**Depreciation Reserve Fund:** Producers set aside funds to replace worn-out fixed assets. With a ₹10 lakh machine and an expected ten-year life, ignoring scrap value, an annual provision of ₹1 lakh finances replacement at the end of its life.\n\n**Why the fund matters:** Without replacement finance, productive assets deteriorate and capacity may fall. Lower investment can reduce output, income and employment, contributing to economic slowdown. Gross investment = net investment + depreciation.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Depreciation is consumption of fixed capital: loss of value while fixed assets are used. Replacement maintains capacity; net investment expands it.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Normal Wear and Tear","text":"Repeated use wears out machines and other fixed assets. A part of gross investment must replace the capital consumed rather than expand the stock.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Accidental Damage and Expected Obsolescence","text":"The chapter includes normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence due to changes in technology or demand. Unexpected catastrophic destruction is a capital loss, distinguished from normal depreciation.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Depreciation Reserve Fund","text":"Producers set aside funds to replace worn-out fixed assets. With a ₹10 lakh machine and an expected ten-year life, ignoring scrap value, an annual provision of ₹1 lakh finances replacement at the end of its life.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Why the fund matters","text":"Without replacement finance, productive assets deteriorate and capacity may fall. Lower investment can reduce output, income and employment, contributing to economic slowdown. Gross investment = net investment + depreciation.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Four sectors and the flows connecting them","textbookHeading":"2.5–2.6 Four Sectors and Intersectoral Interdependence","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-four-sectors-and-the-flows-connecting-them","body":"Each sector depends on other sectors. Real flows move goods and services; money flows record the corresponding payments and receipts.\n\n**Household Sector:** Households consume goods and services and own factors of production. They supply land, labour, capital and entrepreneurial services to producers and receive factor incomes.\n\n**Producer Sector:** Firms hire or purchase factor services and combine them with intermediate inputs to produce goods and services. They depend on households for inputs and demand.\n\n**Government Sector:** Government provides welfare, administration, law and order and defence, and also acts as a producer. It depends on households and firms for tax and non-tax revenue; they depend on it for public services.\n\n**External Sector:** The rest of the world connects through exports, imports and cross-border capital flows. A closed economy excludes this sector; an open economy includes it.\n\n**Real Flows:** Factor services move from households to firms; goods and services move from firms to households. These are actual resource and output flows.\n\n**Money Flows:** Factor payments move from firms to households; consumption expenditure moves from households to firms. They travel opposite to the corresponding real flows and keep income circulating.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Each sector depends on other sectors. Real flows move goods and services; money flows record the corresponding payments and receipts.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Household Sector","text":"Households consume goods and services and own factors of production. They supply land, labour, capital and entrepreneurial services to producers and receive factor incomes.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Producer Sector","text":"Firms hire or purchase factor services and combine them with intermediate inputs to produce goods and services. They depend on households for inputs and demand.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Government Sector","text":"Government provides welfare, administration, law and order and defence, and also acts as a producer. It depends on households and firms for tax and non-tax revenue; they depend on it for public services.","tone":"point"},{"title":"External Sector","text":"The rest of the world connects through exports, imports and cross-border capital flows. A closed economy excludes this sector; an open economy includes it.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Real Flows","text":"Factor services move from households to firms; goods and services move from firms to households. These are actual resource and output flows.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Money Flows","text":"Factor payments move from firms to households; consumption expenditure moves from households to firms. They travel opposite to the corresponding real flows and keep income circulating.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"2","flow":[["Households","Supply factors of production."],["Firms","Pay factor incomes and produce goods."],["Spending","Households buy goods and services."],["Circular flow","Money and real resources move in opposite directions."]]},{"id":"3","title":"National Income and Related Aggregates","intro":"National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year.","lessons":[{"title":"National income, factor incomes and transfer incomes","body":"National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year. Factor incomes are rewards for factor services: compensation of employees, rent, interest and profit.\n\nTransfer incomes such as gifts, charity and old-age pensions are unilateral receipts without a current productive service in return; they are excluded. An employer’s retirement pension earned through service differs from an old-age welfare pension.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.1 CONCEPT OF NATIONAL INCOME"},{"title":"Normal residents and domestic territory","body":"A normal resident ordinarily resides in a country and has a centre of economic interest there. Citizenship alone does not determine residence.\n\nTourists and visitors for medical treatment do not become residents merely through travel. Diplomats remain residents of their home countries; border workers are residents where they live.\n\nDomestic territory is economic territory: Indian embassies abroad belong to India’s domestic territory; foreign embassies in India do not. Ships, aircraft, fishing vessels and floating platforms require the domestic-territory tests explained in the special-cases section.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.1.3 Normal Residents · 3.2.2 Domestic Territory"},{"title":"Domestic income versus national income","body":"Domestic income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic territory, whether earned by residents or non-residents. National income belongs to normal residents, wherever earned.\n\nNet factor income from abroad is factor income received from the rest of the world minus factor income paid to the rest of the world. Remittances that are gifts are transfers, not NFIA.","formula":"NNP at FC = NDP at FC + NFIA\nNFIA = Factor income from abroad − Factor income to abroad","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.2 DOMESTIC AND NATIONAL CONCEPTS OF INCOME","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Domestic income","National income"],"rows":[["Boundary","Domestic territory","Normal residents"],["Includes","Residents and non-residents producing within the territory","Residents’ factor incomes from home and abroad"],["Bridge","Domestic income + NFIA","National income"]]}},{"title":"The three conversion bridges","body":"Use three independent adjustments. Gross to net: subtract depreciation.\n\nDomestic to national: add NFIA. Market price to factor cost: subtract net indirect taxes, which equal indirect taxes minus subsidies.\n\nReverse the sign when travelling backwards. A negative NFIA reduces national income; negative NIT means subsidies exceed indirect taxes.\n\nDo not deduct indirect taxes again if NIT is already supplied.","formula":"NNPFC = GDPMP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA\nGDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.3 GROSS AND NET · 3.4 MARKET PRICE AND FACTOR COST","blocks":[{"title":"","text":"Use three independent adjustments.","tone":"prose"},{"title":"Gross to net","text":"Subtract depreciation.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Domestic to national","text":"Add NFIA.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Market price to factor cost","text":"Subtract net indirect taxes, which equal indirect taxes minus subsidies.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Reverse the sign when travelling backwards. A negative NFIA reduces national income; negative NIT means subsidies exceed indirect taxes.\n\nDo not deduct indirect taxes again if NIT is already supplied.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Eight related aggregates","body":"GDPMP, GDPFC, NDPMP and NDPFC are domestic measures; GNPMP, GNPFC, NNPMP and NNPFC are national measures. Gross measures include consumption of fixed capital; net measures exclude it.\n\nMarket-price measures include net indirect taxes; factor-cost measures exclude them. National income means NNPFC; domestic income means NDPFC.\n\nIdentify all three labels before substituting a number.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.5 AGGREGATES RELATED TO NATIONAL INCOME"},{"title":"Nominal GDP, real GDP and GDP deflator","body":"Nominal GDP is valued at current-year prices; real GDP is valued at base-year prices. Real GDP helps isolate changes in physical production from changes in prices.\n\nNominal GDP may rise with unchanged output simply because prices rise. The GDP deflator is the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP multiplied by 100.\n\nUse the price index as a percentage index, not as a decimal, in the formula below.","formula":"Nominal GDP = Σ(Current price × Current quantity)\nReal GDP = Σ(Base-year price × Current quantity)\nGDP deflator = Nominal GDP ÷ Real GDP × 100\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP ÷ GDP deflator × 100","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.6 NOMINAL AND REAL GDP","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Nominal GDP","Real GDP"],"rows":[["Prices","Current-year prices","Base-year prices"],["Change can reflect","Quantity and price changes","Changes in output quantities"],["Use","Money value of current output","Comparison of production over time"]]},"blocks":[{"title":"Nominal GDP","text":"Nominal GDP is valued at current-year prices.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Real GDP","text":"Real GDP is valued at base-year prices.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Real GDP helps isolate changes in physical production from changes in prices.\n\nNominal GDP may rise with unchanged output simply because prices rise. The GDP deflator is the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP multiplied by 100.\n\nUse the price index as a percentage index, not as a decimal, in the formula below.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"GDP and welfare: all four limitations","body":"Real GDP growth can increase the goods and services available to people. However, the textbook gives four separate reasons why GDP is an incomplete index of welfare.\n\n**1 · Distribution of Income:** If additional income is concentrated among the rich, rising GDP need not improve living standards for most people. An increasing divide between rich and poor can coexist with high aggregate growth.\n\n**2 · Composition of GDP:** What is produced matters. More defence goods can contribute indirectly through security, but do not directly increase household consumption. A rise in luxuries for the rich while necessities remain scarce may do little for broad welfare.\n\n**3 · Non-monetary Exchanges / Transactions:** Barter and payments in kind may go unrecorded or be inadequately measured, understating output and making GDP a weaker welfare measure. Production for own consumption is conceptually included where it can be imputed; absence of cash does not automatically make productive activity non-economic.\n\n**4 · Externalities:** Externalities are benefits or costs imposed on others without a price or compensation. A neighbour enjoys a beautiful garden without paying: positive externality. Factory smoke or polluted water harms others without adequate compensation: negative externality. GDP does not directly adjust for these unpriced welfare effects.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.7.1 Limitations of GDP as an Index of Welfare","key":"restored-gdp-and-welfare-all-four-limitations","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Real GDP growth can increase the goods and services available to people. However, the textbook gives four separate reasons why GDP is an incomplete index of welfare.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Distribution of Income","text":"If additional income is concentrated among the rich, rising GDP need not improve living standards for most people. An increasing divide between rich and poor can coexist with high aggregate growth.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Composition of GDP","text":"What is produced matters. More defence goods can contribute indirectly through security, but do not directly increase household consumption. A rise in luxuries for the rich while necessities remain scarce may do little for broad welfare.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Non-monetary Exchanges / Transactions","text":"Barter and payments in kind may go unrecorded or be inadequately measured, understating output and making GDP a weaker welfare measure. Production for own consumption is conceptually included where it can be imputed; absence of cash does not automatically make productive activity non-economic.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Externalities","text":"Externalities are benefits or costs imposed on others without a price or compensation. A neighbour enjoys a beautiful garden without paying: positive externality. Factory smoke or polluted water harms others without adequate compensation: negative externality. GDP does not directly adjust for these unpriced welfare effects.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Special cases of residents and territory","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Normal Residents of a Country · Components of Domestic Territory","body":"Normal Residents: citizens of another country can be residents here when their centre of economic interest is here; officials and diplomats serving abroad remain residents of their home country; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic Territory: include a country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad, ships and aircraft operated by its residents between countries, and fishing vessels and floating platforms operated by residents in international waters under the accounting conventions.\n\nExclude foreign embassies and international organisations located inside its political borders. Residence and territory are separate tests.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"Normal Residents","text":"Citizens of another country can be residents here when their centre of economic interest is here; officials and diplomats serving abroad remain residents of their home country; border workers are residents where they live.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Domestic Territory","text":"Include a country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad, ships and aircraft operated by its residents between countries, and fishing vessels and floating platforms operated by residents in international waters under the accounting conventions.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations located inside its political borders. Residence and territory are separate tests.","tone":"prose"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Domestic territory and why product equals income","textbookHeading":"3.2.2 Domestic Territory · 3.2.4 Domestic Product","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-domestic-territory-and-why-product-equals-income","body":"Domestic territory is an economic accounting boundary, not simply the geographical land within political borders.\n\n**Geographical Territory and Economic Activity:** It includes territory administered by the government within which persons, goods and capital circulate, subject to the accounting conventions used in the chapter.\n\n**Embassies and Establishments Abroad:** Include the country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad. Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations situated within its geographical borders.\n\n**Ships and Aircraft:** Include ships and aircraft operated by residents between countries under the domestic-territory convention. Physical movement across a border does not by itself change the operator’s economic territory.\n\n**Fishing Vessels and Floating Platforms:** Include resident-operated fishing vessels, oil and gas rigs and floating platforms in international waters, and the relevant extraction rights specified in the accounting convention.\n\n**Domestic Product = Domestic Income:** Production creates value added, and value added is distributed as factor income. Thus the product and income are two views of the same activity, provided the gross/net and price basis are consistent.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Domestic territory is an economic accounting boundary, not simply the geographical land within political borders.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Geographical Territory and Economic Activity","text":"It includes territory administered by the government within which persons, goods and capital circulate, subject to the accounting conventions used in the chapter.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Embassies and Establishments Abroad","text":"Include the country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad. Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations situated within its geographical borders.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Ships and Aircraft","text":"Include ships and aircraft operated by residents between countries under the domestic-territory convention. Physical movement across a border does not by itself change the operator’s economic territory.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fishing Vessels and Floating Platforms","text":"Include resident-operated fishing vessels, oil and gas rigs and floating platforms in international waters, and the relevant extraction rights specified in the accounting convention.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Domestic Product = Domestic Income","text":"Production creates value added, and value added is distributed as factor income. Thus the product and income are two views of the same activity, provided the gross/net and price basis are consistent.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"3","flow":[["Domestic → national","Add NFIA."],["Gross → net","Subtract depreciation."],["Market price → factor cost","Subtract net indirect taxes."]]},{"id":"4","title":"Measurement of National Income","intro":"Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption.","lessons":[{"title":"Value added method: measure production once","body":"Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption. Value of output equals sales plus change in stock, with own-account production added where applicable.\n\nSum GVA at market price across producing units to obtain GDPMP. Deduct depreciation and NIT, then add NFIA for national income.\n\nAlternatively, sum sectoral NVAFC and add NFIA.","formula":"Value of output = Sales + Closing stock − Opening stock\nGVA MP = Value of output − Intermediate consumption\nNNPFC = ΣGVA MP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.2 VALUE ADDED METHOD (OR PRODUCT METHOD)"},{"title":"Value-added method: all inclusions and exclusions","body":"Count value created during the current year once. Both the inclusion decision and the reason are important in theory and classification questions.\n\n**Include: Commission on Second-hand Goods:** The old good itself was counted when produced, but the broker’s current service is new production and must be counted.\n\n**Include: Own-account Production:** Goods made by a producing unit for its own use are included. A car producer using a newly produced car to transport employees still creates current output.\n\n**Include: Goods for Self-consumption:** Impute the value of goods such as wheat produced and consumed by a farming family. Not being sold does not mean that the good was not produced.\n\n**Include: Imputed Rent:** An owner-occupied house provides housing services just as a rented house does. Its imputed rent is included.\n\n**Exclude: Resale of Second-hand Goods:** Reselling an existing asset creates no new output of that asset in the current year. Include only any current service involved in the sale.\n\n**Exclude: Intermediate Goods as a Separate Addition:** Their value is already embodied in final output. Either count final goods or sum value added at each stage, never add both.\n\n**Exclude: Own-household Domestic Services:** Unpaid domestic services within one’s own household are excluded under the textbook’s measurement convention. Paid domestic services are different.\n\n**Illegal Activities: Textbook Treatment:** The chapter excludes these from its measured estimates because reliable recorded data are unavailable. Use this stated treatment for its classification questions.\n\n**Double Counting Example:** Wheat ₹100 → flour ₹160 → bread ₹250. Values added are ₹100, ₹60 and ₹90, totalling ₹250. Adding sales of all three stages gives ₹510 and counts the same intermediate value repeatedly.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.2.2 Precautions · 4.2.3 Double Counting","key":"restored-value-added-method-all-inclusions-and-exclusions","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Count value created during the current year once. Both the inclusion decision and the reason are important in theory and classification questions.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Include: Commission on Second-hand Goods","text":"The old good itself was counted when produced, but the broker’s current service is new production and must be counted.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Include: Own-account Production","text":"Goods made by a producing unit for its own use are included. A car producer using a newly produced car to transport employees still creates current output.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Include: Goods for Self-consumption","text":"Impute the value of goods such as wheat produced and consumed by a farming family. Not being sold does not mean that the good was not produced.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Include: Imputed Rent","text":"An owner-occupied house provides housing services just as a rented house does. Its imputed rent is included.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Resale of Second-hand Goods","text":"Reselling an existing asset creates no new output of that asset in the current year. Include only any current service involved in the sale.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Intermediate Goods as a Separate Addition","text":"Their value is already embodied in final output. Either count final goods or sum value added at each stage, never add both.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Own-household Domestic Services","text":"Unpaid domestic services within one’s own household are excluded under the textbook’s measurement convention. Paid domestic services are different.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Illegal Activities: Textbook Treatment","text":"The chapter excludes these from its measured estimates because reliable recorded data are unavailable. Use this stated treatment for its classification questions.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Double Counting Example","text":"Wheat ₹100 → flour ₹160 → bread ₹250. Values added are ₹100, ₹60 and ₹90, totalling ₹250. Adding sales of all three stages gives ₹510 and counts the same intermediate value repeatedly.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Income method: compensation, surplus and mixed income","body":"Income method measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes generated within domestic territory. Compensation of employees includes wages and salaries in cash and kind and employers’ social-security contributions.\n\nOperating surplus comprises rent and royalty, interest and profit. Profit includes corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings; these are components, so do not add them again if total profit is given.\n\nMixed income of the self-employed combines labour and property income that cannot be separated.","formula":"NDPFC = Compensation of employees + Operating surplus + Mixed income\nOperating surplus = Rent and royalty + Interest + Profit\nNational income = NDPFC + NFIA","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.3 INCOME METHOD","blocks":[{"title":"","text":"Income method measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes generated within domestic territory.","tone":"prose"},{"title":"Compensation of employees","text":"Compensation of employees includes wages and salaries in cash and kind and employers’ social-security contributions.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Operating surplus","text":"Operating surplus comprises rent and royalty, interest and profit. Profit includes corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings; these are components, so do not add them again if total profit is given.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Mixed income of the self-employed","text":"Mixed income of the self-employed combines labour and property income that cannot be separated.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Income method: all inclusions and exclusions","body":"Include income earned from current productive services. Distinguish a productive reward from a transfer, an asset transaction or a capital gain.\n\n**Include: Retirement Pension:** An employment-related retirement pension is treated as compensation for past service in the chapter. It differs from a welfare old-age pension, which is a transfer.\n\n**Include: Current Commissions and Brokerage:** Commission on second-hand goods and brokerage on shares or bonds are rewards for current services. The sale price of the old asset or financial security itself is not factor income.\n\n**Include: Imputed Rent and Self-consumption:** Owner-occupied housing services and goods produced for own consumption generate current income even when no cash sale occurs.\n\n**Include: Productive Interest:** Interest paid by producing units on capital borrowed for production is factor income. Interest on consumption loans is excluded in the textbook treatment.\n\n**Exclude: Transfer Earnings:** Welfare pensions, unemployment allowances, scholarships and pocket money have no corresponding productive service and are not national income.\n\n**Exclude: Illegal Income, Windfall and Capital Gains:** The chapter excludes unrecorded illegal income; lottery winnings and gains from asset-price changes are not rewards for current production.\n\n**Exclude: Spending from Accumulated Savings:** The transfer or use of earlier savings does not itself create current factor income. Count any current goods or services purchased through the relevant production accounts instead.\n\n**Exclude: Indirect Taxes from Factor-cost Income:** Indirect taxes are part of market prices but not rewards paid to factors. Deduct net indirect taxes when converting market-price output to factor-cost income.\n\n**Avoid Repeating Profit Components:** Corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings together form profit. If total profit is supplied, do not add those components again. Likewise, do not add rent or interest again if operating surplus already includes them.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.3.2 Precautions Regarding Income Method","key":"restored-income-method-all-inclusions-and-exclusions","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Include income earned from current productive services. Distinguish a productive reward from a transfer, an asset transaction or a capital gain.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Include: Retirement Pension","text":"An employment-related retirement pension is treated as compensation for past service in the chapter. It differs from a welfare old-age pension, which is a transfer.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Include: Current Commissions and Brokerage","text":"Commission on second-hand goods and brokerage on shares or bonds are rewards for current services. The sale price of the old asset or financial security itself is not factor income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Include: Imputed Rent and Self-consumption","text":"Owner-occupied housing services and goods produced for own consumption generate current income even when no cash sale occurs.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Include: Productive Interest","text":"Interest paid by producing units on capital borrowed for production is factor income. Interest on consumption loans is excluded in the textbook treatment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Transfer Earnings","text":"Welfare pensions, unemployment allowances, scholarships and pocket money have no corresponding productive service and are not national income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Illegal Income, Windfall and Capital Gains","text":"The chapter excludes unrecorded illegal income; lottery winnings and gains from asset-price changes are not rewards for current production.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Spending from Accumulated Savings","text":"The transfer or use of earlier savings does not itself create current factor income. Count any current goods or services purchased through the relevant production accounts instead.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Indirect Taxes from Factor-cost Income","text":"Indirect taxes are part of market prices but not rewards paid to factors. Deduct net indirect taxes when converting market-price output to factor-cost income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Avoid Repeating Profit Components","text":"Corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings together form profit. If total profit is supplied, do not add those components again. Likewise, do not add rent or interest again if operating surplus already includes them.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Expenditure method and its precautions","body":"Expenditure method measures final expenditure on domestically produced output. Add private final consumption expenditure, government final consumption expenditure, gross domestic capital formation and net exports.\n\nGross domestic capital formation includes fixed capital formation and change in stocks. Exclude financial-asset purchases, transfers and second-hand purchases; include current brokerage.\n\nSubtract imports because imported content may already be included in consumption or investment. Do not add change in stock twice if gross domestic capital formation already includes it.","formula":"GDPMP = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M)\nNNPFC = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M) − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.4 EXPENDITURE METHOD","blocks":[{"tone":"definition","title":"What it measures","text":"Final expenditure on domestically produced output during the accounting year."},{"tone":"point","title":"1 · Private consumption (PFCE)","text":"Private final consumption expenditure by households and non-profit institutions serving households."},{"tone":"point","title":"2 · Government consumption (GFCE)","text":"Government final consumption expenditure on goods and services."},{"tone":"point","title":"3 · Capital formation (GDCF)","text":"Gross domestic capital formation includes fixed capital formation and change in stocks."},{"tone":"point","title":"4 · Net exports (X − M)","text":"Add exports and subtract imports: imported content may already be included in consumption or investment."},{"tone":"caution","title":"Exclude these","text":"Financial-asset purchases, transfers and second-hand purchases are not expenditure on current output. Include the current brokerage service."},{"tone":"caution","title":"Avoid counting stocks twice","text":"Do not add change in stock again if gross domestic capital formation already includes it."}]},{"title":"Reconciliation and treatment of tricky items","body":"The three methods measure the same current production from different sides: value added creates factor income, and income finances final expenditure. In an exam, classify each item before calculating.\n\nGovernment services are counted even when provided free; employer provident-fund contributions belong in compensation. Scholarships and welfare pensions are transfers.\n\nForeign factor earnings require a residence/territory check; the purchase of a newly produced house is investment. Use the territory and normal-resident tests to classify domestic and national factor income.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.5 RECONCILIATION OF THREE METHODS · 4.6 TREATMENT OF DIFFERENT ITEMS"},{"title":"Steps under the three measurement methods","textbookHeading":"4.2.1 Steps · 4.3.1 Steps · 4.4.1 Steps","body":"Value Added Method: identify and classify producing units; estimate each unit’s output and intermediate consumption; obtain sectoral value added; aggregate and make depreciation, tax and NFIA adjustments to national income. Income Method: identify producing enterprises; classify factor incomes into compensation, operating surplus and mixed income; sum them to obtain domestic income; add NFIA.\n\nExpenditure Method: identify final expenditure by sectors; classify consumption, investment and net exports; sum to GDPMP; convert to NNPFC. Keep the starting aggregate clear because the adjustments depend on whether it is gross/net and market price/factor cost.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"Value Added Method","text":"Identify and classify producing units; estimate each unit’s output and intermediate consumption; obtain sectoral value added; aggregate and make depreciation, tax and NFIA adjustments to national income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Income Method","text":"Identify producing enterprises; classify factor incomes into compensation, operating surplus and mixed income; sum them to obtain domestic income; add NFIA.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Expenditure Method","text":"Identify final expenditure by sectors; classify consumption, investment and net exports; sum to GDPMP; convert to NNPFC.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Keep the starting aggregate clear because the adjustments depend on whether it is gross/net and market price/factor cost.","tone":"prose"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Expenditure method: the complete precautions","textbookHeading":"4.4.2 Precautions Regarding Expenditure Method","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-expenditure-method-the-complete-precautions","body":"Final spending on current domestic output counts. A transaction moving money or ownership is not automatically expenditure on newly produced goods and services.\n\n**Include: Final Expenditure:** Include private consumption, government final consumption, gross domestic capital formation and net exports. Investment includes fixed capital and change in inventories.\n\n**Include: Brokerage and Commission:** Current brokerage on financial securities and commissions on second-hand goods purchase new services, even though the underlying asset purchase is excluded.\n\n**Include: Imputed Expenditure:** Include the imputed value of goods produced for own consumption and owner-occupied housing services.\n\n**Exclude: Intermediate Expenditure:** Its value is already included in final expenditure. Adding it separately would double count the same output.\n\n**Exclude: Second-hand Purchases:** These purchase output produced in an earlier period. They do not add current output, apart from current sale-related services.\n\n**Exclude: Financial-asset Purchases:** Buying shares, debentures and bonds exchanges financial claims. It does not itself purchase a newly produced final good or service.\n\n**Exclude: Transfer Payments:** Welfare pensions and scholarships transfer purchasing power without corresponding value addition. A later purchase of current output using that money is a separate transaction.\n\n**Net Exports and Stocks:** Subtract imports to remove foreign output contained in expenditure. Add inventory change only if it is not already included in gross domestic capital formation.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Final spending on current domestic output counts. A transaction moving money or ownership is not automatically expenditure on newly produced goods and services.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Include: Final Expenditure","text":"Include private consumption, government final consumption, gross domestic capital formation and net exports. Investment includes fixed capital and change in inventories.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Include: Brokerage and Commission","text":"Current brokerage on financial securities and commissions on second-hand goods purchase new services, even though the underlying asset purchase is excluded.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Include: Imputed Expenditure","text":"Include the imputed value of goods produced for own consumption and owner-occupied housing services.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Intermediate Expenditure","text":"Its value is already included in final expenditure. Adding it separately would double count the same output.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Second-hand Purchases","text":"These purchase output produced in an earlier period. They do not add current output, apart from current sale-related services.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Financial-asset Purchases","text":"Buying shares, debentures and bonds exchanges financial claims. It does not itself purchase a newly produced final good or service.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Exclude: Transfer Payments","text":"Welfare pensions and scholarships transfer purchasing power without corresponding value addition. A later purchase of current output using that money is a separate transaction.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Net Exports and Stocks","text":"Subtract imports to remove foreign output contained in expenditure. Add inventory change only if it is not already included in gross domestic capital formation.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"4","flow":[["Production","Add value at each stage."],["Income","Add the factor incomes generated."],["Expenditure","Add spending on final domestic output."]]},{"id":"5","title":"Money","intro":"Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange.","lessons":[{"title":"Money, barter and the evolution of money","body":"Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange. Barter exchanges goods directly for goods.\n\nIts difficulties include lack of double coincidence of wants, lack of a common measure of value, difficulty storing wealth and difficulty making deferred payments. Money separates selling from buying: the seller need not want the precise good the buyer offers.\n\nEvolution moves through commodity money, metallic money, paper money and bank/credit money.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.1 MEANING AND EVOLUTION OF MONEY"},{"title":"Fiat, fiduciary and legal-tender money","body":"Fiat money is money issued by authority of the government. Fiduciary money is accepted on trust between payer and payee.\n\nLegal tender must be accepted in discharge of debts within the applicable legal conditions; a cheque depends on acceptance and is not legal tender. Full-bodied money has face value equal to intrinsic commodity value; credit money has face value greater than intrinsic value.\n\nFollow the textbook distinctions, not the everyday use of the word “cash”.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.2 FORMS OF MONEY","blocks":[{"title":"Fiat money","text":"Fiat money is money issued by authority of the government.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fiduciary money","text":"Fiduciary money is accepted on trust between payer and payee.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Legal tender","text":"Legal tender must be accepted in discharge of debts within the applicable legal conditions; a cheque depends on acceptance and is not legal tender.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Full-bodied money","text":"Full-bodied money has face value equal to intrinsic commodity value.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Credit money","text":"Credit money has face value greater than intrinsic value.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Follow the textbook distinctions, not the everyday use of the word “cash”.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Functions of money and why each matters","body":"Money has two primary functions and three secondary functions. Explain the function, illustrate it, and connect it to the economic benefit.\n\n**Primary 1 · Medium of Exchange:** Money is accepted between sale and purchase, removing the need for a double coincidence of wants. A farmer can sell wheat for money and later buy clothing from someone else. This expands exchange and markets, encourages production for sale rather than only self-consumption, and supports GDP growth.\n\n**Primary 2 · Measure of Value / Unit of Account:** Prices express different goods, services and assets in a common unit. This permits value comparisons, national-income estimation and price-index construction. A common unit also makes asset markets and investment decisions easier.\n\n**Secondary 1 · Standard of Deferred Payments:** Future debts, principal and interest can be expressed and settled in money. Repaying a monetary amount is simpler than returning goods of exactly the original quality. This supports financial markets, lending, consumption and investment.\n\n**Secondary 2 · Store of Value:** Money carries purchasing power into the future without the physical deterioration associated with many stored goods. Convenient saving can finance future investment; inflation nevertheless erodes real purchasing power. Saving supports growth when channelled into productive investment, while a widespread cut in spending can weaken demand.\n\n**Secondary 3 · Transfer of Value:** Money allows purchasing power to move between people, regions and countries. This supports capital mobility, global financial markets, multinational enterprises and foreign investment, and can help finance development in capital-scarce regions.\n\n**Static versus Dynamic Functions:** Static functions are these conventional exchange, value and payment functions. Dynamic functions concern money’s role in economic stability and growth: regulating its supply can restrain inflation or support demand during slowdown.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.3 Functions of Money · Critical Terms","key":"restored-functions-of-money-and-why-each-matters","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Money has two primary functions and three secondary functions. Explain the function, illustrate it, and connect it to the economic benefit.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Primary 1 · Medium of Exchange","text":"Money is accepted between sale and purchase, removing the need for a double coincidence of wants. A farmer can sell wheat for money and later buy clothing from someone else. This expands exchange and markets, encourages production for sale rather than only self-consumption, and supports GDP growth.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Primary 2 · Measure of Value / Unit of Account","text":"Prices express different goods, services and assets in a common unit. This permits value comparisons, national-income estimation and price-index construction. A common unit also makes asset markets and investment decisions easier.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Secondary 1 · Standard of Deferred Payments","text":"Future debts, principal and interest can be expressed and settled in money. Repaying a monetary amount is simpler than returning goods of exactly the original quality. This supports financial markets, lending, consumption and investment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Secondary 2 · Store of Value","text":"Money carries purchasing power into the future without the physical deterioration associated with many stored goods. Convenient saving can finance future investment; inflation nevertheless erodes real purchasing power. Saving supports growth when channelled into productive investment, while a widespread cut in spending can weaken demand.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Secondary 3 · Transfer of Value","text":"Money allows purchasing power to move between people, regions and countries. This supports capital mobility, global financial markets, multinational enterprises and foreign investment, and can help finance development in capital-scarce regions.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Static versus Dynamic Functions","text":"Static functions are these conventional exchange, value and payment functions. Dynamic functions concern money’s role in economic stability and growth: regulating its supply can restrain inflation or support demand during slowdown.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Money supply and its components","body":"Money supply is the stock of money held by the public at a particular point of time. Interbank deposits and money held by the government/banking system are excluded from money held by the public.\n\nM1 comprises currency with the public, demand deposits with commercial banks and other deposits with the RBI. Demand deposits are withdrawable on demand.\n\nA cheque transfers a deposit; it is not an additional independent stock of money.","formula":"M1 = Currency with public + Demand deposits with banks + Other deposits with RBI","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.4 SUPPLY OF MONEY"},{"title":"M2, M3, M4 and liquidity","body":"Broader money measures add less immediately spendable components to the most liquid measure, M1. These are stock measures at a point in time.\n\n**M1 · The Starting Point:** Currency with the public + demand deposits with commercial banks + other eligible deposits with the RBI. Government and interbank holdings are excluded from money held by the public.\n\n**M2:** M1 + deposits with Post Office Savings Bank accounts.\n\n**M3 · Aggregate Monetary Resources:** M1 + net time deposits with commercial banks. The chapter calls M3 aggregate monetary resources.\n\n**M4:** M3 + total deposits with post offices, excluding National Savings Certificates.\n\n**Narrow versus Broad Money:** The chapter groups M1/M2 as narrow-money concepts and M3/M4 as broad-money concepts. Specify which measure is being used before comparing money supply.\n\n**Liquidity:** Liquidity means ease and speed of conversion into cash. Currency is the most liquid asset; demand deposits are readily usable through payment instructions. Time deposits are less immediately accessible, so broader measures include less-liquid components.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"5.4 Ability Zone · Monetary Measures and Liquidity","key":"restored-m2-m3-m4-and-liquidity","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Broader money measures add less immediately spendable components to the most liquid measure, M1. These are stock measures at a point in time.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"M1 · The Starting Point","text":"Currency with the public + demand deposits with commercial banks + other eligible deposits with the RBI. Government and interbank holdings are excluded from money held by the public.","tone":"point"},{"title":"M2","text":"M1 + deposits with Post Office Savings Bank accounts.","tone":"point"},{"title":"M3 · Aggregate Monetary Resources","text":"M1 + net time deposits with commercial banks. The chapter calls M3 aggregate monetary resources.","tone":"point"},{"title":"M4","text":"M3 + total deposits with post offices, excluding National Savings Certificates.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Narrow versus Broad Money","text":"The chapter groups M1/M2 as narrow-money concepts and M3/M4 as broad-money concepts. Specify which measure is being used before comparing money supply.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Liquidity","text":"Liquidity means ease and speed of conversion into cash. Currency is the most liquid asset; demand deposits are readily usable through payment instructions. Time deposits are less immediately accessible, so broader measures include less-liquid components.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Why money solves barter’s difficulties","textbookHeading":"5.1.3 Barter System of Exchange · 5.3 Functions of Money","body":"Lack of Double Coincidence of Wants: money is accepted as an intermediary, so you can sell to one person and buy from another. Lack of a Common Measure of Value: prices expressed in money make values comparable.\n\nDifficulty in Storing Wealth: money offers a convenient store, though inflation can erode it. Difficulty in Deferred Payments: future payments can be fixed in monetary units.\n\nDifficulty in Transfer of Value: purchasing power can be transferred without moving bulky goods. In theory answers, state the barter difficulty first and then the function that addresses it.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"Lack of Double Coincidence of Wants","text":"Money is accepted as an intermediary, so you can sell to one person and buy from another.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Lack of a Common Measure of Value","text":"Prices expressed in money make values comparable.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Difficulty in Storing Wealth","text":"Money offers a convenient store, though inflation can erode it.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Difficulty in Deferred Payments","text":"Future payments can be fixed in monetary units.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Difficulty in Transfer of Value","text":"Purchasing power can be transferred without moving bulky goods.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"In theory answers, state the barter difficulty first and then the function that addresses it.","tone":"prose"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Evolution of money: stages, gains and limitations","textbookHeading":"5.1.2 Evolution of Money · Digital Currency Box","kind":"Concept and box","key":"restored-evolution-of-money-stages-gains-and-limitations","body":"The chapter traces a journey from barter to metallic currency, paper money, plastic money and electronic payments. Each stage eases some earlier difficulties while creating new issues.\n\n**1 · Barter System:** Goods exchange directly for goods, without money prices. Selling and buying must occur together, limiting exchange mainly to mutually matched needs. The chapter contrasts its lack of monetary-price uncertainty with its severe limits on trade.\n\n**2 · Metallic Currency:** Gold, silver and later alloy coins separate sale from purchase and expand markets. However, large payments are cumbersome, and convenient small denominations can be difficult.\n\n**3 · Paper Money:** Money supply becomes more elastic and easier to expand than a supply tied to metal. This supports national and international markets and investment. Excessive or insufficient expansion can create inflationary or deflationary pressures.\n\n**4 · Plastic Money and e-Money:** Cards, electronic transfers, wallets and digital banking make payments convenient and can broaden financial inclusion while reducing dependence on printed currency. Cybercrime, fraud and access problems require attention.\n\n**Digital Payments, Bitcoin and CBDCs:** A card or bank transfer is a method of accessing/transferring money. Bitcoin is a decentralised digital asset whose value responds to market demand and supply. A Central Bank Digital Currency is issued by the central bank and combines a digital form with state backing. These are different concepts, not interchangeable labels.\n\n**Changing Role of Money:** Initially a medium of exchange, money also became a unit of value, a store of purchasing power and a standard for future payments. “Money is what money does” expresses this functional approach.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"The chapter traces a journey from barter to metallic currency, paper money, plastic money and electronic payments. Each stage eases some earlier difficulties while creating new issues.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Barter System","text":"Goods exchange directly for goods, without money prices. Selling and buying must occur together, limiting exchange mainly to mutually matched needs. The chapter contrasts its lack of monetary-price uncertainty with its severe limits on trade.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Metallic Currency","text":"Gold, silver and later alloy coins separate sale from purchase and expand markets. However, large payments are cumbersome, and convenient small denominations can be difficult.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Paper Money","text":"Money supply becomes more elastic and easier to expand than a supply tied to metal. This supports national and international markets and investment. Excessive or insufficient expansion can create inflationary or deflationary pressures.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Plastic Money and e-Money","text":"Cards, electronic transfers, wallets and digital banking make payments convenient and can broaden financial inclusion while reducing dependence on printed currency. Cybercrime, fraud and access problems require attention.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Digital Payments, Bitcoin and CBDCs","text":"A card or bank transfer is a method of accessing/transferring money. Bitcoin is a decentralised digital asset whose value responds to market demand and supply. A Central Bank Digital Currency is issued by the central bank and combines a digital form with state backing. These are different concepts, not interchangeable labels.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Changing Role of Money","text":"Initially a medium of exchange, money also became a unit of value, a store of purchasing power and a standard for future payments. “Money is what money does” expresses this functional approach.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Who supplies money? Bank money and high-powered money","textbookHeading":"5.4.3 Suppliers of Money · Critical Terms","kind":"Concept and box","key":"restored-who-supplies-money-bank-money-and-high-powered-money","body":"Distinguish the institutions creating money, the money held by the public, and the monetary base supporting deposit creation.\n\n**1 · Central Bank:** The RBI is the principal currency-note issuing authority in the textbook’s account. Note issue follows the minimum reserve system described in the chapter.\n\n**2 · Commercial Banks:** They do not print notes or mint coins, but create demand deposits through lending. These chequeable deposits serve as bank money.\n\n**3 · Government:** The chapter identifies the Ministry of Finance as issuer of ₹1 notes and coins. Distinguish this issue authority from the RBI’s circulation and distribution functions.\n\n**High-powered Money / Monetary Base:** Currency with the public + banks’ vault cash + cash reserves held by banks with the RBI. It supports the banking system’s ability to create deposits.\n\n**Why It Differs from Money Supply:** Currency with the public belongs to both concepts, but banks’ own cash and reserves are not part of money held by the public. Demand deposits belong to money supply and are supported by the monetary base.\n\n**Ideal Supply of Money:** The chapter describes an ideal supply as one consistent with the supply of goods and services, avoiding both inflationary pressure and deficient-demand conditions.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Distinguish the institutions creating money, the money held by the public, and the monetary base supporting deposit creation.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Central Bank","text":"The RBI is the principal currency-note issuing authority in the textbook’s account. Note issue follows the minimum reserve system described in the chapter.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Commercial Banks","text":"They do not print notes or mint coins, but create demand deposits through lending. These chequeable deposits serve as bank money.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Government","text":"The chapter identifies the Ministry of Finance as issuer of ₹1 notes and coins. Distinguish this issue authority from the RBI’s circulation and distribution functions.","tone":"point"},{"title":"High-powered Money / Monetary Base","text":"Currency with the public + banks’ vault cash + cash reserves held by banks with the RBI. It supports the banking system’s ability to create deposits.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Why It Differs from Money Supply","text":"Currency with the public belongs to both concepts, but banks’ own cash and reserves are not part of money held by the public. Demand deposits belong to money supply and are supported by the monetary base.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Ideal Supply of Money","text":"The chapter describes an ideal supply as one consistent with the supply of goods and services, avoiding both inflationary pressure and deficient-demand conditions.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Paper currency, legal tender and minimum reserves","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · Indian Monetary System","kind":"Ability Zone","key":"restored-paper-currency-legal-tender-and-minimum-reserves","body":"India’s textbook monetary system is described as a paper currency standard, even though coins also circulate. Its notes and coins are credit money because face value exceeds material value.\n\n**Limited and Unlimited Legal Tender:** The chapter contrasts coins, accepted up to the prescribed legal limit, with paper notes as unlimited legal tender. “Unlimited” concerns the value of debt settled under the applicable conditions, not an entitlement to pay with withdrawn or invalid notes.\n\n**Minimum Reserve System:** The textbook gives a minimum reserve of ₹200 crore in gold and foreign securities, including at least ₹115 crore of gold. This is a minimum backing requirement, not a rule that reserves must equal a fixed percentage of all currency issued.\n\n**No Conversion into Bullion:** Holding currency does not entitle the holder to demand its face value in gold or silver from the issuing authority. Gold backing and convertibility into gold are different ideas.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"India’s textbook monetary system is described as a paper currency standard, even though coins also circulate. Its notes and coins are credit money because face value exceeds material value.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Limited and Unlimited Legal Tender","text":"The chapter contrasts coins, accepted up to the prescribed legal limit, with paper notes as unlimited legal tender. “Unlimited” concerns the value of debt settled under the applicable conditions, not an entitlement to pay with withdrawn or invalid notes.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Minimum Reserve System","text":"The textbook gives a minimum reserve of ₹200 crore in gold and foreign securities, including at least ₹115 crore of gold. This is a minimum backing requirement, not a rule that reserves must equal a fixed percentage of all currency issued.","tone":"point"},{"title":"No Conversion into Bullion","text":"Holding currency does not entitle the holder to demand its face value in gold or silver from the issuing authority. Gold backing and convertibility into gold are different ideas.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"5","flow":[["Barter problem","Exchange needs a double coincidence of wants."],["Money","A commonly accepted medium connects transactions."],["Other functions","It measures value, stores purchasing power and enables deferred payment."]]},{"id":"6","title":"Banking","intro":"Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit.","lessons":[{"title":"Commercial banks and their functions","body":"Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit. Deposits include current, savings and fixed deposits.\n\nPrimary functions are accepting deposits and lending; other functions include agency services and general-utility services. Banks mobilise saving and create demand deposits through lending.\n\nA central bank is the apex institution regulating the monetary and financial system; its objective is growth with stability rather than commercial profit.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.1 COMMERCIAL BANKS"},{"title":"Credit creation: follow every round","body":"Suppose an initial cash deposit of 1,000 enters the banking system and the reserve ratio is 20%. The first bank keeps 200 and lends 800.\n\nIf 800 is redeposited, the next bank keeps 160 and lends 640. Repeated lending and redepositing expand total deposits to 5,000.\n\nThese results assume no cash leakage, no excess reserves and loans redeposited in the banking system. Total deposits include the initial deposit; additional credit excludes it.","formula":"Deposit multiplier = 1 ÷ Reserve ratio\nTotal deposits = Initial deposit ÷ Reserve ratio\nAdditional credit = Total deposits − Initial deposit","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.2 MONEY CREATION BY THE COMMERCIAL BANKS"},{"title":"Central bank: all seven functions explained","body":"The central bank is the apex institution controlling and regulating the banking and monetary system. Its objective is growth with stability rather than the commercial bank’s private profit.\n\n**1 · Issuer of Currency:** It has the principal monopoly of note issue, subject to the government’s ₹1-note/coin exception described in Money. Centralised issue creates uniformity, public confidence, a central check on supply and flexibility to respond to inflation or deflation. The earnings from issue accrue to the public authority.\n\n**2 · Banker to the Government:** As banker, it maintains accounts, accepts deposits, makes payments and transfers funds. As agent, it manages the issue, purchase, sale and redemption of government securities and debt. As financial adviser, it advises on monetary and financial conditions, stability and borrowing.\n\n**3 · Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role:** It holds commercial banks’ reserve deposits and provides financial accommodation. It supervises compliance with directives and reserve requirements such as CRR and SLR, thereby influencing credit creation.\n\n**4 · Lender of Last Resort:** A bank unable to obtain emergency finance elsewhere can approach the central bank against eligible security. This protects confidence, helps prevent a liquidity problem spreading and supports monetary-system stability.\n\n**5 · Custodian of Foreign Exchange:** It holds and manages official foreign-exchange reserves and may buy or sell foreign currency to moderate exchange-rate instability under managed floating.\n\n**6 · Clearing House Function:** Banks settle mutual claims through accounts with the central bank. For example, offsetting cheques between two banks allows only the net amount to be settled, avoiding unnecessary cash transfers and reducing cash requirements.\n\n**7 · Control of Credit:** It regulates the cost and availability of bank credit. In the textbook framework, tighter credit restrains inflationary demand and easier credit supports activity during deficient demand. Quantitative tools affect overall credit; qualitative tools target particular uses or sectors.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.3.2 Functions of the Central Bank","key":"restored-central-bank-all-seven-functions-explained","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"The central bank is the apex institution controlling and regulating the banking and monetary system. Its objective is growth with stability rather than the commercial bank’s private profit.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Issuer of Currency","text":"It has the principal monopoly of note issue, subject to the government’s ₹1-note/coin exception described in Money. Centralised issue creates uniformity, public confidence, a central check on supply and flexibility to respond to inflation or deflation. The earnings from issue accrue to the public authority.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Banker to the Government","text":"As banker, it maintains accounts, accepts deposits, makes payments and transfers funds. As agent, it manages the issue, purchase, sale and redemption of government securities and debt. As financial adviser, it advises on monetary and financial conditions, stability and borrowing.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role","text":"It holds commercial banks’ reserve deposits and provides financial accommodation. It supervises compliance with directives and reserve requirements such as CRR and SLR, thereby influencing credit creation.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Lender of Last Resort","text":"A bank unable to obtain emergency finance elsewhere can approach the central bank against eligible security. This protects confidence, helps prevent a liquidity problem spreading and supports monetary-system stability.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Custodian of Foreign Exchange","text":"It holds and manages official foreign-exchange reserves and may buy or sell foreign currency to moderate exchange-rate instability under managed floating.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Clearing House Function","text":"Banks settle mutual claims through accounts with the central bank. For example, offsetting cheques between two banks allows only the net amount to be settled, avoiding unnecessary cash transfers and reducing cash requirements.","tone":"point"},{"title":"7 · Control of Credit","text":"It regulates the cost and availability of bank credit. In the textbook framework, tighter credit restrains inflationary demand and easier credit supports activity during deficient demand. Quantitative tools affect overall credit; qualitative tools target particular uses or sectors.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Quantitative credit controls: rates and open-market operations","body":"Quantitative controls influence overall credit rather than one selected activity. For each instrument, explain the action, the effect on bank funds or borrowing cost, and the resulting effect on spending.\n\n**Bank Rate:** The textbook describes this as the rate for central-bank lending to commercial banks, distinguished from secured short-term repo transactions. Raising it tends to raise borrowing costs and restrain credit; lowering it tends to encourage credit.\n\n**Open Market Operations:** The central bank buys or sells government securities in the open market. Purchases inject funds and support bank lending; sales absorb funds and reduce lending capacity. Remember the action from the central bank’s viewpoint: buying adds liquidity, selling withdraws it.\n\n**Repo Rate:** Repo is lending against securities under an agreement to reverse the transaction at a specified date and price. A higher repo rate makes central-bank funds costlier and can raise bank lending rates, reducing demand for credit. Lowering it works in the opposite direction.\n\n**Reverse Repo Rate:** This is the rate associated with banks placing funds with the central bank through reverse-repurchase arrangements. A higher return can encourage banks to park more funds rather than lend; lowering it can encourage lending. The effect depends on banks’ alternative uses for funds.\n\n**Cost versus Availability:** Rates mainly influence the cost and attractiveness of funds. Open-market operations and reserve requirements influence available liquidity. Both channels ultimately affect credit, investment, consumption and aggregate demand.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments of Credit Control","key":"restored-quantitative-credit-controls-rates-and-open-market-operations","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Quantitative controls influence overall credit rather than one selected activity. For each instrument, explain the action, the effect on bank funds or borrowing cost, and the resulting effect on spending.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Bank Rate","text":"The textbook describes this as the rate for central-bank lending to commercial banks, distinguished from secured short-term repo transactions. Raising it tends to raise borrowing costs and restrain credit; lowering it tends to encourage credit.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Open Market Operations","text":"The central bank buys or sells government securities in the open market. Purchases inject funds and support bank lending; sales absorb funds and reduce lending capacity. Remember the action from the central bank’s viewpoint: buying adds liquidity, selling withdraws it.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Repo Rate","text":"Repo is lending against securities under an agreement to reverse the transaction at a specified date and price. A higher repo rate makes central-bank funds costlier and can raise bank lending rates, reducing demand for credit. Lowering it works in the opposite direction.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Reverse Repo Rate","text":"This is the rate associated with banks placing funds with the central bank through reverse-repurchase arrangements. A higher return can encourage banks to park more funds rather than lend; lowering it can encourage lending. The effect depends on banks’ alternative uses for funds.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Cost versus Availability","text":"Rates mainly influence the cost and attractiveness of funds. Open-market operations and reserve requirements influence available liquidity. Both channels ultimately affect credit, investment, consumption and aggregate demand.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"CRR, SLR and all three selective controls","body":"Reserve requirements influence lending capacity; selective controls influence which uses receive credit. State both the direction of the policy and its mechanism.\n\n**Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR):** The prescribed share of bank deposit liabilities held as cash reserves with the RBI. Raising CRR leaves a smaller share available to support lending and reduces the simple credit multiplier; lowering it expands potential credit creation.\n\n**Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR):** The prescribed share of relevant liabilities held by the bank in eligible liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved unencumbered securities. A higher SLR restrains funds available for other lending; a lower SLR can release funds. CRR is held with the RBI; SLR assets are maintained by the bank.\n\n**1 · Margin Requirement:** Margin is collateral value minus the loan granted. With collateral worth ₹1 crore and a loan of ₹80 lakh, the margin is ₹20 lakh or 20%. Raising the margin reduces the maximum loan for given collateral, particularly restraining speculative borrowing; lowering it permits more credit.\n\n**2 · Rationing of Credit:** Fix quotas or limits on credit for selected business activities. Tighter quotas restrict lending to those uses; relaxation increases availability. This is selective credit control.\n\n**3 · Moral Suasion:** The central bank uses advice, persuasion and pressure to obtain banks’ cooperation. It may urge restraint during inflation and more liberal lending during deficient demand. The chapter notes that it can affect both overall and selected credit, though usually classified as qualitative.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.4.1–6.4.2 Reserve Ratios and Qualitative Instruments","key":"restored-crr-slr-and-all-three-selective-controls","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Reserve requirements influence lending capacity; selective controls influence which uses receive credit. State both the direction of the policy and its mechanism.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)","text":"The prescribed share of bank deposit liabilities held as cash reserves with the RBI. Raising CRR leaves a smaller share available to support lending and reduces the simple credit multiplier; lowering it expands potential credit creation.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR)","text":"The prescribed share of relevant liabilities held by the bank in eligible liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved unencumbered securities. A higher SLR restrains funds available for other lending; a lower SLR can release funds. CRR is held with the RBI; SLR assets are maintained by the bank.","tone":"point"},{"title":"1 · Margin Requirement","text":"Margin is collateral value minus the loan granted. With collateral worth ₹1 crore and a loan of ₹80 lakh, the margin is ₹20 lakh or 20%. Raising the margin reduces the maximum loan for given collateral, particularly restraining speculative borrowing; lowering it permits more credit.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Rationing of Credit","text":"Fix quotas or limits on credit for selected business activities. Tighter quotas restrict lending to those uses; relaxation increases availability. This is selective credit control.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Moral Suasion","text":"The central bank uses advice, persuasion and pressure to obtain banks’ cooperation. It may urge restraint during inflation and more liberal lending during deficient demand. The chapter notes that it can affect both overall and selected credit, though usually classified as qualitative.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"When monetary tools work: all success conditions","body":"Changing a policy instrument does not guarantee a proportionate change in lending or spending. The chapter separates four conditions for repo-rate policy and two for open-market operations.\n\n**Repo 1 · Dependence on the Central Bank:** Banks with ample funds of their own depend less on central-bank loans, so a change in the repo rate may have a weaker influence.\n\n**Repo 2 · Sensitivity of Demand for Funds:** Banks may not respond strongly to small rate changes when business expectations or credit demand are weak. A cheaper source of funds does not by itself create willing borrowers.\n\n**Repo 3 · Structure of Interest Rates:** If non-bank financial institutions do not adjust their rates correspondingly, borrowers can switch sources and weaken policy transmission.\n\n**Repo 4 · Overall Supply of Funds:** When non-bank sources are popular and readily available, regulating the price of bank funds has less influence on total financing.\n\n**OMO 1 · Existence of a Securities Market:** Open-market purchases and sales require a well-organised, functioning market for government securities. Without willing counterparties, operations have limited reach.\n\n**OMO 2 · Excess Reserves with Banks:** Large excess reserves can weaken the intended relationship between liquidity withdrawal and lending. Banks can absorb a drain without proportionately reducing loans, while injections may remain idle when demand for credit is low.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · Success of Repo Policy and OMO","key":"restored-when-monetary-tools-work-all-success-conditions","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Changing a policy instrument does not guarantee a proportionate change in lending or spending. The chapter separates four conditions for repo-rate policy and two for open-market operations.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Repo 1 · Dependence on the Central Bank","text":"Banks with ample funds of their own depend less on central-bank loans, so a change in the repo rate may have a weaker influence.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Repo 2 · Sensitivity of Demand for Funds","text":"Banks may not respond strongly to small rate changes when business expectations or credit demand are weak. A cheaper source of funds does not by itself create willing borrowers.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Repo 3 · Structure of Interest Rates","text":"If non-bank financial institutions do not adjust their rates correspondingly, borrowers can switch sources and weaken policy transmission.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Repo 4 · Overall Supply of Funds","text":"When non-bank sources are popular and readily available, regulating the price of bank funds has less influence on total financing.","tone":"point"},{"title":"OMO 1 · Existence of a Securities Market","text":"Open-market purchases and sales require a well-organised, functioning market for government securities. Without willing counterparties, operations have limited reach.","tone":"point"},{"title":"OMO 2 · Excess Reserves with Banks","text":"Large excess reserves can weaken the intended relationship between liquidity withdrawal and lending. Banks can absorb a drain without proportionately reducing loans, while injections may remain idle when demand for credit is low.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Issuer of currency and banker to government","textbookHeading":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions","body":"Issuer of Currency: the central bank has the principal note-issuing authority; in India, the ₹1 note and coins are issued by the Government of India. Centralised issue promotes uniformity and control.\n\nBanker to Government: the RBI keeps government deposits, receives payments, makes disbursements, advances funds within the applicable arrangements and manages public debt. Financial adviser is a related role.\n\nDistinguish issuing authority from circulation: government-issued coins enter circulation through the monetary system.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"Issuer of Currency","text":"The central bank has the principal note-issuing authority; in India, the ₹1 note and coins are issued by the Government of India. Centralised issue promotes uniformity and control.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Banker to Government","text":"The RBI keeps government deposits, receives payments, makes disbursements, advances funds within the applicable arrangements and manages public debt. Financial adviser is a related role.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Distinguish issuing authority from circulation: government-issued coins enter circulation through the monetary system.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Bankers’ bank, lender of last resort and clearing house","textbookHeading":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions","body":"Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role: commercial banks maintain required cash balances with the central bank, which regulates their functioning. Lender of Last Resort: the central bank offers financial accommodation in emergencies when other sources are unavailable.\n\nClearing House: it settles interbank claims by adjusting banks’ accounts and facilitates fund transfers. Custodian of Foreign Exchange: it manages the country’s foreign-exchange reserves and can intervene in the exchange market.\n\nControl of Credit: it regulates the cost and availability of credit to support growth with stability.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role","text":"Commercial banks maintain required cash balances with the central bank, which regulates their functioning.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Lender of Last Resort","text":"The central bank offers financial accommodation in emergencies when other sources are unavailable.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Clearing House","text":"It settles interbank claims by adjusting banks’ accounts and facilitates fund transfers.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Custodian of Foreign Exchange","text":"It manages the country’s foreign-exchange reserves and can intervene in the exchange market.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Control of Credit","text":"It regulates the cost and availability of credit to support growth with stability.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Banks, financial institutions and central-bank differences","textbookHeading":"6.1 Critical Terms · Central versus Commercial Banks","kind":"Concept and box","key":"restored-banks-financial-institutions-and-central-bank-differences","body":"A financial institution is not automatically a bank. The chapter’s banking test combines acceptance of chequeable deposits with lending or advances.\n\n**All Financial Institutions Are Not Banks:** The chapter uses LIC as a lender that does not accept chequeable public deposits and ordinary post-office savings as deposit-taking without the paired commercial-lending function. Accepting a deposit or lending alone does not satisfy its two-function definition.\n\n**Customers and Position:** A commercial bank serves households and businesses; the central bank stands at the apex and mainly serves government and the banking system.\n\n**Role in Money Supply:** The central bank issues currency; commercial banks create deposit money through loans. Credit creation is not currency printing.\n\n**Role in Foreign Exchange:** The central bank is custodian of official reserves and manages exchange-rate interventions. Commercial banks deal in foreign exchange as part of customer services and business.\n\n**Objectives:** Commercial banks seek profit subject to regulation. The central bank aims at monetary stability and growth, supervising the system rather than competing as an ordinary retail bank.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"A financial institution is not automatically a bank. The chapter’s banking test combines acceptance of chequeable deposits with lending or advances.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"All Financial Institutions Are Not Banks","text":"The chapter uses LIC as a lender that does not accept chequeable public deposits and ordinary post-office savings as deposit-taking without the paired commercial-lending function. Accepting a deposit or lending alone does not satisfy its two-function definition.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Customers and Position","text":"A commercial bank serves households and businesses; the central bank stands at the apex and mainly serves government and the banking system.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Role in Money Supply","text":"The central bank issues currency; commercial banks create deposit money through loans. Credit creation is not currency printing.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Role in Foreign Exchange","text":"The central bank is custodian of official reserves and manages exchange-rate interventions. Commercial banks deal in foreign exchange as part of customer services and business.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Objectives","text":"Commercial banks seek profit subject to regulation. The central bank aims at monetary stability and growth, supervising the system rather than competing as an ordinary retail bank.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"6","flow":[["Initial deposit","A bank receives funds."],["Required reserve","A fraction is retained."],["Loan and redeposit","The remainder enters another deposit."],["Repeated rounds","Deposits expand subject to reserve and lending conditions."]]},{"id":"7","title":"Aggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts","intro":"Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure.","lessons":[{"title":"Aggregate demand and aggregate supply","body":"Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure. In a two-sector economy AD = C + I; a closed economy with government adds G; an open economy adds X − M.\n\nAggregate supply refers to production planned by producers. In the textbook’s short-run model with constant prices and spare capacity, AS is identified with income/output and represented by the 45° line.","formula":"AD = C + I + G + (X − M)\nAS = Y = C + S","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"7.1 CONCEPT OF AD · 7.2 COMPONENTS OF AD · 7.3 CONCEPT OF AS"},{"title":"Consumption function: autonomous and induced consumption","body":"Consumption function is the functional relationship between consumption and income. Autonomous consumption is consumption when income is zero; induced consumption is the part that varies with income.\n\nIn C = C̄ + bY, the vertical intercept is C̄ and the slope b is MPC. Consumption rises with income, but normally by less than the rise in income.\n\nA change in income moves along a given function; a change in autonomous consumption shifts it.","formula":"C = C̄ + bY\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY\nInduced consumption = bY","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"7.4 CONSUMPTION FUNCTION"},{"title":"APC and MPC: totals versus increments","body":"Average propensity to consume is total consumption divided by total income; marginal propensity to consume is additional consumption divided by additional income. APC may exceed one when consumption exceeds income.\n\nMPC is the slope of the consumption curve; APC is not. With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC falls as income rises.\n\nAt zero income APC is undefined.","formula":"APC = C ÷ Y\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"APC vs. MPC","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","APC","MPC"],"rows":[["What it compares","Total consumption with total income","Change in consumption with change in income"],["Formula","C ÷ Y","ΔC ÷ ΔY"],["Can exceed 1?","Yes, when C > Y","Not in the standard consumption model with 0 < MPC < 1"]]}},{"title":"Saving function and the break-even point","body":"Saving is income not consumed. Derive S by subtracting the entire consumption function from Y.\n\nThe saving function has a negative intercept equal to autonomous consumption and a slope equal to MPS. At break-even income C = Y and S = 0: APC is one and APS is zero.\n\nBelow it saving is negative; above it saving is positive. Break-even is not necessarily equilibrium, because equilibrium also depends on investment.","formula":"S = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y\nBreak-even income = C̄ ÷ (1 − b)","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"SAVING FUNCTION · Break-even Point"},{"title":"APS, MPS and the two identities","body":"Average propensity to save is S/Y; marginal propensity to save is ΔS/ΔY. APS can be negative when households dissave.\n\nMPS is the slope of the saving function. Since income is split between consumption and saving, APC + APS = 1 and MPC + MPS = 1.\n\nDo not confuse ΔS (change in saving) with AS (aggregate supply): similar letters represent different concepts.","formula":"APS = S ÷ Y; MPS = ΔS ÷ ΔY\nAPC + APS = 1; MPC + MPS = 1","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"APS vs. MPS · Relationship between Propensity to Consume and Propensity to Save"},{"title":"Investment and deriving one function from the other","body":"In the simple model autonomous investment is independent of income, so its curve is horizontal. Induced investment responds to changes in income or activity.\n\nThe expected profitability of investment and rate of interest influence investment decisions. To obtain C from S, use C = Y − S; to obtain S from C, use S = Y − C.\n\nThe two slopes add to one and their vertical intercepts have equal magnitudes with opposite signs.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"INVESTMENT · ABILITY ZONE: Derivation of C-function from S-function"},{"title":"Ranges of APC, MPC, APS and MPS","textbookHeading":"Comparative Table of APC, MPC, APS and MPS · Critical Questions","body":"APC can exceed one when C exceeds Y; it equals one at break-even and is below one when saving is positive. APS is negative when saving is negative, zero at break-even and positive when saving is positive; with positive consumption it remains below one.\n\nMPC and MPS range from zero to one in the textbook model and add to one. At the endpoints, MPC = 1 implies MPS = 0 and all additional income is consumed; MPC = 0 implies MPS = 1 and all additional income is saved.\n\nDo not infer a marginal ratio from a single total-income observation.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"APC","text":"APC can exceed one when C exceeds Y; it equals one at break-even and is below one when saving is positive.","tone":"point"},{"title":"APS","text":"APS is negative when saving is negative, zero at break-even and positive when saving is positive; with positive consumption it remains below one.","tone":"point"},{"title":"MPC and MPS","text":"MPC and MPS range from zero to one in the textbook model and add to one. At the endpoints, MPC = 1 implies MPS = 0 and all additional income is consumed; MPC = 0 implies MPS = 1 and all additional income is saved.\n\nDo not infer a marginal ratio from a single total-income observation.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"AD and AS: assumptions, components and schedules","textbookHeading":"7.1–7.3 Aggregate Demand and Aggregate Supply","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-ad-and-as-assumptions-components-and-schedules","body":"In this course, AD is planned expenditure measured against income, and AS is planned output. The diagram’s axes and assumptions determine how the curves are interpreted.\n\n**AD Means Planned Expenditure:** AD is the expenditure buyers intend to incur on domestically produced goods and services during an accounting year. It is not the sum of physical quantities of different goods, nor necessarily actual expenditure.\n\n**AD at Zero Income:** Some consumption is necessary even when current income is zero. It is financed from past saving or borrowing. This autonomous expenditure makes the AD curve start above the origin.\n\n**Two-, Three- and Four-Sector Components:** In a two-sector closed economy AD = C + I. Add government for AD = C + I + G. In an open economy AD = C + I + G + (X − M). Exports add demand for domestic output; imports remove spending on foreign output.\n\n**AD Schedule and Curve:** The schedule lists planned expenditure at different income levels. With income on the horizontal axis, consumption and AD rise as income rises. This upward-sloping income–expenditure curve differs from an AD curve drawn against the general price level.\n\n**AS, Income and the 45° Line:** Planned production creates income, so AS is represented by Y. With the same scale on both axes, the 45° line represents equality between output/income and expenditure.\n\n**Model Conditions:** Technology and relevant prices are treated as constant; spare capacity allows output to respond to demand before full employment. At full employment, real output cannot keep rising merely because planned spending rises.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"In this course, AD is planned expenditure measured against income, and AS is planned output. The diagram’s axes and assumptions determine how the curves are interpreted.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"AD Means Planned Expenditure","text":"AD is the expenditure buyers intend to incur on domestically produced goods and services during an accounting year. It is not the sum of physical quantities of different goods, nor necessarily actual expenditure.","tone":"point"},{"title":"AD at Zero Income","text":"Some consumption is necessary even when current income is zero. It is financed from past saving or borrowing. This autonomous expenditure makes the AD curve start above the origin.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Two-, Three- and Four-Sector Components","text":"In a two-sector closed economy AD = C + I. Add government for AD = C + I + G. In an open economy AD = C + I + G + (X − M). Exports add demand for domestic output; imports remove spending on foreign output.","tone":"point"},{"title":"AD Schedule and Curve","text":"The schedule lists planned expenditure at different income levels. With income on the horizontal axis, consumption and AD rise as income rises. This upward-sloping income–expenditure curve differs from an AD curve drawn against the general price level.","tone":"point"},{"title":"AS, Income and the 45° Line","text":"Planned production creates income, so AS is represented by Y. With the same scale on both axes, the 45° line represents equality between output/income and expenditure.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Model Conditions","text":"Technology and relevant prices are treated as constant; spare capacity allows output to respond to demand before full employment. At full employment, real output cannot keep rising merely because planned spending rises.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Consumption function: every diagram observation","textbookHeading":"7.4 Consumption Function · Critical Terms","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-consumption-function-every-diagram-observation","body":"The consumption function relates household consumption to income: C = C̄ + bY, where C̄ is autonomous consumption and b is MPC.\n\n**Autonomous Consumption:** C̄ is consumption when Y = 0. The curve starts above the origin on the consumption axis, and saving at zero income is −C̄.\n\n**Induced Consumption:** bY is consumption attributable to income. Higher income raises consumption; the amount consumed out of an additional unit of income is MPC.\n\n**Keynes’s Psychological Law:** As income rises, consumption rises by less than the increase in income, leaving part for saving. In the standard linear model, 0 < MPC < 1 and the consumption line is flatter than the 45° reference line. Constant MPC means equal increments of income produce equal increments of consumption.\n\n**Break-even Point:** Where the consumption line crosses C = Y, all income is consumed and saving is zero. Below that income, C > Y and households dissave; above it, C < Y and saving is positive.\n\n**Slope versus Average:** The consumption-line slope is MPC = ΔC/ΔY. APC = C/Y is the slope of a ray from the origin to a point on the line, not the slope of the consumption line itself.\n\n**Why APC Falls in the Linear Model:** APC = C̄/Y + MPC. As income rises, the autonomous-consumption term becomes smaller relative to income, so APC falls towards MPC when C̄ is positive.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"The consumption function relates household consumption to income: C = C̄ + bY, where C̄ is autonomous consumption and b is MPC.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Autonomous Consumption","text":"C̄ is consumption when Y = 0. The curve starts above the origin on the consumption axis, and saving at zero income is −C̄.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Induced Consumption","text":"bY is consumption attributable to income. Higher income raises consumption; the amount consumed out of an additional unit of income is MPC.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Keynes’s Psychological Law","text":"As income rises, consumption rises by less than the increase in income, leaving part for saving. In the standard linear model, 0 < MPC < 1 and the consumption line is flatter than the 45° reference line. Constant MPC means equal increments of income produce equal increments of consumption.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Break-even Point","text":"Where the consumption line crosses C = Y, all income is consumed and saving is zero. Below that income, C > Y and households dissave; above it, C < Y and saving is positive.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Slope versus Average","text":"The consumption-line slope is MPC = ΔC/ΔY. APC = C/Y is the slope of a ray from the origin to a point on the line, not the slope of the consumption line itself.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Why APC Falls in the Linear Model","text":"APC = C̄/Y + MPC. As income rises, the autonomous-consumption term becomes smaller relative to income, so APC falls towards MPC when C̄ is positive.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Deriving saving and consumption curves from one another","textbookHeading":"7.5 Saving Function · Ability Zone","kind":"Ability Zone","key":"restored-deriving-saving-and-consumption-curves-from-one-another","body":"Consumption and saving exhaust income: Y = C + S. Use this identity to derive either function and check their common break-even income.\n\n**Consumption to Saving:** From C = C̄ + bY, S = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y. The saving intercept is the negative of autonomous consumption and the saving slope is MPS = 1 − MPC.\n\n**Saving to Consumption:** From S = a + mY, C = Y − S = −a + (1 − m)Y. A negative saving intercept produces a positive consumption intercept.\n\n**Locate the Two Intercepts:** At Y = 0, consumption is C̄ and saving is −C̄. Mark these on their respective vertical axes; do not start both curves at the origin.\n\n**Locate the Shared Break-even Income:** Set S = 0 or C = Y. Both give Y = C̄/(1 − b). The saving line crosses the income axis directly below the consumption line’s intersection with the 45° line.\n\n**Complete the Curves:** Draw consumption with slope b and saving with slope 1 − b. The slopes add to one; below break-even income saving is negative, and above it saving is positive.\n\n**Undefined Ratios at Zero Income:** APC and APS divide by Y and are undefined when income is zero. MPC and MPS concern changes and can remain well defined; do not report an ordinary finite APC or APS at zero income.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Consumption and saving exhaust income: Y = C + S. Use this identity to derive either function and check their common break-even income.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Consumption to Saving","text":"From C = C̄ + bY, S = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y. The saving intercept is the negative of autonomous consumption and the saving slope is MPS = 1 − MPC.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Saving to Consumption","text":"From S = a + mY, C = Y − S = −a + (1 − m)Y. A negative saving intercept produces a positive consumption intercept.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Locate the Two Intercepts","text":"At Y = 0, consumption is C̄ and saving is −C̄. Mark these on their respective vertical axes; do not start both curves at the origin.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Locate the Shared Break-even Income","text":"Set S = 0 or C = Y. Both give Y = C̄/(1 − b). The saving line crosses the income axis directly below the consumption line’s intersection with the 45° line.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Complete the Curves","text":"Draw consumption with slope b and saving with slope 1 − b. The slopes add to one; below break-even income saving is negative, and above it saving is positive.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Undefined Ratios at Zero Income","text":"APC and APS divide by Y and are undefined when income is zero. MPC and MPS concern changes and can remain well defined; do not report an ordinary finite APC or APS at zero income.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"7","flow":[["Income earned","Disposable income is available."],["Consumption","Part is spent: C = C̄ + MPC × Y."],["Saving","The remainder: S = Y − C."]]},{"id":"8","title":"Short Run Equilibrium Output","intro":"The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output.","lessons":[{"title":"Short run and equilibrium output","body":"The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output. Equilibrium output is the level where intended production equals intended expenditure.\n\nProducers have no unplanned change in inventories and no incentive to change output. In the two-sector economy Y = C + I and Y = C + S, so the same equilibrium condition can be written S = I.","formula":"Y = AD = C + I\nC + S = C + I ⇒ S = I\nY* = (C̄ + I) ÷ (1 − MPC)","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"8.1 CONCEPT OF SHORT RUN · 8.2 CONCEPT OF EQUILIBRIUM OUTPUT"},{"title":"How inventories restore equilibrium","body":"When AD is below AS, goods remain unsold and actual inventories exceed desired inventories. Producers cut output and employment until planned saving equals planned investment.\n\nWhen AD exceeds AS, inventories fall unexpectedly and producers expand output, provided spare capacity exists. The direction is easy to reverse by mistake: excess supply causes inventory accumulation; excess demand causes inventory depletion.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Equilibrium GDP · Inventory Adjustment","blocks":[{"title":"AD below AS","text":"When AD is below AS, goods remain unsold and actual inventories exceed desired inventories. Producers cut output and employment until planned saving equals planned investment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"AD above AS","text":"When AD exceeds AS, inventories fall unexpectedly and producers expand output, provided spare capacity exists. The direction is easy to reverse by mistake: excess supply causes inventory accumulation; excess demand causes inventory depletion.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Ex-ante versus ex-post saving and investment","body":"Ex-ante means planned; ex-post means realised. Planned saving and planned investment need not be equal away from equilibrium.\n\nActual saving and actual investment are equal by accounting identity because realised investment includes unintended inventory changes. Equilibrium requires equality of planned S and planned I, not merely the ex-post identity.","formula":"","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"Ex-ante Saving and Ex-ante Investment","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Ex-ante","Ex-post"],"rows":[["Meaning","Planned or intended","Actually realised"],["Saving and investment","May be unequal","Equal in national-income accounting"],["Adjustment","Mismatch changes unplanned inventories","Actual investment includes inventory changes"]]}},{"title":"Effective demand and underemployment equilibrium","body":"Effective demand is aggregate demand at the equilibrium level of income/output. Equilibrium can occur below full employment: firms’ production plans can match spending plans while workers and productive capacity remain idle. An increase in autonomous expenditure shifts AD upward and raises equilibrium output in the model with spare capacity; a fall lowers it.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Effective Demand · Underemployment Equilibrium"},{"title":"Investment multiplier: size and derivation","body":"Investment multiplier is the ratio of change in income to change in investment. Starting with ΔY = ΔC + ΔI and ΔC = MPC × ΔY gives ΔY(1 − MPC) = ΔI.\n\nHigher MPC means more income is respent, so the multiplier is larger. Higher MPS means more income leaks into saving, so the multiplier is smaller.\n\nWith MPC zero, k = 1; as MPC approaches one, k becomes very large.","formula":"k = ΔY ÷ ΔI = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS\nΔY = k × ΔI","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"8.5 INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER"},{"title":"Multiplier mechanism, assumptions and reverse operation","body":"An investment injection is someone’s income. They consume a fraction, which becomes someone else’s income; successive rounds generate ΔI, MPC × ΔI, MPC² × ΔI and so on.\n\nSaving reduces each next round. The simple multiplier assumes constant MPC, available spare capacity, constant prices, and no additional leakages such as imports or taxes.\n\nA fall in investment works in reverse, producing a multiplied fall in income. The final increase in saving equals the initial increase in investment.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Multiplier Mechanism"},{"title":"Break-even income versus equilibrium income","textbookHeading":"Consumption Function · Equilibrium Output","body":"Break-even Income: consumption equals income, so saving is zero; use C = Y. Equilibrium Income: planned output equals planned expenditure, so saving equals investment; use Y = C + I or S = I.\n\nWith positive autonomous investment and positive MPS, equilibrium income exceeds break-even income. The two coincide only when autonomous investment is zero in the simple model.\n\nIf you are asked for dissaving at zero income, solve for autonomous consumption and report saving as its negative.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"Break-even Y = C̄/MPS\nEquilibrium Y = (C̄ + I)/MPS","blocks":[{"title":"Break-even Income","text":"Consumption equals income, so saving is zero; use C = Y.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Equilibrium Income","text":"Planned output equals planned expenditure, so saving equals investment; use Y = C + I or S = I.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"With positive autonomous investment and positive MPS, equilibrium income exceeds break-even income. The two coincide only when autonomous investment is zero in the simple model.\n\nIf you are asked for dissaving at zero income, solve for autonomous consumption and report saving as its negative.","tone":"prose"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Equilibrium: conditions, stock signals and the two approaches","textbookHeading":"8.2 Equilibrium Output · Planned and Actual Stocks","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-equilibrium-conditions-stock-signals-and-the-two-approaches","body":"Equilibrium requires intended output to equal intended expenditure. The producer’s desired inventory may be positive; equilibrium means no unintended change in it, not zero stocks.\n\n**AD = AS Approach:** At equilibrium, Y = C + I in the two-sector model. Intended purchases match intended production, actual stocks equal desired stocks, and producers have no reason to change output.\n\n**S = I Approach:** Since Y = C + S and AD = C + I, equating Y and AD gives C + S = C + I, hence planned S = planned I. Saving is a leakage and investment an injection; equilibrium requires their equality.\n\n**When AS > AD:** Actual stocks exceed desired stocks. Firms cut planned production and employment. Falling income reduces saving until planned S equals planned I.\n\n**When AD > AS:** Stocks fall below the desired level, and some demand goes unfulfilled. With spare capacity, firms expand production and employment; income and saving rise towards equilibrium.\n\n**Equilibrium Is Not Necessarily Full Employment:** Spending plans can match production plans while workers remain involuntarily unemployed. Full-employment equilibrium additionally requires fuller utilisation of available resources.\n\n**Ex-post Identity Is Different:** Actual investment includes unplanned inventory changes, so actual saving and actual investment are equal in accounting. This identity holds even when planned saving and investment differ.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Equilibrium requires intended output to equal intended expenditure. The producer’s desired inventory may be positive; equilibrium means no unintended change in it, not zero stocks.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"AD = AS Approach","text":"At equilibrium, Y = C + I in the two-sector model. Intended purchases match intended production, actual stocks equal desired stocks, and producers have no reason to change output.","tone":"point"},{"title":"S = I Approach","text":"Since Y = C + S and AD = C + I, equating Y and AD gives C + S = C + I, hence planned S = planned I. Saving is a leakage and investment an injection; equilibrium requires their equality.","tone":"point"},{"title":"When AS > AD","text":"Actual stocks exceed desired stocks. Firms cut planned production and employment. Falling income reduces saving until planned S equals planned I.","tone":"point"},{"title":"When AD > AS","text":"Stocks fall below the desired level, and some demand goes unfulfilled. With spare capacity, firms expand production and employment; income and saving rise towards equilibrium.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Equilibrium Is Not Necessarily Full Employment","text":"Spending plans can match production plans while workers remain involuntarily unemployed. Full-employment equilibrium additionally requires fuller utilisation of available resources.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Ex-post Identity Is Different","text":"Actual investment includes unplanned inventory changes, so actual saving and actual investment are equal in accounting. This identity holds even when planned saving and investment differ.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"8","flow":[["Planned spending","Compare AD with output."],["Inventories change","Unplanned stocks signal the mismatch."],["Output adjusts","Producers change production and employment."],["Equilibrium","Planned AD = output; planned S = I."]]},{"id":"9","title":"Problem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand","intro":"Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work.","lessons":[{"title":"Full employment and unemployment","body":"Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work. Voluntary unemployment reflects unwillingness to work at the existing wage; involuntary unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs.\n\nThe textbook also distinguishes natural unemployment: frictional unemployment during job transitions and structural unemployment caused by changing industries, skills or production techniques. Underemployment equilibrium has AD = AS with unutilised capacity.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"9.1 SOME CRITICAL CONCEPTS","blocks":[{"title":"Full employment","text":"Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Voluntary unemployment","text":"Voluntary unemployment reflects unwillingness to work at the existing wage.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Involuntary unemployment","text":"Involuntary unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"The textbook also distinguishes natural unemployment: frictional unemployment during job transitions and structural unemployment caused by changing industries, skills or production techniques. Underemployment equilibrium has AD = AS with unutilised capacity.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Deficient demand and deflationary gap","body":"Deficient demand occurs when aggregate demand is less than the aggregate supply corresponding to full employment. The deflationary gap is the shortfall of AD from the level required to maintain full employment; measure it vertically at full-employment output.\n\nIt reduces output and employment and creates involuntary unemployment. The gap in expenditure is not the same as the shortfall in equilibrium income: the multiplier links the two.","formula":"Deflationary gap = Yf − AD at Yf\nRequired increase in autonomous expenditure = (Yf − Y*) ÷ k","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"DEFICIENT DEMAND · Deflationary Gap","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Deficient demand","Excess demand"],"rows":[["Compared with","AD required at full employment","AD required at full employment"],["Position","AD is too low","AD is too high"],["Gap","Deflationary","Inflationary"],["Policy direction","Raise AD","Reduce AD"]]}},{"title":"Excess demand and inflationary gap","body":"Excess demand occurs when AD exceeds the demand required to sustain full employment. The inflationary gap is the excess of AD over full-employment AS, measured at Yf.\n\nOnce resources are fully employed, real output cannot expand in the simple model; excess spending raises the general price level. A calculated equilibrium above Yf is a signal of inflationary pressure, not evidence that the economy can actually produce beyond its capacity.","formula":"Inflationary gap = AD at Yf − Yf","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"EXCESS DEMAND · Inflationary Gap"},{"title":"Fiscal measures: expenditure, taxation and borrowing","body":"To correct deficient demand, raise government expenditure, reduce taxes, reduce public borrowing and increase borrowing from the central bank, following the textbook framework. These measures raise spending power or public expenditure.\n\nFor excess demand, reverse the direction. Explain the chain: lower taxes → higher disposable income → more consumption → higher AD.\n\nBorrowing from the public can withdraw purchasing power; borrowing from the central bank can expand it.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Fiscal Policy"},{"title":"Monetary measures: cost and availability of credit","body":"For deficient demand, lower bank/repo/reverse-repo rates, lower CRR and SLR, buy securities, lower margins, relax credit rationing and encourage lending. For excess demand, raise rates and reserve requirements, sell securities, raise margins and restrict lending.\n\nMonetary policy is the central bank’s policy; fiscal policy is the government’s revenue and expenditure policy. Always connect the instrument to credit and then to aggregate demand.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Monetary Policy"},{"formula":"","title":"Deficient demand: all six causes","textbookHeading":"9.2.3 Causes of Deficient Demand","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-deficient-demand-all-six-causes","body":"Deficient demand means planned AD is below the level required for full employment. Start from AD = C + I + G + (X − M); taxation influences disposable income and consumption.\n\n**1 · Reduction in Private Consumption Expenditure:** A lower propensity to consume, or higher propensity to save, reduces C at a given income and shifts AD down. In mature economies, consumption may grow more slowly once many wants are satisfied.\n\n**2 · Reduction in Private Investment Expenditure:** Poor or bearish business expectations reduce expected returns and investment. Lower I directly reduces planned expenditure, with further effects through the multiplier.\n\n**3 · Reduction in Government Expenditure:** Budget constraints or spending cuts can reduce government consumption or investment. Both lower AD.\n\n**4 · Decline in Exports:** Lower foreign purchases mean less expenditure on domestically produced goods and services, reducing AD and domestic income.\n\n**5 · Rise in Imports:** When buyers switch towards cheaper foreign products, more spending goes to foreign output. Imports enter with a negative sign, reducing demand for domestic output, other things equal.\n\n**6 · Increase in Tax Rates:** Higher taxes leave households with less disposable income. Consumption can fall even if their propensity to consume is unchanged, pulling AD down.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Deficient demand means planned AD is below the level required for full employment. Start from AD = C + I + G + (X − M); taxation influences disposable income and consumption.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Reduction in Private Consumption Expenditure","text":"A lower propensity to consume, or higher propensity to save, reduces C at a given income and shifts AD down. In mature economies, consumption may grow more slowly once many wants are satisfied.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Reduction in Private Investment Expenditure","text":"Poor or bearish business expectations reduce expected returns and investment. Lower I directly reduces planned expenditure, with further effects through the multiplier.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Reduction in Government Expenditure","text":"Budget constraints or spending cuts can reduce government consumption or investment. Both lower AD.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Decline in Exports","text":"Lower foreign purchases mean less expenditure on domestically produced goods and services, reducing AD and domestic income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Rise in Imports","text":"When buyers switch towards cheaper foreign products, more spending goes to foreign output. Imports enter with a negative sign, reducing demand for domestic output, other things equal.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Increase in Tax Rates","text":"Higher taxes leave households with less disposable income. Consumption can fall even if their propensity to consume is unchanged, pulling AD down.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Deficient demand: all four consequences","textbookHeading":"9.2.4 Consequences of Deficient Demand","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-deficient-demand-all-four-consequences","body":"Demand below the full-employment requirement leaves capacity unused. The textbook groups the effects into four situations, with a connected downward spiral.\n\n**1 · Underemployment Equilibrium:** AD and AS can intersect at output below potential GDP. Firms have no incentive to produce more when planned purchases are insufficient, despite idle resources and involuntary unemployment.\n\n**2 · Deflationary Gap:** The gap is the shortfall of planned AD from required AD at full-employment output. Weak demand hurts inducement to invest; low investment reduces output and income, which weakens demand again: the low-level equilibrium trap.\n\n**3 · Undesired Stocks:** Sales fall short of production plans, so unwanted inventories accumulate. Producers may cut prices to clear stocks and reduce future output and employment.\n\n**4 · Loss of Profits:** Unsold output and price reductions reduce profitability. Bearish expectations can cause another cut in investment, reinforcing weak income and demand.\n\n**Overall Effects:** Output falls, involuntary unemployment rises and prices face downward pressure. The basic fixed-price income model emphasises falling output; the chapter’s broader discussion also describes deflationary price pressure and contraction in the business cycle.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Demand below the full-employment requirement leaves capacity unused. The textbook groups the effects into four situations, with a connected downward spiral.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Underemployment Equilibrium","text":"AD and AS can intersect at output below potential GDP. Firms have no incentive to produce more when planned purchases are insufficient, despite idle resources and involuntary unemployment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Deflationary Gap","text":"The gap is the shortfall of planned AD from required AD at full-employment output. Weak demand hurts inducement to invest; low investment reduces output and income, which weakens demand again: the low-level equilibrium trap.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Undesired Stocks","text":"Sales fall short of production plans, so unwanted inventories accumulate. Producers may cut prices to clear stocks and reduce future output and employment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Loss of Profits","text":"Unsold output and price reductions reduce profitability. Bearish expectations can cause another cut in investment, reinforcing weak income and demand.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Overall Effects","text":"Output falls, involuntary unemployment rises and prices face downward pressure. The basic fixed-price income model emphasises falling output; the chapter’s broader discussion also describes deflationary price pressure and contraction in the business cycle.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Excess demand: all six causes","textbookHeading":"9.3.3 Causes of Excess Demand","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-excess-demand-all-six-causes","body":"Excess demand occurs when planned spending exceeds the AD consistent with full-employment output. The causes reverse the directions of the deficient-demand causes.\n\n**1 · Increase in Private Consumption Expenditure:** A higher propensity to consume or lower propensity to save raises C at a given income. The demonstration effect—adopting the spending patterns of richer groups—can also increase consumption.\n\n**2 · Increase in Private Investment Expenditure:** Bullish business expectations and expected high returns raise investment, increasing AD directly and through induced consumption.\n\n**3 · Increase in Government Expenditure:** Expansion of development and welfare commitments raises government consumption or investment, adding to AD.\n\n**4 · Increase in Exports:** More foreign demand for domestic output, potentially encouraged by relatively lower domestic prices, raises AD.\n\n**5 · Decrease in Imports:** Higher foreign prices can shift purchases towards domestic output. A lower M increases net exports and therefore AD, other things equal.\n\n**6 · Cut in Tax Rates:** Lower taxes leave more disposable income, enabling higher consumption and planned expenditure.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Excess demand occurs when planned spending exceeds the AD consistent with full-employment output. The causes reverse the directions of the deficient-demand causes.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Increase in Private Consumption Expenditure","text":"A higher propensity to consume or lower propensity to save raises C at a given income. The demonstration effect—adopting the spending patterns of richer groups—can also increase consumption.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Increase in Private Investment Expenditure","text":"Bullish business expectations and expected high returns raise investment, increasing AD directly and through induced consumption.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Increase in Government Expenditure","text":"Expansion of development and welfare commitments raises government consumption or investment, adding to AD.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Increase in Exports","text":"More foreign demand for domestic output, potentially encouraged by relatively lower domestic prices, raises AD.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Decrease in Imports","text":"Higher foreign prices can shift purchases towards domestic output. A lower M increases net exports and therefore AD, other things equal.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Cut in Tax Rates","text":"Lower taxes leave more disposable income, enabling higher consumption and planned expenditure.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Excess demand: all four consequences","textbookHeading":"9.3.4 Consequences of Excess Demand","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-excess-demand-all-four-consequences","body":"At full employment, with technology and productive capacity given, extra nominal demand cannot create an unlimited increase in real output.\n\n**1 · Inflationary Gap:** Planned AD exceeds the full-employment requirement. Pressure on fully used resources raises costs and the general price level. Measure the gap vertically at full-employment income.\n\n**2 · Static GDP:** Real GDP remains at the full-employment level because there is no spare capacity. Nominal GDP can rise as prices rise. Always specify real GDP when saying output remains constant.\n\n**3 · Wage–Price Spiral:** Higher demand raises pressure on wages and other costs; higher prices raise living costs and wage claims, which can raise costs and prices again. Persistent escalation threatens economic stability.\n\n**4 · Loss of Profit from Unfulfilled Demand:** The textbook’s point concerns potential profit forgone because producers cannot expand supplies to satisfy additional demand. It is distinct from claiming that every firm’s actual money profit must fall during inflation.\n\n**Overall Effects:** Real output and employment remain at capacity in this model, while prices rise. Compare this with deficient demand, which can reduce output and employment.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"At full employment, with technology and productive capacity given, extra nominal demand cannot create an unlimited increase in real output.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Inflationary Gap","text":"Planned AD exceeds the full-employment requirement. Pressure on fully used resources raises costs and the general price level. Measure the gap vertically at full-employment income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Static GDP","text":"Real GDP remains at the full-employment level because there is no spare capacity. Nominal GDP can rise as prices rise. Always specify real GDP when saying output remains constant.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Wage–Price Spiral","text":"Higher demand raises pressure on wages and other costs; higher prices raise living costs and wage claims, which can raise costs and prices again. Persistent escalation threatens economic stability.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Loss of Profit from Unfulfilled Demand","text":"The textbook’s point concerns potential profit forgone because producers cannot expand supplies to satisfy additional demand. It is distinct from claiming that every firm’s actual money profit must fall during inflation.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Overall Effects","text":"Real output and employment remain at capacity in this model, while prices rise. Compare this with deficient demand, which can reduce output and employment.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"9","flow":[["Compare demand","Is AD consistent with full-employment output?"],["Identify the gap","Excess demand or deficient demand."],["Choose the policy","Reduce or raise spending accordingly."],["Explain transmission","Connect the policy to AD and the gap."]]},{"id":"10","title":"Government Budget and the Economy","intro":"Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy.","lessons":[{"title":"Government budget and its objectives","body":"Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy. Objectives include GDP growth, allocation of resources, redistribution of income and wealth, economic stability, management of public enterprises and employment generation.\n\nPublic infrastructure can encourage private investment; progressive taxation and social spending can reduce inequality. Fiscal discipline means managing public finances responsibly to avoid unsustainable deficits and debt.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.1 CONCEPT OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET · 10.2 OBJECTIVES"},{"title":"Revenue receipts versus capital receipts","body":"Revenue receipts neither create a liability nor reduce assets. They include tax revenue and non-tax revenue such as fees, fines, interest, profits/dividends and grants.\n\nCapital receipts create a liability or reduce assets: borrowing creates liabilities, while loan recovery and disinvestment reduce assets. Do not classify a receipt by size or regularity alone; use the liability/asset test.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.3.1 Budget Receipts","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Revenue receipts","Capital receipts"],"rows":[["Liability","Do not create a liability","May create a liability"],["Assets","Do not reduce assets","May reduce assets"],["Examples","Taxes, fees, dividends","Borrowing, loan recovery, disinvestment"]]},"blocks":[{"title":"Revenue receipts","text":"Revenue receipts neither create a liability nor reduce assets. They include tax revenue and non-tax revenue such as fees, fines, interest, profits/dividends and grants.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Capital receipts","text":"Capital receipts create a liability or reduce assets: borrowing creates liabilities, while loan recovery and disinvestment reduce assets. Do not classify a receipt by size or regularity alone; use the liability/asset test.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Direct taxes, indirect taxes and non-tax revenue","body":"Direct taxes have impact and incidence on the same person, such as income tax and corporation tax. Indirect taxes allow the burden to be shifted, such as GST.\n\nA fee is a payment for a specific government service; a fine penalises an offence. A tax is compulsory and does not promise a direct equivalent service.\n\nProgressive taxes take a larger proportion as income rises; proportional taxes keep the same proportion; regressive burdens fall relatively more heavily on lower incomes.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Tax Revenue · Non-tax Revenue","blocks":[{"title":"Direct taxes","text":"Direct taxes have impact and incidence on the same person, such as income tax and corporation tax.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Indirect taxes","text":"Indirect taxes allow the burden to be shifted, such as GST.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fee","text":"A fee is a payment for a specific government service.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fine","text":"A fine penalises an offence.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Tax","text":"A tax is compulsory and does not promise a direct equivalent service.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Progressive tax","text":"Progressive taxes take a larger proportion as income rises.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Proportional tax","text":"Proportional taxes keep the same proportion.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Regressive tax","text":"Regressive burdens fall relatively more heavily on lower incomes.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Revenue expenditure versus capital expenditure","body":"Revenue expenditure neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities. Salaries, pensions, subsidies and interest payments are examples.\n\nCapital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities: construction of a highway, purchase of equipment, lending by government and repayment of loan principal. Repayment of principal is capital expenditure; interest is revenue expenditure.\n\nGrants to states remain revenue expenditure for the granting government even if the recipient uses them to build assets, following the textbook convention.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.3.2 Budget Expenditure","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Revenue expenditure","Capital expenditure"],"rows":[["Asset / liability test","Neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities","Creates assets or reduces liabilities"],["Examples","Salaries, interest, pensions, subsidies","Infrastructure creation, loan repayment"],["Common trap","Recurring is not the decisive accounting test","Apply the asset/liability test"]]},"blocks":[{"title":"Revenue expenditure","text":"Revenue expenditure neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities. Salaries, pensions, subsidies and interest payments are examples.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Capital expenditure","text":"Capital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities: construction of a highway, purchase of equipment, lending by government and repayment of loan principal. Repayment of principal is capital expenditure; interest is revenue expenditure.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Grants to states remain revenue expenditure for the granting government even if the recipient uses them to build assets, following the textbook convention.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit","body":"Revenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts. Fiscal deficit is total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings; it measures borrowing requirements.\n\nPrimary deficit deducts interest payments from fiscal deficit and indicates the current borrowing requirement excluding past-debt interest. A zero revenue deficit does not guarantee zero fiscal deficit.\n\nA zero primary deficit means borrowing covers interest payments.","formula":"Revenue deficit = Revenue expenditure − Revenue receipts\nFiscal deficit = Total expenditure − Revenue receipts − Non-debt capital receipts\nPrimary deficit = Fiscal deficit − Interest payments","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.4 BUDGET DEFICIT: REVENUE DEFICIT, FISCAL DEFICIT AND PRIMARY DEFICIT","blocks":[{"title":"Revenue deficit","text":"Revenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fiscal deficit","text":"Fiscal deficit is total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings; it measures borrowing requirements.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Primary deficit","text":"Primary deficit deducts interest payments from fiscal deficit and indicates the current borrowing requirement excluding past-debt interest. A zero revenue deficit does not guarantee zero fiscal deficit.\n\nA zero primary deficit means borrowing covers interest payments.","tone":"point"}]},{"title":"Implications, balanced and unbalanced budgets","body":"A revenue deficit indicates government dissaving: borrowing may finance current consumption rather than asset creation. Fiscal deficits may raise debt and interest burdens and crowd out private borrowing; financing expenditure can also support demand and productive capacity, depending on its use.\n\nA balanced budget has receipts equal to expenditure; a surplus budget has receipts above expenditure and can restrain inflation; a deficit budget can support demand in recession. Consider both the size and composition of expenditure.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.5 BALANCED AND UNBALANCED BUDGET"},{"title":"Ability Zone: public, private, common and club goods","body":"Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence. Private goods are rival and excludable.\n\nCommon goods are rival but non-excludable, such as fish in an open pond. Club goods are excludable but non-rival until congestion, such as a subscription service.\n\nRivalry means one person’s use reduces availability to others; excludability means users can be denied access. Public expenditure supports infrastructure, redistribution, stability and an investment-friendly environment.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Public Goods, Private Goods, Common Goods and Club Goods","blocks":[{"title":"Public goods","text":"Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Private goods","text":"Private goods are rival and excludable.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Common goods","text":"Common goods are rival but non-excludable, such as fish in an open pond.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Club goods","text":"Club goods are excludable but non-rival until congestion, such as a subscription service.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Rivalry means one person’s use reduces availability to others; excludability means users can be denied access. Public expenditure supports infrastructure, redistribution, stability and an investment-friendly environment.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Budget objectives: the headings to reproduce","textbookHeading":"10.2 OBJECTIVES OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET","body":"GDP Growth: create infrastructure and induce private investment through suitable incentives. Allocation of Resources: influence production through taxes, subsidies and public provision.\n\nProvision of Public Goods: provide collective services that markets may undersupply. Redistribution of Income and Wealth: use progressive taxation and welfare spending.\n\nBalanced Regional Growth: direct infrastructure and incentives to less-developed regions. Employment Opportunities: expand activity and labour demand.\n\nEconomic Stability: raise spending during deficient demand and restrain it during excess demand. Select and explain the number of objectives requested, using these headings.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"GDP Growth","text":"Create infrastructure and induce private investment through suitable incentives.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Allocation of Resources","text":"Influence production through taxes, subsidies and public provision.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Provision of Public Goods","text":"Provide collective services that markets may undersupply.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Redistribution of Income and Wealth","text":"Use progressive taxation and welfare spending.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Balanced Regional Growth","text":"Direct infrastructure and incentives to less-developed regions.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Employment Opportunities","text":"Expand activity and labour demand.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Economic Stability","text":"Raise spending during deficient demand and restrain it during excess demand.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"Select and explain the number of objectives requested, using these headings.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Free-rider problem","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Free Rider Problem","body":"A free rider enjoys the benefits of goods and services without contributing the full or partial cost of providing them. Non-excludability makes it difficult to withhold benefits from non-payers.\n\nPrivate providers may be unable to collect enough revenue to cover costs, so the market can fail to provide a needed public good. National defence and street lighting illustrate why public provision can be necessary.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""},{"title":"Tax rates and tax burden are different","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Progressive and Regressive Nature of Taxation is Different from Progressive and Regressive Rates of Taxation","body":"Progressive Rate: the tax rate rises with income. Regressive Rate: the tax rate falls with income.\n\nProportionate Rate: the tax rate stays constant as income changes. Progressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the rich.\n\nRegressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the poor. The textbook stresses that a proportionate tax can still have a regressive real burden.\n\nWhen answering, specify whether you mean the numerical rate or the economic burden.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":"","blocks":[{"title":"Progressive Rate","text":"The tax rate rises with income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Regressive Rate","text":"The tax rate falls with income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Proportionate Rate","text":"The tax rate stays constant as income changes.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Progressive Nature","text":"The real burden is greater on the rich.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Regressive Nature","text":"The real burden is greater on the poor.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"The textbook stresses that a proportionate tax can still have a regressive real burden.\n\nWhen answering, specify whether you mean the numerical rate or the economic burden.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Does a balanced budget leave AD unchanged?","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Does Balanced Budget Leave Aggregate Demand Unaffected in the Economy?","body":"No. Equal tax collection and government expenditure do not necessarily have equal first-round effects on AD.\n\nGovernment expenditure enters AD in full; a tax increase reduces disposable income, and consumption falls by MPC times the tax rise. If G and taxes each rise by ₹100 and MPC is 0.5, expenditure raises AD by ₹100 while the initial consumption loss is ₹50, giving a net initial rise of ₹50.\n\nUnder the simple lump-sum-tax model, later rounds imply a balanced-budget multiplier of one. Distinguish the initial AD change from the final income change.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":"Initial ΔAD = ΔG − MPC × ΔT\nIf ΔG = ΔT: initial ΔAD = (1 − MPC)ΔG"},{"formula":"","title":"Revenue deficit and fiscal deficit: all implications","textbookHeading":"10.4.2 Implications of Budget Deficits","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-revenue-deficit-and-fiscal-deficit-all-implications","body":"Deficit definitions measure different gaps. Their consequences depend on the method of financing, the use of funds and the economy’s available capacity.\n\n**Revenue Deficit 1 · Pressure on Welfare Spending:** A shortfall of revenue receipts against current expenditure can force cuts to subsidies or welfare programmes, potentially reducing social welfare.\n\n**Revenue Deficit 2 · Higher Liabilities:** Borrowing from the public, central bank or abroad can finance the shortfall but increases liabilities and future debt-service obligations.\n\n**Revenue Deficit 3 · Disinvestment Pressure:** Selling public shareholdings can raise funds, but reduces public ownership. The textbook also notes possible increased foreign control when buyers are foreign firms.\n\n**Fiscal Deficit 1 · Inflationary Spiral:** Central-bank financing can expand money and nominal spending. If output cannot respond because capacity is fully used, prices rise. Inflation is therefore a conditional consequence, not an automatic result of every deficit.\n\n**Fiscal Deficit 2 · National Debt:** Repeated borrowing increases debt and interest obligations. More resources spent servicing past debt can limit funds for development.\n\n**Fiscal Deficit 3 · Vicious Circle of High Deficit and Low Growth:** Debt service and fiscal pressures may constrain productive spending; higher taxes can weaken private demand and investment. Weak growth then limits revenue, keeping the deficit high. Productive public investment can instead support growth, so composition matters.\n\n**Fiscal Deficit 4 · Crowding-out:** Heavy public borrowing can reduce funds available to private investors and put upward pressure on financing costs, discouraging private investment.\n\n**Fiscal Deficit 5 · Erosion of Government Credibility:** Unsustainable borrowing can weaken confidence and creditworthiness, discourage investors and increase financing difficulty. A deficit used responsibly for productive capacity has different implications from persistent financing of unproductive spending.\n\n**Primary Deficit:** Primary deficit removes interest on past debt from fiscal deficit. Zero primary deficit means the remaining borrowing requirement equals interest payments; a positive primary deficit indicates an additional gap even before those interest payments.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Deficit definitions measure different gaps. Their consequences depend on the method of financing, the use of funds and the economy’s available capacity.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Revenue Deficit 1 · Pressure on Welfare Spending","text":"A shortfall of revenue receipts against current expenditure can force cuts to subsidies or welfare programmes, potentially reducing social welfare.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Revenue Deficit 2 · Higher Liabilities","text":"Borrowing from the public, central bank or abroad can finance the shortfall but increases liabilities and future debt-service obligations.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Revenue Deficit 3 · Disinvestment Pressure","text":"Selling public shareholdings can raise funds, but reduces public ownership. The textbook also notes possible increased foreign control when buyers are foreign firms.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fiscal Deficit 1 · Inflationary Spiral","text":"Central-bank financing can expand money and nominal spending. If output cannot respond because capacity is fully used, prices rise. Inflation is therefore a conditional consequence, not an automatic result of every deficit.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fiscal Deficit 2 · National Debt","text":"Repeated borrowing increases debt and interest obligations. More resources spent servicing past debt can limit funds for development.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fiscal Deficit 3 · Vicious Circle of High Deficit and Low Growth","text":"Debt service and fiscal pressures may constrain productive spending; higher taxes can weaken private demand and investment. Weak growth then limits revenue, keeping the deficit high. Productive public investment can instead support growth, so composition matters.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fiscal Deficit 4 · Crowding-out","text":"Heavy public borrowing can reduce funds available to private investors and put upward pressure on financing costs, discouraging private investment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Fiscal Deficit 5 · Erosion of Government Credibility","text":"Unsustainable borrowing can weaken confidence and creditworthiness, discourage investors and increase financing difficulty. A deficit used responsibly for productive capacity has different implications from persistent financing of unproductive spending.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Primary Deficit","text":"Primary deficit removes interest on past debt from fiscal deficit. Zero primary deficit means the remaining borrowing requirement equals interest payments; a positive primary deficit indicates an additional gap even before those interest payments.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Balanced, surplus and deficit budgets: complete appraisal","textbookHeading":"10.5 Balanced and Unbalanced Budgets","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-balanced-surplus-and-deficit-budgets-complete-appraisal","body":"A budget’s appropriateness depends on economic conditions. The textbook gives separate merits and demerits for balanced, surplus and deficit budgets.\n\n**Balanced Budget · Meaning and Merits:** Receipts equal expenditure. Its stated merits are restraint of wasteful expenditure and a signal of financial stability and fiscal discipline.\n\n**Balanced Budget · Demerits:** It does not necessarily remedy unemployment caused by inadequate AD. In developing economies, insisting on balance can prevent the large initial public-investment push needed for growth.\n\n**Surplus Budget · Meaning and Merits:** Receipts exceed expenditure. Greater revenue collection and/or lower spending can reduce AD, making a surplus useful against an inflationary gap.\n\n**Surplus Budget · Demerits:** It can worsen depression by weakening AD further. Persistently suppressing spending can reduce output and employment and contribute to a low-level equilibrium trap.\n\n**Deficit Budget · Meaning and Merits:** Expenditure exceeds receipts before the financing needed to close the gap. Keynes favoured its use during depression: public spending raises AD directly and can induce further consumption and investment.\n\n**Deficit Budget · Demerits:** At full employment, additional spending can enlarge excess demand without increasing real output, intensifying inflation and possibly a wage–price spiral. Debt sustainability and the use of borrowed funds also matter.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"A budget’s appropriateness depends on economic conditions. The textbook gives separate merits and demerits for balanced, surplus and deficit budgets.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Balanced Budget · Meaning and Merits","text":"Receipts equal expenditure. Its stated merits are restraint of wasteful expenditure and a signal of financial stability and fiscal discipline.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Balanced Budget · Demerits","text":"It does not necessarily remedy unemployment caused by inadequate AD. In developing economies, insisting on balance can prevent the large initial public-investment push needed for growth.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Surplus Budget · Meaning and Merits","text":"Receipts exceed expenditure. Greater revenue collection and/or lower spending can reduce AD, making a surplus useful against an inflationary gap.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Surplus Budget · Demerits","text":"It can worsen depression by weakening AD further. Persistently suppressing spending can reduce output and employment and contribute to a low-level equilibrium trap.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Deficit Budget · Meaning and Merits","text":"Expenditure exceeds receipts before the financing needed to close the gap. Keynes favoured its use during depression: public spending raises AD directly and can induce further consumption and investment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Deficit Budget · Demerits","text":"At full employment, additional spending can enlarge excess demand without increasing real output, intensifying inflation and possibly a wage–price spiral. Debt sustainability and the use of borrowed funds also matter.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Public expenditure: all four importance points","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · Significance of Public Expenditure","kind":"Ability Zone","key":"restored-public-expenditure-all-four-importance-points","body":"Public expenditure has growth and welfare roles. Explain what the government spends on and the channel through which it affects the economy.\n\n**1 · Accelerates GDP Growth:** Investment in public enterprises, capital grants for equipment, input subsidies and farm procurement support production and investment. Infrastructure also adds productive capacity.\n\n**2 · Promotes Equality:** Welfare pensions and access to food, education and healthcare improve the position of poorer households, reducing inequalities in opportunities and living standards.\n\n**3 · Restores Economic Stability:** During recession, public consumption and investment raise AD. This can interrupt the cycle of weak demand, weak output and weak income.\n\n**4 · Creates an Investment-friendly Environment:** Roads, dams, bridges and reliable transport reduce private production costs and improve access to markets. This encourages private investment rather than replacing every private activity.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Public expenditure has growth and welfare roles. Explain what the government spends on and the channel through which it affects the economy.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Accelerates GDP Growth","text":"Investment in public enterprises, capital grants for equipment, input subsidies and farm procurement support production and investment. Infrastructure also adds productive capacity.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Promotes Equality","text":"Welfare pensions and access to food, education and healthcare improve the position of poorer households, reducing inequalities in opportunities and living standards.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Restores Economic Stability","text":"During recession, public consumption and investment raise AD. This can interrupt the cycle of weak demand, weak output and weak income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Creates an Investment-friendly Environment","text":"Roads, dams, bridges and reliable transport reduce private production costs and improve access to markets. This encourages private investment rather than replacing every private activity.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Non-tax revenue: all seven sources","textbookHeading":"10.3.1 Non-tax Receipts","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-non-tax-revenue-all-seven-sources","body":"Non-tax receipts arise from sources other than taxes and are part of revenue receipts when they neither create liabilities nor reduce government assets.\n\n**1 · Fees:** Payments for specified administrative or judicial services, such as passports, registration and court services. The chapter distinguishes these from prices charged for ordinary commercial services.\n\n**2 · Fines:** Economic penalties for breaking laws. Their primary purpose is enforcement and deterrence rather than raising revenue.\n\n**3 · Escheat:** Property without a legal heir or valid claimant can pass to the state, generating income for the government under the chapter’s classification.\n\n**4 · Special Assessment:** Owners may pay when public development raises their property value, helping recover part of the cost of roads, drainage or similar improvements.\n\n**5 · Income from Public Enterprises:** Profits and dividends from government-owned enterprises provide revenue. Selling government shares is different: it reduces an asset and is a capital receipt.\n\n**6 · Spectrum Receipts:** The chapter classifies income from allocating telecom spectrum as a non-tax receipt.\n\n**7 · Grants and Donations:** Assistance and voluntary donations received by government, including during natural calamities, are revenue receipts when they carry no repayment liability.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Non-tax receipts arise from sources other than taxes and are part of revenue receipts when they neither create liabilities nor reduce government assets.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Fees","text":"Payments for specified administrative or judicial services, such as passports, registration and court services. The chapter distinguishes these from prices charged for ordinary commercial services.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Fines","text":"Economic penalties for breaking laws. Their primary purpose is enforcement and deterrence rather than raising revenue.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Escheat","text":"Property without a legal heir or valid claimant can pass to the state, generating income for the government under the chapter’s classification.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Special Assessment","text":"Owners may pay when public development raises their property value, helping recover part of the cost of roads, drainage or similar improvements.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Income from Public Enterprises","text":"Profits and dividends from government-owned enterprises provide revenue. Selling government shares is different: it reduces an asset and is a capital receipt.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Spectrum Receipts","text":"The chapter classifies income from allocating telecom spectrum as a non-tax receipt.","tone":"point"},{"title":"7 · Grants and Donations","text":"Assistance and voluntary donations received by government, including during natural calamities, are revenue receipts when they carry no repayment liability.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Plan and non-plan expenditure: the historical distinction","textbookHeading":"10.3.2 Plan and Non-plan Expenditure","kind":"Textbook distinction","key":"restored-plan-and-non-plan-expenditure-the-historical-distinction","body":"The textbook also explains this older budget classification. It is separate from the revenue/capital classification; learn what each term meant rather than treating them as synonyms.\n\n**Plan Expenditure:** Expenditure linked to specified development plans and programmes, including central assistance to states. It could include revenue spending, such as assistance, and capital spending, such as roads, bridges and hospitals.\n\n**Non-plan Expenditure:** Expenditure outside the plan category, including routine government functioning, law and order, defence and subsidies in the chapter’s account.\n\n**Two Different Classification Bases:** Plan/non-plan asks about a spending programme’s relationship to the planning framework. Revenue/capital asks whether it creates assets or reduces liabilities. Plan expenditure is not automatically capital expenditure.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"The textbook also explains this older budget classification. It is separate from the revenue/capital classification; learn what each term meant rather than treating them as synonyms.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Plan Expenditure","text":"Expenditure linked to specified development plans and programmes, including central assistance to states. It could include revenue spending, such as assistance, and capital spending, such as roads, bridges and hospitals.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Non-plan Expenditure","text":"Expenditure outside the plan category, including routine government functioning, law and order, defence and subsidies in the chapter’s account.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Two Different Classification Bases","text":"Plan/non-plan asks about a spending programme’s relationship to the planning framework. Revenue/capital asks whether it creates assets or reduces liabilities. Plan expenditure is not automatically capital expenditure.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"10","flow":[["Receipts","Revenue and capital receipts."],["Expenditure","Revenue and capital spending."],["Deficits","Measure the financing gap."],["Policy objectives","Allocation, redistribution and stability."]]},{"id":"11","title":"Foreign Exchange Rate","intro":"Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency.","lessons":[{"title":"Foreign exchange and the quotation convention","body":"Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency. Foreign exchange rate is the price paid in domestic currency for a unit of foreign currency.\n\nThroughout these lessons, quote the rate as ₹ per US dollar. A rise from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ means the rupee buys fewer dollars and has depreciated; a fall to ₹76/$ means rupee appreciation.\n\nAlways identify which currency is in the numerator before describing a movement.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.1 FOREIGN EXCHANGE AND FOREIGN EXCHANGE RATE"},{"title":"Demand, supply and flexible exchange-rate determination","body":"Flexible exchange rate is determined by demand and supply in the foreign exchange market. Demand for foreign exchange comes from imports, overseas investment, foreign travel, loan repayment, income payments, grants and speculation.\n\nSupply comes from exports, foreign investment, inward loans, tourism receipts, income receipts, grants and remittances. With ₹/$ on the vertical axis, demand slopes downward and supply upward; their intersection is the equilibrium rate.\n\nA higher exchange rate makes foreign goods costlier in rupees and normally reduces import-related foreign-exchange demand.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.2.1 Flexible Exchange Rate"},{"title":"Movements versus shifts, appreciation versus depreciation","body":"A change in the exchange rate causes movement along a given demand or supply curve. A change in imports, investment preferences, income or foreign demand shifts a curve.\n\nGreater demand for dollars shifts demand right, raising ₹/$ and depreciating the rupee. Greater supply of dollars shifts supply right, lowering ₹/$ and appreciating the rupee.\n\nDepreciation can make exports cheaper for foreigners and imports costlier for residents; actual quantity responses depend on demand conditions.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Appreciation and Depreciation of Domestic Currency"},{"title":"Fixed rate, devaluation and revaluation","body":"Fixed exchange rate is set and maintained by the monetary authority. An official rise in ₹/$ is devaluation of the rupee; an official fall is revaluation.\n\nDepreciation/appreciation describe market-driven changes under a flexible system. To defend a fixed rate when demand for dollars exceeds supply, the authority sells foreign currency from reserves; when dollar supply exceeds demand it buys foreign currency.\n\nAn overvalued domestic currency tends to create excess demand for foreign currency at the official rate.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.2.2 Fixed Exchange Rate"},{"title":"Managed floating and merits/demerits","body":"Managed floating combines market determination with occasional central-bank intervention to limit undesirable movements; the textbook also calls it dirty floating. Fixed rates reduce exchange uncertainty and can support trade but require reserves and may misallocate resources.\n\nFlexible rates need less reserve defence and adjust through markets but create uncertainty and exposure to external shocks. The four appraisal sections below explain all five merits and five demerits of each system.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.3 MANAGED FLOATING · 11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS"},{"title":"Foreign exchange market: transfer, credit and hedging","body":"The foreign exchange market is the market for national currencies. The transfer function moves purchasing power between countries.\n\nThe credit function supports trade through financing arrangements. Hedging reduces exchange-rate risk.\n\nSpot transactions exchange currency for current delivery; forward transactions agree today on a rate for delivery at a future date. A forward contract fixes an exchange rate; it does not guarantee that the future spot rate will equal that rate.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.7 FOREIGN EXCHANGE MARKET","blocks":[{"title":"","text":"The foreign exchange market is the market for national currencies.","tone":"prose"},{"title":"Transfer function","text":"The transfer function moves purchasing power between countries.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Credit function","text":"The credit function supports trade through financing arrangements.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Hedging","text":"Hedging reduces exchange-rate risk.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Spot transactions","text":"Spot transactions exchange currency for current delivery.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Forward transactions","text":"Forward transactions agree today on a rate for delivery at a future date.","tone":"point"},{"title":"","text":"A forward contract fixes an exchange rate; it does not guarantee that the future spot rate will equal that rate.","tone":"prose"}]},{"title":"Fixed exchange rate: all five merits","textbookHeading":"11.4.1(a) Fixed Exchange Rate System: Merits","body":"A fixed rate is officially set and defended. The chapter’s arguments concern the benefits of greater exchange-rate predictability, subject to successful maintenance of the rate.\n\n**1 · Market Stability and Mobility of Capital:** Reducing exchange-rate fluctuations lowers one source of uncertainty for international investors and can encourage cross-border capital movement.\n\n**2 · Growth of International Trade:** Exporters and importers can plan contracts with more predictable domestic-currency returns and costs, supporting international trade.\n\n**3 · Stable Macroeconomic Policies:** The textbook argues that a stable exchange rate removes frequent exchange-rate changes as a source of policy disturbance. Maintaining the peg still places constraints on policy; it does not give unlimited monetary independence.\n\n**4 · Devaluation as a Tool to Expand Foreign Markets:** An official downward adjustment in the domestic currency’s value can make exports cheaper to foreigners and improve producers’ international competitiveness, depending on demand responses.\n\n**5 · A Check on Inflation:** High domestic inflation makes exports less competitive at a fixed rate, weakening receipts and reserves. Pressure to preserve the rate can encourage policies restraining inflation.","kind":"Concept","formula":"","key":"restored-fixed-exchange-rate-all-five-merits","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"A fixed rate is officially set and defended. The chapter’s arguments concern the benefits of greater exchange-rate predictability, subject to successful maintenance of the rate.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Market Stability and Mobility of Capital","text":"Reducing exchange-rate fluctuations lowers one source of uncertainty for international investors and can encourage cross-border capital movement.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Growth of International Trade","text":"Exporters and importers can plan contracts with more predictable domestic-currency returns and costs, supporting international trade.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Stable Macroeconomic Policies","text":"The textbook argues that a stable exchange rate removes frequent exchange-rate changes as a source of policy disturbance. Maintaining the peg still places constraints on policy; it does not give unlimited monetary independence.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Devaluation as a Tool to Expand Foreign Markets","text":"An official downward adjustment in the domestic currency’s value can make exports cheaper to foreigners and improve producers’ international competitiveness, depending on demand responses.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · A Check on Inflation","text":"High domestic inflation makes exports less competitive at a fixed rate, weakening receipts and reserves. Pressure to preserve the rate can encourage policies restraining inflation.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Fixed exchange rate: all five demerits","textbookHeading":"11.4.1(b) Fixed Exchange Rate System: Demerits","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-fixed-exchange-rate-all-five-demerits","body":"The same commitment that provides predictability requires defence and can prevent the official rate from adjusting to market conditions.\n\n**1 · Reserves of Forex:** The authority needs foreign-exchange reserves to meet excess demand for foreign currency and defend the official rate. Sustained pressure can exhaust reserves.\n\n**2 · Inefficient Allocation of Resources:** An official rate differing from the market-clearing rate distorts the relative prices guiding production, imports and exports.\n\n**3 · Small Size of Forex Market:** The chapter argues that a tightly fixed rate limits foreign exchange as an independently traded commodity, restricting the scope of currency-market activity.\n\n**4 · Discourages Venture Capital:** In the chapter’s usage, this means funds placed in foreign-currency trading to profit from exchange movements. A tightly fixed rate limits these opportunities; this is different from ordinary start-up venture finance.\n\n**5 · Divergence from Equilibrium:** The official rate can remain above or below the equilibrium rate. Persistent excess demand or supply then requires intervention and can impair efficient production and trade.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"The same commitment that provides predictability requires defence and can prevent the official rate from adjusting to market conditions.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Reserves of Forex","text":"The authority needs foreign-exchange reserves to meet excess demand for foreign currency and defend the official rate. Sustained pressure can exhaust reserves.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Inefficient Allocation of Resources","text":"An official rate differing from the market-clearing rate distorts the relative prices guiding production, imports and exports.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Small Size of Forex Market","text":"The chapter argues that a tightly fixed rate limits foreign exchange as an independently traded commodity, restricting the scope of currency-market activity.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Discourages Venture Capital","text":"In the chapter’s usage, this means funds placed in foreign-currency trading to profit from exchange movements. A tightly fixed rate limits these opportunities; this is different from ordinary start-up venture finance.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Divergence from Equilibrium","text":"The official rate can remain above or below the equilibrium rate. Persistent excess demand or supply then requires intervention and can impair efficient production and trade.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Flexible exchange rate: all five merits","textbookHeading":"11.4.2(a) Flexible Exchange Rate System: Merits","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-flexible-exchange-rate-all-five-merits","body":"A flexible rate moves with demand and supply. Its advantages arise from allowing prices to adjust rather than defending a prescribed value.\n\n**1 · Large Forex Reserves Not Required for Rate Defence:** No official peg needs continuous defence. Market adjustment can equilibrate currency demand and supply, although countries may still hold reserves for other reasons.\n\n**2 · Efficient Allocation of Resources:** Market-clearing exchange rates transmit scarcity and demand signals, helping guide trade, production and investment across countries.\n\n**3 · Large Size of Forex Market:** Currency itself becomes a traded asset, expanding financial-market activity and related business opportunities.\n\n**4 · International Mobility of Liquidity:** Less foreign exchange needs to remain committed to defending a fixed rate, potentially freeing funds for international transactions and investment.\n\n**5 · Encourages Venture Capital in Currency Trading:** Exchange-rate movements create opportunities for investors willing to bear currency risk. This is the chapter’s currency-market meaning of venture capital.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"A flexible rate moves with demand and supply. Its advantages arise from allowing prices to adjust rather than defending a prescribed value.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Large Forex Reserves Not Required for Rate Defence","text":"No official peg needs continuous defence. Market adjustment can equilibrate currency demand and supply, although countries may still hold reserves for other reasons.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Efficient Allocation of Resources","text":"Market-clearing exchange rates transmit scarcity and demand signals, helping guide trade, production and investment across countries.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Large Size of Forex Market","text":"Currency itself becomes a traded asset, expanding financial-market activity and related business opportunities.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · International Mobility of Liquidity","text":"Less foreign exchange needs to remain committed to defending a fixed rate, potentially freeing funds for international transactions and investment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Encourages Venture Capital in Currency Trading","text":"Exchange-rate movements create opportunities for investors willing to bear currency risk. This is the chapter’s currency-market meaning of venture capital.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Flexible exchange rate: all five demerits","textbookHeading":"11.4.2(b) Flexible Exchange Rate System: Demerits","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-flexible-exchange-rate-all-five-demerits","body":"Market adjustment creates uncertainty and can transmit foreign disturbances into the domestic economy.\n\n**1 · Marginalisation of Weak Currencies:** Low demand for a weak currency can lead to steep depreciation. Imports and foreign-currency obligations become more expensive in domestic money, creating pressure for smaller or vulnerable economies.\n\n**2 · Uncertainty of the Market:** Frequent exchange-rate changes make future export receipts, import costs and investment returns harder to predict.\n\n**3 · Difficulty Maintaining Stable Monetary Policy:** Exchange movements affect inflation, capital flows and interest-rate decisions. Responding to them can complicate a stable policy path.\n\n**4 · Difficulty with Bilateral Trade Agreements:** Changing currency values make it harder to predict the value of payments under long-term trade arrangements. Hedging can reduce, but not eliminate, this problem.\n\n**5 · External Shocks:** Foreign-currency appreciation can raise the domestic-currency cost of imports even when their foreign price is unchanged. A stronger dollar, for example, increases the rupee bill for a fixed dollar payment.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Market adjustment creates uncertainty and can transmit foreign disturbances into the domestic economy.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Marginalisation of Weak Currencies","text":"Low demand for a weak currency can lead to steep depreciation. Imports and foreign-currency obligations become more expensive in domestic money, creating pressure for smaller or vulnerable economies.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Uncertainty of the Market","text":"Frequent exchange-rate changes make future export receipts, import costs and investment returns harder to predict.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Difficulty Maintaining Stable Monetary Policy","text":"Exchange movements affect inflation, capital flows and interest-rate decisions. Responding to them can complicate a stable policy path.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Difficulty with Bilateral Trade Agreements","text":"Changing currency values make it harder to predict the value of payments under long-term trade arrangements. Hedging can reduce, but not eliminate, this problem.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · External Shocks","text":"Foreign-currency appreciation can raise the domestic-currency cost of imports even when their foreign price is unchanged. A stronger dollar, for example, increases the rupee bill for a fixed dollar payment.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Demand for foreign exchange: all seven components","textbookHeading":"11.5 Components of Demand for Foreign Exchange","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-demand-for-foreign-exchange-all-seven-components","body":"Foreign exchange is demanded to make payments abroad or acquire foreign-currency assets. Distinguish a change in the exchange rate from other factors shifting demand.\n\n**1 · Repayment of International Loans:** Foreign-currency loan principal must be repaid in the agreed currency, creating demand for foreign exchange.\n\n**2 · Investment in the Rest of the World:** Residents buying foreign securities, businesses or other foreign assets need the relevant currency.\n\n**3 · Imports:** Payments for imported goods and services create demand for foreign exchange.\n\n**4 · Direct Purchases Abroad:** Foreign travel, study and medical treatment require payments abroad. The chapter includes tourists’ direct purchases here.\n\n**5 · Grants and Donations:** Unilateral payments sent abroad also require foreign exchange, even though no goods or productive service is received in return.\n\n**6 · Payment of Incomes:** Factor incomes such as wages, rent, interest and profits paid to non-residents create an outward flow.\n\n**7 · Speculative Trading:** People may acquire foreign currency expecting its value to rise and hoping to profit from a later sale.\n\n**Why Demand Slopes Downward:** With ₹ per dollar on the vertical axis, a lower dollar price makes foreign goods and investment cheaper in rupees, increasing demand for dollars, other things equal. A price change causes movement along the curve; other determinants shift it.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Foreign exchange is demanded to make payments abroad or acquire foreign-currency assets. Distinguish a change in the exchange rate from other factors shifting demand.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Repayment of International Loans","text":"Foreign-currency loan principal must be repaid in the agreed currency, creating demand for foreign exchange.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Investment in the Rest of the World","text":"Residents buying foreign securities, businesses or other foreign assets need the relevant currency.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Imports","text":"Payments for imported goods and services create demand for foreign exchange.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Direct Purchases Abroad","text":"Foreign travel, study and medical treatment require payments abroad. The chapter includes tourists’ direct purchases here.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Grants and Donations","text":"Unilateral payments sent abroad also require foreign exchange, even though no goods or productive service is received in return.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Payment of Incomes","text":"Factor incomes such as wages, rent, interest and profits paid to non-residents create an outward flow.","tone":"point"},{"title":"7 · Speculative Trading","text":"People may acquire foreign currency expecting its value to rise and hoping to profit from a later sale.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Why Demand Slopes Downward","text":"With ₹ per dollar on the vertical axis, a lower dollar price makes foreign goods and investment cheaper in rupees, increasing demand for dollars, other things equal. A price change causes movement along the curve; other determinants shift it.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Supply of foreign exchange: all seven sources","textbookHeading":"11.6 Components of Supply of Foreign Exchange","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-supply-of-foreign-exchange-all-seven-sources","body":"The supply of foreign exchange comes from receipts from abroad and conversion of foreign currency into domestic currency.\n\n**1 · Exports:** Foreign buyers pay for domestic goods and services, generating foreign-exchange receipts.\n\n**2 · Investment from the Rest of the World:** Inward FDI and portfolio investment bring foreign funds into the economy.\n\n**3 · Direct Purchases by Non-residents:** Foreign tourists and other non-residents purchasing domestically contribute foreign-exchange receipts.\n\n**4 · Loans from the Rest of the World:** Borrowing abroad brings in foreign exchange now, while creating future repayment obligations.\n\n**5 · Grants and Donations from Abroad:** Foreign assistance, gifts and donations create receipts without a corresponding repayment or current productive service.\n\n**6 · Income Receipts:** Factor incomes earned from abroad—including wages, interest, rent and profits—bring in foreign exchange.\n\n**7 · Remittances by Non-residents:** Money sent by non-residents to domestic recipients is a source of foreign exchange. A gift remittance is a transfer, not automatically factor income.\n\n**Why Supply Slopes Upward:** A higher ₹-per-dollar rate gives more rupees per dollar. It can make domestic goods cheaper for foreign buyers and improve the attractiveness of domestic purchases or investments, increasing foreign-exchange supply, other things equal.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"The supply of foreign exchange comes from receipts from abroad and conversion of foreign currency into domestic currency.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Exports","text":"Foreign buyers pay for domestic goods and services, generating foreign-exchange receipts.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Investment from the Rest of the World","text":"Inward FDI and portfolio investment bring foreign funds into the economy.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Direct Purchases by Non-residents","text":"Foreign tourists and other non-residents purchasing domestically contribute foreign-exchange receipts.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Loans from the Rest of the World","text":"Borrowing abroad brings in foreign exchange now, while creating future repayment obligations.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Grants and Donations from Abroad","text":"Foreign assistance, gifts and donations create receipts without a corresponding repayment or current productive service.","tone":"point"},{"title":"6 · Income Receipts","text":"Factor incomes earned from abroad—including wages, interest, rent and profits—bring in foreign exchange.","tone":"point"},{"title":"7 · Remittances by Non-residents","text":"Money sent by non-residents to domestic recipients is a source of foreign exchange. A gift remittance is a transfer, not automatically factor income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Why Supply Slopes Upward","text":"A higher ₹-per-dollar rate gives more rupees per dollar. It can make domestic goods cheaper for foreign buyers and improve the attractiveness of domestic purchases or investments, increasing foreign-exchange supply, other things equal.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"11","flow":[["Foreign-currency demand","Imports and other payments abroad."],["Foreign-currency supply","Exports and other receipts from abroad."],["Exchange rate","Interaction of demand and supply determines the market rate."]]},{"id":"12","title":"Balance of Payments","intro":"Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year.","lessons":[{"title":"Balance of payments: account and recording rule","body":"Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year. It records goods, services, income, transfers and asset-related transactions between residents and non-residents.\n\nForeign-exchange receipts are credit items; payments are debit items. The textbook uses current, capital and official-reserves accounts.\n\nKeep its school-level account terminology when answering.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.1 BoP (or BoP ACCOUNTS)"},{"title":"Current account, trade balance and invisibles","body":"Current account records exports and imports of goods and services, factor income and current transfers. Goods are visible trade or merchandise; services, factor incomes and transfers are invisibles.\n\nBalance of trade covers goods only. A gift from abroad is a current transfer; wages for a service are factor income.\n\nThe supplied purple box treats direct tourist purchases as invisible trade: preserve that convention for this textbook.","formula":"Trade balance = Goods exports − Goods imports\nCurrent-account balance = Trade balance + Net services + Net factor income + Net current transfers","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.1 Current Account"},{"title":"Capital account and asset/liability changes","body":"Capital account records transactions that change a country’s assets or liabilities in relation to the rest of the world. The textbook includes borrowing, foreign investment, NRI deposits, banking capital and short-term debt.\n\nInward FDI or an external loan is a credit; an outward investment or repayment of foreign loan principal is a debit. Interest on a foreign loan belongs in the current account; the principal belongs in the capital account.\n\nFDI involves a lasting productive interest/control; portfolio investment purchases financial securities.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.2 Capital Account"},{"title":"Official reserves, deficit and surplus","body":"If autonomous foreign-exchange payments exceed autonomous receipts, there is a BoP deficit, financed by accommodating transactions such as a drawdown of reserves or official borrowing. A surplus can increase official reserves.\n\nA decrease in reserve assets finances a shortfall; an increase uses foreign exchange. Follow the sign convention explicitly stated in a numerical instead of confusing a positive stock increase with a credit entry.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.3 Official Reserves Account"},{"title":"Autonomous versus accommodating transactions","body":"Autonomous transactions are undertaken for independent economic motives such as profit, rather than to correct a BoP imbalance; they are above-the-line items. Accommodating transactions are undertaken to finance or correct the imbalance and are below the line.\n\nA foreign loan may be autonomous when raised commercially or accommodating when arranged specifically to finance a deficit. Classification depends on purpose, not merely the type of transaction.","formula":"","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"12.3.1 Autonomous and Accommodating Items of BoP Account","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Autonomous transactions","Accommodating transactions"],"rows":[["Motive","Independent economic reasons","Finance a BoP imbalance"],["Also called","Above-the-line items","Below-the-line items"],["Role","Determine the autonomous surplus or deficit","Help meet the resulting financing need"]]}},{"title":"Accounting balance versus operational balance","body":"BoP balances in the accounting sense because every transaction has a counterpart and financing entries close the accounts. This does not mean autonomous receipts equal autonomous payments or that the economy has no external problem.\n\nA current-account deficit financed by foreign borrowing is consistent with accounting balance and increasing indebtedness. For example, a current-account deficit of 100 can be financed by capital inflows of 100; accounting balance coexists with the current-account shortfall.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense"},{"title":"BoP disequilibrium: all economic, political and social causes","body":"Disequilibrium can result from changes in trade or capital flows. A factor can improve or worsen the position depending on the direction of the change.\n\n**Economic 1 · Development Expenditure:** Large development programmes can require substantial imports of equipment and inputs, increasing foreign payments and potentially creating a deficit.\n\n**Economic 2 · Business Cycles:** Depression abroad may reduce demand for exports; expansion abroad can raise it. The effect depends on the countries affected and the response of imports and other flows.\n\n**Economic 3 · Domestic Inflation:** Higher domestic prices can make imports relatively attractive and exports less competitive, worsening the external balance.\n\n**Economic 4 · Import Substitutes:** Domestic alternatives reduce import requirements and can improve the balance, provided their production is viable.\n\n**Economic 5 · Changing Cost Structure:** Technological or managerial improvements alter relative competitiveness. Favourable domestic cost changes can increase exports; improvements by trading partners can have the opposite effect.\n\n**Political 1 · Instability:** Uncertainty can discourage direct and portfolio investment, reducing capital inflows or causing withdrawals.\n\n**Political 2 · Government Policy:** Large cuts in import duties can encourage imports and widen a current-account deficit, other things equal. Other policy changes can alter trade and investment incentives.\n\n**Social 1 · Tastes and Preferences:** Changing consumer preferences can raise exports when foreign demand favours domestic products, or raise imports when residents prefer foreign goods.\n\n**Social 2 · Cross-border Prejudices:** Political or social hostility can divert trade towards costlier import sources or less profitable export markets, weakening the external position.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · Causes of Disequilibrium in BoP","key":"restored-bop-disequilibrium-all-economic-political-and-social-causes","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"Disequilibrium can result from changes in trade or capital flows. A factor can improve or worsen the position depending on the direction of the change.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Economic 1 · Development Expenditure","text":"Large development programmes can require substantial imports of equipment and inputs, increasing foreign payments and potentially creating a deficit.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Economic 2 · Business Cycles","text":"Depression abroad may reduce demand for exports; expansion abroad can raise it. The effect depends on the countries affected and the response of imports and other flows.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Economic 3 · Domestic Inflation","text":"Higher domestic prices can make imports relatively attractive and exports less competitive, worsening the external balance.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Economic 4 · Import Substitutes","text":"Domestic alternatives reduce import requirements and can improve the balance, provided their production is viable.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Economic 5 · Changing Cost Structure","text":"Technological or managerial improvements alter relative competitiveness. Favourable domestic cost changes can increase exports; improvements by trading partners can have the opposite effect.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Political 1 · Instability","text":"Uncertainty can discourage direct and portfolio investment, reducing capital inflows or causing withdrawals.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Political 2 · Government Policy","text":"Large cuts in import duties can encourage imports and widen a current-account deficit, other things equal. Other policy changes can alter trade and investment incentives.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Social 1 · Tastes and Preferences","text":"Changing consumer preferences can raise exports when foreign demand favours domestic products, or raise imports when residents prefer foreign goods.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Social 2 · Cross-border Prejudices","text":"Political or social hostility can divert trade towards costlier import sources or less profitable export markets, weakening the external position.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"Balance of payments: all five significance points","textbookHeading":"12.3.2 Significance of BoP Accounts / Data","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-balance-of-payments-all-five-significance-points","body":"BoP data reveal the economy’s external position and inform decisions. A single flow should be interpreted with its purpose and the wider account.\n\n**1 · Financial Status of the Domestic Economy:** The accounts show external receipts, payments and financing needs. Heavy reliance on borrowing can indicate vulnerability or dependence, though borrowing may also finance productive investment.\n\n**2 · Net Factor Income from Abroad:** Factor-income receipts and payments provide information needed for NFIA, the bridge from domestic income to national income.\n\n**3 · Net Exports as a Component of AD:** Exports minus imports form part of aggregate demand. A rise in X − M increases demand for domestic output and can raise output and employment where spare capacity exists.\n\n**4 · Market Potential:** Foreign investment flows can provide evidence about investors’ expectations of market opportunities and returns. Their size is one indicator, not a complete measure, of market potential.\n\n**5 · Monetary and Fiscal Policies:** External inflows and outflows influence currency demand, reserves and financial conditions, which the RBI considers in monetary policy. Investment flows and the tax environment can also inform fiscal-policy decisions.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"BoP data reveal the economy’s external position and inform decisions. A single flow should be interpreted with its purpose and the wider account.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"1 · Financial Status of the Domestic Economy","text":"The accounts show external receipts, payments and financing needs. Heavy reliance on borrowing can indicate vulnerability or dependence, though borrowing may also finance productive investment.","tone":"point"},{"title":"2 · Net Factor Income from Abroad","text":"Factor-income receipts and payments provide information needed for NFIA, the bridge from domestic income to national income.","tone":"point"},{"title":"3 · Net Exports as a Component of AD","text":"Exports minus imports form part of aggregate demand. A rise in X − M increases demand for domestic output and can raise output and employment where spare capacity exists.","tone":"point"},{"title":"4 · Market Potential","text":"Foreign investment flows can provide evidence about investors’ expectations of market opportunities and returns. Their size is one indicator, not a complete measure, of market potential.","tone":"point"},{"title":"5 · Monetary and Fiscal Policies","text":"External inflows and outflows influence currency demand, reserves and financial conditions, which the RBI considers in monetary policy. Investment flows and the tax environment can also inform fiscal-policy decisions.","tone":"point"}]},{"formula":"","title":"BoP equilibrium, surplus, deficit and reserve movements","textbookHeading":"12.2–12.3 BoP Balances and Official Reserves","kind":"Concept","key":"restored-bop-equilibrium-surplus-deficit-and-reserve-movements","body":"The chapter separates ordinary current/capital flows from the official-reserves account. Accounting balance and equilibrium of autonomous transactions are different concepts.\n\n**Equilibrium:** Ignoring errors and omissions, current-account balance + capital-account balance = zero with no reserve movement. A current-account deficit can be offset by an equal capital-account surplus.\n\n**Surplus:** Receipts on the relevant non-reserve transactions exceed payments. The authority can acquire foreign assets, increasing official reserves.\n\n**Deficit:** Payments exceed receipts. A drawdown of reserves or other accommodating finance covers the shortfall.\n\n**Reserve Entry versus Reserve Stock:** Buying a foreign reserve asset is a use of foreign exchange, while selling a reserve asset provides foreign exchange. An increase in the reserve stock therefore does not mean a credit inflow; follow the sign convention in the question.\n\n**Accounting Always Balances:** Once counterpart entries and financing are included, total accounts balance. This does not imply the country has no deficit, external debt or financing problem. For example, a current deficit of ₹100 with ordinary capital inflow of ₹60 leaves ₹40 to be financed through reserve use or other accommodation.","blocks":[{"title":"Core idea","text":"The chapter separates ordinary current/capital flows from the official-reserves account. Accounting balance and equilibrium of autonomous transactions are different concepts.","tone":"definition"},{"title":"Equilibrium","text":"Ignoring errors and omissions, current-account balance + capital-account balance = zero with no reserve movement. A current-account deficit can be offset by an equal capital-account surplus.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Surplus","text":"Receipts on the relevant non-reserve transactions exceed payments. The authority can acquire foreign assets, increasing official reserves.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Deficit","text":"Payments exceed receipts. A drawdown of reserves or other accommodating finance covers the shortfall.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Reserve Entry versus Reserve Stock","text":"Buying a foreign reserve asset is a use of foreign exchange, while selling a reserve asset provides foreign exchange. An increase in the reserve stock therefore does not mean a credit inflow; follow the sign convention in the question.","tone":"point"},{"title":"Accounting Always Balances","text":"Once counterpart entries and financing are included, total accounts balance. This does not imply the country has no deficit, external debt or financing problem. For example, a current deficit of ₹100 with ordinary capital inflow of ₹60 leaves ₹40 to be financed through reserve use or other accommodation.","tone":"point"}]}],"subject":"macro","number":"12","flow":[["Record transactions","Residents’ dealings with the rest of the world."],["Classify accounts","Current or capital account."],["Identify the motive","Autonomous or accommodating."],["Assess imbalance","Examine autonomous receipts against payments."]]},{"id":"i1","number":"1","subject":"ied","title":"Indian Economy on the Eve of Independence","intro":"","lessons":[{"key":"india-before-british-rule","title":"India before British rule","textbookHeading":"1.1.1 Indian Economy prior to British Rule","kind":"Concept","body":"India had a prosperous handicraft sector and a largely self-contained rural economy. Cultivators produced crops, while village artisans and service providers met many local needs. Agriculture relied on traditional methods, but the textbook distinguishes this economy from the later colonial stagnation.\n\n**Handicrafts and foreign trade:** fine cotton and silk textiles, metal work and precious-stone products enjoyed an international reputation. Finished handicrafts were exported and bullion—gold and silver—flowed into India. Bengal’s muslin, including fine malmal associated with royal use, illustrates the quality of specialised Indian production.\n\nThe important comparison is the change in economic structure: before colonial transformation, India exported valued finished products; under British rule, it increasingly supplied raw materials and imported manufactures.","formula":""},{"key":"the-two-sides-of-colonial-exploitation","title":"The two sides of colonial exploitation","textbookHeading":"1.1.2 Exploitation and Transformation during British Rule","kind":"Concept","body":"Colonial exploitation aimed to maximise Britain’s economic advantage. **Supply aspect:** India supplied cheap raw materials such as cotton and jute for British factories. **Demand aspect:** India became a market for British manufactured goods. Destroying competing Indian handicrafts helped achieve both objectives.\n\nAgriculture was subjected to heavy land-revenue demands. Cultivators could lose rights when unable to meet them, while intermediaries extracted surplus without investing adequately in farming. The transformation was therefore not simply a change in trade: it weakened the productive structure and the cultivator’s ability to improve it.\n\nFor a question on the colonial objective, state both sides—raw-material supply and a market for British goods—then explain how policies in agriculture, industry and trade served that objective.","formula":""},{"key":"seven-overall-features-of-the-colonial-economy","title":"Seven overall features of the colonial economy","textbookHeading":"1.1.3 Features on the Eve of Independence","kind":"Concept","body":"**Stagnant economy:** real output and per-capita income grew very slowly. **Backward economy:** low productivity, poverty, unemployment and inadequate basic necessities kept living standards low. Stagnation concerns the pace of change; backwardness concerns the economy’s low level of development.\n\n**Dominance of agriculture:** about 85% of people depended directly or indirectly on agriculture, while roughly 70–75% of workers were employed in the primary sector. **Bleak industrialisation:** handicrafts declined and modern heavy/capital-goods industries remained inadequate.\n\n**Heavy dependence on imports:** machinery, defence requirements and many consumer goods had to be imported. **Limited urbanisation:** most people lived in villages with few opportunities outside farming. **Semi-feudal economy:** landlords controlled land and appropriated surplus, while cultivators retained little beyond subsistence. These features reinforced one another.","formula":""},{"key":"national-income-estimates-and-the-meaning-of-stagnation","title":"National-income estimates and the meaning of stagnation","textbookHeading":"1.1 Overall Features · Ability Zone","kind":"Box concept","body":"No comprehensive, regular official national-income series was developed under colonial rule. Individual estimates were attempted by Dadabhai Naoroji, William Digby, Findlay Shirras, V.K.R.V. Rao and R.C. Desai. Their estimates differed, but Rao’s work is described as especially systematic.\n\nThe chapter’s Ability Zone cites approximately 2% national-income growth and 0.5% per-capita-income growth during the first half of the twentieth century. Treat these as historical estimates rather than a precise modern series. Per-capita income grows slowly when output barely outpaces population.\n\nThis explains why a modest increase in total output need not produce a meaningful improvement in the average person’s living conditions.","formula":""},{"key":"the-seven-features-of-colonial-agriculture","title":"The seven features of colonial agriculture","textbookHeading":"1.2 Agricultural Sector on the Eve of Independence","kind":"Concept","body":"**Low production and productivity:** total output was small and output per hectare was low. **Exploitative land settlement:** revenue arrangements favoured intermediaries rather than cultivators. **Forced commercialisation:** farmers were pushed towards crops needed by British industry.\n\n**Lack of investment in agricultural infrastructure:** irrigation, improved technology, drainage, terracing and flood protection were neglected. Dependence on rainfall made farming uncertain. **Gulf between owners and tillers:** owners received surplus without sharing cultivation costs, while actual cultivators lacked funds to invest.\n\n**Small and fragmented holdings:** scattered, uneconomic plots raised cultivation costs and made improved methods difficult. **Subsistence outlook:** farming was mainly a way to feed the family, rather than a profit-oriented enterprise. Together these explain why agriculture could employ most workers and still produce widespread poverty.","formula":""},{"key":"how-the-zamindari-system-kept-agriculture-stagnant","title":"How the zamindari system kept agriculture stagnant","textbookHeading":"1.2 Exploitative Land Settlement System","kind":"Concept","body":"Under the Permanent Settlement in the Bengal Presidency, zamindars were recognised as landowners and paid a fixed revenue amount to the government. They could extract rent from cultivators, and their main interest was collection rather than improvement of land.\n\n**Cultivators lacked means:** rent left them with little surplus, pushing them towards debt and subsistence. **Owners lacked incentive:** landlords could receive income without improving cultivation and often spent it on conspicuous consumption. The people doing the work and the people receiving the surplus were different.\n\nThis is the investment trap: cultivators wanted better output but could not finance improvements; owners had resources but little reason to invest. Insecure rights, exploitation and low investment sustained low productivity.","formula":""},{"key":"commercial-crops-famines-and-partition","title":"Commercial crops, famines and Partition","textbookHeading":"1.2 Forced Commercialisation · Highlights","kind":"Box concept","body":"**Commercialisation** means growing for sale rather than self-consumption. Under colonial rule, farmers were forced towards crops such as indigo for British textiles. Market production exposed subsistence farmers to price fluctuations and could reduce the land available for food crops; higher commercial output did not guarantee higher farmer welfare.\n\n**Famines:** drought, poor purchasing power, inadequate public-health services and weak relief made crop failures devastating. The highlights discuss repeated famines, including Bengal in 1769–70 and the famine of 1943. Rail transport later made moving relief food easier, but it did not eliminate colonial famine suffering.\n\n**Partition:** India received about 82% of the population but only about 65% of the foodgrain area in the chapter’s figures. Productive areas of West Punjab and Sindh went to Pakistan, aggravating food shortages. This is a separate shock from the earlier colonial policies.","formula":""},{"key":"five-reasons-indian-handicrafts-declined","title":"Five reasons Indian handicrafts declined","textbookHeading":"1.3.1 Systematic De-industrialisation","kind":"Concept","body":"**Discriminatory tariff policy:** British manufactured goods entered Indian markets on favourable terms, Indian handicraft exports faced heavy duties, and raw-material exports to Britain were encouraged. This weakened Indian goods both at home and abroad.\n\n**Expansion of railways:** cheap British products reached the interior. **Low cost of British goods:** machinery and large-scale production made imports difficult for handmade goods to compete with. **Disappearance of Indian kingdoms:** the decline of royal courts removed important patrons of specialised crafts.\n\n**Loss of skilled artisans and lack of capital:** declining demand and low returns forced artisans out of their trades and reduced investment. De-industrialisation means this decline was not offset by enough modern industrial employment. Consequences included unemployment, loss of skills, pressure on agriculture and a larger market for British manufactures.","formula":""},{"key":"modern-industry-five-observations","title":"Modern industry: five observations","textbookHeading":"1.3.2 Growth of Modern Industry during 1850–1947","kind":"Concept","body":"**Textile mills:** cotton mills developed mainly in western India and were largely Indian-owned; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal and largely foreign-owned. **Iron and steel:** TISCO was established in 1907; other branches developed gradually, including sugar, cement and paper.\n\n**Weak capital-goods industry:** machinery and industrial plants were not produced on the scale needed for broad industrialisation. **Small overall contribution:** modern industry’s contribution to output and employment was too limited to replace the lost handicraft base.\n\n**Limited state participation:** government activity was concentrated in railways, power, communications, ports and selected departmental undertakings. The conclusion is limited and uneven modernisation—not the complete absence of factories.","formula":""},{"key":"three-characteristics-of-colonial-foreign-trade","title":"Three characteristics of colonial foreign trade","textbookHeading":"1.4 Foreign Trade on the Eve of Independence","kind":"Concept","body":"**Exporter of raw materials, importer of finished goods:** exports included raw cotton, silk, wool, jute, indigo and sugar; imports included manufactured consumer goods and machinery. This reversed the earlier reputation for exporting finished handicrafts.\n\n**Monopoly control:** more than half of foreign trade was directed towards Britain; other partners included China, Ceylon and Persia. Britain largely determined the composition and direction of trade.\n\n**Surplus trade benefiting Britain:** an export surplus did not bring equivalent domestic prosperity. It financed administrative expenses, British war expenditure and payments on behalf of the colonial government. Essential supplies could remain scarce in India while exports generated resources used abroad. This is the drain of wealth.","formula":""},{"key":"suez-canal-and-access-to-indian-markets","title":"Suez Canal and access to Indian markets","textbookHeading":"1.4 Highlights · Suez Canal","kind":"Box concept","body":"The Suez Canal opened in 1869 and provided a shorter sea route between Europe and Asia, avoiding the longer journey around Africa. Lower transport costs made trading between Britain and India easier.\n\nIn the colonial setting, this strengthened Britain’s control over Indian trade and made British goods more competitive in Indian markets. The general benefit of a cheaper transport route must therefore be distinguished from who controlled the gains.","formula":""},{"key":"birth-death-infant-mortality-life-expectancy-and-literacy","title":"Birth, death, infant mortality, life expectancy and literacy","textbookHeading":"1.5 Demographic Profile","kind":"Concept","body":"**Birth and death rates:** approximately 48 and 40 per thousand respectively in the chapter’s historical account. Both were high, so the gap—and therefore population growth—was initially small. Poverty, poor sanitation, recurrent disease and inadequate healthcare kept mortality high.\n\n**Infant mortality:** about 218 deaths below age one per 1,000 live births. **Life expectancy:** roughly 32 years. These indicate poor public health and living conditions. Infant mortality uses live births as its denominator, not the total population.\n\n**Literacy:** below 16%, with female literacy around 7%. The very low female rate also reflects gender bias. Explain what the indicators reveal rather than presenting numbers alone; all figures here describe the historical period, not present-day India.","formula":""},{"key":"the-census-1921-and-maintenance-investment","title":"The census, 1921 and maintenance investment","textbookHeading":"1.5 Highlights · Census and Demographic Transition","kind":"Box concept","body":"The chapter identifies 1881 as the beginning of regular decennial population censuses. A census records population size and its demographic characteristics, enabling comparison over time.\n\n**1921—the Year of the Great Divide:** before it, population growth fluctuated, with increases and decreases across censuses. Afterwards, population rose more consistently. Both birth and death rates remained high until around 1951, keeping growth relatively modest; a subsequent decline in mortality with high births widened the gap.\n\n**Maintenance investment** is expenditure needed to maintain the existing population. When population needs absorb a large share of resources, less is available to expand productive capacity. The point is the resource constraint, not that spending on people has no value.","formula":""},{"key":"occupational-structure-and-regional-differences","title":"Occupational structure and regional differences","textbookHeading":"1.6 Occupational Structure","kind":"Concept","body":"Occupational structure is the distribution of workers across primary, secondary and tertiary activities. The chapter uses 1951 figures because reliable 1947 figures are unavailable: agriculture employed about 70–75%, industry about 10% and services about 15–20%.\n\n**Agriculture as the principal occupation:** heavy dependence reduced land available per worker and reinforced subsistence farming. **Industry as an insignificant occupation:** manufacturing could not absorb surplus workers. **Insignificant transition:** the movement from agriculture to industry was too weak to produce an economic take-off.\n\nRegional exceptions existed: parts of the Madras Presidency, Bombay and Bengal showed declining dependence on agriculture; Odisha, Rajasthan and Punjab showed an increase. The overall pattern remained backward and lopsided. Do not confuse workers’ sectoral shares with sectors’ contributions to GDP.","formula":""},{"key":"roads-railways-ports-waterways-and-communications","title":"Roads, railways, ports, waterways and communications","textbookHeading":"1.7 Infrastructure","kind":"Concept","body":"Infrastructure is the support system for growth. Economic infrastructure includes transport, communications, banking and power; social infrastructure includes education, health and housing.\n\n**Railways** moved British goods to Indian interiors and raw materials towards ports; the first passenger train ran from Bombay to Thane in 1853. **Ports** handled this trade. **Inland waterways** received limited development; the expensive Odisha coast canal could not compete with railways and was abandoned.\n\n**Roads** primarily served military movement and transport of materials. All-weather rural access remained inadequate, worsening hardship during floods and famines. **Post and telegraph** strengthened administration and law and order, while postal services remained insufficient. Some infrastructure developed, but its colonial purpose explains its limited contribution to broad welfare.","formula":""},{"key":"positive-side-effects-and-a-balanced-evaluation","title":"Positive side-effects and a balanced evaluation","textbookHeading":"1.7.1 Positive Impact · Highlights on Railways","kind":"Box concept","body":"The textbook lists five positive side-effects: **commercial outlook of farmers**, who became more aware of market prices; **new employment opportunities** in transport and related activity; **better movement of famine relief**; a wider **monetary system of exchange**, supporting specialisation; and an **administrative framework** inherited at independence.\n\nRailways also widened domestic markets, encouraged commercialisation, connected distant regions and promoted cultural interaction. Faster movement of food could reduce the impact of shortages. These are benefits of the means used, even though the underlying motive remained exploitation.\n\nThe negative side was easier export of Indian raw materials and easier distribution of British manufactures. A balanced answer explains both effects and concludes that colonial priorities, rather than Indian development, determined the system.","formula":""}],"flow":[["Colonial objective","Raw materials for Britain; a market for its goods."],["Policy channels","Land revenue, trade rules and infrastructure."],["Economic effects","Low productivity, deindustrialisation and income drain."],["Independence challenge","Rebuild productive capacity and living standards."]]},{"id":"i2","number":"2","subject":"ied","title":"Indian Economy 1950–1990","intro":"","lessons":[{"key":"capitalist-socialist-and-mixed-economies","title":"Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies","textbookHeading":"2.1 Economic Systems","kind":"Concept","body":"**Capitalist economy:** private ownership, market allocation and profit maximisation. Producers respond to consumers’ purchasing power, so consumer choice and incentives can encourage efficiency and growth. The limitation is that needs without purchasing power may go unmet, and income/wealth can become concentrated.\n\n**Socialist economy:** collective/public ownership and allocation by a central authority to maximise social welfare. Production, distribution and prices are directed according to assessed needs. Its strength is the priority given to equity; its limitations include restricted consumer choice and weaker incentives that can reduce efficiency.\n\n**Mixed economy:** public and private ownership coexist. Private firms pursue profit within regulation; public activity pursues social welfare. It seeks growth with social justice, but public-sector inefficiency/corruption and weak coordination can undermine results. India chose this approach rather than an extreme version of either system.","formula":""},{"key":"why-india-adopted-planning-and-a-mixed-economy","title":"Why India adopted planning and a mixed economy","textbookHeading":"2.1 Mixed Economic System · 2.2.1 Planning","kind":"Concept","body":"India inherited low productivity, poverty, weak industry and scarce capital. Market forces alone were unlikely to undertake the large, coordinated investment needed in infrastructure and basic industries. Government therefore supplied a big push while retaining private property and democracy.\n\nEconomic planning means using resources in accordance with national priorities: a central authority specifies targets, policies and programmes for a stated period. A plan explains how limited resources will be used to achieve defined objectives. Goals are essential because resources cannot be allocated purposefully without knowing what is to be achieved.\n\nThe Planning Commission was established in 1950 under Jawaharlal Nehru; the First Five Year Plan began in 1951. A **perspective plan** sets longer-term aims, commonly over 15–25 years, while each Five Year Plan identifies nearer-term priorities. P.C. Mahalanobis, the architect of Indian planning, is especially associated with the Second Plan’s industrial strategy, the Indian Statistical Institute and Sankhya.","formula":""},{"key":"growth-modernisation-self-reliance-self-sufficiency-and-equity","title":"Growth, modernisation, self-reliance, self-sufficiency and equity","textbookHeading":"2.2.2 Common Goals of Five Year Plans","kind":"Concept","body":"**Growth:** increase the capacity to produce goods and services. It can come from a larger resource base, more productive capital and supporting services, or better technology. Sustained growth in real GDP indicates an expanding output flow.\n\n**Modernisation:** adopt new technology and improve social outlook, including gender equality and women’s participation. **Self-reliance:** reduce dependence on foreign financial resources. **Self-sufficiency:** reduce dependence on foreign supplies of essentials. The goal protected economic decisions from political pressure attached to aid or food imports.\n\n**Equity:** spread benefits across society so everyone can meet basic needs—food, clothing, shelter, education and healthcare. A plan may emphasise one goal more than another, but growth without widespread benefits is not enough. The four common headings are growth, modernisation, self-reliance/self-sufficiency and equity.","formula":""},{"key":"growth-versus-development-equal-versus-equitable","title":"Growth versus development; equal versus equitable","textbookHeading":"2.2.2 Highlights · Structural Change and Equity","kind":"Box concept","body":"**Structural composition** means each sector’s share in GDP. **Structural change** means these shares change over time, normally with agriculture’s share declining and industry/services expanding. India experienced a particularly strong movement towards services. A smaller agricultural GDP share does not mean farming should be neglected.\n\n**Growth** is a sustained rise in output. **Development** combines growth with equity and structural improvement. **Equal distribution** would give everyone the same income; **equitable distribution** permits differences related to skills and responsibilities while preventing excessive deprivation and ensuring basic opportunities.\n\n**Inclusive growth** creates opportunities and distributes benefits widely. Modernisation was expected to support jobs through productivity → higher income → greater demand → more employment. Labour-saving technology can weaken that chain, producing jobless growth. Distinguish the intended complementarity from the possible trade-off.","formula":""},{"key":"five-land-reforms-and-their-purpose","title":"Five land reforms and their purpose","textbookHeading":"2.3.1 Recap · 2.3.2 Land Reforms","kind":"Concept","body":"The inherited problems were low productivity, low investment, scattered holdings, subsistence farming and exploitation. **Institutional reforms** changed agrarian ownership and relationships; **technical reforms** changed production methods. The basic institutional objective was equity through “land to the tiller”.\n\n**Abolition of intermediaries:** restore cultivators’ rights, end exploitative rent collection and connect them directly with government. **Ceilings on holdings:** fix a maximum holding and redistribute surplus to small holders/landless workers. **Consolidation:** replace scattered plots with land in one place, reducing cultivation costs.\n\n**Regulation of rent:** prevent excessive/illegal extraction; the chapter describes rent generally being limited to about one-third of crop value. **Cooperative farming:** pool resources to buy inputs cheaply, sell collectively and strengthen bargaining, without confusing cooperation with compulsory state ownership.","formula":""},{"key":"why-land-reforms-achieved-uneven-results","title":"Why land reforms achieved uneven results","textbookHeading":"2.3.2 Hurdles in Implementation","kind":"Concept","body":"**Eviction and evasion:** some landlords evicted tenants or presented themselves as actual cultivators to avoid transferring rights. **Incomplete coverage:** many landless agricultural labourers remained outside the benefits of ownership reforms.\n\n**Court challenges and delay:** large owners challenged ceiling laws; the delay allowed them to transfer holdings to relatives and evade limits. Implementation also depended on political commitment and local administration. West Bengal and Kerala are highlighted as relatively successful examples.\n\nThe intended chain was secure rights → stronger incentive to improve land → higher productivity and equity. Weak implementation interrupted that chain. Therefore distinguish the purpose of the reform from its actual reach.","formula":""},{"key":"the-green-revolution-input-package","title":"The Green Revolution input package","textbookHeading":"2.3.3 Technical Reforms: HYV Technology","kind":"Concept","body":"Green Revolution refers to a sharp increase in crop output associated with HYV technology, especially from the mid-1960s/1967–68. “Green” refers to crops and “revolution” to the substantial jump in production.\n\nIts four textbook elements are **HYV seeds**, **chemical fertilisers**, **insecticides/pesticides for crop protection**, and **expanded irrigation**. High-yield seeds require complementary nutrients and reliable water; crop protection helps preserve the potential gain. Credit and extension services make the package accessible.\n\nThe package, rather than any single input, broke the earlier stagnation. Irrigation projects and plant-protection services supported adoption. A farmer receiving seeds alone may fail to benefit if water, inputs and finance are missing.","formula":""},{"key":"the-two-phases-and-the-role-of-government-support","title":"The two phases and the role of government support","textbookHeading":"2.3.4 Two Distinct Phases of Green Revolution","kind":"Concept","body":"**Mid-1960s to mid-1970s:** gains were concentrated in wheat/rice-growing regions with adequate water. The supplied textbook names Punjab, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu. Adoption was initially easiest for prosperous farmers who could afford expensive complementary inputs.\n\n**Mid-1970s to mid-1980s:** coverage widened across regions and crops. Low-interest loans and subsidised fertilisers/pesticides helped smaller farmers access the package. Government assistance therefore helped spread benefits beyond the initial adopters.\n\nThe second phase widened access but did not eliminate every regional and income inequality. In an answer on phases, include both the time periods and the change in geographical/social coverage—not just “first limited, second widespread”.","formula":""},{"key":"the-five-gains-of-green-revolution","title":"The five gains of Green Revolution","textbookHeading":"2.3.5 Gains of Green Revolution","kind":"Concept","body":"**Spurt in crop productivity:** more output per hectare ended a long period of agricultural stagnation. **Substantial rise in acreage:** shorter crop duration and improved cultivation made multiple cropping more feasible; gross cropped area can rise even without an equal rise in physical land area.\n\n**Shift from subsistence to commercial farming:** output beyond the farmer’s own needs created a marketable surplus. **Change in farmers’ outlook:** farming became a source of profit and reinvestment, rather than only survival.\n\n**Self-sufficiency in foodgrains and buffer stocks:** larger supplies reduced import dependence and enabled reserves for lean seasons/emergencies. More food reaching the market can restrain prices and support consumers. These are distinct gains—do not collapse them into “production increased”.","formula":""},{"key":"marketable-surplus-fallowing-cropping-and-buffer-stocks","title":"Marketable surplus, fallowing, cropping and buffer stocks","textbookHeading":"2.3.5 Highlights · Marketable Surplus and Buffer Stock","kind":"Box concept","body":"**Marketable surplus** is output above the farmer’s on-farm requirements. Selling it provides cash for producer goods and household consumption. It indicates commercialisation, whereas total production includes what the family keeps.\n\n**Fallowing** means leaving land uncultivated for a period. **Double/multiple cropping** uses land for more than one crop during a year; it raises gross cropped area and employment opportunities. Do not confuse physical land area with the sum of area cultivated across seasons.\n\n**Buffer stock** is a reserve held for emergencies and seasonal shortages. The textbook links its introduction to the Fourth Plan. Foodgrain reserves support PDS distribution and respond to crop failure or natural disasters.","formula":"Marketable surplus = Output − On-farm consumption"},{"key":"four-limitations-and-the-equity-problem","title":"Four limitations and the equity problem","textbookHeading":"2.3.6 Limitations of Green Revolution","kind":"Concept","body":"**Limited crops:** the strongest gains were concentrated in wheat and rice, without a comparable breakthrough in pulses and many commercial crops. **Uneven spread:** better-watered and supported states gained more; several eastern/central regions benefited less.\n\n**Limited farming population:** the high input cost excluded many small and marginal farmers. **Economic divide:** prosperous farmers adopted more easily, so differences between rich and poor could widen despite subsidies. These headings concern crop coverage, geography, access and inequality respectively.\n\nExcessive chemical and groundwater use also raises sustainability concerns discussed in rural development. Higher overall food output is a major achievement, but it does not prove that equity was achieved. A balanced appraisal gives the gains and limitations separately.","formula":""},{"key":"arguments-for-and-against-agricultural-subsidies","title":"Arguments for and against agricultural subsidies","textbookHeading":"2.3.7 Agricultural Subsidies: Vice or Virtue?","kind":"Concept","body":"**A vice—huge fiscal burden:** support becomes costly if continued indiscriminately after a technology is established. **Misappropriated benefits:** input suppliers and prosperous farmers may capture benefits intended for poor cultivators. **Wasteful consumption:** artificially cheap water, electricity and chemicals encourage overuse and environmental harm.\n\n**A virtue—risk and poverty:** farmers face uncertain harvests and many cannot afford the new input package. Subsidies encourage adoption and prevent technology being accessible only to rich farmers. Removing support can widen inequality.\n\nThe balanced conclusion is to improve targeting and use, rather than treating every subsidy as either necessary forever or wasteful by definition. Explain the mechanism after each heading: cheap water → little incentive to conserve → excessive extraction.","formula":""},{"key":"why-public-sector-leadership-was-necessary","title":"Why public-sector leadership was necessary","textbookHeading":"2.4.1 Public and Private Sectors","kind":"Concept","body":"**Lack of capital:** the investment needed for basic/heavy industry was beyond the capacity of the few established private industrial houses. **Low inducement to invest:** low purchasing power and small markets reduced expected returns; infrastructure projects also had long gestation periods.\n\n**Growth with social justice:** private profitability alone would not ensure regional balance or prevent concentration of wealth. Public investment could build capacity in neglected areas and supply essential infrastructure.\n\n**Commanding heights** are strategic industries that support the rest of the economy—transport, communications, basic industry and defence. State control was intended to secure this foundation. The public sector was the leading investor, while private firms still participated in the mixed economy.","formula":""},{"key":"ipr-1956-classification-licensing-and-concessions","title":"IPR 1956: classification, licensing and concessions","textbookHeading":"2.4.2 Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956","kind":"Concept","body":"**Three-fold classification:** the first group was exclusively state-owned; the second allowed both sectors with the state taking the leading role; the third was left to regulated private enterprise. This established the state’s leading role and supported the Second Plan’s industrial strategy.\n\n**Industrial licensing:** private firms needed a licence to establish or expand production. The state could direct scarce resources and encourage investment in backward regions. **Industrial concessions:** tax holidays, subsidised power and related incentives made investment in these regions more attractive.\n\nThe intention was coordinated industrialisation and regional equity. The later criticism was that excessive licensing also obstructed entry and competition. Keep the original purpose separate from weaknesses in implementation.","formula":""},{"key":"small-industries-three-advantages-and-protection","title":"Small industries: three advantages and protection","textbookHeading":"2.4.3 Small-Scale Industry","kind":"Concept","body":"The Karve Committee (1955) emphasised small-scale industry’s role in rural development. The chapter historically defines small industry through the investment permitted in plant/machinery; classification thresholds change, so the economic rationale matters more than treating an old limit as permanent.\n\n**Labour-intensive/employment-oriented:** more workers can be employed with limited capital. **Locational flexibility/equality across regions:** small units can operate outside major industrial centres. **Small investment/equity across people:** entry is feasible for smaller entrepreneurs, reducing concentration of wealth.\n\nProtection included reservation of products, lower excise duties and concessional credit. Weak finance, technology and marketing justified support, though prolonged protection could also reduce competitive pressure. Large industry built the basic industrial base; small industry pursued employment and decentralisation.","formula":""},{"key":"import-substitution-versus-export-promotion","title":"Import substitution versus export promotion","textbookHeading":"2.5 Trade Policy: Import Substitution","kind":"Concept","body":"**Import substitution** saves foreign exchange by producing domestically what was previously imported. Scarce foreign exchange could then finance essential developmental imports such as machinery. **Export promotion** earns foreign exchange by making domestic products competitive abroad. They address the same scarcity from different sides.\n\nProtection took two forms. **Tariffs** raise the price of imported goods through taxation. **Quotas** limit the quantity that may enter. Both reduce foreign competition and help domestic industries establish themselves.\n\nThe infant-industry rationale is that new domestic firms need time to develop. The limitation is that protection without pressure to improve can produce high costs, poor quality and weak international competitiveness. Saving foreign exchange is not the same as building export capacity.","formula":""},{"key":"the-seven-features-of-the-1950-1990-strategy","title":"The seven features of the 1950–1990 strategy","textbookHeading":"2.6.1 Characteristic Features of Growth Strategy","kind":"Concept","body":"**Mixed economy and Five Year Plans** formed the overall framework. **Public enterprises** had the central industrial role; **private enterprises** played a regulated, secondary role in strategic industrialisation.\n\n**Import substitution** guided production priorities to achieve self-reliance and conserve foreign exchange. **Protection from foreign competition** operated through duties and import quotas.\n\n**Large-scale industry** built infrastructure and basic/heavy industry. **Small-scale industry** promoted employment, decentralisation and equity. Explain how each instrument related to a planning goal: for example, regional concessions supported equity, while machinery imports supported productive capacity.","formula":""},{"key":"achievements-and-limitations-of-the-planning-strategy","title":"Achievements and limitations of the planning strategy","textbookHeading":"2.6.2 Pros and Cons","kind":"Concept","body":"**Achievements:** agriculture received a breakthrough through land/technical reforms; industrial output expanded; the industrial base diversified beyond cotton and jute into heavy industry and electronics; steel plants and other large projects created infrastructure; small units supported employment and regional spread.\n\n**Excessive public-sector dominance:** government entered activities private firms could handle. **Public monopolies and losses:** weak accountability encouraged inefficiency, leakage and corruption. **Licence raj:** established firms could obtain licences to block competitors rather than expand production.\n\n**Protection without competitiveness:** domestic growth did not always deliver internationally competitive prices/quality. **Inward-looking foreign-exchange policy:** import substitution received more emphasis than earning foreign exchange through exports. Agricultural productivity rose, but adequate non-farm jobs did not emerge to absorb surplus labour. The strategy broke stagnation while leaving important structural problems.","formula":""},{"key":"plan-wise-objectives-and-the-annual-plan-breaks","title":"Plan-wise objectives and the annual-plan breaks","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · Short Period Objectives","kind":"Ability Zone","body":"**First Plan, 1951–56:** agriculture and equitable distribution. **Second, 1956–61:** industrial production and heavy industry. **Third, 1961–66:** foodgrain self-sufficiency, employment and lower inequality. Three Annual Plans followed in 1966–69.\n\n**Fourth, 1969–74:** growth and price stability. **Fifth, 1974–79:** living standards, especially weaker sections. An Annual Plan followed in 1979–80. **Sixth, 1980–85:** poverty removal, reduced inequality and infrastructure. **Seventh, 1985–90:** jobs and agricultural productivity. Two Annual Plans followed in 1990–92.\n\n**Eighth, 1992–97:** fuller manpower use, elementary education and infrastructure. **Ninth, 1997–2002:** agriculture/rural development, stable growth and population control. **Tenth, 2002–07:** quality of life, health, education and consumption. **Eleventh, 2007–12:** inclusive growth, education/health quality, a second agricultural push, quality jobs and environment. **Twelfth, 2012–17:** faster, sustainable and more inclusive growth. These are the extension table’s short-period priorities; the four common goals apply across plans.","formula":""},{"key":"niti-aayog-five-functions","title":"NITI Aayog: five functions","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · NITI Aayog","kind":"Ability Zone","body":"NITI Aayog—the National Institution for Transforming India—was established on 1 January 2015, replacing the Planning Commission. It is the government’s policy think tank, chaired by the Prime Minister.\n\n**Policy formulation:** develop long-term strategies. **Monitoring and evaluation:** assess implementation and offer expert advice. **Cooperative and competitive federalism:** give Centre/states a common platform and encourage improvement through comparative performance.\n\n**Technology upgradation and capacity building:** improve institutional and technical capability. **Promoting equity:** support inclusive growth so benefits reach all sections. Cooperative federalism means working together; competitive federalism means learning from comparison and striving to perform better.","formula":""},{"key":"why-growth-did-not-automatically-solve-poverty","title":"Why growth did not automatically solve poverty","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · Distribution and Interventionist State","kind":"Ability Zone","body":"An **interventionist state** regulates markets and directly supports provision so essential goods reach poorer sections. Markets allocate according to purchasing power, which is different from allocating according to need.\n\nThe textbook’s starvation-with-food-stocks example shows the distribution problem: sufficient food production can coexist with hunger when households lack income or PDS delivery fails. The same distinction applies to income, consumption expenditure, land and industrial wealth: an increase in totals does not establish equitable ownership or access.\n\nLand reforms improved some cultivators’ rights, but shrinking/fragmented holdings constrained efficiency; industrial assets could remain concentrated in large houses. When interpreting poverty or tax data, distinguish a percentage from an absolute number—a falling share can coexist with a rising count if the population grows enough.","formula":""},{"key":"why-higher-investment-need-not-produce-rapid-growth","title":"Why higher investment need not produce rapid growth","textbookHeading":"Ability Zone · Paradox of Saving and Investment","kind":"Ability Zone","body":"The chapter asks why saving and investment could rise while output growth remained slow. **High capital-output ratio:** a larger amount of capital was needed for each extra unit of output. **Inventory rather than fixed-capital formation:** foodgrain stocks are investment in accounting terms but do not create machinery or productive capacity in the same way.\n\n**Low-productivity investment:** resources placed in traditional activities using backward technology generated relatively low returns. The issue is therefore the composition and efficiency of investment, not only its quantity.\n\nThis is the planning chapter’s saving–investment paradox. Do not confuse it with Macro’s paradox of thrift, where a collective attempt to save more can reduce demand and income.","formula":""}],"flow":[["Choose a system","A mixed economy with planned development."],["Set the goals","Growth, modernisation, self-reliance and equity."],["Build capacity","Land reforms, Green Revolution and industry."],["Assess outcomes","Compare achievements with continuing weaknesses."]]},{"id":"i3","number":"3","subject":"ied","title":"Economic Reforms Since 1991","intro":"","lessons":[{"key":"why-reforms-became-necessary-in-1991","title":"Why reforms became necessary in 1991","textbookHeading":"3.1 Background of Economic Reforms","kind":"Concept","body":"The earlier mixed-economy strategy built an industrial base, but restrictions, inefficient public enterprises and weak competition increasingly constrained productivity. Government expenditure exceeded revenue, public borrowing rose, and debt servicing absorbed resources. Inflation raised living costs and production costs.\n\nThe immediate trigger was a **balance-of-payments crisis**: foreign-exchange reserves were insufficient for more than about two weeks of imports, making essential imports and external payments difficult. India approached the World Bank and IMF for assistance of approximately $7 billion. Assistance came with conditions promoting reforms and greater participation in world markets.\n\nThe **New Economic Policy** sought a more competitive economy through liberalisation, privatisation and globalisation. Distinguish the immediate foreign-exchange crisis from the deeper fiscal and structural problems that made the economy vulnerable.","formula":""},{"key":"meaning-and-purpose-of-liberalisation","title":"Meaning and purpose of liberalisation","textbookHeading":"3.2 Liberalisation","kind":"Concept and box","body":"**Liberalisation** means removing unnecessary government controls and restrictions so enterprises have greater freedom to make economic decisions. Earlier controls included industrial licensing, restrictions on expansion, import licensing, foreign-exchange controls and regulated prices or interest rates.\n\nThe expectation was that competition, market signals and freedom to invest would improve efficiency, quality and productivity. Reforms covered industry, finance, taxation, foreign exchange, trade and investment. Greater freedom does not mean that safety standards, banking supervision or every form of government intervention disappears.\n\n**Laissez-faire**, discussed in the box, is the stronger idea of minimal state interference, with the state largely protecting life, property and contracts. Liberalisation in India involves reducing controls within a regulated economy, rather than adopting that extreme position.","formula":""},{"key":"five-changes-in-industrial-regulation","title":"Five changes in industrial regulation","textbookHeading":"3.2.1 Industrial Sector Reforms","kind":"Concept","body":"**Abolition of industrial licensing:** most industries could begin production without obtaining an industrial licence. The textbook lists alcohol, cigarettes, defence equipment, industrial explosives and dangerous chemicals as historical exceptions. These illustrate retained controls in sensitive activities, rather than a permanent list of today’s licensing rules.\n\n**Contraction of public-sector reservation:** more activities were opened to private enterprise; the chapter traces reservation from 17 industries to eight and subsequently three in its historical account. **De-reservation of small-scale industries:** many goods earlier reserved for small producers could be produced by larger firms.\n\n**Freedom to expand capacity:** firms obtained greater freedom to determine their scale of production according to market demand. **Freedom to import capital goods and technology:** easier access to machinery and international technology agreements supported modernisation. Explain the control removed and its intended effect, rather than merely writing “less regulation”.","formula":""},{"key":"from-financial-controller-to-facilitator","title":"From financial controller to facilitator","textbookHeading":"3.2.2 Financial Sector Reforms","kind":"Concept","body":"The financial sector includes banks, stock exchanges and other institutions that mobilise savings and provide funds. Reforms aimed to shift the RBI’s role from detailed control towards facilitating efficient financial activity while retaining prudential supervision and financial stability.\n\n**Greater competition:** private Indian banks and foreign banks were allowed greater participation. **Operational freedom:** banks obtained more scope to decide lending and deposit interest rates and open branches subject to applicable conditions. **Access to funds:** financial institutions could raise funds more freely in domestic and overseas markets.\n\n**Foreign institutional investors:** institutions such as mutual funds, pension funds and merchant bankers gained access to Indian financial markets. Greater foreign participation expanded the potential supply of finance. The chapter’s particular ownership ceilings describe its policy period; the lasting concept is reduced entry restrictions combined with continued regulation.","formula":""},{"key":"demonetisation-intended-gains-and-immediate-costs","title":"Demonetisation: intended gains and immediate costs","textbookHeading":"3.2.2 Financial Reforms · Demonetisation","kind":"Important box","body":"On **8 November 2016**, the existing ₹500 and ₹1,000 notes ceased to be legal tender under the announced demonetisation programme. People were given a specified period to deposit or exchange eligible notes, and new notes were introduced. Demonetisation changes the legal-tender status of currency; it is different from inflation reducing currency’s purchasing power.\n\n**Intended gains:** expose unaccounted money and reduce the shadow economy; widen the tax base and tax revenue; increase bank deposits and funds available for lending; disrupt counterfeit currency and terror financing; encourage digital payments; and promote formal banking and financial inclusion. These are policy objectives and possible channels, not proof that every objective was fully achieved.\n\n**Immediate costs:** cash shortages disrupted everyday transactions; production and trade suffered, especially in the cash-dependent informal sector; workers lost employment or earnings; long queues imposed time and inconvenience; and weaker consumption and investment slowed activity. An appraisal should explain both the intended mechanism and these adjustment costs.","formula":""},{"key":"direct-taxes-indirect-taxes-and-tax-reform","title":"Direct taxes, indirect taxes and tax reform","textbookHeading":"3.2.3 Tax Reforms","kind":"Concept","body":"**Fiscal policy** concerns government revenue and expenditure. A **direct tax** is paid by the person on whom its legal burden falls, as with income tax; its burden is not normally shifted to another person. An **indirect tax**, such as GST or customs duty, can be passed on through prices.\n\nTax reforms sought to rationalise rates, simplify procedures and improve compliance. Very high rates and complicated rules can encourage evasion and discourage enterprise. Lower or more reasonable rates, fewer complications and a wider tax base were expected to encourage voluntary payment and improve revenue collection.\n\nThe objective is not simply “cut every tax”. A sound explanation connects reasonable rates and simpler administration to incentives, compliance, coverage and the government’s ability to finance development.","formula":""},{"key":"gst-value-addition-advantages-and-limitations","title":"GST: value addition, advantages and limitations","textbookHeading":"3.2.3 Tax Reforms · GST","kind":"Important box","body":"**Goods and Services Tax**, introduced on **1 July 2017**, brought many indirect taxes into a common framework. **Supply-based tax:** liability is linked to the supply of goods and services. **Value addition:** tax is intended to apply to the value added at successive stages. **Input-tax credit:** eligible tax already paid on inputs can be credited against output-tax liability, reducing tax-on-tax or cascading.\n\n**Advantages:** a simpler and more uniform indirect-tax system; a more integrated national market; greater transparency through recorded transactions; and potential improvement in compliance and revenue, with less scope for unrecorded business. A common framework does not mean one identical rate for every product.\n\n**Limitations:** some products remain outside GST’s ordinary coverage, including alcoholic liquor for human consumption and specified petroleum products; electricity is also outside GST. Firms can face compliance burdens and uncertainty when procedures or rates change. Learn the mechanism and these strengths and constraints rather than treating a changing rate table as the definition.","formula":""},{"key":"devaluation-and-market-based-exchange-rates","title":"Devaluation and market-based exchange rates","textbookHeading":"3.2.4 Foreign Exchange Reforms","kind":"Concept","body":"In 1991, the rupee was devalued to address the external-payments crisis. A lower external value makes Indian exports cheaper to foreign buyers and imports more expensive in rupees, potentially improving export competitiveness and restraining imports. The textbook describes the two-step adjustment in July 1991.\n\nExchange-rate reforms subsequently gave market demand and supply a greater role in determining the rupee’s value. This facilitated international transactions and linked India more closely with world markets. **Devaluation** is an official reduction under a fixed or administered arrangement; **depreciation** is a fall associated with market forces.\n\nThe effect on the trade balance is not automatic: buyers must respond to price changes, and essential imports may remain necessary. The intended channel is the change in the relative prices of exports and imports.","formula":""},{"key":"opening-trade-and-investment","title":"Opening trade and investment","textbookHeading":"3.2.5 Trade and Investment Policy Reforms","kind":"Concept","body":"**Removal of quantitative restrictions:** limits on the amount of many imports were reduced or removed. **Reduction of tariffs:** lower import duties reduced protection and exposed domestic producers to competition. **Relaxation of import licensing:** most imports no longer required the earlier licence system, with sensitive health, safety and environmental restrictions retained.\n\n**Removal or reduction of export duties and restrictions:** these measures aimed to encourage exports and improve international competitiveness. Easier foreign investment and access to imported inputs, machinery and technology were intended to improve production quality and efficiency.\n\nTariffs change the price of imports; quotas restrict their quantity. For an exam answer, keep that distinction explicit and explain why trade reform can benefit consumers while placing adjustment pressure on protected domestic firms.","formula":""},{"key":"privatisation-disinvestment-and-the-public-sector-journey","title":"Privatisation, disinvestment and the public-sector journey","textbookHeading":"3.3 Privatisation","kind":"Concept and box","body":"**Privatisation** means reducing or withdrawing government ownership or management of an enterprise. It may involve transfer of management, sale of part of the government’s equity, or complete sale to private owners. **Disinvestment** means selling government shareholdings: a minority share sale need not transfer management or make an enterprise fully private.\n\nPublic enterprises originally helped diversify production, build basic industries and infrastructure, and address activities private capital could not adequately undertake. Over time, some faced inefficiency, losses, excessive interference, leakages, pilferage and corruption. Reforms aimed to reduce this burden and improve accountability and performance.\n\n**Navratna, Maharatna and Miniratna** status gives selected public enterprises greater managerial and financial autonomy. Autonomy can improve investment decisions and competitiveness while government ownership continues. These categories are therefore not themselves privatisation, and the status of individual firms can change.","formula":""},{"key":"benefits-and-social-costs-of-privatisation","title":"Benefits and social costs of privatisation","textbookHeading":"3.3 Privatisation · Appraisal","kind":"Concept and case","body":"**Efficiency:** owners with a direct financial stake have an incentive to control costs and improve output. **Competition and modernisation:** pressure to survive can encourage better technology and management. **Diversification and reinvestment:** commercially viable firms can develop new products and reinvest profits. **Consumer choice:** competing firms may offer better quality and a wider range of goods.\n\n**Social-interest concern:** commercial profitability may receive priority over affordable essential services or employment. **Weak purchasing power:** poorer groups may be unable to buy services even when the market supplies them efficiently. Regulation and public support remain important where social benefits exceed private returns.\n\nThe **Sircilla powerloom case** connects higher electricity charges and production costs to reduced output, lower wages and distress among weavers. Use the chain “input cost → viability → employment and earnings” to explain why a reform’s distributional effects matter, rather than assuming either all private enterprise or all public enterprise is inherently efficient.","formula":""},{"key":"globalisation-and-its-five-policy-supports","title":"Globalisation and its five policy supports","textbookHeading":"3.4 Globalisation","kind":"Concept","body":"**Globalisation** is the increasing integration and interdependence of economies through flows of goods, services, capital, technology and expertise. Liberalisation and privatisation created conditions for greater integration; globalisation describes the resulting connections with the world economy.\n\n**Encouragement of foreign investment:** relaxation of equity restrictions and a more facilitative foreign-exchange framework, including FEMA. **Rupee convertibility for current transactions:** easier conversion for trade in goods and services, interest, dividends and remittances; this is distinct from unrestricted capital-account convertibility.\n\n**Long-term trade policy:** a more predictable outward-looking framework. **Reduction in tariffs:** lower import duties. **Reduction in quantitative restrictions:** removal of many import quantity controls, with the chapter highlighting 2001 in the historical reform sequence. These measures increased competition and international access; they did not abolish every restriction.","formula":""},{"key":"outsourcing-why-india-became-a-service-destination","title":"Outsourcing: why India became a service destination","textbookHeading":"3.4 Globalisation · Outsourcing","kind":"Important box","body":"**Outsourcing** occurs when an organisation contracts work to an outside provider instead of performing it internally. International outsourcing places that work in another country. Examples include call centres, record keeping, accountancy, banking support, music and film editing, medical transcription, clinical services and online teaching.\n\nImproved information and communication technology allows work and results to move rapidly across borders. India’s relatively low labour costs and large pool of skilled, English-speaking workers made it attractive for many service activities. Firms seek cost savings and expertise; Indian workers and firms can gain income, employment and export opportunities.\n\nThe benefit depends on skills and access to infrastructure, so it may be concentrated in particular workers and cities. Outsourcing is one channel of globalisation, not a synonym for every foreign investment or every export.","formula":""},{"key":"wto-role-expected-benefits-and-unequal-access","title":"WTO: role, expected benefits and unequal access","textbookHeading":"3.4 Globalisation · WTO","kind":"Concept","body":"The **World Trade Organization** began on **1 January 1995**, succeeding the GATT framework established in 1948. The Marrakesh agreement associated with its creation involved 123 participating countries. Its purpose is a rules-based international trading system.\n\n**Five roles:** promote freer trade; discourage arbitrary restrictions through common rules; improve competition and market access; provide a framework for trade agreements and their implementation; and expand markets so resources can be used more efficiently. A **bilateral** agreement involves two countries; a **multilateral** agreement involves several.\n\n**India’s expected gains:** more export opportunities; textile employment and exports following removal of Multi-Fibre Arrangement quotas; and agricultural opportunities if developed countries reduced subsidies and barriers. **Concern:** implementation can be unequal—richer countries may retain support or barriers, and capital often moves more freely than labour. Expected gains should be distinguished from guaranteed gains.","formula":""},{"key":"first-generation-and-second-generation-reforms","title":"First-generation and second-generation reforms","textbookHeading":"3.4 Ability Zone · Generations of Reform","kind":"Ability Zone","body":"**First-generation reforms** mainly changed administrative and executive controls, such as licensing, trade restrictions and investment approvals. These could often be implemented through government policy decisions.\n\n**Second-generation reforms** seek deeper institutional changes, including legal frameworks and areas such as labour, governance and public services. They may require legislation, coordination and broader political agreement, making implementation more difficult or slower.\n\nThe distinction explains why announcing market-opening measures is easier than ensuring effective institutions, skills, infrastructure and social protection. Those complementary conditions affect whether the initial reforms deliver broad benefits.","formula":""},{"key":"two-different-jobs-of-the-reform-programme","title":"Two different jobs of the reform programme","textbookHeading":"3.5 Appraisal · Stabilisation and Structural Adjustment","kind":"Concept","body":"**Stabilisation measures** address immediate macroeconomic imbalances: the balance-of-payments crisis, inflation and unsustainable fiscal deficits. They aim to restore the economy’s ability to make payments and maintain stability in the short run.\n\n**Structural adjustment measures** change how the economy operates over the longer run: improving efficiency, increasing competition, reducing restrictive controls and encouraging investment and modernisation. Industrial, financial and trade reforms belong to this broader restructuring.\n\nThe two are related but different. Securing foreign exchange can ease an immediate crisis without by itself making firms efficient; improving competition can raise long-term productivity while imposing short-run adjustment costs.","formula":""},{"key":"nine-favourable-outcomes-growth-stability-and-choice","title":"Nine favourable outcomes: growth, stability and choice","textbookHeading":"3.5.1 Positive Impact of LPG Reforms","kind":"Concept","body":"**Vibrant economy:** greater openness and competition encouraged economic activity. **Stimulant to industrial production:** access to capital, technology and markets improved opportunities for expansion and modernisation. **Check on fiscal deficit:** fiscal discipline and reform aimed to reduce the government’s imbalance. **Larger foreign-exchange reserves:** improved external inflows strengthened the payments position.\n\n**Check on inflation:** stabilisation and improved supplies helped contain price pressures in parts of the reform period. **Consumer sovereignty:** wider choice and competition increased the importance of consumer preferences. **Private and foreign investment:** a more liberal framework encouraged new capital and enterprise.\n\n**Emerging economic power:** faster growth and international integration increased India’s economic significance. **From monopoly to competition:** reduced protection and entry barriers challenged established producers. These are the chapter’s favourable appraisal headings; performance varied by sector and year, so a rise in investment or reserves alone does not prove that every social objective was met.","formula":""},{"key":"fdi-versus-foreign-institutional-investment","title":"FDI versus foreign institutional investment","textbookHeading":"3.5 Appraisal · FDI and FII","kind":"Important box","body":"**Foreign direct investment (FDI)** involves a lasting business interest and a significant role in ownership or management—for example, establishing a factory or acquiring a controlling business stake. It can bring technology, management and production capacity as well as finance.\n\n**Foreign institutional investment (FII)** generally involves institutional investment in financial securities such as shares and bonds without taking managerial control of the enterprise. It supplies portfolio finance and can be more readily withdrawn as investors change their preferences.\n\nBoth are foreign investment, but their relationship with production, control and financial-market volatility differs. Do not identify every purchase of an Indian share by a foreign institution as the establishment of a foreign-controlled business.","formula":""},{"key":"why-agriculture-and-industry-did-not-benefit-equally","title":"Why agriculture and industry did not benefit equally","textbookHeading":"3.5.2 Negative Impact · Agriculture and Industry","kind":"Concept","body":"**Neglect of agriculture:** lower public investment in irrigation, power, roads and research constrained productivity; reduced subsidies could raise input costs; lower import duties exposed farmers to foreign competition; and a shift towards export-oriented cash crops could create risks for food production. Agricultural weakness also hurts industry by reducing raw-material supply and rural demand.\n\n**Industrial slowdown and uneven competition:** cheaper imports could displace domestic production, while smaller firms struggled to upgrade technology. Indian exporters could still face barriers in richer countries, creating an uneven playing field. Infrastructure and finance constraints limited the ability to respond to competition.\n\n**Unemployment or jobless growth:** capital-intensive technology can expand output without proportionate employment. Workers displaced from protected or public enterprises may lack the skills required in expanding sectors. Explain why higher GDP does not automatically mean enough suitable jobs.","formula":""},{"key":"the-remaining-six-criticisms-of-reforms","title":"The remaining six criticisms of reforms","textbookHeading":"3.5.2 Negative Impact · Fiscal and Distributional Concerns","kind":"Appraisal and box","body":"**Disinvestment:** selling valuable public assets cheaply or using proceeds mainly for routine expenditure can weaken long-term public wealth. **Fiscal policy:** lower customs duties and tax concessions can constrain revenue and funds for social expenditure if wider activity and compliance do not compensate.\n\n**Lopsided growth:** rapid expansion of services, particularly IT, can coexist with weak agriculture and manufacturing. **Urban concentration:** firms prefer cities with better infrastructure and skilled labour, widening regional and rural–urban gaps. **Economic colonialism:** excessive dependence on foreign firms, technology or markets can reduce domestic economic autonomy.\n\n**Consumerism:** advertising and demonstration effects may encourage consumption beyond means and weaken saving priorities. The related **dualism** box describes the coexistence of modern, high-income activities and traditional, low-productivity ones. A balanced policy encourages useful investment and competition while supporting infrastructure, domestic capabilities, weaker sections and strategic needs.","formula":""}],"flow":[["1991 crisis","External payments and fiscal pressures."],["Stabilise","Address immediate imbalances."],["Restructure","Liberalisation, privatisation and globalisation."],["Appraise","Efficiency and choice alongside jobs and distribution."]]},{"id":"i4","number":"4","subject":"ied","title":"Human Capital Formation in India","intro":"","lessons":[{"key":"human-resources-human-capital-and-formation","title":"Human resources, human capital and formation","textbookHeading":"4.1 Human Capital and Human Capital Formation","kind":"Concept","body":"People become an economic resource through their capacity to work, learn and organise production. **Human capital** is the stock of knowledge, skills, abilities and health embodied in people that increases their productive capacity. **Human capital formation** is the process of adding to this stock through education, training, health and related investments.\n\nThe stock–flow distinction matters: a person’s existing skills are human capital; acquiring additional skills during a period is human capital formation. Expenditure is an investment when it develops capabilities that generate benefits over time, rather than merely meeting a current need.\n\nHuman capital formation can raise earnings and national output, but its benefits also include improved judgement, adaptability and participation. The textbook’s definition associated with Meier emphasises developing people’s skills and capabilities for productive activity.","formula":""},{"key":"productive-capacity-versus-human-well-being","title":"Productive capacity versus human well-being","textbookHeading":"4.1 Human Capital and Human Development","kind":"Concept","body":"**Human capital** treats education and health as means of raising productivity and income. **Human development** treats people’s well-being, capabilities and freedom as valuable ends in themselves. Education and good health therefore matter even when their immediate contribution to market output is small.\n\nAn educated, healthy worker may produce more; that is the human-capital argument. A person also deserves opportunities to learn and live a healthy life; that is the human-development argument. The approaches overlap, but the second has a broader purpose.\n\nFor a distinction question, compare the objective, the role of education and health, and the criterion of success: higher productive capacity and earnings versus a better and more capable human life.","formula":"","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Human capital","Human development"],"rows":[["Purpose","Increase productive capacity and earnings","Improve well-being, capabilities and freedom"],["Education and health","Means to higher productivity","Valuable ends as well as means"],["Common ground","Investment in people","Investment in people"]]}},{"key":"seven-bases-of-distinction","title":"Seven bases of distinction","textbookHeading":"4.1 Physical Capital versus Human Capital","kind":"Concept","body":"**Formation:** physical capital is created using material inputs; human capital develops through education, health and training. **Decision making:** entrepreneurs usually make conscious investment decisions about machinery, whereas human-capital decisions may initially be made by parents, institutions or government on a person’s behalf.\n\n**Tangibility:** machines and buildings are tangible; skills and knowledge are embodied capabilities. **Separability:** a machine can be sold separately from its owner, but a person’s skills cannot be separated from that person. **Mobility:** both can move, but human movement is also constrained by nationality, culture, family and immigration rules.\n\n**Depreciation:** physical capital wears out; human capabilities can deteriorate through ageing, poor health or obsolete knowledge, making retraining important. **Benefits:** physical investment mainly generates private returns, while education and health also produce substantial social benefits. Use these as comparison bases, not as a claim that physical investment never benefits society.","formula":""},{"key":"education-and-health-as-investments","title":"Education and health as investments","textbookHeading":"4.2 Sources of Human Capital Formation","kind":"Concept","body":"**Expenditure on education** develops knowledge and skills, improves employability and productivity, and can generate higher lifetime earnings. Its costs include fees and materials as well as the earnings forgone while studying. Benefits arrive over time, so education is treated as an investment in people.\n\n**Expenditure on health** improves the ability to work regularly and effectively. The textbook includes **preventive medicine** such as vaccination, **curative medicine** for treatment, **social medicine** through health awareness, and **sanitation** through safe water, hygiene and waste disposal.\n\nHealthy people lose fewer working days, learn more effectively and can use their skills better. Education and health reinforce each other: knowledge improves health choices, while health improves learning and attendance.","formula":""},{"key":"learning-beyond-school","title":"Learning beyond school","textbookHeading":"4.2 Sources · On-the-job Training and Adult Education","kind":"Concept","body":"**On-the-job training** builds skills while a person is employed. It may take place inside the firm under experienced workers or through an external training institution. Firms incur training costs because better skills can improve quality, efficiency and output; arrangements encouraging workers to remain help firms recover that investment.\n\n**Adult education** gives people opportunities to gain literacy, knowledge and practical capabilities after the usual school age. Government agencies and voluntary organisations can provide such programmes, enabling people excluded from initial schooling to participate more effectively in work and society.\n\nTraining is especially important when technology changes. It maintains the value of existing human capital and addresses the difference between possessing a qualification and possessing the particular skills a job requires.","formula":""},{"key":"why-moving-and-searching-can-build-productive-capacity","title":"Why moving and searching can build productive capacity","textbookHeading":"4.2 Sources · Migration and Information","kind":"Concept","body":"**Migration** may be internal or international. People move to obtain better employment and earnings or to use their skills more effectively. Costs include travel, higher living expenses and the psychological cost of separation from family and familiar surroundings; the decision compares these with expected future benefits.\n\n**Information** about educational institutions, training, vacancies, wages and working conditions helps people choose where to invest in skills and where to work. Collecting it takes time and money, but can reduce mismatch and improve returns to human capabilities.\n\nThese sources do not necessarily create a new skill immediately. They improve the acquisition, allocation or use of human capital—an important reason the textbook includes them alongside education, health and training.","formula":""},{"key":"three-ways-human-capital-supports-development","title":"Three ways human capital supports development","textbookHeading":"4.3 Human Capital Formation and Economic Growth","kind":"Concept","body":"**Change in emotional and material environment:** education broadens aspirations, reasoning and attitudes towards change, while a skilled workforce improves the material conditions of production. People become better able to adopt new methods and pursue improved living standards.\n\n**Higher productivity of physical capital:** trained and healthy workers use equipment efficiently, reduce waste and develop innovations and inventions. The same machinery can yield different output depending on the capabilities of those using it.\n\n**Greater participation and equality:** wider access to capabilities enables more people, including disadvantaged groups and women, to contribute to development and share its benefits. The Seventh Five-Year Plan recognised human resources as central to development. The relationship is two-way: human capital promotes growth, and higher income finances further education and health.","formula":""},{"key":"why-years-of-schooling-do-not-tell-the-whole-story","title":"Why years of schooling do not tell the whole story","textbookHeading":"4.3 Ability Zone · Measuring Human Capital","kind":"Ability Zone","body":"Indicators such as enrolment, years of schooling and teacher–pupil ratios measure access or inputs, but do not completely capture learning quality. Similarly, health expenditure does not automatically measure how healthy people are or how effectively healthcare is delivered.\n\nCountries with similar measured education or health can have different growth rates because infrastructure, institutions, technology and employment opportunities also matter. This does not show that human capital is unimportant; it shows that measurement is incomplete and growth has several causes.\n\nUse the full chain: investment must improve actual capabilities, those capabilities must be used productively, and complementary conditions must support their use.","formula":""},{"key":"five-obstacles-in-india","title":"Five obstacles in India","textbookHeading":"4.4 Problems of Human Capital Formation","kind":"Concept","body":"**Rising population:** resources must serve more people, limiting improvements per person when investment does not keep pace. **Brain drain:** skilled people trained using domestic resources may migrate abroad, reducing the immediate domestic return on that investment.\n\n**Deficient manpower planning:** education and training may not match the number and type of jobs available, producing skill shortages alongside educated unemployment. **Inadequate on-the-job training:** especially in traditional and small activities, workers may have few opportunities to update practical skills.\n\n**Low academic standards:** weak teaching, facilities or assessment can produce qualifications without adequate learning. The answer is therefore not merely “spend more”: expand access, improve quality, align training with opportunities and make effective use of existing skills.","formula":""},{"key":"literacy-education-and-seven-benefits","title":"Literacy, education and seven benefits","textbookHeading":"4.5 Education · Meaning and Importance","kind":"Concept","body":"**Literacy** is the ability to read and write with understanding; **education** is the broader development of knowledge, reasoning, skills and personality. A literate person is not necessarily well educated. Education is commonly organised into primary, secondary and tertiary levels.\n\n**Responsible citizens:** informed people make better social and civic choices. **Science and technology:** education supports invention and adoption of improved methods. **Efficient use of resources:** knowledge reduces waste and improves decisions. **Wider mental horizon:** it develops reasoning and openness to new ideas.\n\n**Participation in growth:** skills allow people to enter productive activities. **Higher cultural standards:** education develops understanding of social and cultural life. **Human personality and empowerment:** it builds confidence, independence and agency, particularly for women and disadvantaged groups. These social gains extend beyond the student’s own earnings.","formula":""},{"key":"four-reasons-for-public-involvement","title":"Four reasons for public involvement","textbookHeading":"4.5 Government Intervention in Education and Health","kind":"Concept","body":"**Large investment requirements:** institutions, teachers and facilities need substantial resources and long-term commitment. **Affordability:** private providers charging to recover costs can exclude poorer families, even when educating them would benefit society.\n\n**Standards and information:** students and patients cannot always judge quality in advance; regulation and reliable standards help protect them. **Rights and social responsibility:** access to basic education and healthcare should not depend entirely on purchasing power.\n\nGovernment therefore finances provision, operates institutions, regulates quality and supports access. NCERT supports school curriculum and educational materials, UGC has roles in higher education, and AICTE concerns technical education. ICMR supports medical research. Different bodies serve different functions; do not treat them as interchangeable.","formula":""},{"key":"how-to-interpret-expenditure-and-the-6-recommendation","title":"How to interpret expenditure and the 6% recommendation","textbookHeading":"4.5 Public Expenditure and Educational Policy","kind":"Concept and policy box","body":"Educational expenditure can be expressed as a **share of total government expenditure**, showing its budget priority, or as a **share of GDP**, showing public resources committed relative to the economy’s size. These denominators answer different questions.\n\nThe textbook reports a rise from 7.92% to 16.54% of government expenditure and from 0.64% to 4.47% of GDP between 1952 and 2020. These are historical public-expenditure figures, not today’s rates and not total public-plus-private spending. The Education Commission of 1964–66 recommended expenditure of around **6% of GDP**.\n\nThe Tapas Majumdar Committee in 1999 estimated substantial additional resources—about ₹1.37 lakh crore over its proposed programme—for universal elementary education. The **Right to Education Act, 2009** provides for free and compulsory education for children aged **6–14**. The chapter also discusses education cess as a financing measure; distinguish historical financing arrangements from the enduring right and spending objective.","formula":""},{"key":"elementary-higher-and-state-level-spending","title":"Elementary, higher and state-level spending","textbookHeading":"4.5 Educational Expenditure · Distribution","kind":"Concept","body":"Elementary education receives a large absolute share because it serves many children, while expenditure **per student** is generally higher in tertiary education. A larger total allocation and a larger per-student allocation are not the same thing.\n\nThe lesson is to expand resources across levels, rather than finance basic education only by weakening higher education. Higher education develops teachers, researchers, professionals and technical capabilities needed throughout the system. States differ greatly in resources, enrolment needs and spending per person; state governments finance much of public education.\n\nWhen comparing states, specify the year, the denominator and what is included. Higher spending alone does not establish better outcomes unless facilities, teaching quality, attendance and learning also improve.","formula":""},{"key":"progress-knowledge-economy-and-nep-2020","title":"Progress, knowledge economy and NEP 2020","textbookHeading":"4.5 Achievements and Knowledge Economy","kind":"Concept and box","body":"India has expanded educational institutions, enrolment and literacy since independence. The textbook compares literacy of **18.33% in 1951** with **74.04% in 2011**. These census figures demonstrate long-run progress, while also showing that universal literacy had not yet been achieved.\n\nA **knowledge economy** relies heavily on the creation, communication and productive use of knowledge. IT, digital networks and access to information can improve productivity, but require both access and quality skills. A large number of graduates is not sufficient if their learning does not match changing work.\n\nThe **National Education Policy 2020** emphasises broader and multidisciplinary learning. Areas such as artificial intelligence and big data demand combinations of mathematical, scientific, technical and communication skills. The challenge is to connect expanded educational access with strong learning and adaptability.","formula":""},{"key":"six-continuing-gaps-and-the-poverty-cycle","title":"Six continuing gaps and the poverty cycle","textbookHeading":"4.5 Problems and Challenges in Education","kind":"Concept","body":"**Large number of illiterates:** progress in the literacy rate still leaves many people without basic capabilities. **Inadequate vocational orientation:** degree-focused education and preference for white-collar work can coexist with shortages of practical skills. **Gender bias:** girls may face early withdrawal, household responsibilities and unequal opportunities.\n\n**Low rural access:** distance, inadequate facilities and teacher shortages restrict learning. **Expensive private education:** unequal purchasing power creates unequal educational opportunities. **Inadequate public expenditure:** weak funding limits institutions, staff and quality.\n\nThe education pyramid narrows sharply towards higher levels. Poverty can force children out of school, while poor education limits earnings and reproduces poverty. Formal entitlement under RTE is not enough if documentation barriers, indirect costs, poor facilities and weak teaching prevent meaningful participation. The remedy combines access, retention, quality and practical relevance.","formula":""}],"flow":[["Invest in people","Education, health, training and access."],["Build capabilities","Knowledge, skills, health and adaptability."],["Use them productively","Higher efficiency, participation and innovation."],["Reinvest gains","Higher income can fund further human development."]]},{"id":"i5","number":"5","subject":"ied","title":"Rural Development","intro":"","lessons":[{"key":"meaning-importance-and-the-rural-development-agenda","title":"Meaning, importance and the rural development agenda","textbookHeading":"5.1 Rural Development","kind":"Concept","body":"**Rural development** is a comprehensive action plan to improve rural economic and social life. It integrates productive employment, income, education, health, infrastructure and environmental sustainability. Agriculture is central, but rural development is broader than increasing crop output.\n\nA large population depends on rural activities, and poverty and low productivity remain concentrated there. Slower agricultural growth and limited non-farm opportunities can widen rural–urban differences even when national GDP rises. Development must improve crop productivity and profitability while creating opportunities outside cultivation.\n\n**Lingering challenges:** adequate credit and effective agricultural marketing. **Emerging challenges:** diversification of productive activities and sustainable practices such as organic farming. Roads, power, irrigation, research, education, healthcare and skills support all these efforts.","formula":""},{"key":"why-farmers-borrow-and-the-three-credit-periods","title":"Why farmers borrow and the three credit periods","textbookHeading":"5.2 Rural Credit · Need and Types","kind":"Concept","body":"Farmers incur costs before receiving harvest income. Small holdings and low retained surplus make it difficult to finance seeds, fertilisers, electricity and labour out of savings. Credit bridges the gap between sowing and sale and finances lasting improvements.\n\n**Short-term credit, roughly 6–12 months:** seasonal inputs and recurring expenses. **Medium-term credit, about 1–5 years:** machinery, fencing and improvement of wells. **Long-term credit, about 5–20 years:** land purchase and permanent improvements. The purpose and repayment period should match the investment’s benefits.\n\n**Productive credit** supports income-generating activity; **consumption or other non-productive credit** meets household and social expenditure. Borrowing for essential consumption may still be necessary, but repayment is difficult when it does not create additional income.","formula":"Credit need = expenditure before harvest + investment needs − available savings"},{"key":"from-moneylenders-to-social-banking","title":"From moneylenders to social banking","textbookHeading":"5.2 Evolution of Rural Credit","kind":"Concept","body":"Moneylenders, traders and commission agents traditionally dominated rural credit. High interest, manipulation of accounts, tied sales and weak bargaining power could leave farmers in a persistent debt trap. Their accessibility did not ensure fair terms.\n\n**Bank nationalisation in 1969** promoted social banking and wider rural access. The Green Revolution increased the need for credit because purchased inputs and commercial production required more working capital. The aim was to provide timely, affordable institutional finance instead of dependence on exploitative arrangements.\n\nThe **multi-agency approach** uses government, cooperative institutions, commercial banks, regional rural banks and land-development banks, supported by NABARD and the RBI. These institutions have complementary roles rather than identical functions.","formula":""},{"key":"cooperatives-commercial-banks-rrbs-and-land-development-banks","title":"Cooperatives, commercial banks, RRBs and land-development banks","textbookHeading":"5.2 Institutional Sources of Rural Credit","kind":"Concept","body":"**Cooperative credit societies** pool resources and provide accessible, timely credit. Their objectives include reducing moneylender dependence, spreading finance across regions and supporting weaker or neglected areas; some also provide agricultural advice and related assistance.\n\n**Commercial banks**, including SBI established in 1955 and banks brought under public ownership in 1969, finance agriculture directly and through cooperative arrangements. **Regional rural banks** focus on rural and relatively backward areas, particularly smaller farmers and weaker sections.\n\n**Land-development banks** mainly provide longer-term finance for land and permanent agricultural improvements. Availability alone is insufficient: loan timing, terms, accessibility and suitability for small borrowers determine whether credit helps production.","formula":""},{"key":"apex-support-and-coordination-of-rural-finance","title":"Apex support and coordination of rural finance","textbookHeading":"5.2 NABARD and RBI","kind":"Concept","body":"**NABARD**, established in **1982**, is the apex institution supporting agricultural and rural development finance. Its main roles are providing or refinancing funds, strengthening and restructuring the credit-delivery system, training and coordinating institutions, and monitoring projects supported through its refinancing.\n\nThe **RBI’s supporting functions** in the textbook include short-term refinancing; developing cooperative credit; supporting funds for longer-term rural finance; helping state participation in cooperative institutions; training personnel; undertaking rural-credit surveys; encouraging rural branch expansion; and providing remittance facilities and guidance.\n\nNABARD and the RBI strengthen the system behind the lender. A farmer’s ordinary loan may come from a bank or cooperative even when an apex institution supplies refinancing or institutional support.","formula":""},{"key":"how-shgs-reach-borrowers-without-collateral","title":"How SHGs reach borrowers without collateral","textbookHeading":"5.2 Microcredit and Self-Help Groups","kind":"Concept and box","body":"**Self-help groups (SHGs)** bring members, often poor rural women, together to save small amounts regularly. Pooled savings and bank linkages provide small loans for household needs and self-employment. **Microcredit** means credit in small amounts suited to borrowers poorly served by conventional banking.\n\nMembers’ mutual knowledge and group responsibility can substitute for conventional collateral, with comparatively simple formalities and manageable interest. Activities such as livestock rearing, food processing and small enterprises can increase income, confidence and financial independence.\n\nThe **Kudumbashree example in Kerala** illustrates women’s savings and group-based enterprise. The value is not just the size of a loan: regular saving, collective decision making and access to institutions can strengthen bargaining power and participation.","formula":""},{"key":"timely-farm-credit-and-access-to-banking","title":"Timely farm credit and access to banking","textbookHeading":"5.2 Financial Inclusion · KCC and Jan Dhan","kind":"Important box","body":"The **Kisan Credit Card (KCC)** provides a convenient credit arrangement for eligible agricultural needs, with an assessed limit and access when funds are required. It reduces the need to complete a fresh full loan process for every seasonal expense.\n\n**Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana**, launched in **2014**, expands access to bank accounts and associated financial services. The chapter discusses basic accounts, eligible overdraft and insurance facilities, and direct receipt of wages, pensions or benefits into accounts.\n\nFinancial inclusion involves usable services, not just opening an account. Affordable borrowing, regular saving, reliable payments and understanding how to use facilities determine whether access improves a household’s position.","formula":""},{"key":"achievements-weaknesses-and-the-way-forward","title":"Achievements, weaknesses and the way forward","textbookHeading":"5.2 Appraisal of Rural Banking","kind":"Concept","body":"**Achievements:** wider organised credit, rural branch expansion and NABARD support reduced some dependence on moneylenders. Credit helped farmers buy modern inputs and move towards commercial production.\n\n**Weaknesses:** collateral and formalities can exclude poorer borrowers; low saving and weak deposit mobilisation constrain lending; overdue loans weaken institutions; and the social priority of rural lending can weaken under commercial pressure. Distinguish genuine inability to repay after crop failure from wilful default.\n\nCredit should support viable production, with suitable repayment terms, insurance, information and marketing support. Repeated lending without a realistic income source does not remove poverty; equally, treating every poor borrower as uncreditworthy excludes the people social banking is meant to serve.","formula":""},{"key":"marketing-is-a-chain-not-just-a-sale","title":"Marketing is a chain, not just a sale","textbookHeading":"5.2 Agricultural Marketing","kind":"Concept","body":"**Agricultural marketing** includes assembling produce, processing, grading, packaging, storing, transporting, selling and distributing it. A farmer’s final return depends on this entire chain, not only on the price quoted at the farm gate.\n\nFarmers may face faulty weights, manipulated accounts, excessive commissions, inadequate market information and poor transport or storage. Debt can tie them to a particular trader. Without storage or cash to wait, they may sell immediately after harvest when prices are low.\n\nThese weaknesses justify intervention: improving infrastructure and fair dealing increases the share of the final price received by producers and reduces waste. Credit and marketing are linked because urgent repayment can force a distress sale.","formula":""},{"key":"four-groups-of-government-measures","title":"Four groups of government measures","textbookHeading":"5.2 Measures to Improve Agricultural Marketing","kind":"Concept","body":"**Regulated markets:** market committees and rules improve weighing, transparency and trading practices, reducing manipulation. **Physical infrastructure:** roads, rail links, warehouses, cold storage and processing facilities reduce transport costs, spoilage and forced sale.\n\n**Cooperative marketing:** farmers combine produce and bargain collectively, sharing services and reducing intermediary dependence. Gujarat’s milk cooperatives illustrate its potential; weak finance, coordination and management can limit success.\n\n**Support-price and distribution measures:** MSP provides a declared support benchmark for specified crops; procurement by agencies such as FCI contributes to buffer stocks; and the PDS distributes essential goods to eligible households through fair-price shops. These are distinct functions—price support, stock holding and consumer distribution—not three names for the same operation.","formula":""},{"key":"direct-markets-and-contracts-with-firms","title":"Direct markets and contracts with firms","textbookHeading":"5.2 Emerging Marketing Channels","kind":"Concept","body":"**Direct farmer–consumer markets** reduce intermediary stages and can improve the producer’s share while offering consumers fresher produce. Examples include Apni Mandi in Punjab and Haryana, Rythu Bazaars in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, and Uzhavar Sandies in Tamil Nadu.\n\n**Contract arrangements with firms** can specify price, quality and purchase conditions before production. Firms may provide inputs, technical knowledge or assured markets, reducing some uncertainty for farmers.\n\nBenefits depend on fair terms and enforcement. A small farmer may have weaker bargaining power than a large buyer, and rejection or quality disputes can create new risks. Explain both the opportunity and the conditions needed for it to work.","formula":""},{"key":"two-dimensions-of-diversification","title":"Two dimensions of diversification","textbookHeading":"5.3 Diversification of Agricultural Activities","kind":"Concept","body":"**Crop diversification** means growing a wider variety of crops rather than relying on one crop or the traditional pattern. It can spread weather and price risks and open opportunities in higher-value crops. Subsistence farmers may hesitate because familiar food crops provide household security.\n\n**Diversification of productive activity** means moving some labour and resources from cultivation into allied and non-farm activities. Animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services create additional sources of income.\n\nBenefits include higher and more stable earnings, productive use of surplus or disguised-unemployed labour, balanced rural development and less pressure to migrate to cities. Diversification should create viable opportunities rather than merely move people into another low-income activity.","formula":""},{"key":"livestock-and-the-white-revolution","title":"Livestock and the White Revolution","textbookHeading":"5.3 Animal Husbandry · Operation Flood","kind":"Concept and box","body":"**Animal husbandry** includes rearing livestock for milk, meat, eggs, wool and other products. Mixed crop–livestock farming provides income at different times and can support households in dry regions. Women and landless households often participate, making livestock important for inclusive rural livelihoods.\n\nConstraints include low animal productivity, inadequate knowledge, poor breeds, insufficient credit and limited veterinary care. Better breeding, feed, disease control, training and market access can raise returns.\n\n**Operation Flood**, launched in **1970**, organised milk collection, grading, processing and distribution through cooperative networks. Pooling milk and linking rural producers with urban markets improved bargaining and income opportunities. The resulting expansion is associated with the **White Revolution**, with Gujarat’s cooperatives a prominent example.","formula":""},{"key":"two-other-sources-of-rural-employment","title":"Two other sources of rural employment","textbookHeading":"5.3 Fisheries and Horticulture","kind":"Concept","body":"**Fisheries:** inland fishing uses rivers, canals, ponds and other inland waters; marine fishing uses seas and oceans. Fishing and related processing and marketing support livelihoods, including women’s employment. Poverty, indebtedness, inadequate technology and finance constrain producers, while overfishing and pollution threaten sustainability.\n\n**Horticulture** includes fruits, vegetables, flowers, plantation crops, nurseries and related activities. Diverse climatic conditions create opportunities for higher-value production and employment. The chapter’s **Golden Revolution** discussion links research and technology transfer with expansion in fruits, vegetables and honey.\n\nHorticulture needs irrigation, reliable electricity, cold chains, transport, processing, research and market information because many products perish quickly. Switching acreage can also reduce production of staples such as pulses. Assess profitability alongside nutrition, risk and environmental needs.","formula":""},{"key":"tanwa-rural-information-and-sagy","title":"TANWA, rural information and SAGY","textbookHeading":"5.3 Women, Information Technology and Model Villages","kind":"Important boxes","body":"**Tamil Nadu Women in Agriculture (TANWA)** trained women in improved agricultural practices. Better skills raised productivity and encouraged savings, groups and enterprises such as mushroom production and small manufactures. The example links training to both earnings and empowerment.\n\n**Information technology** can provide weather forecasts, soil advice, prices, employment information and access to government services. Rural information kiosks and the National Virtual Academy for Rural Prosperity illustrate connecting villages with useful knowledge. Information helps only when it is timely, understandable and supported by access to the relevant service.\n\n**Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY)**, launched in October 2014, encourages MPs to develop model villages through local participation and coordinated improvements in health, nutrition, education and livelihoods. The chapter describes indicative village populations of 3,000–5,000 in plains and 1,000–3,000 in hilly areas, and selection outside the MP’s own or spouse’s village.","formula":""},{"key":"meaning-and-five-advantages-of-organic-farming","title":"Meaning and five advantages of organic farming","textbookHeading":"5.4 Organic Farming","kind":"Concept","body":"**Organic farming** relies on ecological processes, organic manure, compost and biological methods to maintain soil health and manage pests, reducing reliance on synthetic chemical inputs. Its aim is sustainable production that protects the resource base for future generations.\n\n**Reduced dependence on non-renewable inputs:** many synthetic inputs rely on petroleum-based production. **Environmental protection:** less chemical runoff can reduce soil and groundwater pollution. **Soil fertility:** organic matter helps maintain soil structure and biological activity.\n\n**Food and health appeal:** consumers may value reduced chemical residues and product quality, though organic status alone does not guarantee every nutritional claim. **Suitability for small producers:** locally available inputs and labour-based methods can reduce dependence on purchased chemicals. These benefits depend on appropriate practices, rather than merely stopping fertiliser use.","formula":""},{"key":"exports-transition-costs-and-the-cotton-example","title":"Exports, transition costs and the cotton example","textbookHeading":"5.4 Organic Farming · Prospects and Challenges","kind":"Concept and box","body":"India’s abundant labour can be an advantage in labour-intensive organic production. Certified products may obtain premium prices and export opportunities. However, certification, consumer trust, market connections and practical knowledge are necessary to realise those gains.\n\n**Challenges:** yields can fall during transition; lower output can raise cost per unit; products may have a shorter shelf life; certification and marketing can be difficult; and farmers need training and suitable inputs. A sudden universal switch without support can threaten farm incomes and food availability.\n\nThe Maharashtra organic-cotton case involving Kisan Mehta and Prakruti illustrates farmers experimenting with chemical-free production and international standards. The broader lesson is to combine soil protection with economic viability. The chapter also discusses **PM-PRANAM** as an initiative encouraging alternative fertilisers and balanced fertiliser use; organic farming is part of the wider sustainability agenda.","formula":""}],"flow":[["Finance production","Timely, accessible rural credit."],["Improve returns","Fair marketing and useful infrastructure."],["Diversify","Allied activities and non-farm livelihoods."],["Sustain resources","Protect soil, water and future productive capacity."]]},{"id":"i6","number":"6","subject":"ied","title":"Employment","intro":"","lessons":[{"key":"who-counts-as-a-worker","title":"Who counts as a worker?","textbookHeading":"6.1 Workers and Employment","kind":"Concept","body":"A **worker** contributes to the production of goods and services counted in economic activity. Workers include employees, self-employed people and unpaid helpers in family enterprises. Payment may be in cash or kind, and someone working for only part of the year can still be a worker.\n\nA farmer, street vendor, teacher and unpaid family-business helper can all be workers. Being a consumer does not itself make a person a worker. Unpaid domestic services within one’s own household are generally excluded from measured economic production, although they are valuable work in everyday life.\n\nEmployment is studied to understand the quantity and quality of human resources, sectoral contributions, job security, exploitation, child labour and training needs. People work for income and survival, but also for self-worth, participation and contribution to society.","formula":""},{"key":"three-employment-statuses","title":"Three employment statuses","textbookHeading":"6.1 Self-employed and Hired Workers","kind":"Concept","body":"**Self-employed workers** operate their own enterprises or farms and earn returns from that activity rather than receiving a wage from an employer. **Regular salaried employees** work under a continuing employment arrangement, usually with more predictable pay and conditions.\n\n**Casual wage labourers** are hired for short periods or particular tasks, often on daily wages, without assured continuing work. Construction labour and seasonal agricultural labour are common examples. Regular status usually offers better security, but the actual benefits depend on the contract and enforcement.\n\nEmployment status is different from industry. A person in manufacturing could be self-employed, regularly employed or casually hired; do not treat “factory worker” as automatically a secure salaried job.","formula":""},{"key":"people-available-people-employed-and-hours-supplied","title":"People available, people employed and hours supplied","textbookHeading":"6.1 Labour Supply, Labour Force and Workforce","kind":"Concept","body":"**Labour force** consists of employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work and seeking it under the relevant measurement definition. **Workforce** consists only of the employed. People neither employed nor seeking or available for work are outside the labour force.\n\n**Labour supply** concerns the amount of work offered, measured in worker-hours or worker-days. The same number of people can supply more labour by working longer hours. Thus a change in hours need not mean a change in the number of people in the labour force.\n\nKeep denominators separate: unemployment is measured relative to the labour force, while worker–population ratio relates workers to the population. A student not seeking work is not automatically unemployed.","formula":"Labour force = employed + unemployed\nUnemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labour force × 100\nWorker–population ratio = workforce ÷ population × 100","comparison":{"heads":["Measure","Who or what is counted","Important distinction"],"rows":[["Workforce","Employed people","Excludes unemployed people"],["Labour force","Employed + unemployed seeking/available for work","Excludes people outside the labour force"],["Labour supply","Hours or days of work offered","Can change without a change in headcount"]]}},{"key":"worker-population-ratio-and-rural-urban-differences","title":"Worker–population ratio and rural–urban differences","textbookHeading":"6.2 Participation in Employment · WPR","kind":"Concept","body":"**Worker–population ratio (WPR)** is the percentage of the population engaged in work. It indicates participation in measured employment, not the quality, productivity or pay of that work. A higher WPR does not by itself establish greater prosperity.\n\nThe textbook’s **2022–23** figures are: rural men 54%, urban men 55.6%, all men 54.4%; rural women 30%, urban women 18.7%, all women 27%; and total rural 42.3%, urban 37.7%, all persons 41.1%. These are a dated comparison, not current live statistics.\n\nRural participation may be higher because poverty pushes people into work earlier and fewer people can remain in education or wait for suitable jobs. Urban opportunities for study and job search can reduce measured participation. Interpret rates together with age, education, income and the character of employment.","formula":""},{"key":"why-participation-differs-between-groups","title":"Why participation differs between groups","textbookHeading":"6.2 Participation · Gender and Employment Status","kind":"Concept","body":"Men’s measured participation is higher than women’s because of unequal educational access, household responsibilities, social restrictions, limited suitable opportunities and under-recognition of women’s productive work. Rural women may participate in family farms or enterprises; such productive assistance should be counted even without a separate wage.\n\nRural employment is dominated by self-employment because many households cultivate land or run small activities and have limited salaried opportunities. Urban areas offer more regular employment in firms, offices and services. Casual labour remains important where people lack assets, skills or secure contracts.\n\nWomen can be concentrated in home-based, traditional or low-paid activities because mobility and opportunities are restricted. Do not confuse unpaid family-enterprise work, which is economic activity, with own-household domestic services, which are generally outside the measured production boundary.","formula":""},{"key":"industry-groups-and-sectoral-distribution","title":"Industry groups and sectoral distribution","textbookHeading":"6.3 Employment in Different Sectors","kind":"Concept","body":"**Primary sector:** agriculture and allied activities, mining and quarrying. **Secondary sector:** manufacturing, electricity/gas/water supply and construction. **Tertiary sector:** trade, transport, storage and other services. These groups classify what workers produce, rather than their employment status.\n\nThe chapter’s **2022–23 national distribution** is about **46.1% primary, 24.9% secondary and 29% tertiary**. Rural employment depends more heavily on agriculture, while urban employment is concentrated in industry and services. Women are particularly concentrated in agricultural and family-based work.\n\nAgriculture’s large employment share alongside a much smaller output share suggests low average output per worker and pressure on land. Structural development requires productive opportunities outside farming, rather than displacement without alternative livelihoods.","formula":""},{"key":"a-slow-shift-and-new-ways-of-working","title":"A slow shift and new ways of working","textbookHeading":"6.3 Changing Employment Structure","kind":"Concept and box","body":"The textbook compares **1972–73** employment shares of about **74.3% primary, 10.9% secondary and 14.8% tertiary** with the later distribution. The primary share has fallen and the other shares have risen, but agriculture remains the largest source of employment.\n\nThis is a structural shift, though slower than the transformation of output. A growing services share in GDP need not absorb all workers leaving farming, especially when expanding services require skills that many workers lack.\n\nHome-based work is not entirely new: weaving and other household production have long existed. Digital technology expanded remote work, particularly during the COVID-19 period. Its opportunities depend on connectivity, skills and the nature of the job.","formula":""},{"key":"jobless-growth-and-the-output-employment-gap","title":"Jobless growth and the output–employment gap","textbookHeading":"6.4 Growth and Employment","kind":"Concept","body":"**Jobless growth** means output grows without a corresponding expansion in employment. It does not necessarily mean literally zero jobs are created; the concern is that employment growth is inadequate relative to output growth and the number seeking work.\n\nCapital-intensive technology, automation and efficiency improvements can increase production using relatively fewer workers. Growth concentrated in high-skill activities may not provide suitable opportunities for the wider labour force. Some existing jobs can also disappear during restructuring.\n\nTo evaluate growth, ask how many jobs it creates, who can access them, and whether they provide productive, secure livelihoods. Labour-intensive activities, skills, infrastructure and support for smaller enterprises help connect output growth with employment.","formula":""},{"key":"what-casualisation-means-and-what-the-trend-actually-shows","title":"What casualisation means—and what the trend actually shows","textbookHeading":"6.4 Casualisation of Workforce","kind":"Concept","body":"**Casualisation** is a shift towards casual wage work relative to self-employment or regular salaried work. Workers may face uncertain hours, weak bargaining power, easy dismissal and limited benefits. Rural surplus labour migrating without adequate skills can enter such work; educated workers may also accept insecure contracts when alternatives are scarce.\n\nThe textbook’s series is not a continuous rise: the casual share increased from **23.2% in 1972–73** to **31.8% in 1993–94**, but was around **30% in 2011–12** and **23.3% in 2022–23**. State the relevant period rather than claiming that casualisation increased in every year.\n\nThe concept concerns the quality and composition of work. A rise in total employment can coexist with insecurity if new jobs are predominantly casual or poorly protected.","formula":""},{"key":"organised-versus-unorganised-employment","title":"Organised versus unorganised employment","textbookHeading":"6.5 Formal and Informal Sectors","kind":"Concept","body":"The textbook classifies **all public-sector establishments and private establishments employing ten or more workers** as the formal or organised sector. Smaller private enterprises and many own-account activities belong to the informal or unorganised sector.\n\nFormal employment is more likely to have written conditions, regular pay, social-security benefits and enforceable protection. Informal workers often face uncertain earnings, weak implementation of safeguards, limited collective bargaining and greater exposure to market fluctuations. This does not mean informal workers have no legal rights.\n\nThe chapter’s **2011–12** comparison puts roughly **30 million workers, or 6%, in the formal sector**, and **443 million, or 94%, in the informal sector**. These are historical textbook figures. A worker’s actual job protection should still be examined; establishment size alone does not guarantee every benefit.","formula":""},{"key":"why-protection-matters","title":"Why protection matters","textbookHeading":"6.5 Informalisation · Cases and Labour Codes","kind":"Important box","body":"**Informalisation** describes the predominance or spread of employment outside effectively protected formal arrangements. Workers may be employed yet remain vulnerable to poverty, accidents, illness and loss of work. Social security, skills and enforceable conditions therefore matter alongside the number of jobs.\n\nThe **Ahmedabad textile-mill case** describes large-scale closures and displacement from previously organised employment. Loss of wages and benefits pushed households towards insecure work and financial distress. The lesson is that industrial restructuring needs support for workers’ transition.\n\nThe textbook’s **labour-code box** highlights social protection for gig and platform workers, appointment letters and clearly stated terms, health checks for workers over 40, and safeguards for women working at night. These illustrate efforts to extend protection and improve working conditions rather than merely count employment.","formula":""},{"key":"who-is-unemployed-and-where-data-come-from","title":"Who is unemployed and where data come from","textbookHeading":"6.6 Unemployment · Meaning and Measurement","kind":"Concept","body":"**Unemployment** occurs when people willing and able to work at the prevailing terms cannot obtain work. Someone choosing not to seek work is different from someone seeking a job unsuccessfully. An employed person in unsuitable or insufficient work may face underemployment rather than open unemployment.\n\nThe chapter identifies the **Census**, employment surveys of the national statistical system, including the **Periodic Labour Force Survey**, and **employment-exchange registrations** as sources of information. Registered applicants are only a subset of all unemployed people; survey definitions and reference periods also matter.\n\nTo interpret a figure, ask whether it measures people, days or hours and what willingness, availability and job-search criteria were used. Do not calculate the unemployment rate by dividing unemployed people by the entire population.","formula":""},{"key":"two-important-rural-forms","title":"Two important rural forms","textbookHeading":"6.6 Disguised and Seasonal Unemployment","kind":"Concept","body":"**Disguised unemployment:** more people are engaged in an activity than are required, so some can be withdrawn without reducing total output. Their marginal contribution is effectively zero. A joint-family farm may divide a fixed amount of work among many members when alternative jobs are unavailable.\n\n**Seasonal unemployment:** work is available during particular seasons but not throughout the year. A single-crop area may provide work during sowing and harvesting while leaving workers idle in the off-season. Similar patterns can occur in seasonal processing or construction activities.\n\nDisguised unemployment concerns excess workers at a given time; seasonal unemployment concerns lack of work during part of the year. Irrigation, multiple cropping, allied activities and non-farm employment can help. Disguised unemployment can also occur outside agriculture when enterprises are overstaffed.","formula":"","comparison":{"heads":["Basis","Disguised unemployment","Seasonal unemployment"],"rows":[["Problem","Excess workers for the available work","Work unavailable in part of the year"],["Key test","Some workers can leave without reducing output","The worker has employment in one season but not another"],["Example","Too many family members on a small farm","Agricultural off-season without alternative work"]]}},{"key":"eight-causes-and-their-mechanisms","title":"Eight causes and their mechanisms","textbookHeading":"6.6 Causes of Unemployment","kind":"Concept","body":"**Slow economic growth:** output and investment may not expand enough to absorb jobseekers. **Rapid population growth:** the labour force can grow faster than employment opportunities. **Seasonal agriculture:** limited irrigation and single cropping leave long off-seasons. **Joint-family system:** workers remain attached to a farm despite insufficient productive work.\n\n**Decline of cottage and small industries:** loss of traditional livelihoods is not always offset by new opportunities. **Low investment:** inadequate capital formation limits enterprises and job creation. **Capital-intensive technology:** output can rise with relatively little additional labour.\n\n**Low mobility of labour:** language, family ties, social conditions, information and moving costs restrict movement to available jobs. These causes can reinforce each other, so remedies must address both labour demand and people’s ability to reach and perform the jobs.","formula":""},{"key":"direct-and-indirect-employment","title":"Direct and indirect employment","textbookHeading":"6.7 Government and Employment Generation","kind":"Concept","body":"**Direct employment:** government departments and public enterprises hire workers to provide administration, education, health, transport, infrastructure and other services. These workers are employed by the public institution itself.\n\n**Indirect employment:** public production, procurement and infrastructure create opportunities elsewhere. A public steel plant supplies inputs to downstream private industries, while roads make new businesses viable. Suppliers, contractors and linked enterprises can employ additional workers.\n\nGovernment also promotes self-employment, credit, skills and wage-work programmes. Distinguish an immediate public job from a policy that enables another employer or an individual enterprise to create work.","formula":""},{"key":"self-employment-rural-enterprises-and-roads","title":"Self-employment, rural enterprises and roads","textbookHeading":"6.7 Employment Programmes · Historical Framework","kind":"Concept","body":"**PMRY (1993):** assisted educated unemployed youth in establishing enterprises through credit and support. **SJSRY (1997):** addressed urban poverty through wage employment and self-employment arrangements. **REGP (1995):** promoted rural industries and artisan employment.\n\n**PMEGP (2008):** a credit-linked subsidy programme supporting micro-enterprises in manufacturing and services. **SGSY (1999):** promoted rural self-employment through groups, credit and assistance; its approach was subsequently restructured under **NRLM/Aajeevika**, with an emphasis on sustainable livelihoods.\n\n**PMGSY (December 2000):** all-weather rural road connectivity improves access to markets, services and employment. These programme names belong to different periods; learn each programme’s mechanism and distinguish a historical scheme from its successor. PMEGP should not be described as discontinued simply because a textbook passage groups it with older schemes.","formula":""},{"key":"mgnrega-and-the-textbook-s-updated-guarantee-box","title":"MGNREGA and the textbook’s updated guarantee box","textbookHeading":"6.7 Rural Employment Guarantee","kind":"Concept and updated box","body":"**MGNREGA, 2005**, historically guaranteed **100 days of wage employment per rural household per financial year** to adult members volunteering for unskilled manual work. It is a household guarantee, not 100 days separately for every adult. Its framework included employment within the prescribed period, unemployment allowance where applicable, and a minimum one-third participation provision for women.\n\nThe purpose is livelihood security combined with useful rural assets and local work opportunities. It helps households during agricultural slack periods and can strengthen workers’ bargaining power.\n\nThe chapter’s newer box discusses the **Viksit Bharat—Guarantee for Rozgar and Ajeevika Mission (Gramin) Act, 2025**, which replaces the earlier framework and raises the guarantee to **125 days per rural household**. Keep the date and framework explicit: answer 100 days when asked about MGNREGA’s original provision, and 125 days when asked about the updated Act.","formula":""},{"key":"mudra-skills-make-in-india-and-start-up-india","title":"MUDRA, skills, Make in India and Start-up India","textbookHeading":"6.7 Recent Initiatives · Credit, Skills and Enterprise","kind":"Concept","body":"**MUDRA**, launched in 2015, supports credit for eligible small and micro enterprises, enabling self-employment and expansion. **PMKVY** supports industry-relevant skills through the skills-development framework and training partners. Credit and training work best when supported by markets and viable business opportunities.\n\n**Make in India**, launched on 25 September 2014, seeks to encourage production, investment and manufacturing activity with employment opportunities. **Start-up India** encourages entrepreneurship and enterprise creation, which can generate jobs for founders and others.\n\n**DAY-NULM** promotes urban poor households’ livelihoods through skills, wage employment and self-employment support. These programmes use different channels—finance, skills, investment and enterprise—so an answer should connect each name to its employment mechanism rather than merely list schemes.","formula":""},{"key":"covid-era-support-and-employer-incentives","title":"COVID-era support and employer incentives","textbookHeading":"6.7 Recent Initiatives · Employment Incentives","kind":"Important box","body":"**Atmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY, 2020)** used employment-related incentives to encourage new hiring or re-employment during recovery from COVID disruption. **Pradhan Mantri Rojgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY, 2016)** supported eligible employer contributions to encourage formal job creation; the chapter’s account refers to a defined registration and benefit period.\n\n**Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan**, launched on 20 June 2020, was a **125-day campaign** supporting livelihoods and rural works for returning migrants and affected rural households. Its 125-day campaign duration is different from a household’s annual legal employment guarantee under the later Act.\n\nThe wider challenge is to create productive work while improving security, matching skills with demand and extending opportunities to women and disadvantaged groups. Employment policy succeeds when people can earn a sustainable livelihood, not simply when a scheme has been announced.","formula":""}],"flow":[["Count work correctly","Distinguish workforce, labour force and participation."],["Examine its quality","Status, sector, pay and security."],["Identify the gap","Unemployment, underemployment and jobless growth."],["Connect policy","Jobs, skills, credit and protection."]]}],"questions":[{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a macroeconomic issue?","options":["One household’s saving","Price of one brand of shoes","Output of one firm","General price level"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The general price level is an economy-wide variable.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q1"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Studying the equilibrium of the steel industry is:","options":["National-income accounting","Microeconomics","Always macroeconomics","Neither branch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Aggregation alone does not make a study macroeconomic; one industry remains a microeconomic unit.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q2"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: More saving by every household can reduce total income. Reason: Lower consumption can reduce aggregate demand.","options":["Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion false; reason true","Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason does not explain assertion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is the micro–macro paradox under demand-constrained conditions.","tag":"Assertion–reason","id":"q3"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Partial equilibrium studies:","options":["Only foreign trade","Only government accounts","All markets simultaneously","One market, holding other markets unchanged"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Partial equilibrium is the textbook’s method of microeconomic analysis.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q4"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A bakery buys flour to make bread. The flour is:","options":["Final consumption","Fixed investment","A capital good","An intermediate good"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Its end use is as a raw material in current production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q5"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A new machine used repeatedly by a factory is:","options":["A non-durable consumer good","A final capital good","An intermediate good","A transfer payment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Capital goods are final goods used repeatedly to produce other goods.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q6"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which pair consists entirely of stocks?","options":["Capital and wealth","Consumption and expenditure","Income and saving","Investment and output"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stocks are measured at a point of time; all the other pairs are flows.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q7"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If gross investment is less than depreciation, net investment is:","options":["Equal to gross investment","Positive","Zero","Negative"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net investment = gross investment − depreciation; the capital stock falls.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q8"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a real flow from households to firms?","options":["Wages","Consumption expenditure","Rent payments","Factor services"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Households supply factor services; firms pay money incomes in the opposite direction.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q9"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A car bought for private household use and an identical car bought to run as a taxi are respectively:","options":["Intermediate and final","Capital and consumer goods","Consumer durable and capital good","Both intermediate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Classification depends on end use, not physical appearance.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q10"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Closing stock is 80 and opening stock is 110. Inventory investment equals:","options":["80","190","−30","30"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Change in stock = 80 − 110 = −30.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q11"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"National income is:","options":["NNPFC","GDPMP","GNPMP","NDPMP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The textbook defines national income as net national product at factor cost.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q12"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"To convert GDPMP to NDPFC, subtract:","options":["Depreciation and NFIA","Depreciation and NIT","Subsidies only","NFIA only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gross → net removes depreciation; market price → factor cost removes NIT.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q13"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Factor income paid abroad exceeds factor income received from abroad. NFIA is:","options":["Necessarily equal to GDP","Zero","Negative","Positive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NFIA = factor income from abroad − factor income to abroad.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q14"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which belongs to India’s domestic territory?","options":["Every foreign branch owned by an Indian citizen","US embassy in New Delhi","Indian embassy in Japan","An Indian tourist’s hotel room abroad"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An Indian embassy abroad is part of India’s economic territory.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q15"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A gift received from a relative abroad is:","options":["Domestic product","A current transfer","Factor income from abroad","Depreciation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A gift does not reward factor services; it is not NFIA.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q16"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Nominal GDP rises 10% while quantities are unchanged. This can be explained by:","options":["A fall in all prices","A fall in the GDP deflator","An increase in real output","An increase in prices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Nominal GDP uses current prices, so price increases can raise it without more output.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q17"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a limitation of GDP as a welfare index?","options":["It has an accounting period","It ignores income distribution","It includes final goods","It measures current production"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The same GDP can coexist with very different distributions of income.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q18"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement 1: Citizenship and normal residence are identical. Statement 2: Domestic income can include factor income earned by non-residents.","options":["Both false","Only 1 true","Both true","Only 2 true"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Residence depends on economic interest and residence conventions; domestic income depends on territory.","tag":"Two statements","id":"q19"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Sales are 500, opening stock 40 and closing stock 70. Value of output is:","options":["530","610","500","470"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Output = sales + change in stock = 500 + 70 − 40 = 530.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q20"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is included in compensation of employees?","options":["Employer contribution to provident fund","Dividend","Old-age welfare pension","Lottery winnings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Employer social-security contributions form part of compensation of employees.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q21"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Operating surplus excludes:","options":["Interest","Rent","Wages","Profit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wages are compensation of employees; operating surplus includes rent, interest and profit.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q22"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A dealer sells a second-hand car and charges commission. Current GDP includes:","options":["Resale value only","Commission only","Full resale value and commission","Neither"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The car was counted when first produced; the dealer’s current service is new production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q23"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"GDCF is given in an expenditure-method question along with change in stock. You should normally:","options":["Add both in full","Ignore all investment","Deduct change in stock","Use GDCF without adding stock again"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GDCF already includes inventory investment; check the label before adding components.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q24"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The same wheat becomes flour and then bread. Counting every stage’s full sales value causes:","options":["Double counting","A transfer payment","NFIA","Depreciation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Intermediate value is already embodied in the final product.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q25"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which purchase is investment expenditure in GDP?","options":["An old bond","Existing shares","A second-hand house’s full value","A newly built house"],"answer":3,"explanation":"New residential construction adds to the capital stock.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q26"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Arrange the value-added steps: A add NFIA; B calculate output; C deduct intermediate consumption; D deduct depreciation and NIT.","options":["B → C → D → A","C → A → B → D","A → B → C → D","B → D → A → C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Output minus intermediate consumption gives GVA MP; then convert to national income.","tag":"Sequence","id":"q27"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Barter requires:","options":["Paper currency","A central bank","Demand deposits","Double coincidence of wants"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each party must want the good offered by the other.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q28"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A price tag of ₹300 illustrates money as:","options":["Store of value","Measure of value","Standard of deferred payment","Transfer of ownership"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Money supplies a common unit of account.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q29"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Repaying a loan in fixed rupee instalments illustrates:","options":["Barter","Only store of value","Credit rationing","Standard of deferred payments"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Future obligations are expressed and settled in money.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q30"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is included in M1?","options":["Interbank deposits","Shares held by public","Demand deposits with commercial banks","Time deposits only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"M1 includes currency with public, demand deposits and other deposits with RBI.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q31"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement 1: Money supply is a flow. Statement 2: Money held by the public is part of money supply.","options":["Only 2 true","Only 1 true","Both false","Both true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Money supply is a stock at a point of time.","tag":"Two statements","id":"q32"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Liquidity refers to:","options":["Face value of a note","Rate of inflation","Amount of government debt","Convertibility into cash"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An asset is more liquid when it converts to cash more readily.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q33"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A cheque is:","options":["A second stock of money in addition to the deposit","Unlimited legal tender in every transaction","A capital good","An instruction to transfer a bank deposit"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Counting the cheque and the underlying deposit separately would duplicate the same money.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q34"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"With a 20% reserve ratio, the simple deposit multiplier is:","options":["2","20","0.2","5"],"answer":3,"explanation":"1 ÷ 0.20 = 5 under the simple model’s assumptions.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q35"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"RBI buys government securities from the market. Other things equal, bank liquidity:","options":["Rises","Becomes zero","Is necessarily unchanged","Falls"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RBI purchases inject liquidity, supporting credit creation.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q36"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which action can restrict credit?","options":["Purchase securities","Raise margin requirement","Lower CRR","Lower repo rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A larger margin means a smaller loan for the same collateral.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q37"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"CRR reserves are maintained:","options":["Only with the government treasury","Only as gold in each bank","With the borrower","With the RBI"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CRR is the required percentage of deposits kept with the central bank.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q38"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An RBI emergency loan to a bank unable to borrow elsewhere illustrates:","options":["Portfolio investment","Lender of last resort","Tax collection","Banker to government"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The central bank supports banks when normal sources of funds fail.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q39"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a qualitative instrument?","options":["Rationing of credit","SLR","CRR","Repo rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rationing directs credit to specified uses or sectors.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q40"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If banks keep excess reserves or borrowers keep cash, actual deposit expansion is:","options":["Potentially less than the simple maximum","Exactly infinite","Unaffected by leakage","Always greater than 1/reserve ratio"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The maximum assumes all lendable reserves are used and loans are redeposited.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q41"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"C = 60 + 0.75Y. Autonomous consumption is:","options":["0.25","0.75","60","60Y"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The intercept is consumption at Y = 0.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q42"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The slope of the consumption curve equals:","options":["APS","MPS","APC","MPC"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Slope = ΔC/ΔY, which is MPC.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q43"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"At the break-even point:","options":["MPC = 1 and MPS = 0 necessarily","Saving equals investment necessarily","APC = 0 and APS = 1","APC = 1 and APS = 0"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Break-even means C = Y and S = 0; it does not by itself establish macroeconomic equilibrium.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q44"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If APC is 1.2, APS is:","options":["0.2","−0.2","2.2","1.2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"APC + APS = 1, hence APS = −0.2.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q45"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"In an open economy, AD is:","options":["C + S","Y − S","C + I + G + X + M","C + I + G + X − M"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net exports are exports minus imports.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q46"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC normally:","options":["Always equals MPC","Falls with income","Always exceeds two","Rises with income"],"answer":1,"explanation":"APC = C̄/Y + MPC; the first term falls as Y rises.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q47"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If S = −80 + 0.2Y, consumption is:","options":["80 + 0.8Y","−80 + 1.2Y","80 + 0.2Y","−80 + 0.8Y"],"answer":0,"explanation":"C = Y − S = 80 + 0.8Y.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q48"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"At zero income APC is:","options":["Equal to MPC","Zero","Undefined","One"],"answer":2,"explanation":"APC = C/Y cannot be calculated with Y = 0.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q49"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If planned AD is below output, unplanned inventories:","options":["Decrease","Stay equal to desired inventories","Become exports automatically","Increase"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Unsold goods accumulate, leading firms to cut production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q50"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Equilibrium in the two-sector model requires:","options":["C = Y always","Planned S = planned I","Unemployment = zero","APC = MPC"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Planned saving equals planned investment; full employment is not guaranteed.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q51"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Ex-post saving and investment are equal because:","options":["Every plan succeeds","Unplanned inventory investment is included","Government balances its budget","MPC is zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Realised investment includes unintended changes in stocks.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q52"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"MPC rises from 0.6 to 0.8. The multiplier:","options":["Falls to 1","Stays at 4","Rises from 2.5 to 5","Falls from 5 to 2.5"],"answer":2,"explanation":"k = 1/(1 − MPC).","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q53"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Equilibrium can coexist with unemployment. Reason: Planned expenditure can equal planned output below capacity.","options":["Assertion false; reason true","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason does not explain assertion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Underemployment equilibrium matches plans without full utilisation.","tag":"Assertion–reason","id":"q54"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An investment decline of 40 with MPC 0.75 causes an income change of:","options":["−160","−10","160","−40"],"answer":0,"explanation":"k = 4; ΔY = 4 × (−40) = −160.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q55"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The inflationary gap is measured at:","options":["Zero output","Full-employment output","Break-even consumption income only","Any arbitrary income"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It is excess AD over AS at full employment.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q56"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which policy helps correct deficient demand?","options":["Raise taxes","Buy securities through OMO","Cut government spending","Raise CRR"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Buying securities injects liquidity and supports lending and spending.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q57"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Beyond full-employment capacity, excess AD principally raises:","options":["The capital stock immediately","Real output without limit","The general price level","Unemployment by definition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In the simple model real output cannot expand beyond capacity.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q58"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is fiscal policy?","options":["RBI securities sale","SLR change","Government expenditure increase","Repo-rate reduction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fiscal policy concerns government revenue and expenditure.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q59"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Workers willing to work at the existing wage but unable to find work face:","options":["Involuntary unemployment","Only leisure preference","Voluntary unemployment","Full employment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The inability to obtain work despite willingness distinguishes involuntary unemployment.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q60"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which receipt creates a liability?","options":["A fine","Government borrowing","Income tax","A fee"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Borrowing is a capital receipt because it creates a repayment obligation.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q61"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Recovery of a loan by government is:","options":["Revenue receipt","A tax","Revenue expenditure","Capital receipt"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It reduces the government’s financial asset: outstanding loans.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q62"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is capital expenditure?","options":["Loan-principal repayment","A food subsidy","Interest on past debt","Employee salaries"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Repayment reduces liabilities; interest does not.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q63"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Fiscal deficit measures:","options":["Exports minus imports","Only revenue receipts","Borrowing requirement","Only interest payments"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It is expenditure minus receipts other than borrowings.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q64"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If primary deficit is zero:","options":["Fiscal deficit is zero necessarily","Government debt is zero","Fiscal deficit equals interest payments","Revenue deficit is zero necessarily"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Primary deficit = fiscal deficit − interest payments.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q65"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"National defence is generally:","options":["Rival and excludable","Non-rival and non-excludable","Rival and non-excludable","Non-rival and excludable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"These are the two characteristics of a public good.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q66"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Progressive taxation and spending on low-income households primarily serve:","options":["Credit creation","Redistribution","Exchange-rate hedging","Currency issue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"They can reduce inequality in income and wealth.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q67"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A grant given to a state government for building a hospital is classified by the granting government as:","options":["A loan-principal repayment","Revenue expenditure","Disinvestment","A capital receipt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The textbook convention treats grants as revenue expenditure for the grantor, even when used to create assets.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q68"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The rate rises from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ under a flexible system. The rupee has:","options":["Appreciated","Been revalued officially","Become a fixed currency","Depreciated"],"answer":3,"explanation":"More rupees are needed per dollar: the rupee’s external value falls.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q69"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"More inward FDI raises the supply of dollars. Other things equal, ₹/$:","options":["Must stay fixed","Rises","Becomes negative","Falls"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A rightward supply shift reduces the price of dollars and appreciates the rupee.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q70"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An official reduction in the external value of a currency under a fixed system is:","options":["A floating appreciation","Devaluation","Revaluation","Appreciation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Devaluation is an official change; depreciation is market driven.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q71"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"When excess demand for dollars threatens a fixed rate, the central bank can:","options":["Buy dollars","Sell dollars from reserves","Ban all domestic saving","Issue foreign shares itself"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selling dollars supplies the shortage and supports the domestic currency.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q72"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A contract today fixing an exchange rate for payment three months later is a:","options":["Currency reserve ratio","Spot contract","Forward contract","Transfer payment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forward markets allow future exchange on terms agreed today.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q73"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A rise in ₹/$ makes a $100 import cost:","options":["Zero rupees","Fewer rupees","The same rupees always","More rupees"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The foreign-currency price times a larger rupee/dollar rate gives a larger rupee cost.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q74"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An export of goods is recorded as:","options":["Capital-account debit","Official-reserve loss automatically","Current-account credit","Current-account debit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exports bring foreign-exchange receipts and are current-account credits.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q75"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An Indian resident sends a gift abroad. The entry is:","options":["Current-account debit","Domestic factor income","A goods export","Capital-account credit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An outward current transfer is a debit in the current account.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q76"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Interest paid on a foreign loan and repayment of its principal belong respectively to:","options":["Current and capital accounts","Both trade balance","Both transfers","Capital and current accounts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Interest is factor income; principal changes an external liability.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q77"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A BoP accounting balance proves that:","options":["All autonomous transactions balance","Counterpart and financing entries close the accounts","Foreign borrowing is zero","There is no external deficit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accounting balance can coexist with an autonomous deficit financed through reserves or loans.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q78"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Accommodating transactions are undertaken:","options":["Only to earn profit","Only as goods exports","To finance or correct an external imbalance","Only by tourists"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Their purpose is adjustment of the BoP imbalance; they are below-the-line items.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q79"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Balance of trade includes:","options":["All current transfers","Goods only","All capital flows","Goods and all services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trade balance is merchandise exports minus merchandise imports.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q80"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Under the supplied textbook’s convention, direct purchases by foreign tourists in domestic markets are:","options":["Merchandise exports","Domestic borrowing","Capital-account debits","Invisible trade"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The purple box classifies direct tourist purchases as invisible trade.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q81"},{"chapter":"2","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Gross fixed investment is ₹420 crore. Opening inventories are ₹90 crore and closing inventories ₹120 crore. Depreciation is ₹70 crore. Find inventory investment, gross investment and net investment.","solution":"1. Inventory investment = 120 − 90 = ₹30 crore.\n2. Gross investment = 420 + 30 = ₹450 crore.\n3. Net investment = 450 − 70 = ₹380 crore.\nHeading to remember: Gross Investment, Net Investment and Depreciation.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q82"},{"chapter":"2","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A machine costs ₹12 lakh, has scrap value ₹2 lakh and an expected life of 5 years. Find annual depreciation. If annual gross investment is ₹7 lakh, find net investment.","solution":"Annual depreciation = (12 − 2)/5 = ₹2 lakh.\nNet investment = 7 − 2 = ₹5 lakh.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q83"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"GDPMP = ₹8,000 crore; depreciation = ₹500 crore; indirect taxes = ₹700 crore; subsidies = ₹100 crore; factor income from abroad = ₹180 crore; factor income paid abroad = ₹260 crore. Find NIT, NFIA, NDPFC and national income.","solution":"NIT = 700 − 100 = ₹600 crore.\nNFIA = 180 − 260 = −₹80 crore.\nNDPFC = 8,000 − 500 − 600 = ₹6,900 crore.\nNNPFC = 6,900 − 80 = ₹6,820 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q84"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"National income is ₹6,200 crore, depreciation ₹300 crore, NIT ₹450 crore and NFIA −₹50 crore. Find GDPMP.","solution":"GDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA\n= 6,200 + 300 + 450 − (−50) = ₹7,000 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q85"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"GDPMP is ₹4,800 crore and GNPMP is ₹4,700 crore. Depreciation is ₹200 crore and NIT is ₹300 crore. Find NFIA and national income.","solution":"NFIA = GNPMP − GDPMP = 4,700 − 4,800 = −₹100 crore.\nNNPFC = GNPMP − Depreciation − NIT = 4,700 − 200 − 300 = ₹4,200 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q86"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Nominal GDP is ₹7,200 crore and real GDP is ₹6,000 crore. Calculate the GDP deflator. If nominal GDP later becomes ₹8,100 crore with deflator 125, find real GDP.","solution":"GDP deflator = 7,200/6,000 × 100 = 120.\nLater real GDP = 8,100/125 × 100 = ₹6,480 crore.\nAn index of 125 means prices are 25% above the base-year level, not necessarily 25% above the immediately preceding year.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q87"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"An economy produces only rice and cloth. Base prices are ₹20/kg and ₹50/metre. Current prices are ₹25/kg and ₹60/metre. Current quantities are 100 kg and 80 metres. Find nominal GDP, real GDP and the deflator.","solution":"Nominal GDP = 25 × 100 + 60 × 80 = ₹7,300.\nReal GDP = 20 × 100 + 50 × 80 = ₹6,000.\nDeflator = 7,300/6,000 × 100 = 121.67 (approximately).\nUse current quantities in both measures.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q88"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"GDPFC = ₹5,200 crore; NDPFC = ₹4,900 crore; indirect taxes ₹200 crore; subsidies ₹260 crore; NFIA ₹80 crore. Find depreciation, GDPMP and NNPMP.","solution":"Depreciation = 5,200 − 4,900 = ₹300 crore.\nNIT = 200 − 260 = −₹60 crore.\nGDPMP = GDPFC + NIT = 5,200 − 60 = ₹5,140 crore.\nNNPMP = GDPMP − Depreciation + NFIA = 5,140 − 300 + 80 = ₹4,920 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q89"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"A firm has domestic sales ₹900 crore, exports ₹100 crore, opening stock ₹80 crore, closing stock ₹110 crore and intermediate purchases ₹430 crore. Find output and GVA MP. Then find NVA FC if depreciation is ₹50 crore and NIT ₹40 crore.","solution":"Total sales = 900 + 100 = ₹1,000 crore.\nChange in stock = 110 − 80 = ₹30 crore.\nOutput = 1,000 + 30 = ₹1,030 crore.\nGVA MP = 1,030 − 430 = ₹600 crore.\nNVA FC = 600 − 50 − 40 = ₹510 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q90"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A farmer sells wheat for ₹100 to a mill; the mill sells flour for ₹170 to a bakery; the bakery sells bread for ₹280 to households. No other intermediate inputs exist. Find each producer’s value added and total GDP.","solution":"Farmer GVA = 100 − 0 = ₹100.\nMill GVA = 170 − 100 = ₹70.\nBakery GVA = 280 − 170 = ₹110.\nTotal = ₹280, equal to final bread value. Adding full sales gives ₹550 and double counts ₹270.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q91"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Wages in cash = ₹1,000 crore, wages in kind = ₹100 crore, employer social contributions = ₹80 crore, rent and royalty = ₹200 crore, interest on productive loans = ₹120 crore, corporate tax = ₹50 crore, dividends = ₹70 crore, retained earnings = ₹30 crore, mixed income = ₹250 crore and NFIA = −₹40 crore. Find national income.","solution":"Compensation = 1,000 + 100 + 80 = ₹1,180 crore.\nProfit = 50 + 70 + 30 = ₹150 crore.\nOperating surplus = 200 + 120 + 150 = ₹470 crore.\nNDPFC = 1,180 + 470 + 250 = ₹1,900 crore.\nNNPFC = 1,900 − 40 = ₹1,860 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q92"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"PFCE ₹1,200 crore; GFCE ₹400 crore; gross domestic fixed capital formation ₹500 crore; change in stock −₹20 crore; exports ₹180 crore; imports ₹230 crore; depreciation ₹100 crore; NIT ₹80 crore; NFIA ₹30 crore. Find GDPMP and national income.","solution":"GDCF = 500 − 20 = ₹480 crore.\nNet exports = 180 − 230 = −₹50 crore.\nGDPMP = 1,200 + 400 + 480 − 50 = ₹2,030 crore.\nNNPFC = 2,030 − 100 − 80 + 30 = ₹1,880 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q93"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"For an economy, total output ₹2,500 crore and intermediate consumption ₹700 crore. PFCE ₹800 crore, GFCE ₹300 crore, GDCF ₹600 crore, exports ₹250 crore and imports ₹150 crore. Show that production and expenditure methods agree.","solution":"Value-added method: GDPMP = 2,500 − 700 = ₹1,800 crore.\nExpenditure method: GDPMP = 800 + 300 + 600 + (250 − 150) = ₹1,800 crore.\nBoth measure the same final production from different sides. Do not add inventories separately when already included in GDCF.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q94"},{"chapter":"5","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Currency held by the public is ₹600 crore; demand deposits ₹900 crore; other deposits with RBI ₹20 crore; net time deposits ₹1,500 crore; post-office savings deposits ₹100 crore. Find M1, M2 and M3 under the textbook definitions.","solution":"M1 = 600 + 900 + 20 = ₹1,520 crore.\nM2 = 1,520 + 100 = ₹1,620 crore.\nM3 = 1,520 + 1,500 = ₹3,020 crore.\nM3 adds time deposits to M1, not to M2.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q95"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"An initial deposit of ₹2,000 enters the banking system. The required reserve ratio is 10%, there are no excess reserves and every loan is redeposited. Find total deposits, additional credit and reserves in the final position.","solution":"Multiplier = 1/0.10 = 10.\nTotal deposits = 2,000 × 10 = ₹20,000.\nAdditional credit = 20,000 − 2,000 = ₹18,000.\nTotal reserves = 10% × 20,000 = ₹2,000.\nDistinguish total deposits from new credit.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q96"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Initial deposit is ₹1,000 and reserve ratio falls from 20% to 10%. Find the maximum total deposits before and after, and their increase.","solution":"Before: 1,000/0.20 = ₹5,000.\nAfter: 1,000/0.10 = ₹10,000.\nIncrease = ₹5,000.\nThis assumes the same initial reserves, full lending and complete redepositing.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q97"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A bank lends against collateral worth ₹80 lakh. If the margin rises from 20% to 35%, find the maximum loan at each margin and the reduction.","solution":"At 20% margin: loan = 80 × 0.80 = ₹64 lakh.\nAt 35% margin: loan = 80 × 0.65 = ₹52 lakh.\nReduction = ₹12 lakh.\nHigher margin restricts credit against unchanged security.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q98"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 100 + 0.8Y. At Y = ₹500 crore, find C, S, APC, APS, MPC and MPS.","solution":"C = 100 + 0.8 × 500 = ₹500 crore.\nS = 500 − 500 = 0.\nAPC = 500/500 = 1; APS = 0.\nMPC = 0.8; MPS = 0.2.\nThis is the break-even point, but not necessarily equilibrium.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q99"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Income rises from ₹400 crore to ₹600 crore and consumption rises from ₹360 crore to ₹510 crore. Find MPC and MPS. Find APC and APS at the new income.","solution":"ΔC = 150; ΔY = 200.\nMPC = 150/200 = 0.75; MPS = 0.25.\nAt Y = 600: APC = 510/600 = 0.85; S = 90; APS = 90/600 = 0.15.\nMPC + MPS = 1 and APC + APS = 1.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q100"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Saving function is S = −60 + 0.25Y. Derive consumption function and find break-even income. Calculate saving at Y = ₹160 crore.","solution":"C = Y − S = Y − (−60 + 0.25Y) = 60 + 0.75Y.\nAt break-even S = 0: 0.25Y = 60, so Y = ₹240 crore.\nAt Y = 160: S = −60 + 40 = −₹20 crore (dissaving).","tag":"Numerical","id":"q101"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"At income ₹1,000 crore, APC is 0.9. If income rises by ₹200 crore and MPS is 0.25, find new consumption, saving, APC and APS.","solution":"Initial C = 0.9 × 1,000 = ₹900 crore.\nMPC = 1 − 0.25 = 0.75; ΔC = 0.75 × 200 = ₹150 crore.\nNew C = ₹1,050 crore; new Y = ₹1,200 crore; new S = ₹150 crore.\nNew APC = 1,050/1,200 = 0.875; APS = 0.125.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q102"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"C = 80 + 0.75Y and autonomous investment is ₹120 crore. Determine equilibrium income, consumption and saving.","solution":"Y = C + I = 80 + 0.75Y + 120.\n0.25Y = 200 ⇒ Y* = ₹800 crore.\nC = 80 + 0.75 × 800 = ₹680 crore.\nS = 800 − 680 = ₹120 crore = I.\nCheck planned S = planned I.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q103"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"S = −150 + 0.3Y and planned investment is ₹210 crore. Find equilibrium income. At Y = ₹1,000 crore, explain inventory adjustment.","solution":"Equilibrium: −150 + 0.3Y = 210 ⇒ 0.3Y = 360 ⇒ Y = ₹1,200 crore.\nAt Y = 1,000: planned S = ₹150 crore < I = ₹210 crore.\nAD exceeds output by ₹60 crore; inventories fall unexpectedly and firms expand production.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q104"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Investment rises by ₹80 crore and equilibrium income rises by ₹400 crore. Find multiplier, MPC, MPS and change in consumption.","solution":"k = 400/80 = 5.\nMPS = 1/5 = 0.2; MPC = 0.8.\nΔC = ΔY − ΔI = 400 − 80 = ₹320 crore.\nFinal additional saving is ₹80 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q105"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"MPC is 0.6 and investment falls by ₹50 crore. Calculate the final changes in income, consumption and saving.","solution":"k = 1/(1 − 0.6) = 2.5.\nΔY = 2.5 × (−50) = −₹125 crore.\nΔC = 0.6 × (−125) = −₹75 crore.\nΔS = −125 − (−75) = −₹50 crore = ΔI.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q106"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An investment injection of ₹100 crore occurs with MPC 0.8. State income generated in the first four rounds and the eventual total.","solution":"Rounds: ₹100 crore, ₹80 crore, ₹64 crore, ₹51.2 crore.\nFirst four rounds total = ₹295.2 crore.\nEventual total = 100/(1 − 0.8) = ₹500 crore.\nLater rounds continue; the first four do not equal the final multiplier effect.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q107"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 100 + 0.8Y, I = ₹200 crore and full-employment income is ₹2,000 crore. Find equilibrium income, the deflationary gap and the required increase in autonomous expenditure.","solution":"Y* = (100 + 200)/0.2 = ₹1,500 crore.\nAt Yf = 2,000: AD = 100 + 0.8 × 2,000 + 200 = ₹1,900 crore.\nDeflationary gap = 2,000 − 1,900 = ₹100 crore.\nIncome shortfall = ₹500 crore. With k = 5, required autonomous expenditure increase = 500/5 = ₹100 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q108"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 120 + 0.75Y, I = ₹280 crore and Yf = ₹1,200 crore. Determine the demand situation and the autonomous-expenditure correction.","solution":"Notional Y* = (120 + 280)/0.25 = ₹1,600 crore, above capacity.\nAt Yf: AD = 120 + 0.75 × 1,200 + 280 = ₹1,300 crore.\nInflationary gap = 1,300 − 1,200 = ₹100 crore.\nReduce autonomous expenditure by ₹100 crore. Real output cannot rise indefinitely above full employment in this model.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q109"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Full-employment income is ₹2,400 crore; current equilibrium income is ₹1,800 crore; MPS is 0.25. Find multiplier and the autonomous investment increase needed for full employment.","solution":"k = 1/0.25 = 4.\nRequired ΔY = 2,400 − 1,800 = ₹600 crore.\nΔI = ΔY/k = 600/4 = ₹150 crore.\nThe expenditure gap is ₹150 crore, while the income gap is ₹600 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q110"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Revenue receipts = ₹900 crore; revenue expenditure = ₹1,050 crore; capital expenditure = ₹450 crore; non-debt capital receipts = ₹100 crore; interest payments = ₹120 crore. Calculate revenue, fiscal and primary deficits.","solution":"Revenue deficit = 1,050 − 900 = ₹150 crore.\nTotal expenditure = 1,050 + 450 = ₹1,500 crore.\nFiscal deficit = 1,500 − 900 − 100 = ₹500 crore.\nPrimary deficit = 500 − 120 = ₹380 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q111"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Total expenditure is ₹2,000 crore, fiscal deficit ₹500 crore, non-tax revenue ₹200 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹100 crore. Find tax revenue.","solution":"Fiscal deficit = TE − Tax revenue − Non-tax revenue − Non-debt capital receipts.\n500 = 2,000 − T − 200 − 100.\nT = 2,000 − 200 − 100 − 500 = ₹1,200 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q112"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Revenue deficit is ₹80 crore, revenue expenditure ₹600 crore, fiscal deficit ₹200 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹50 crore. Find revenue receipts and capital expenditure.","solution":"Revenue receipts = RE − RD = 600 − 80 = ₹520 crore.\nTE = FD + RR + NDCR = 200 + 520 + 50 = ₹770 crore.\nCapital expenditure = 770 − 600 = ₹170 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q113"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Fiscal deficit is ₹700 crore and primary deficit is ₹460 crore. Find interest payments. If revenue receipts are ₹1,000 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹150 crore, find total expenditure.","solution":"Interest payments = FD − PD = 700 − 460 = ₹240 crore.\nTE = FD + RR + NDCR = 700 + 1,000 + 150 = ₹1,850 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q114"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"The exchange rate changes from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$. A $500 import is bought at each rate. Find the rupee cost before and after, and identify the domestic currency movement.","solution":"Before: 500 × 80 = ₹40,000.\nAfter: 500 × 84 = ₹42,000.\nRupee cost rises by ₹2,000. The rupee depreciates under a flexible system.\nThe ₹/$ quote rises 5%; the rupee’s dollar value falls by (1 − 80/84) × 100 ≈ 4.76%.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q115"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Dollar demand is D = 200 − 2e and supply is S = 20 + e, where e is ₹/$ and quantities are arbitrary units. Find equilibrium. Demand then becomes D = 230 − 2e. Find the new rate and explain the movement.","solution":"Initially: 200 − 2e = 20 + e ⇒ 3e = 180 ⇒ e = ₹60/$; Q = 80.\nAfter shift: 230 − 2e = 20 + e ⇒ 3e = 210 ⇒ e = ₹70/$; Q = 90.\nGreater dollar demand raises ₹/$ and depreciates the rupee.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q116"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A central bank fixes the rate at ₹70/$. At this rate dollar demand is $100 million and supply is $75 million. Find the intervention needed and its reserve effect.","solution":"Excess dollar demand = 100 − 75 = $25 million.\nThe central bank sells $25 million from reserves to meet the shortage and defend the rate.\nIts foreign-exchange reserves decrease by $25 million. This is not a market-clearing rate without intervention.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q117"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Goods exports ₹600 crore, goods imports ₹800 crore, services receipts ₹250 crore, services payments ₹150 crore, factor income receipts ₹40 crore, factor income payments ₹70 crore, transfers received ₹90 crore and transfers paid ₹20 crore. Find trade and current-account balances.","solution":"Trade balance = 600 − 800 = −₹200 crore.\nNet services = 250 − 150 = ₹100 crore.\nNet factor income = 40 − 70 = −₹30 crore.\nNet transfers = 90 − 20 = ₹70 crore.\nCurrent-account balance = −200 + 100 − 30 + 70 = −₹60 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q118"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An economy has a current-account deficit of ₹180 crore and autonomous net capital inflow of ₹130 crore. Find its autonomous BoP balance and reserve change if reserves provide the only financing.","solution":"Autonomous balance = −180 + 130 = −₹50 crore.\nA ₹50 crore deficit must be financed by drawing down official reserves.\nReserve assets decrease by ₹50 crore; the accommodating financing entry closes the accounting balance.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q119"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Exports of goods ₹900 crore, imports ₹850 crore, net services ₹100 crore, net factor income −₹70 crore, net transfers ₹20 crore and autonomous capital-account balance −₹60 crore. Find the current-account and overall autonomous balances.","solution":"Trade balance = 900 − 850 = ₹50 crore.\nCurrent account = 50 + 100 − 70 + 20 = ₹100 crore surplus.\nAutonomous overall balance = 100 − 60 = ₹40 crore surplus.\nIf absorbed solely into reserves, reserve assets rise by ₹40 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q120"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Meaning, origin and principal concerns’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Meaning, origin and principal concerns\nMacroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole. The term Macro comes from Makros, meaning large.\nKeynes’ The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) established macroeconomics as a separate branch. Its principal concerns are employment, inflation, growth of output and depression.\nKeynes stressed government intervention when inadequate demand reduces output and employment.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q121"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Microeconomics versus macroeconomics’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Micro versus macro: seven bases of distinction\nBoth study scarcity and choice, but at different levels. An industry aggregates firms, yet studying one industry is still microeconomics.\n**1 · Concept:** Microeconomics studies individuals, households, firms and industries; macroeconomics studies the economy as a whole.\n**2 · Central Issue:** Micro focuses on allocation of resources: consumers maximise satisfaction and producers maximise profit. Macro focuses on overall output and employment, and policies that maximise growth with stability.\n**3 · Economic Variables:** Micro uses variables such as an individual consumer’s demand and a producer’s supply. Macro uses aggregate demand, aggregate supply, national income, economy-wide consumption, investment and employment.\n**4 · Aggregation of Economic Units:** Micro involves limited aggregation, such as all firms in one industry. Macro involves the wider aggregation of economic units across the whole economy.\n**5 · Economic Agents:** Micro emphasises individual agents such as consumers and producers pursuing personal gains. Macro examines institutional decision makers such as the RBI, government and regulators, whose policies affect growth and stability.\n**6 · Method of Study:** Partial equilibrium studies one market assuming other markets unchanged. General equilibrium studies simultaneous equilibrium and interdependence across markets. The textbook associates the former with micro and the latter with macro.\n**7 · Assumptions of Study:** In the textbook’s simplified comparison, micro takes overall output and employment as given while studying allocation and distribution; macro varies overall output and employment while taking distribution as given. These are analytical assumptions, not a claim that macro can never study inequality.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q122"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Micro–macro paradox and interdependence’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Micro–macro paradox and interdependence\nWhat is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level. Saving may improve one person’s future prosperity; if everyone saves more by spending less, aggregate demand, investment, output and employment may fall.\nMacro affects micro when an RBI interest-rate change affects one producer’s borrowing. Micro affects macro when individual increases in spending together raise aggregate demand.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q123"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Scope and significance’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Scope of macroeconomics: all six areas\nScope means the field of study. The chapter identifies six leading areas; each needs its own heading and explanation.\n**1 · Estimation of National Income and Related Aggregates:** Macroeconomics begins with national income and measures the economy’s overall economic activity. It estimates related aggregates such as Gross Domestic Product (GDP) and Net Domestic Product (NDP), allowing comparison of output and income over time.\n**2 · Theory of Employment:** It studies employment and unemployment in the economy. Keynesian theory explains why inadequate aggregate demand can leave willing workers unemployed, and identifies remedies for restoring output and employment.\n**3 · Theory of Money:** It studies money and the creation of credit by commercial banks. It also examines the central bank’s role—in India, the RBI—in regulating money supply and credit in the economy.\n**4 · Theory of General Price Level:** It explains movements in the general price level, including inflation and deflation. Inflation is a persistent increase in the general price level; deflation is a persistent decrease. A change in one product’s price is different from a change in the economy-wide price level.\n**5 · Government Budget:** It studies how government receipts and expenditure affect the level of economic activity. Through taxation, public spending and borrowing, budgetary policy influences aggregate demand, employment, growth and economic stability.\n**6 · Exchange Rate and Balance of Payments:** It studies the determination and management of the foreign exchange rate. Balance of payments records a country’s economic transactions with the rest of the world, connecting the domestic economy with international trade, income and financial flows.\nSignificance explains why studying these economy-wide relationships is useful. Keep these six headings separate from the six areas of scope.\n**1 · Description of the Economy:** National-income estimates across sectors reveal the nature and level of economic activity. Unemployment data show the extent of the employment problem and help identify remedies. The government budget shows how the state regulates economic activity. Together these provide a systematic picture of the economy’s performance.\n**2 · Roadmap for Business Decisions:** The description of the economy helps businesses decide whether to expand investment and production. For example, rising national income and consumer expenditure suggest stronger demand; firms may invest more, expanding output and employment. Macroeconomic conditions therefore guide individual business decisions.\n**3 · Policy Formulation:** Macroeconomic data help governments design policies and programmes. For example, information about people below the poverty line helps target Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT): money is transferred directly to beneficiaries’ linked bank accounts. The policy should respond to the scale and nature of the problem identified.\n**4 · Global Economic Issues:** It brings international issues into view, including exchange rates and the movement of Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) between countries. These affect trade, financing, productive capacity and the relationship between the domestic economy and the rest of the world.\n**5 · Environmental Pollution and Sustainable Development:** Macroeconomic analysis helps assess environmental costs and formulate a strategy of sustainable development. Sustainable development meets development needs without depleting the resources available to future generations; output growth must therefore be assessed alongside its environmental consequences.\n**6 · Structural Changes and Growth Path of the Economy:** It helps identify changes in the economy’s structure and trace the nature and pace of growth. Changes in occupational structure—for example, workers moving from agriculture towards industry and services—show how development is changing the economy, beyond an increase in total GDP.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q124"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Final goods, intermediate goods and end use’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Final goods, intermediate goods and end use\nFinal goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users. They lead to final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure.\nIntermediate goods are purchased by one firm from another for resale or for use as raw material in producing other goods. Classification depends on end use, not the physical nature of the good: milk bought by a household is final; milk bought by an ice-cream producer is intermediate.\nUnsold additions to inventory count as investment.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q125"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption goods versus capital goods’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Consumption goods versus capital goods\nConsumption goods directly satisfy human wants. They include durable goods, non-durable goods, semi-durable goods and services.\nCapital goods are fixed assets of producers, repeatedly used in production: durable-use producer goods such as plant and machinery. A household car is a durable consumption good; a taxi used to produce transport services is a capital good.\nBoth are final goods. A capital good is not intermediate merely because it helps produce something.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q126"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption expenditure and investment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Consumption expenditure and investment\nConsumption expenditure includes expenditure by households, government and non-profit private institutions. Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital.\nFixed investment adds to fixed assets; inventory investment changes stocks of raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods. New residential construction is investment.\nBuying existing shares transfers financial ownership; it does not itself create new capital goods. Inventory investment can be negative when closing stock is less than opening stock.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q127"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Gross investment, net investment and depreciation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Gross investment, net investment and depreciation\nNet investment refers to the increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year. Gross investment includes replacement of worn-out capital.\nDepreciation, also called consumption of fixed capital, refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use through normal wear and tear, normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence. Unexpected destruction is a capital loss rather than normal depreciation.\nA depreciation reserve fund is kept to cope with depreciation losses.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q128"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Stocks and flows’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Stocks and flows\nStocks are quantities measured at a point of time; flows are quantities measured over a specified period of time. Wealth, money supply, capital and bank balances are stocks.\nIncome, consumption, investment and saving are flows. Capital is a stock; investment is the flow adding to it. “Money supply on 31 March” is a stock even though money changes hands repeatedly.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q129"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Four sectors and circular flow of income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Four sectors and circular flow of income\nThe household sector supplies factor services and consumes goods. The producing sector produces goods and services.\nGovernment acts as a welfare agency and producer; the external sector links the domestic economy with the rest of the world. Real flows are flows of goods and factor services; money flows are payments and receipts in the opposite direction.\nCircular flow of income refers to the circularity of production, income and expenditure across sectors. In a simple two-sector model, production generates income and income generates expenditure.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q130"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘National income, factor incomes and transfer incomes’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"National income, factor incomes and transfer incomes\nNational income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year. Factor incomes are rewards for factor services: compensation of employees, rent, interest and profit.\nTransfer incomes such as gifts, charity and old-age pensions are unilateral receipts without a current productive service in return; they are excluded. An employer’s retirement pension earned through service differs from an old-age welfare pension.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q131"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Normal residents and domestic territory’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Normal residents and domestic territory\nA normal resident ordinarily resides in a country and has a centre of economic interest there. Citizenship alone does not determine residence.\nTourists and visitors for medical treatment do not become residents merely through travel. Diplomats remain residents of their home countries; border workers are residents where they live.\nDomestic territory is economic territory: Indian embassies abroad belong to India’s domestic territory; foreign embassies in India do not. Ships, aircraft, fishing vessels and floating platforms require the domestic-territory tests explained in the special-cases section.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q132"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Domestic income versus national income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Domestic income versus national income\nDomestic income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic territory, whether earned by residents or non-residents. National income belongs to normal residents, wherever earned.\nNet factor income from abroad is factor income received from the rest of the world minus factor income paid to the rest of the world. Remittances that are gifts are transfers, not NFIA.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q133"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘The three conversion bridges’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"The three conversion bridges\nUse three independent adjustments. Gross to net: subtract depreciation.\nDomestic to national: add NFIA. Market price to factor cost: subtract net indirect taxes, which equal indirect taxes minus subsidies.\nReverse the sign when travelling backwards. A negative NFIA reduces national income; negative NIT means subsidies exceed indirect taxes.\nDo not deduct indirect taxes again if NIT is already supplied.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q134"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Eight related aggregates’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Eight related aggregates\nGDPMP, GDPFC, NDPMP and NDPFC are domestic measures; GNPMP, GNPFC, NNPMP and NNPFC are national measures. Gross measures include consumption of fixed capital; net measures exclude it.\nMarket-price measures include net indirect taxes; factor-cost measures exclude them. National income means NNPFC; domestic income means NDPFC.\nIdentify all three labels before substituting a number.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q135"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Nominal GDP, real GDP and GDP deflator’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Nominal GDP, real GDP and GDP deflator\nNominal GDP is valued at current-year prices; real GDP is valued at base-year prices. Real GDP helps isolate changes in physical production from changes in prices.\nNominal GDP may rise with unchanged output simply because prices rise. The GDP deflator is the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP multiplied by 100.\nUse the price index as a percentage index, not as a decimal, in the formula below.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q136"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘GDP and welfare: why output is an incomplete measure’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"GDP and welfare: all four limitations\nReal GDP growth can increase the goods and services available to people. However, the textbook gives four separate reasons why GDP is an incomplete index of welfare.\n**1 · Distribution of Income:** If additional income is concentrated among the rich, rising GDP need not improve living standards for most people. An increasing divide between rich and poor can coexist with high aggregate growth.\n**2 · Composition of GDP:** What is produced matters. More defence goods can contribute indirectly through security, but do not directly increase household consumption. A rise in luxuries for the rich while necessities remain scarce may do little for broad welfare.\n**3 · Non-monetary Exchanges / Transactions:** Barter and payments in kind may go unrecorded or be inadequately measured, understating output and making GDP a weaker welfare measure. Production for own consumption is conceptually included where it can be imputed; absence of cash does not automatically make productive activity non-economic.\n**4 · Externalities:** Externalities are benefits or costs imposed on others without a price or compensation. A neighbour enjoys a beautiful garden without paying: positive externality. Factory smoke or polluted water harms others without adequate compensation: negative externality. GDP does not directly adjust for these unpriced welfare effects.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q137"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Value added method: measure production once’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Value added method: measure production once\nValue added is value of output minus intermediate consumption. Value of output equals sales plus change in stock, with own-account production added where applicable.\nSum GVA at market price across producing units to obtain GDPMP. Deduct depreciation and NIT, then add NFIA for national income.\nAlternatively, sum sectoral NVAFC and add NFIA.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q138"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Double counting and value-added precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Value-added method: all inclusions and exclusions\nCount value created during the current year once. Both the inclusion decision and the reason are important in theory and classification questions.\n**Include: Commission on Second-hand Goods:** The old good itself was counted when produced, but the broker’s current service is new production and must be counted.\n**Include: Own-account Production:** Goods made by a producing unit for its own use are included. A car producer using a newly produced car to transport employees still creates current output.\n**Include: Goods for Self-consumption:** Impute the value of goods such as wheat produced and consumed by a farming family. Not being sold does not mean that the good was not produced.\n**Include: Imputed Rent:** An owner-occupied house provides housing services just as a rented house does. Its imputed rent is included.\n**Exclude: Resale of Second-hand Goods:** Reselling an existing asset creates no new output of that asset in the current year. Include only any current service involved in the sale.\n**Exclude: Intermediate Goods as a Separate Addition:** Their value is already embodied in final output. Either count final goods or sum value added at each stage, never add both.\n**Exclude: Own-household Domestic Services:** Unpaid domestic services within one’s own household are excluded under the textbook’s measurement convention. Paid domestic services are different.\n**Illegal Activities: Textbook Treatment:** The chapter excludes these from its measured estimates because reliable recorded data are unavailable. Use this stated treatment for its classification questions.\n**Double Counting Example:** Wheat ₹100 → flour ₹160 → bread ₹250. Values added are ₹100, ₹60 and ₹90, totalling ₹250. Adding sales of all three stages gives ₹510 and counts the same intermediate value repeatedly.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q139"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Income method: compensation, surplus and mixed income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Income method: compensation, surplus and mixed income\nIncome method measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes generated within domestic territory. Compensation of employees includes wages and salaries in cash and kind and employers’ social-security contributions.\nOperating surplus comprises rent and royalty, interest and profit. Profit includes corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings; these are components, so do not add them again if total profit is given.\nMixed income of the self-employed combines labour and property income that cannot be separated.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q140"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Income-method precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Income method: all inclusions and exclusions\nInclude income earned from current productive services. Distinguish a productive reward from a transfer, an asset transaction or a capital gain.\n**Include: Retirement Pension:** An employment-related retirement pension is treated as compensation for past service in the chapter. It differs from a welfare old-age pension, which is a transfer.\n**Include: Current Commissions and Brokerage:** Commission on second-hand goods and brokerage on shares or bonds are rewards for current services. The sale price of the old asset or financial security itself is not factor income.\n**Include: Imputed Rent and Self-consumption:** Owner-occupied housing services and goods produced for own consumption generate current income even when no cash sale occurs.\n**Include: Productive Interest:** Interest paid by producing units on capital borrowed for production is factor income. Interest on consumption loans is excluded in the textbook treatment.\n**Exclude: Transfer Earnings:** Welfare pensions, unemployment allowances, scholarships and pocket money have no corresponding productive service and are not national income.\n**Exclude: Illegal Income, Windfall and Capital Gains:** The chapter excludes unrecorded illegal income; lottery winnings and gains from asset-price changes are not rewards for current production.\n**Exclude: Spending from Accumulated Savings:** The transfer or use of earlier savings does not itself create current factor income. Count any current goods or services purchased through the relevant production accounts instead.\n**Exclude: Indirect Taxes from Factor-cost Income:** Indirect taxes are part of market prices but not rewards paid to factors. Deduct net indirect taxes when converting market-price output to factor-cost income.\n**Avoid Repeating Profit Components:** Corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings together form profit. If total profit is supplied, do not add those components again. Likewise, do not add rent or interest again if operating surplus already includes them.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q141"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Expenditure method and its precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Expenditure method and its precautions\nExpenditure method measures final expenditure on domestically produced output. Add private final consumption expenditure, government final consumption expenditure, gross domestic capital formation and net exports.\nGross domestic capital formation includes fixed capital formation and change in stocks. Exclude financial-asset purchases, transfers and second-hand purchases; include current brokerage.\nSubtract imports because imported content may already be included in consumption or investment. Do not add change in stock twice if gross domestic capital formation already includes it.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q142"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Reconciliation and treatment of tricky items’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Reconciliation and treatment of tricky items\nThe three methods measure the same current production from different sides: value added creates factor income, and income finances final expenditure. In an exam, classify each item before calculating.\nGovernment services are counted even when provided free; employer provident-fund contributions belong in compensation. Scholarships and welfare pensions are transfers.\nForeign factor earnings require a residence/territory check; the purchase of a newly produced house is investment. Use the territory and normal-resident tests to classify domestic and national factor income.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q143"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Money, barter and the evolution of money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Money, barter and the evolution of money\nMoney is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange. Barter exchanges goods directly for goods.\nIts difficulties include lack of double coincidence of wants, lack of a common measure of value, difficulty storing wealth and difficulty making deferred payments. Money separates selling from buying: the seller need not want the precise good the buyer offers.\nEvolution moves through commodity money, metallic money, paper money and bank/credit money.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q144"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fiat, fiduciary and legal-tender money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Fiat, fiduciary and legal-tender money\nFiat money is money issued by authority of the government. Fiduciary money is accepted on trust between payer and payee.\nLegal tender must be accepted in discharge of debts within the applicable legal conditions; a cheque depends on acceptance and is not legal tender. Full-bodied money has face value equal to intrinsic commodity value; credit money has face value greater than intrinsic value.\nFollow the textbook distinctions, not the everyday use of the word “cash”.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q145"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Primary and secondary functions of money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Functions of money and why each matters\nMoney has two primary functions and three secondary functions. Explain the function, illustrate it, and connect it to the economic benefit.\n**Primary 1 · Medium of Exchange:** Money is accepted between sale and purchase, removing the need for a double coincidence of wants. A farmer can sell wheat for money and later buy clothing from someone else. This expands exchange and markets, encourages production for sale rather than only self-consumption, and supports GDP growth.\n**Primary 2 · Measure of Value / Unit of Account:** Prices express different goods, services and assets in a common unit. This permits value comparisons, national-income estimation and price-index construction. A common unit also makes asset markets and investment decisions easier.\n**Secondary 1 · Standard of Deferred Payments:** Future debts, principal and interest can be expressed and settled in money. Repaying a monetary amount is simpler than returning goods of exactly the original quality. This supports financial markets, lending, consumption and investment.\n**Secondary 2 · Store of Value:** Money carries purchasing power into the future without the physical deterioration associated with many stored goods. Convenient saving can finance future investment; inflation nevertheless erodes real purchasing power. Saving supports growth when channelled into productive investment, while a widespread cut in spending can weaken demand.\n**Secondary 3 · Transfer of Value:** Money allows purchasing power to move between people, regions and countries. This supports capital mobility, global financial markets, multinational enterprises and foreign investment, and can help finance development in capital-scarce regions.\n**Static versus Dynamic Functions:** Static functions are these conventional exchange, value and payment functions. Dynamic functions concern money’s role in economic stability and growth: regulating its supply can restrain inflation or support demand during slowdown.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q146"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Money supply and its components’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Money supply and its components\nMoney supply is the stock of money held by the public at a particular point of time. Interbank deposits and money held by the government/banking system are excluded from money held by the public.\nM1 comprises currency with the public, demand deposits with commercial banks and other deposits with the RBI. Demand deposits are withdrawable on demand.\nA cheque transfers a deposit; it is not an additional independent stock of money.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q147"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: broader measures and liquidity’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"M2, M3, M4 and liquidity\nBroader money measures add less immediately spendable components to the most liquid measure, M1. These are stock measures at a point in time.\n**M1 · The Starting Point:** Currency with the public + demand deposits with commercial banks + other eligible deposits with the RBI. Government and interbank holdings are excluded from money held by the public.\n**M2:** M1 + deposits with Post Office Savings Bank accounts.\n**M3 · Aggregate Monetary Resources:** M1 + net time deposits with commercial banks. The chapter calls M3 aggregate monetary resources.\n**M4:** M3 + total deposits with post offices, excluding National Savings Certificates.\n**Narrow versus Broad Money:** The chapter groups M1/M2 as narrow-money concepts and M3/M4 as broad-money concepts. Specify which measure is being used before comparing money supply.\n**Liquidity:** Liquidity means ease and speed of conversion into cash. Currency is the most liquid asset; demand deposits are readily usable through payment instructions. Time deposits are less immediately accessible, so broader measures include less-liquid components.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q148"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Commercial banks and their functions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Commercial banks and their functions\nCommercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit. Deposits include current, savings and fixed deposits.\nPrimary functions are accepting deposits and lending; other functions include agency services and general-utility services. Banks mobilise saving and create demand deposits through lending.\nA central bank is the apex institution regulating the monetary and financial system; its objective is growth with stability rather than commercial profit.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q149"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Credit creation: follow every round’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Credit creation: follow every round\nSuppose an initial cash deposit of 1,000 enters the banking system and the reserve ratio is 20%. The first bank keeps 200 and lends 800.\nIf 800 is redeposited, the next bank keeps 160 and lends 640. Repeated lending and redepositing expand total deposits to 5,000.\nThese results assume no cash leakage, no excess reserves and loans redeposited in the banking system. Total deposits include the initial deposit; additional credit excludes it.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q150"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Central bank: seven functions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Central bank: all seven functions explained\nThe central bank is the apex institution controlling and regulating the banking and monetary system. Its objective is growth with stability rather than the commercial bank’s private profit.\n**1 · Issuer of Currency:** It has the principal monopoly of note issue, subject to the government’s ₹1-note/coin exception described in Money. Centralised issue creates uniformity, public confidence, a central check on supply and flexibility to respond to inflation or deflation. The earnings from issue accrue to the public authority.\n**2 · Banker to the Government:** As banker, it maintains accounts, accepts deposits, makes payments and transfers funds. As agent, it manages the issue, purchase, sale and redemption of government securities and debt. As financial adviser, it advises on monetary and financial conditions, stability and borrowing.\n**3 · Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role:** It holds commercial banks’ reserve deposits and provides financial accommodation. It supervises compliance with directives and reserve requirements such as CRR and SLR, thereby influencing credit creation.\n**4 · Lender of Last Resort:** A bank unable to obtain emergency finance elsewhere can approach the central bank against eligible security. This protects confidence, helps prevent a liquidity problem spreading and supports monetary-system stability.\n**5 · Custodian of Foreign Exchange:** It holds and manages official foreign-exchange reserves and may buy or sell foreign currency to moderate exchange-rate instability under managed floating.\n**6 · Clearing House Function:** Banks settle mutual claims through accounts with the central bank. For example, offsetting cheques between two banks allows only the net amount to be settled, avoiding unnecessary cash transfers and reducing cash requirements.\n**7 · Control of Credit:** It regulates the cost and availability of bank credit. In the textbook framework, tighter credit restrains inflationary demand and easier credit supports activity during deficient demand. Quantitative tools affect overall credit; qualitative tools target particular uses or sectors.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q151"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Quantitative instruments: rates and open market operations’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Quantitative credit controls: rates and open-market operations\nQuantitative controls influence overall credit rather than one selected activity. For each instrument, explain the action, the effect on bank funds or borrowing cost, and the resulting effect on spending.\n**Bank Rate:** The textbook describes this as the rate for central-bank lending to commercial banks, distinguished from secured short-term repo transactions. Raising it tends to raise borrowing costs and restrain credit; lowering it tends to encourage credit.\n**Open Market Operations:** The central bank buys or sells government securities in the open market. Purchases inject funds and support bank lending; sales absorb funds and reduce lending capacity. Remember the action from the central bank’s viewpoint: buying adds liquidity, selling withdraws it.\n**Repo Rate:** Repo is lending against securities under an agreement to reverse the transaction at a specified date and price. A higher repo rate makes central-bank funds costlier and can raise bank lending rates, reducing demand for credit. Lowering it works in the opposite direction.\n**Reverse Repo Rate:** This is the rate associated with banks placing funds with the central bank through reverse-repurchase arrangements. A higher return can encourage banks to park more funds rather than lend; lowering it can encourage lending. The effect depends on banks’ alternative uses for funds.\n**Cost versus Availability:** Rates mainly influence the cost and attractiveness of funds. Open-market operations and reserve requirements influence available liquidity. Both channels ultimately affect credit, investment, consumption and aggregate demand.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q152"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Reserve ratios and qualitative instruments’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"CRR, SLR and all three selective controls\nReserve requirements influence lending capacity; selective controls influence which uses receive credit. State both the direction of the policy and its mechanism.\n**Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR):** The prescribed share of bank deposit liabilities held as cash reserves with the RBI. Raising CRR leaves a smaller share available to support lending and reduces the simple credit multiplier; lowering it expands potential credit creation.\n**Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR):** The prescribed share of relevant liabilities held by the bank in eligible liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved unencumbered securities. A higher SLR restrains funds available for other lending; a lower SLR can release funds. CRR is held with the RBI; SLR assets are maintained by the bank.\n**1 · Margin Requirement:** Margin is collateral value minus the loan granted. With collateral worth ₹1 crore and a loan of ₹80 lakh, the margin is ₹20 lakh or 20%. Raising the margin reduces the maximum loan for given collateral, particularly restraining speculative borrowing; lowering it permits more credit.\n**2 · Rationing of Credit:** Fix quotas or limits on credit for selected business activities. Tighter quotas restrict lending to those uses; relaxation increases availability. This is selective credit control.\n**3 · Moral Suasion:** The central bank uses advice, persuasion and pressure to obtain banks’ cooperation. It may urge restraint during inflation and more liberal lending during deficient demand. The chapter notes that it can affect both overall and selected credit, though usually classified as qualitative.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q153"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: when monetary tools succeed’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"When monetary tools work: all success conditions\nChanging a policy instrument does not guarantee a proportionate change in lending or spending. The chapter separates four conditions for repo-rate policy and two for open-market operations.\n**Repo 1 · Dependence on the Central Bank:** Banks with ample funds of their own depend less on central-bank loans, so a change in the repo rate may have a weaker influence.\n**Repo 2 · Sensitivity of Demand for Funds:** Banks may not respond strongly to small rate changes when business expectations or credit demand are weak. A cheaper source of funds does not by itself create willing borrowers.\n**Repo 3 · Structure of Interest Rates:** If non-bank financial institutions do not adjust their rates correspondingly, borrowers can switch sources and weaken policy transmission.\n**Repo 4 · Overall Supply of Funds:** When non-bank sources are popular and readily available, regulating the price of bank funds has less influence on total financing.\n**OMO 1 · Existence of a Securities Market:** Open-market purchases and sales require a well-organised, functioning market for government securities. Without willing counterparties, operations have limited reach.\n**OMO 2 · Excess Reserves with Banks:** Large excess reserves can weaken the intended relationship between liquidity withdrawal and lending. Banks can absorb a drain without proportionately reducing loans, while injections may remain idle when demand for credit is low.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q154"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Aggregate demand and aggregate supply’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Aggregate demand and aggregate supply\nAggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure. In a two-sector economy AD = C + I; a closed economy with government adds G; an open economy adds X − M.\nAggregate supply refers to production planned by producers. In the textbook’s short-run model with constant prices and spare capacity, AS is identified with income/output and represented by the 45° line.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q155"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption function: autonomous and induced consumption’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Consumption function: autonomous and induced consumption\nConsumption function is the functional relationship between consumption and income. Autonomous consumption is consumption when income is zero; induced consumption is the part that varies with income.\nIn C = C̄ + bY, the vertical intercept is C̄ and the slope b is MPC. Consumption rises with income, but normally by less than the rise in income.\nA change in income moves along a given function; a change in autonomous consumption shifts it.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q156"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘APC and MPC: totals versus increments’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"APC and MPC: totals versus increments\nAverage propensity to consume is total consumption divided by total income; marginal propensity to consume is additional consumption divided by additional income. APC may exceed one when consumption exceeds income.\nMPC is the slope of the consumption curve; APC is not. With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC falls as income rises.\nAt zero income APC is undefined.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q157"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Saving function and the break-even point’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Saving function and the break-even point\nSaving is income not consumed. Derive S by subtracting the entire consumption function from Y.\nThe saving function has a negative intercept equal to autonomous consumption and a slope equal to MPS. At break-even income C = Y and S = 0: APC is one and APS is zero.\nBelow it saving is negative; above it saving is positive. Break-even is not necessarily equilibrium, because equilibrium also depends on investment.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q158"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘APS, MPS and the two identities’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"APS, MPS and the two identities\nAverage propensity to save is S/Y; marginal propensity to save is ΔS/ΔY. APS can be negative when households dissave.\nMPS is the slope of the saving function. Since income is split between consumption and saving, APC + APS = 1 and MPC + MPS = 1.\nDo not confuse ΔS (change in saving) with AS (aggregate supply): similar letters represent different concepts.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q159"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Investment and deriving one function from the other’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Investment and deriving one function from the other\nIn the simple model autonomous investment is independent of income, so its curve is horizontal. Induced investment responds to changes in income or activity.\nThe expected profitability of investment and rate of interest influence investment decisions. To obtain C from S, use C = Y − S; to obtain S from C, use S = Y − C.\nThe two slopes add to one and their vertical intercepts have equal magnitudes with opposite signs.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q160"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Short run and equilibrium output’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Short run and equilibrium output\nThe textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output. Equilibrium output is the level where intended production equals intended expenditure.\nProducers have no unplanned change in inventories and no incentive to change output. In the two-sector economy Y = C + I and Y = C + S, so the same equilibrium condition can be written S = I.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q161"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘How inventories restore equilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"How inventories restore equilibrium\nWhen AD is below AS, goods remain unsold and actual inventories exceed desired inventories. Producers cut output and employment until planned saving equals planned investment.\nWhen AD exceeds AS, inventories fall unexpectedly and producers expand output, provided spare capacity exists. The direction is easy to reverse by mistake: excess supply causes inventory accumulation; excess demand causes inventory depletion.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q162"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ex-ante versus ex-post saving and investment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Ex-ante versus ex-post saving and investment\nEx-ante means planned; ex-post means realised. Planned saving and planned investment need not be equal away from equilibrium.\nActual saving and actual investment are equal by accounting identity because realised investment includes unintended inventory changes. Equilibrium requires equality of planned S and planned I, not merely the ex-post identity.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q163"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Effective demand and underemployment equilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Effective demand and underemployment equilibrium\nEffective demand is aggregate demand at the equilibrium level of income/output. Equilibrium can occur below full employment: firms’ production plans can match spending plans while workers and productive capacity remain idle. An increase in autonomous expenditure shifts AD upward and raises equilibrium output in the model with spare capacity; a fall lowers it.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q164"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Investment multiplier: size and derivation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Investment multiplier: size and derivation\nInvestment multiplier is the ratio of change in income to change in investment. Starting with ΔY = ΔC + ΔI and ΔC = MPC × ΔY gives ΔY(1 − MPC) = ΔI.\nHigher MPC means more income is respent, so the multiplier is larger. Higher MPS means more income leaks into saving, so the multiplier is smaller.\nWith MPC zero, k = 1; as MPC approaches one, k becomes very large.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q165"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Multiplier mechanism, assumptions and reverse operation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Multiplier mechanism, assumptions and reverse operation\nAn investment injection is someone’s income. They consume a fraction, which becomes someone else’s income; successive rounds generate ΔI, MPC × ΔI, MPC² × ΔI and so on.\nSaving reduces each next round. The simple multiplier assumes constant MPC, available spare capacity, constant prices, and no additional leakages such as imports or taxes.\nA fall in investment works in reverse, producing a multiplied fall in income. The final increase in saving equals the initial increase in investment.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q166"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Full employment and unemployment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Full employment and unemployment\nFull employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work. Voluntary unemployment reflects unwillingness to work at the existing wage; involuntary unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs.\nThe textbook also distinguishes natural unemployment: frictional unemployment during job transitions and structural unemployment caused by changing industries, skills or production techniques. Underemployment equilibrium has AD = AS with unutilised capacity.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q167"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Deficient demand and deflationary gap’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Deficient demand and deflationary gap\nDeficient demand occurs when aggregate demand is less than the aggregate supply corresponding to full employment. The deflationary gap is the shortfall of AD from the level required to maintain full employment; measure it vertically at full-employment output.\nIt reduces output and employment and creates involuntary unemployment. The gap in expenditure is not the same as the shortfall in equilibrium income: the multiplier links the two.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q168"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Excess demand and inflationary gap’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Excess demand and inflationary gap\nExcess demand occurs when AD exceeds the demand required to sustain full employment. The inflationary gap is the excess of AD over full-employment AS, measured at Yf.\nOnce resources are fully employed, real output cannot expand in the simple model; excess spending raises the general price level. A calculated equilibrium above Yf is a signal of inflationary pressure, not evidence that the economy can actually produce beyond its capacity.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q169"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fiscal measures: expenditure, taxation and borrowing’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Fiscal measures: expenditure, taxation and borrowing\nTo correct deficient demand, raise government expenditure, reduce taxes, reduce public borrowing and increase borrowing from the central bank, following the textbook framework. These measures raise spending power or public expenditure.\nFor excess demand, reverse the direction. Explain the chain: lower taxes → higher disposable income → more consumption → higher AD.\nBorrowing from the public can withdraw purchasing power; borrowing from the central bank can expand it.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q170"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Monetary measures: cost and availability of credit’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Monetary measures: cost and availability of credit\nFor deficient demand, lower bank/repo/reverse-repo rates, lower CRR and SLR, buy securities, lower margins, relax credit rationing and encourage lending. For excess demand, raise rates and reserve requirements, sell securities, raise margins and restrict lending.\nMonetary policy is the central bank’s policy; fiscal policy is the government’s revenue and expenditure policy. Always connect the instrument to credit and then to aggregate demand.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q171"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Government budget and its objectives’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Government budget and its objectives\nGovernment budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy. Objectives include GDP growth, allocation of resources, redistribution of income and wealth, economic stability, management of public enterprises and employment generation.\nPublic infrastructure can encourage private investment; progressive taxation and social spending can reduce inequality. Fiscal discipline means managing public finances responsibly to avoid unsustainable deficits and debt.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q172"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue receipts versus capital receipts’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Revenue receipts versus capital receipts\nRevenue receipts neither create a liability nor reduce assets. They include tax revenue and non-tax revenue such as fees, fines, interest, profits/dividends and grants.\nCapital receipts create a liability or reduce assets: borrowing creates liabilities, while loan recovery and disinvestment reduce assets. Do not classify a receipt by size or regularity alone; use the liability/asset test.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q173"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Direct taxes, indirect taxes and non-tax revenue’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Direct taxes, indirect taxes and non-tax revenue\nDirect taxes have impact and incidence on the same person, such as income tax and corporation tax. Indirect taxes allow the burden to be shifted, such as GST.\nA fee is a payment for a specific government service; a fine penalises an offence. A tax is compulsory and does not promise a direct equivalent service.\nProgressive taxes take a larger proportion as income rises; proportional taxes keep the same proportion; regressive burdens fall relatively more heavily on lower incomes.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q174"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue expenditure versus capital expenditure’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Revenue expenditure versus capital expenditure\nRevenue expenditure neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities. Salaries, pensions, subsidies and interest payments are examples.\nCapital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities: construction of a highway, purchase of equipment, lending by government and repayment of loan principal. Repayment of principal is capital expenditure; interest is revenue expenditure.\nGrants to states remain revenue expenditure for the granting government even if the recipient uses them to build assets, following the textbook convention.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q175"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit\nRevenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts. Fiscal deficit is total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings; it measures borrowing requirements.\nPrimary deficit deducts interest payments from fiscal deficit and indicates the current borrowing requirement excluding past-debt interest. A zero revenue deficit does not guarantee zero fiscal deficit.\nA zero primary deficit means borrowing covers interest payments.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q176"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Implications, balanced and unbalanced budgets’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Implications, balanced and unbalanced budgets\nA revenue deficit indicates government dissaving: borrowing may finance current consumption rather than asset creation. Fiscal deficits may raise debt and interest burdens and crowd out private borrowing; financing expenditure can also support demand and productive capacity, depending on its use.\nA balanced budget has receipts equal to expenditure; a surplus budget has receipts above expenditure and can restrain inflation; a deficit budget can support demand in recession. Consider both the size and composition of expenditure.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q177"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: public, private, common and club goods’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Ability Zone: public, private, common and club goods\nPublic goods are non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence. Private goods are rival and excludable.\nCommon goods are rival but non-excludable, such as fish in an open pond. Club goods are excludable but non-rival until congestion, such as a subscription service.\nRivalry means one person’s use reduces availability to others; excludability means users can be denied access. Public expenditure supports infrastructure, redistribution, stability and an investment-friendly environment.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q178"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Foreign exchange and the quotation convention’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Foreign exchange and the quotation convention\nForeign exchange refers to foreign currency. Foreign exchange rate is the price paid in domestic currency for a unit of foreign currency.\nThroughout these lessons, quote the rate as ₹ per US dollar. A rise from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ means the rupee buys fewer dollars and has depreciated; a fall to ₹76/$ means rupee appreciation.\nAlways identify which currency is in the numerator before describing a movement.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q179"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Demand, supply and flexible exchange-rate determination’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Demand, supply and flexible exchange-rate determination\nFlexible exchange rate is determined by demand and supply in the foreign exchange market. Demand for foreign exchange comes from imports, overseas investment, foreign travel, loan repayment, income payments, grants and speculation.\nSupply comes from exports, foreign investment, inward loans, tourism receipts, income receipts, grants and remittances. With ₹/$ on the vertical axis, demand slopes downward and supply upward; their intersection is the equilibrium rate.\nA higher exchange rate makes foreign goods costlier in rupees and normally reduces import-related foreign-exchange demand.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q180"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Movements versus shifts, appreciation versus depreciation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Movements versus shifts, appreciation versus depreciation\nA change in the exchange rate causes movement along a given demand or supply curve. A change in imports, investment preferences, income or foreign demand shifts a curve.\nGreater demand for dollars shifts demand right, raising ₹/$ and depreciating the rupee. Greater supply of dollars shifts supply right, lowering ₹/$ and appreciating the rupee.\nDepreciation can make exports cheaper for foreigners and imports costlier for residents; actual quantity responses depend on demand conditions.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q181"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fixed rate, devaluation and revaluation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Fixed rate, devaluation and revaluation\nFixed exchange rate is set and maintained by the monetary authority. An official rise in ₹/$ is devaluation of the rupee; an official fall is revaluation.\nDepreciation/appreciation describe market-driven changes under a flexible system. To defend a fixed rate when demand for dollars exceeds supply, the authority sells foreign currency from reserves; when dollar supply exceeds demand it buys foreign currency.\nAn overvalued domestic currency tends to create excess demand for foreign currency at the official rate.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q182"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Managed floating and merits/demerits’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Managed floating and merits/demerits\nManaged floating combines market determination with occasional central-bank intervention to limit undesirable movements; the textbook also calls it dirty floating. Fixed rates reduce exchange uncertainty and can support trade but require reserves and may misallocate resources.\nFlexible rates need less reserve defence and adjust through markets but create uncertainty and exposure to external shocks. The four appraisal sections below explain all five merits and five demerits of each system.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q183"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Foreign exchange market: transfer, credit and hedging’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Foreign exchange market: transfer, credit and hedging\nThe foreign exchange market is the market for national currencies. The transfer function moves purchasing power between countries.\nThe credit function supports trade through financing arrangements. Hedging reduces exchange-rate risk.\nSpot transactions exchange currency for current delivery; forward transactions agree today on a rate for delivery at a future date. A forward contract fixes an exchange rate; it does not guarantee that the future spot rate will equal that rate.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q184"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Balance of payments: account and recording rule’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Balance of payments: account and recording rule\nBalance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year. It records goods, services, income, transfers and asset-related transactions between residents and non-residents.\nForeign-exchange receipts are credit items; payments are debit items. The textbook uses current, capital and official-reserves accounts.\nKeep its school-level account terminology when answering.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q185"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Current account, trade balance and invisibles’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Current account, trade balance and invisibles\nCurrent account records exports and imports of goods and services, factor income and current transfers. Goods are visible trade or merchandise; services, factor incomes and transfers are invisibles.\nBalance of trade covers goods only. A gift from abroad is a current transfer; wages for a service are factor income.\nThe supplied purple box treats direct tourist purchases as invisible trade: preserve that convention for this textbook.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q186"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Capital account and asset/liability changes’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Capital account and asset/liability changes\nCapital account records transactions that change a country’s assets or liabilities in relation to the rest of the world. The textbook includes borrowing, foreign investment, NRI deposits, banking capital and short-term debt.\nInward FDI or an external loan is a credit; an outward investment or repayment of foreign loan principal is a debit. Interest on a foreign loan belongs in the current account; the principal belongs in the capital account.\nFDI involves a lasting productive interest/control; portfolio investment purchases financial securities.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q187"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Official reserves, deficit and surplus’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Official reserves, deficit and surplus\nIf autonomous foreign-exchange payments exceed autonomous receipts, there is a BoP deficit, financed by accommodating transactions such as a drawdown of reserves or official borrowing. A surplus can increase official reserves.\nA decrease in reserve assets finances a shortfall; an increase uses foreign exchange. Follow the sign convention explicitly stated in a numerical instead of confusing a positive stock increase with a credit entry.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q188"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Autonomous versus accommodating transactions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Autonomous versus accommodating transactions\nAutonomous transactions are undertaken for independent economic motives such as profit, rather than to correct a BoP imbalance; they are above-the-line items. Accommodating transactions are undertaken to finance or correct the imbalance and are below the line.\nA foreign loan may be autonomous when raised commercially or accommodating when arranged specifically to finance a deficit. Classification depends on purpose, not merely the type of transaction.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q189"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Accounting balance versus operational balance’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Accounting balance versus operational balance\nBoP balances in the accounting sense because every transaction has a counterpart and financing entries close the accounts. This does not mean autonomous receipts equal autonomous payments or that the economy has no external problem.\nA current-account deficit financed by foreign borrowing is consistent with accounting balance and increasing indebtedness. For example, a current-account deficit of 100 can be financed by capital inflows of 100; accounting balance coexists with the current-account shortfall.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q190"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Causes and significance of disequilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"BoP disequilibrium: all economic, political and social causes\nDisequilibrium can result from changes in trade or capital flows. A factor can improve or worsen the position depending on the direction of the change.\n**Economic 1 · Development Expenditure:** Large development programmes can require substantial imports of equipment and inputs, increasing foreign payments and potentially creating a deficit.\n**Economic 2 · Business Cycles:** Depression abroad may reduce demand for exports; expansion abroad can raise it. The effect depends on the countries affected and the response of imports and other flows.\n**Economic 3 · Domestic Inflation:** Higher domestic prices can make imports relatively attractive and exports less competitive, worsening the external balance.\n**Economic 4 · Import Substitutes:** Domestic alternatives reduce import requirements and can improve the balance, provided their production is viable.\n**Economic 5 · Changing Cost Structure:** Technological or managerial improvements alter relative competitiveness. Favourable domestic cost changes can increase exports; improvements by trading partners can have the opposite effect.\n**Political 1 · Instability:** Uncertainty can discourage direct and portfolio investment, reducing capital inflows or causing withdrawals.\n**Political 2 · Government Policy:** Large cuts in import duties can encourage imports and widen a current-account deficit, other things equal. Other policy changes can alter trade and investment incentives.\n**Social 1 · Tastes and Preferences:** Changing consumer preferences can raise exports when foreign demand favours domestic products, or raise imports when residents prefer foreign goods.\n**Social 2 · Cross-border Prejudices:** Political or social hostility can divert trade towards costlier import sources or less profitable export markets, weakening the external position.\nBoP data reveal the economy’s external position and inform decisions. A single flow should be interpreted with its purpose and the wider account.\n**1 · Financial Status of the Domestic Economy:** The accounts show external receipts, payments and financing needs. Heavy reliance on borrowing can indicate vulnerability or dependence, though borrowing may also finance productive investment.\n**2 · Net Factor Income from Abroad:** Factor-income receipts and payments provide information needed for NFIA, the bridge from domestic income to national income.\n**3 · Net Exports as a Component of AD:** Exports minus imports form part of aggregate demand. A rise in X − M increases demand for domestic output and can raise output and employment where spare capacity exists.\n**4 · Market Potential:** Foreign investment flows can provide evidence about investors’ expectations of market opportunities and returns. Their size is one indicator, not a complete measure, of market potential.\n**5 · Monetary and Fiscal Policies:** External inflows and outflows influence currency demand, reserves and financial conditions, which the RBI considers in monetary policy. Investment flows and the tax environment can also inform fiscal-policy decisions.","tag":"Full-topic review","id":"q191"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“An increase in GDP necessarily raises welfare.” Defend or refute with four textbook points.","solution":"Refute.\n1. Distribution of income: growth concentrated among a few may not improve most people’s welfare.\n2. Composition of GDP: output may rise through goods that do not improve ordinary consumption.\n3. Non-monetary transactions: unpaid household services are omitted.\n4. Externalities: pollution costs and unpaid external benefits are not fully reflected in GDP.\nUse “limitations of GDP as an index of welfare” as the answer heading.","tag":"Theory","id":"q192"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"State six precautions when estimating national income by the expenditure method.","solution":"Precautions regarding Expenditure Method\n1. Count final expenditure only; exclude intermediate expenditure.\n2. Exclude purchases of second-hand goods; include current brokerage.\n3. Exclude financial-asset purchases such as existing shares and bonds.\n4. Exclude transfer payments; the recipient’s later purchase of output is separate.\n5. Include own-account investment and imputed services where measured.\n6. Subtract imports and include exports to isolate domestic production.\nAvoid adding change in stock twice if GDCF already includes it.","tag":"Theory","id":"q193"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain how an increase in CRR can control excess demand.","solution":"Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)\n1. CRR is the required percentage of bank deposits kept with the RBI.\n2. Higher CRR means more required reserves against the same deposits.\n3. Banks’ capacity to extend credit and create deposits falls.\n4. Lower credit availability restrains consumption and investment expenditure, reducing AD and inflationary pressure.","tag":"Theory","id":"q194"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“Ex-post saving equals ex-post investment, so every economy is always in equilibrium.” Is the conclusion valid?","solution":"Refute the conclusion.\nEx-post Saving and Investment: realised saving equals realised investment because actual investment includes unplanned inventory changes.\nEx-ante Saving and Investment: equilibrium requires equality of planned saving and planned investment.\nIf output exceeds planned expenditure, inventories accumulate unexpectedly; firms reduce production. The ex-post identity holds even while firms are adjusting away from disequilibrium.","tag":"Theory","id":"q195"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Distinguish deficient demand and excess demand under the headings meaning, gap, effects and correction.","solution":"1. Meaning: deficient demand is AD below the full-employment requirement; excess demand is AD above it.\n2. Gap: deficient demand creates a deflationary gap; excess demand creates an inflationary gap, each measured at full-employment output.\n3. Effects: deficient demand lowers output and employment; excess demand raises prices when output is at capacity.\n4. Correction: expand public spending and ease credit for deficient demand; restrain public spending and tighten credit for excess demand.","tag":"Theory","id":"q196"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Distinguish revenue receipts and capital receipts, giving two examples of each.","solution":"Revenue Receipts\nNeither create a liability nor reduce assets. Examples: income tax and government fees.\nCapital Receipts\nCreate a liability or reduce assets. Examples: borrowing creates a liability; recovery of government loans reduces a financial asset.\nThe distinction rests on the asset/liability effect, not the amount or frequency of the receipt.","tag":"Theory","id":"q197"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"An increase in foreign investment enters an economy with a flexible exchange rate. Explain the foreign-exchange market effect using ₹ per dollar as the quotation.","solution":"Supply of Foreign Exchange\nForeign investment brings an inflow of foreign currency, shifting dollar supply to the right.\nDetermination of Flexible Exchange Rate\nWith dollar demand unchanged, the equilibrium ₹/$ rate falls and the equilibrium quantity of dollars increases.\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\nFewer rupees are required for one dollar; the rupee appreciates. Draw ₹/$ vertically, dollars horizontally, a downward D curve and S shifting right.","tag":"Case-based","id":"q198"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A country finances a current-account deficit through overseas borrowing. Explain why BoP accounting balance does not prove a sound external position.","solution":"Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense\n1. The loan produces a capital-account inflow that can offset the current-account deficit.\n2. Double-entry recording and financing entries make the accounts balance.\n3. The borrowing increases external liabilities and future interest/principal obligations.\n4. Accounting balance does not establish equality of autonomous receipts and payments or absence of external vulnerability.","tag":"Case-based","id":"q199"},{"id":"q200","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Types of consumption goods using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Types of consumption goods\nDurable-use consumer goods serve consumers repeatedly over a long period, such as cars and refrigerators. Semi-durable goods serve over a shorter period, such as clothes.\nNon-durable goods are used up in a single act of consumption, such as food. Services are intangible acts satisfying wants, such as teaching or medical treatment.\nDurability alone does not make an item a capital good: capital goods are used by producers in production.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q201","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Why final goods are counted and intermediate goods are not using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Why final goods are counted and intermediate goods are not\nIntermediate inputs are embodied in final output. Counting both adds the same value more than once.\nIf cloth worth ₹5,000 becomes shirts worth ₹7,000, final output is ₹7,000, not ₹12,000. Under the value-added method, the cloth producer’s value and the shirt producer’s additional ₹2,000 are each counted once.\nIt is incorrect to say intermediate goods contribute nothing: their contribution is included in final value.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q202","chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Special cases of residents and territory using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Special cases of residents and territory\nNormal Residents: citizens of another country can be residents here when their centre of economic interest is here; officials and diplomats serving abroad remain residents of their home country; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic Territory: include a country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad, ships and aircraft operated by its residents between countries, and fishing vessels and floating platforms operated by residents in international waters under the accounting conventions.\nExclude foreign embassies and international organisations located inside its political borders. Residence and territory are separate tests.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q203","chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Steps under the three measurement methods using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Steps under the three measurement methods\nValue Added Method: identify and classify producing units; estimate each unit’s output and intermediate consumption; obtain sectoral value added; aggregate and make depreciation, tax and NFIA adjustments to national income. Income Method: identify producing enterprises; classify factor incomes into compensation, operating surplus and mixed income; sum them to obtain domestic income; add NFIA.\nExpenditure Method: identify final expenditure by sectors; classify consumption, investment and net exports; sum to GDPMP; convert to NNPFC. Keep the starting aggregate clear because the adjustments depend on whether it is gross/net and market price/factor cost.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q204","chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Why money solves barter’s difficulties using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Why money solves barter’s difficulties\nLack of Double Coincidence of Wants: money is accepted as an intermediary, so you can sell to one person and buy from another. Lack of a Common Measure of Value: prices expressed in money make values comparable.\nDifficulty in Storing Wealth: money offers a convenient store, though inflation can erode it. Difficulty in Deferred Payments: future payments can be fixed in monetary units.\nDifficulty in Transfer of Value: purchasing power can be transferred without moving bulky goods. In theory answers, state the barter difficulty first and then the function that addresses it.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q205","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Issuer of currency and banker to government using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Issuer of currency and banker to government\nIssuer of Currency: the central bank has the principal note-issuing authority; in India, the ₹1 note and coins are issued by the Government of India. Centralised issue promotes uniformity and control.\nBanker to Government: the RBI keeps government deposits, receives payments, makes disbursements, advances funds within the applicable arrangements and manages public debt. Financial adviser is a related role.\nDistinguish issuing authority from circulation: government-issued coins enter circulation through the monetary system.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q206","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Bankers’ bank, lender of last resort and clearing house using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Bankers’ bank, lender of last resort and clearing house\nBankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role: commercial banks maintain required cash balances with the central bank, which regulates their functioning. Lender of Last Resort: the central bank offers financial accommodation in emergencies when other sources are unavailable.\nClearing House: it settles interbank claims by adjusting banks’ accounts and facilitates fund transfers. Custodian of Foreign Exchange: it manages the country’s foreign-exchange reserves and can intervene in the exchange market.\nControl of Credit: it regulates the cost and availability of credit to support growth with stability.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q207","chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Ranges of APC, MPC, APS and MPS using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Ranges of APC, MPC, APS and MPS\nAPC can exceed one when C exceeds Y; it equals one at break-even and is below one when saving is positive. APS is negative when saving is negative, zero at break-even and positive when saving is positive; with positive consumption it remains below one.\nMPC and MPS range from zero to one in the textbook model and add to one. At the endpoints, MPC = 1 implies MPS = 0 and all additional income is consumed; MPC = 0 implies MPS = 1 and all additional income is saved.\nDo not infer a marginal ratio from a single total-income observation.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q208","chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Break-even income versus equilibrium income using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Break-even income versus equilibrium income\nBreak-even Income: consumption equals income, so saving is zero; use C = Y. Equilibrium Income: planned output equals planned expenditure, so saving equals investment; use Y = C + I or S = I.\nWith positive autonomous investment and positive MPS, equilibrium income exceeds break-even income. The two coincide only when autonomous investment is zero in the simple model.\nIf you are asked for dissaving at zero income, solve for autonomous consumption and report saving as its negative.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q209","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Budget objectives: the headings to reproduce using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Budget objectives: the headings to reproduce\nGDP Growth: create infrastructure and induce private investment through suitable incentives. Allocation of Resources: influence production through taxes, subsidies and public provision.\nProvision of Public Goods: provide collective services that markets may undersupply. Redistribution of Income and Wealth: use progressive taxation and welfare spending.\nBalanced Regional Growth: direct infrastructure and incentives to less-developed regions. Employment Opportunities: expand activity and labour demand.\nEconomic Stability: raise spending during deficient demand and restrain it during excess demand. Select and explain the number of objectives requested, using these headings.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q210","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Free-rider problem using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Free-rider problem\nA free rider enjoys the benefits of goods and services without contributing the full or partial cost of providing them. Non-excludability makes it difficult to withhold benefits from non-payers.\nPrivate providers may be unable to collect enough revenue to cover costs, so the market can fail to provide a needed public good. National defence and street lighting illustrate why public provision can be necessary.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q211","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Tax rates and tax burden are different using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Tax rates and tax burden are different\nProgressive Rate: the tax rate rises with income. Regressive Rate: the tax rate falls with income.\nProportionate Rate: the tax rate stays constant as income changes. Progressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the rich.\nRegressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the poor. The textbook stresses that a proportionate tax can still have a regressive real burden.\nWhen answering, specify whether you mean the numerical rate or the economic burden.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q212","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Does a balanced budget leave AD unchanged? using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Does a balanced budget leave AD unchanged?\nNo. Equal tax collection and government expenditure do not necessarily have equal first-round effects on AD.\nGovernment expenditure enters AD in full; a tax increase reduces disposable income, and consumption falls by MPC times the tax rise. If G and taxes each rise by ₹100 and MPC is 0.5, expenditure raises AD by ₹100 while the initial consumption loss is ₹50, giving a net initial rise of ₹50.\nUnder the simple lump-sum-tax model, later rounds imply a balanced-budget multiplier of one. Distinguish the initial AD change from the final income change.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q213","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Fixed versus flexible rates: named merits and demerits using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Fixed exchange rate: all five merits\nA fixed rate is officially set and defended. The chapter’s arguments concern the benefits of greater exchange-rate predictability, subject to successful maintenance of the rate.\n**1 · Market Stability and Mobility of Capital:** Reducing exchange-rate fluctuations lowers one source of uncertainty for international investors and can encourage cross-border capital movement.\n**2 · Growth of International Trade:** Exporters and importers can plan contracts with more predictable domestic-currency returns and costs, supporting international trade.\n**3 · Stable Macroeconomic Policies:** The textbook argues that a stable exchange rate removes frequent exchange-rate changes as a source of policy disturbance. Maintaining the peg still places constraints on policy; it does not give unlimited monetary independence.\n**4 · Devaluation as a Tool to Expand Foreign Markets:** An official downward adjustment in the domestic currency’s value can make exports cheaper to foreigners and improve producers’ international competitiveness, depending on demand responses.\n**5 · A Check on Inflation:** High domestic inflation makes exports less competitive at a fixed rate, weakening receipts and reserves. Pressure to preserve the rate can encourage policies restraining inflation.\nThe same commitment that provides predictability requires defence and can prevent the official rate from adjusting to market conditions.\n**1 · Reserves of Forex:** The authority needs foreign-exchange reserves to meet excess demand for foreign currency and defend the official rate. Sustained pressure can exhaust reserves.\n**2 · Inefficient Allocation of Resources:** An official rate differing from the market-clearing rate distorts the relative prices guiding production, imports and exports.\n**3 · Small Size of Forex Market:** The chapter argues that a tightly fixed rate limits foreign exchange as an independently traded commodity, restricting the scope of currency-market activity.\n**4 · Discourages Venture Capital:** In the chapter’s usage, this means funds placed in foreign-currency trading to profit from exchange movements. A tightly fixed rate limits these opportunities; this is different from ordinary start-up venture finance.\n**5 · Divergence from Equilibrium:** The official rate can remain above or below the equilibrium rate. Persistent excess demand or supply then requires intervention and can impair efficient production and trade.\nA flexible rate moves with demand and supply. Its advantages arise from allowing prices to adjust rather than defending a prescribed value.\n**1 · Large Forex Reserves Not Required for Rate Defence:** No official peg needs continuous defence. Market adjustment can equilibrate currency demand and supply, although countries may still hold reserves for other reasons.\n**2 · Efficient Allocation of Resources:** Market-clearing exchange rates transmit scarcity and demand signals, helping guide trade, production and investment across countries.\n**3 · Large Size of Forex Market:** Currency itself becomes a traded asset, expanding financial-market activity and related business opportunities.\n**4 · International Mobility of Liquidity:** Less foreign exchange needs to remain committed to defending a fixed rate, potentially freeing funds for international transactions and investment.\n**5 · Encourages Venture Capital in Currency Trading:** Exchange-rate movements create opportunities for investors willing to bear currency risk. This is the chapter’s currency-market meaning of venture capital.\nMarket adjustment creates uncertainty and can transmit foreign disturbances into the domestic economy.\n**1 · Marginalisation of Weak Currencies:** Low demand for a weak currency can lead to steep depreciation. Imports and foreign-currency obligations become more expensive in domestic money, creating pressure for smaller or vulnerable economies.\n**2 · Uncertainty of the Market:** Frequent exchange-rate changes make future export receipts, import costs and investment returns harder to predict.\n**3 · Difficulty Maintaining Stable Monetary Policy:** Exchange movements affect inflation, capital flows and interest-rate decisions. Responding to them can complicate a stable policy path.\n**4 · Difficulty with Bilateral Trade Agreements:** Changing currency values make it harder to predict the value of payments under long-term trade arrangements. Hedging can reduce, but not eliminate, this problem.\n**5 · External Shocks:** Foreign-currency appreciation can raise the domestic-currency cost of imports even when their foreign price is unchanged. A stronger dollar, for example, increases the rupee bill for a fixed dollar payment.","tag":"Full-topic review"},{"id":"q214","chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"In a two-sector economy, MPS is 0.25, planned investment ₹150 crore and equilibrium income ₹1,000 crore. Find autonomous consumption and dissaving at zero income.","solution":"At equilibrium S = I.\nS = −C̄ + 0.25Y.\n150 = −C̄ + 0.25 × 1,000 ⇒ C̄ = ₹100 crore.\nAt Y = 0, saving = −₹100 crore; the magnitude of dissaving is ₹100 crore.","tag":"Numerical"},{"id":"q215","chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Two firms A and B have equal GVA MP. B’s output is ₹1,200 crore and its purchases from A are ₹300 crore. A purchases ₹350 crore of inputs from B, exports ₹200 crore and adds ₹100 crore to stock. Assuming no other intermediate inputs, find A’s domestic sales.","solution":"GVA of B = 1,200 − 300 = ₹900 crore.\nGVA of A also equals ₹900 crore.\nOutput of A = GVA + Intermediate consumption = 900 + 350 = ₹1,250 crore.\nSales of A = Output − Change in stock = 1,250 − 100 = ₹1,150 crore.\nDomestic sales = Total sales − Exports = 1,150 − 200 = ₹950 crore.","tag":"Numerical"},{"id":"q216","chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A business borrows abroad to buy equipment; the government separately obtains an overseas loan specifically to finance a BoP deficit. Classify each as autonomous or accommodating and explain why the account type alone does not decide this.","solution":"Autonomous Items\nThe business loan is autonomous because it is taken for an independent investment/profit motive.\nAccommodating Items\nThe government loan is accommodating because its stated purpose is financing the external imbalance.\nBoth may involve capital-account borrowing, but the purpose determines the autonomous/accommodating classification.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-mcq-1","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The principal purpose of colonial economic policy in India was to:","options":["Achieve Indian industrial self-reliance","Serve Britain’s economic interests","Develop Indian consumer welfare","Eliminate agricultural exports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"India supplied raw materials and became a market for British manufactured goods.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-2","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An export surplus under British rule did not necessarily benefit India because:","options":["India exported only services","All export earnings went to cultivators","The surplus financed colonial payments abroad","India had no imports"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The surplus financed administrative, war and other British expenses: the drain of wealth.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-3","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Agriculture dominated colonial employment. Statement II: This necessarily implied high agricultural productivity.","options":["I is false; II is true","I is true; II is false","Both are false","Both are true"],"answer":1,"explanation":"High dependence on agriculture coexisted with low productivity and stagnation.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-4","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a cause of agricultural stagnation under British rule?","options":["Abundant institutional credit","Exploitative land settlement","Heavy investment in irrigation","Universal ownership rights for tenants"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Insecure tenure and high rent discouraged investment, alongside low technology and inadequate irrigation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-5","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"De-industrialisation means:","options":["A fall in agricultural employment only","Decline of handicrafts without adequate modern industrial replacement","Growth of every manufacturing industry","A ban on all imports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Artisans lost livelihoods while modern industry grew too slowly to absorb them.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-6","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"1921 is called the Year of the Great Divide in the context of:","options":["Industrial licensing","GST","Population growth","Public-sector disinvestment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It marks the transition from irregular/stagnant population growth towards sustained growth.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-7","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Commercialisation of agriculture primarily concerns:","options":["Abolishing land revenue","Producing for market sale","Replacing every crop with machinery","Producing only for family consumption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Its defining feature is production for sale; improved farmer welfare does not automatically follow.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-8","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Railways had some beneficial effects on India. Reason: Their primary colonial objective was Indian social welfare.","options":["Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason explains assertion","Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Assertion false; reason true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transport and communication benefits existed, but colonial strategic and commercial interests were the primary motive.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-9","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Occupational structure measures:","options":["Distribution of workers across activities","Only exports by commodity","Distribution of tax rates","Distribution of government debt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It concerns where people work, rather than sectors’ GDP shares.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-10","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The decline of royal courts mainly weakened:","options":["The government’s GST collections","The supply of British machine-made imports","Modern service exports","Demand for traditional handicrafts"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Royal patronage had supported specialised handicrafts.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-11","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is NOT one of the four common goals of planning?","options":["Growth","Modernisation","Permanent dependence on imports","Equity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The fourth goal is self-reliance.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-12","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Replacing imports with domestically produced goods is:","options":["Import substitution","Disinvestment","Outsourcing","Informalisation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An inward-looking strategy supported domestic industry through protection.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-13","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A quota differs from a tariff because a quota:","options":["Always makes imports free","Is only a subsidy","Is a tax on income","Sets a quantitative limit on imports"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A tariff is a tax on imports; a quota restricts their quantity.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-14","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Abolition of intermediaries is an example of:","options":["Foreign-exchange reform","A monetary-policy instrument","Institutional land reform","Technical reform"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It changes agrarian ownership and tenure relations.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-15","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"HYV seeds produced strong results when combined with:","options":["Irrigation, fertilisers and supporting inputs","Only rain and no capital","A ban on farm credit","Removal of all storage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Green Revolution was an input package, not seeds alone.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-16","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Marketable surplus is:","options":["Only imported food","Government tax revenue","Output remaining for sale after the farmer’s own requirements","Total farm output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It is the portion available for the market.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-17","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The Karve Committee (1955) is associated with:","options":["Demonetisation","WTO establishment","Currency devaluation","Small-scale industries"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It recognised small industries’ contribution to rural development and employment.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-18","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Equity as a planning goal emphasises:","options":["Only imported machinery","Eliminating all private firms","Only a rise in total output","Benefits reaching all sections and meeting basic needs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Growth and equity are distinct: higher GDP alone does not ensure fair distribution.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-19","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Initial Green Revolution gains were regionally uneven. Reason: Access to irrigation and complementary inputs varied across regions.","options":["Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Assertion false; reason true","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Regions with irrigation and resources could adopt the package more effectively.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-20","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is an argument AGAINST indiscriminate input subsidies?","options":["They eliminate all inequality","They can encourage excessive water and chemical use","They always raise government revenue","They make every crop organic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fiscal burdens and ecological damage are central criticisms, alongside unequal benefits.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-21","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A mixed economy has:","options":["Both public and private sectors","No planning or markets","Only private ownership","Only government ownership"],"answer":0,"explanation":"India combined public-sector leadership with regulated private enterprise.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-22","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Modernisation includes:","options":["Only land ceilings","Complete withdrawal from foreign trade","Only machinery, with no change in social outlook","Technology and progressive changes in social outlook"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The textbook includes social attitudes, such as women’s participation, alongside technology.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-23","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The severe external crisis of 1991 was marked by:","options":["No import requirements","Very low reserves and a balance-of-payments problem","Foreign-exchange reserves sufficient for decades","The introduction of MGNREGA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reserves were barely enough for about two weeks of imports.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-24","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"LPG stands for:","options":["Liberalisation, privatisation, globalisation","Licensing, planning, grants","Loans, production, government","Labour, prices, growth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"These are the three main elements of the New Economic Policy.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-25","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Selling a minority government shareholding without transferring control is:","options":["Import substitution","Necessarily full privatisation","Nationalisation","Disinvestment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Disinvestment can occur without a transfer of management control.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-26","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Liberalisation reduces certain direct controls. Statement II: It eliminates all government regulation.","options":["Both false","I false; II true","I true; II false","Both true"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prudential, safety and competition regulation can remain.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-27","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An official reduction in currency value in a fixed or managed arrangement is:","options":["Appreciation","Informalisation","Revaluation","Devaluation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Distinguish devaluation from market-driven depreciation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-28","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An Indian firm providing accounting services to a foreign company illustrates:","options":["Import substitution","Buffer stocking","Land ceiling","Outsourcing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The client contracts an activity to an external provider.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-29","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"WTO was established in:","options":["1951","1947","1991","1995"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It succeeded GATT and provides a framework for multilateral trade rules.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-30","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a criticism of reform outcomes?","options":["The absence of foreign competition","Guaranteed equal benefits for all farmers","The abolition of every private enterprise","Uneven gains across sectors and regions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Agriculture, employment and regional imbalance are important appraisal points.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-31","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"GST was introduced in India on:","options":["8 November 2016","1 January 1991","1 July 2017","26 January 1950"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Demonetisation occurred in November 2016; GST followed in July 2017.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-32","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Consumer sovereignty increased with reforms mainly through:","options":["A universal ban on imports","Greater choice and competition","Elimination of product variety","Government ownership of every shop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Competition and entry expanded consumers’ range of goods and services.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-33","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Public-enterprise autonomy necessarily means the enterprise has been sold to private owners. This statement is:","options":["False; autonomy can expand while public ownership remains","True only for schools","True in every case","The definition of a tariff"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Managerial autonomy and ownership transfer are distinct.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-34","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Human capital is best described as:","options":["Knowledge, skills and health embodied in people","Only the population count","The number of buildings","Government financial debt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Capabilities, rather than headcount alone, determine human capital.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-35","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Human development treats education and health as:","options":["Physical machinery","Ends valuable for people’s well-being","Only means to raise productivity","A form of indirect tax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Human capital stresses productive returns; human development values well-being itself.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-36","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is NOT a source of human capital formation?","options":["Information about jobs","On-the-job training","Health expenditure","Buying an existing machine without training"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The machine is physical capital; the other options improve people’s capabilities.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-37","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Vaccination is an example of:","options":["Disinvestment","Seasonal unemployment","Only curative treatment","Preventive medicine"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Preventive care reduces the likelihood of illness.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-38","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Migration can be an investment in human capital. Reason: Its costs may be undertaken to obtain higher future earnings.","options":["Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false","Assertion false; reason true"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Travel and settlement costs are weighed against expected returns.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-39","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which distinction is correct?","options":["Human capital is separable from its owner","Physical capital is intangible","Human capital cannot ever lose value","Human capital is inseparable from its owner"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A person supplies the services of human capital; the capability is embodied in that person.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-40","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The Kothari Commission recommendation discussed in the textbook is education expenditure of:","options":["60% of all wages","6% of GDP","6% of population","0.6% of exports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Be precise about the denominator: GDP, not total government spending.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-41","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The RTE Act, 2009 concerns free and compulsory education for children aged:","options":["15–21","0–3","6–14","Only above 18"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The age group is 6–14 years.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-42","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Years of schooling alone may inadequately measure human capital because:","options":["Education has no benefits","Health is always irrelevant","Quality and acquired skills also matter","Every school is identical"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quantity of schooling cannot fully capture effective capability.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-43","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Brain drain means:","options":["Migration of skilled people abroad","A rise in groundwater","A tariff on books","A fall in machinery imports"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It can reduce the domestic availability of skilled human resources.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-44","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Rural development is:","options":["Only the construction of urban roads","The abolition of all farming","Only an increase in wheat output","A comprehensive improvement in rural economic and social conditions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It includes credit, marketing, infrastructure, human resources and diversification.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-45","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"NABARD was established in:","options":["1982","2017","1995","1955"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It is the apex institution discussed for rural credit and development.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-46","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a non-institutional source of rural credit?","options":["Commercial bank","Regional rural bank","Moneylender","Cooperative credit society"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Moneylenders, traders and landlords are non-institutional sources.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-47","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"SHGs generally begin by:","options":["Selling central-bank reserves","Fixing import quotas","Guaranteeing unlimited corporate loans","Pooling small savings of members"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pooled savings support small loans and can facilitate bank linkage.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-48","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A distress sale occurs when a farmer:","options":["Receives a guaranteed premium","Sells urgently at an unfavourable price due to immediate needs","Waits indefinitely for the best price","Always exports organic crops"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Debt and lack of storage may force sale immediately after harvest.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-49","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Agricultural marketing includes:","options":["Only obtaining a bank loan","Only retail sale","Only growing seeds","Processing, grading, storage, transport and sale"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It covers the chain between production and consumption.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-50","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Operation Flood is associated with:","options":["Currency devaluation","Wheat imports","Milk cooperatives and the White Revolution","Coal nationalisation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It linked milk producers and consumers through cooperative organisation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-51","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Diversification can reduce seasonal unemployment by:","options":["Creating allied and non-farm work","Banning rural services","Eliminating storage","Making every farmer grow only one seasonal crop"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alternative activities provide work outside the crop season.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-52","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a constraint of organic farming?","options":["Guaranteed premium prices for all farmers","Certification and marketing difficulties","No need for any knowledge","Automatic high yields in every transition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Training, yields, shelf life and market access also matter.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-53","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Rythu Bazaars illustrate:","options":["Direct agricultural marketing","Foreign-exchange dealing","Disinvestment","Industrial licensing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Direct links can reduce intermediaries and improve farmers’ returns.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-54","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Horticulture needs cold chains mainly because:","options":["Its output is always metal","It never needs transport","It is unrelated to agriculture","Its output is often perishable"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Storage and transport reduce spoilage and improve market access.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-55","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A family member helping produce crops without a separate wage is:","options":["Always outside the workforce","An unpaid family worker engaged in economic activity","Necessarily openly unemployed","Only a consumer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A separate wage is not required for participation in economic production.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-56","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Labour force equals:","options":["Only people receiving salaries","Workforce plus unemployed people seeking/available for work","Total population in every case","Only casual workers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Workforce counts employed people; labour force also includes the unemployed.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-57","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A household has six farm workers; two leave and output is unchanged. The two were:","options":["Necessarily formally employed","Seasonally unemployed by definition","Disguisedly unemployed","Not members of the labour force"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Their marginal productivity was zero.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-mcq-58","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Casualisation concerns:","options":["A rise only in formal-sector social security","Only the growth of exports","An increase in university buildings","Movement towards casual wage work"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It is about employment status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-59","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The textbook’s formal-sector definition includes:","options":["Every household service","Government departments and private establishments with ten or more workers","Only own-account farmers","Only private firms with fewer than ten workers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Public enterprises are also included in the formal sector.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-60","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Jobless growth means:","options":["Every person has a government job","Zero output growth in every year","All workers become farmers","Output growth without adequate corresponding employment growth"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GDP may grow much faster than employment.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-61","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The MGNREGA employment guarantee is primarily:","options":["100 days for every adult separately","A permanent salaried urban job","A subsidy only for machinery","100 days per rural household in a financial year"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adult household members volunteer for unskilled manual work; the entitlement is household-based.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-62","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Worker-population ratio uses which denominator?","options":["Number of factories","Labour force","Only unemployed people","Population"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Workers/population ×100. The unemployment rate instead uses labour force.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-63","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A daily-wage construction worker is classified by employment status as:","options":["Outside all economic activity","Casual wage labourer","Necessarily a regular salaried employee","Always self-employed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Construction is the sector; casual wage labour is the employment status.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-64","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Informal workers generally lack many formal social-security benefits. Statement II: Informalisation and casualisation mean exactly the same thing.","options":["I true; II false","I false; II true","Both false","Both true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"One concerns protection/organisation; the other concerns employment status.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-65","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If agriculture employs a much larger share of workers than its share of GDP, it suggests:","options":["The GDP and employment shares must be equal","Agriculture produces all GDP","Every worker has zero output","Relatively low average output per agricultural worker"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The comparison indicates a productivity gap, not zero productivity for every worker.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-written-1","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A village has 2,000 people, 900 employed people and 100 unemployed people seeking work. Calculate its labour force, worker-population ratio and unemployment rate.","solution":"Labour force = 900 + 100 = 1,000.\nWorker-population ratio = 900/2,000 ×100 = 45%.\nUnemployment rate = 100/1,000 ×100 = 10%.\nThe two rates use different denominators.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-2","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A region’s population is 5 lakh and its worker-population ratio is 40%. Its labour force is 2.5 lakh. Calculate workers, unemployed people and the unemployment rate.","solution":"Workers = 5 ×0.40 = 2 lakh.\nUnemployed = 2.5 −2 = 0.5 lakh.\nUnemployment rate = 0.5/2.5 ×100 = 20%.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-3","chapter":"i2","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A farm produces 120 quintals of wheat, retaining 35 quintals for family consumption and seed requirements. Find the marketable surplus.","solution":"Marketable surplus = Total output − Retained requirements.\n= 120 −35 = 85 quintals.\nMarketable surplus is not total farm production.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-4","chapter":"i4","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"Education spending is ₹60 crore, GDP is ₹1,000 crore and total government expenditure is ₹300 crore. Express education spending as a percentage of each denominator.","solution":"Share of GDP = 60/1,000 ×100 = 6%.\nShare of government spending = 60/300 ×100 = 20%.\nBoth describe the same spending, but they answer different questions.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-5","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An economy produces output worth 1,000 units, of which agriculture contributes 200. Agriculture employs 50 of the economy’s 100 workers. Compare average output per worker in agriculture and the rest of the economy.","solution":"Agriculture: 200/50 = 4 output units per worker.\nOther sectors: (1,000−200)/(100−50) = 800/50 = 16 units per worker.\nAverage output per worker outside agriculture is four times as high. Employment shares and output shares are not interchangeable.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-6","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“Railways prove that colonial economic policy primarily aimed to develop India.” Evaluate this statement.","solution":"Colonial infrastructure: purpose and effects\nThe statement confuses beneficial effects with the primary objective.\nRailways moved raw materials, expanded markets for British manufactures and served military/administrative needs.\nThey also improved transport, movement of people and commercial links.\nThese benefits should be acknowledged, but colonial interests determined priorities and design.","tag":"Evaluate a statement"},{"id":"ied-written-7","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A small farmer cannot adopt HYV seeds because irrigation and affordable credit are missing. Explain why distributing seeds alone may fail, and identify two suitable measures.","solution":"Green Revolution: complementary inputs\nHYV technology requires irrigation, fertilisers, plant protection and supporting finance. Seeds alone cannot secure the expected yield.\nExpand reliable irrigation and provide timely institutional credit on suitable terms.\nTraining and access to inputs/marketing can support adoption.\nTargeting small farmers can reduce unequal gains.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-8","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"The government sells 20% of its equity in an enterprise but retains control. Another public enterprise receives greater managerial autonomy. Are both necessarily privatisation? Explain.","solution":"Disinvestment, privatisation and autonomy\nThe equity sale is disinvestment. A minority sale with government control retained is not necessarily full privatisation.\nManagerial autonomy increases freedom to make decisions; it does not itself transfer ownership.\nPrivatisation involves a greater private role through ownership or management transfer.\nIdentify the actual change in control rather than relying only on the word reform.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-9","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A health programme is justified solely by its effect on worker productivity. Explain the human-capital view and add the human-development perspective.","solution":"Human capital versus human development\nThe human-capital view treats health as an investment improving efficiency, attendance and productive earnings.\nThe human-development view regards health and a better quality of life as ends in themselves.\nIt supports health access even when immediate measured productivity gains are limited.\nThe perspectives overlap in valuing health, but differ in the ultimate purpose.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-10","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"A farmer sells immediately after harvest at a low price to repay a moneylender. Explain the linked credit and marketing problems, and suggest three measures.","solution":"Rural credit and agricultural marketing\nImmediate repayment needs and inadequate storage create a distress sale, weakening bargaining power.\nTimely affordable institutional credit can reduce dependence on the moneylender.\nStorage and warehouse facilities allow the farmer to delay sale.\nCooperative/direct marketing, better price information and fair regulated transactions improve returns.\nExplain how each measure addresses a specific difficulty.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-11","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Output rises rapidly while secure jobs grow slowly and more workers take daily-wage work. Identify and distinguish the two processes.","solution":"Jobless growth and casualisation\nJobless growth: output increases without adequate corresponding employment growth. Capital-intensive techniques can contribute.\nCasualisation: movement towards casual wage work rather than regular salaried work or self-employment.\nThe first compares output and job growth; the second concerns employment status and security.\nDo not assume they are identical or that every casual worker is openly unemployed.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-review-1-india-before-british-rule","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: India before British rule. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"India before British rule\nIndia had a prosperous handicraft sector and a largely self-contained rural economy. Cultivators produced crops, while village artisans and service providers met many local needs. Agriculture relied on traditional methods, but the textbook distinguishes this economy from the later colonial stagnation.\n**Handicrafts and foreign trade:** fine cotton and silk textiles, metal work and precious-stone products enjoyed an international reputation. Finished handicrafts were exported and bullion—gold and silver—flowed into India. Bengal’s muslin, including fine malmal associated with royal use, illustrates the quality of specialised Indian production.\nThe important comparison is the change in economic structure: before colonial transformation, India exported valued finished products; under British rule, it increasingly supplied raw materials and imported manufactures."},{"id":"ied-review-1-the-two-sides-of-colonial-exploitation","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: The two sides of colonial exploitation. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"The two sides of colonial exploitation\nColonial exploitation aimed to maximise Britain’s economic advantage. **Supply aspect:** India supplied cheap raw materials such as cotton and jute for British factories. **Demand aspect:** India became a market for British manufactured goods. Destroying competing Indian handicrafts helped achieve both objectives.\nAgriculture was subjected to heavy land-revenue demands. Cultivators could lose rights when unable to meet them, while intermediaries extracted surplus without investing adequately in farming. The transformation was therefore not simply a change in trade: it weakened the productive structure and the cultivator’s ability to improve it.\nFor a question on the colonial objective, state both sides—raw-material supply and a market for British goods—then explain how policies in agriculture, industry and trade served that objective."},{"id":"ied-review-1-seven-overall-features-of-the-colonial-economy","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Seven overall features of the colonial economy. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Seven overall features of the colonial economy\n**Stagnant economy:** real output and per-capita income grew very slowly. **Backward economy:** low productivity, poverty, unemployment and inadequate basic necessities kept living standards low. Stagnation concerns the pace of change; backwardness concerns the economy’s low level of development.\n**Dominance of agriculture:** about 85% of people depended directly or indirectly on agriculture, while roughly 70–75% of workers were employed in the primary sector. **Bleak industrialisation:** handicrafts declined and modern heavy/capital-goods industries remained inadequate.\n**Heavy dependence on imports:** machinery, defence requirements and many consumer goods had to be imported. **Limited urbanisation:** most people lived in villages with few opportunities outside farming. **Semi-feudal economy:** landlords controlled land and appropriated surplus, while cultivators retained little beyond subsistence. These features reinforced one another."},{"id":"ied-review-1-national-income-estimates-and-the-meaning-of-stagnation","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: National-income estimates and the meaning of stagnation. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"National-income estimates and the meaning of stagnation\nNo comprehensive, regular official national-income series was developed under colonial rule. Individual estimates were attempted by Dadabhai Naoroji, William Digby, Findlay Shirras, V.K.R.V. Rao and R.C. Desai. Their estimates differed, but Rao’s work is described as especially systematic.\nThe chapter’s Ability Zone cites approximately 2% national-income growth and 0.5% per-capita-income growth during the first half of the twentieth century. Treat these as historical estimates rather than a precise modern series. Per-capita income grows slowly when output barely outpaces population.\nThis explains why a modest increase in total output need not produce a meaningful improvement in the average person’s living conditions."},{"id":"ied-review-1-the-seven-features-of-colonial-agriculture","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: The seven features of colonial agriculture. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"The seven features of colonial agriculture\n**Low production and productivity:** total output was small and output per hectare was low. **Exploitative land settlement:** revenue arrangements favoured intermediaries rather than cultivators. **Forced commercialisation:** farmers were pushed towards crops needed by British industry.\n**Lack of investment in agricultural infrastructure:** irrigation, improved technology, drainage, terracing and flood protection were neglected. Dependence on rainfall made farming uncertain. **Gulf between owners and tillers:** owners received surplus without sharing cultivation costs, while actual cultivators lacked funds to invest.\n**Small and fragmented holdings:** scattered, uneconomic plots raised cultivation costs and made improved methods difficult. **Subsistence outlook:** farming was mainly a way to feed the family, rather than a profit-oriented enterprise. Together these explain why agriculture could employ most workers and still produce widespread poverty."},{"id":"ied-review-1-how-the-zamindari-system-kept-agriculture-stagnant","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: How the zamindari system kept agriculture stagnant. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"How the zamindari system kept agriculture stagnant\nUnder the Permanent Settlement in the Bengal Presidency, zamindars were recognised as landowners and paid a fixed revenue amount to the government. They could extract rent from cultivators, and their main interest was collection rather than improvement of land.\n**Cultivators lacked means:** rent left them with little surplus, pushing them towards debt and subsistence. **Owners lacked incentive:** landlords could receive income without improving cultivation and often spent it on conspicuous consumption. The people doing the work and the people receiving the surplus were different.\nThis is the investment trap: cultivators wanted better output but could not finance improvements; owners had resources but little reason to invest. Insecure rights, exploitation and low investment sustained low productivity."},{"id":"ied-review-1-commercial-crops-famines-and-partition","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Commercial crops, famines and Partition. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Commercial crops, famines and Partition\n**Commercialisation** means growing for sale rather than self-consumption. Under colonial rule, farmers were forced towards crops such as indigo for British textiles. Market production exposed subsistence farmers to price fluctuations and could reduce the land available for food crops; higher commercial output did not guarantee higher farmer welfare.\n**Famines:** drought, poor purchasing power, inadequate public-health services and weak relief made crop failures devastating. The highlights discuss repeated famines, including Bengal in 1769–70 and the famine of 1943. Rail transport later made moving relief food easier, but it did not eliminate colonial famine suffering.\n**Partition:** India received about 82% of the population but only about 65% of the foodgrain area in the chapter’s figures. Productive areas of West Punjab and Sindh went to Pakistan, aggravating food shortages. This is a separate shock from the earlier colonial policies."},{"id":"ied-review-1-five-reasons-indian-handicrafts-declined","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Five reasons Indian handicrafts declined. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Five reasons Indian handicrafts declined\n**Discriminatory tariff policy:** British manufactured goods entered Indian markets on favourable terms, Indian handicraft exports faced heavy duties, and raw-material exports to Britain were encouraged. This weakened Indian goods both at home and abroad.\n**Expansion of railways:** cheap British products reached the interior. **Low cost of British goods:** machinery and large-scale production made imports difficult for handmade goods to compete with. **Disappearance of Indian kingdoms:** the decline of royal courts removed important patrons of specialised crafts.\n**Loss of skilled artisans and lack of capital:** declining demand and low returns forced artisans out of their trades and reduced investment. De-industrialisation means this decline was not offset by enough modern industrial employment. Consequences included unemployment, loss of skills, pressure on agriculture and a larger market for British manufactures."},{"id":"ied-review-1-modern-industry-five-observations","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Modern industry: five observations. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Modern industry: five observations\n**Textile mills:** cotton mills developed mainly in western India and were largely Indian-owned; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal and largely foreign-owned. **Iron and steel:** TISCO was established in 1907; other branches developed gradually, including sugar, cement and paper.\n**Weak capital-goods industry:** machinery and industrial plants were not produced on the scale needed for broad industrialisation. **Small overall contribution:** modern industry’s contribution to output and employment was too limited to replace the lost handicraft base.\n**Limited state participation:** government activity was concentrated in railways, power, communications, ports and selected departmental undertakings. The conclusion is limited and uneven modernisation—not the complete absence of factories."},{"id":"ied-review-1-three-characteristics-of-colonial-foreign-trade","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Three characteristics of colonial foreign trade. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Three characteristics of colonial foreign trade\n**Exporter of raw materials, importer of finished goods:** exports included raw cotton, silk, wool, jute, indigo and sugar; imports included manufactured consumer goods and machinery. This reversed the earlier reputation for exporting finished handicrafts.\n**Monopoly control:** more than half of foreign trade was directed towards Britain; other partners included China, Ceylon and Persia. Britain largely determined the composition and direction of trade.\n**Surplus trade benefiting Britain:** an export surplus did not bring equivalent domestic prosperity. It financed administrative expenses, British war expenditure and payments on behalf of the colonial government. Essential supplies could remain scarce in India while exports generated resources used abroad. This is the drain of wealth."},{"id":"ied-review-1-suez-canal-and-access-to-indian-markets","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Suez Canal and access to Indian markets. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Suez Canal and access to Indian markets\nThe Suez Canal opened in 1869 and provided a shorter sea route between Europe and Asia, avoiding the longer journey around Africa. Lower transport costs made trading between Britain and India easier.\nIn the colonial setting, this strengthened Britain’s control over Indian trade and made British goods more competitive in Indian markets. The general benefit of a cheaper transport route must therefore be distinguished from who controlled the gains."},{"id":"ied-review-1-birth-death-infant-mortality-life-expectancy-and-literacy","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Birth, death, infant mortality, life expectancy and literacy. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Birth, death, infant mortality, life expectancy and literacy\n**Birth and death rates:** approximately 48 and 40 per thousand respectively in the chapter’s historical account. Both were high, so the gap—and therefore population growth—was initially small. Poverty, poor sanitation, recurrent disease and inadequate healthcare kept mortality high.\n**Infant mortality:** about 218 deaths below age one per 1,000 live births. **Life expectancy:** roughly 32 years. These indicate poor public health and living conditions. Infant mortality uses live births as its denominator, not the total population.\n**Literacy:** below 16%, with female literacy around 7%. The very low female rate also reflects gender bias. Explain what the indicators reveal rather than presenting numbers alone; all figures here describe the historical period, not present-day India."},{"id":"ied-review-1-the-census-1921-and-maintenance-investment","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: The census, 1921 and maintenance investment. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"The census, 1921 and maintenance investment\nThe chapter identifies 1881 as the beginning of regular decennial population censuses. A census records population size and its demographic characteristics, enabling comparison over time.\n**1921—the Year of the Great Divide:** before it, population growth fluctuated, with increases and decreases across censuses. Afterwards, population rose more consistently. Both birth and death rates remained high until around 1951, keeping growth relatively modest; a subsequent decline in mortality with high births widened the gap.\n**Maintenance investment** is expenditure needed to maintain the existing population. When population needs absorb a large share of resources, less is available to expand productive capacity. The point is the resource constraint, not that spending on people has no value."},{"id":"ied-review-1-occupational-structure-and-regional-differences","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Occupational structure and regional differences. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Occupational structure and regional differences\nOccupational structure is the distribution of workers across primary, secondary and tertiary activities. The chapter uses 1951 figures because reliable 1947 figures are unavailable: agriculture employed about 70–75%, industry about 10% and services about 15–20%.\n**Agriculture as the principal occupation:** heavy dependence reduced land available per worker and reinforced subsistence farming. **Industry as an insignificant occupation:** manufacturing could not absorb surplus workers. **Insignificant transition:** the movement from agriculture to industry was too weak to produce an economic take-off.\nRegional exceptions existed: parts of the Madras Presidency, Bombay and Bengal showed declining dependence on agriculture; Odisha, Rajasthan and Punjab showed an increase. The overall pattern remained backward and lopsided. Do not confuse workers’ sectoral shares with sectors’ contributions to GDP."},{"id":"ied-review-1-roads-railways-ports-waterways-and-communications","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Roads, railways, ports, waterways and communications. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Roads, railways, ports, waterways and communications\nInfrastructure is the support system for growth. Economic infrastructure includes transport, communications, banking and power; social infrastructure includes education, health and housing.\n**Railways** moved British goods to Indian interiors and raw materials towards ports; the first passenger train ran from Bombay to Thane in 1853. **Ports** handled this trade. **Inland waterways** received limited development; the expensive Odisha coast canal could not compete with railways and was abandoned.\n**Roads** primarily served military movement and transport of materials. All-weather rural access remained inadequate, worsening hardship during floods and famines. **Post and telegraph** strengthened administration and law and order, while postal services remained insufficient. Some infrastructure developed, but its colonial purpose explains its limited contribution to broad welfare."},{"id":"ied-review-1-positive-side-effects-and-a-balanced-evaluation","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Positive side-effects and a balanced evaluation. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Positive side-effects and a balanced evaluation\nThe textbook lists five positive side-effects: **commercial outlook of farmers**, who became more aware of market prices; **new employment opportunities** in transport and related activity; **better movement of famine relief**; a wider **monetary system of exchange**, supporting specialisation; and an **administrative framework** inherited at independence.\nRailways also widened domestic markets, encouraged commercialisation, connected distant regions and promoted cultural interaction. Faster movement of food could reduce the impact of shortages. These are benefits of the means used, even though the underlying motive remained exploitation.\nThe negative side was easier export of Indian raw materials and easier distribution of British manufactures. A balanced answer explains both effects and concludes that colonial priorities, rather than Indian development, determined the system."},{"id":"ied-review-2-capitalist-socialist-and-mixed-economies","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies\n**Capitalist economy:** private ownership, market allocation and profit maximisation. Producers respond to consumers’ purchasing power, so consumer choice and incentives can encourage efficiency and growth. The limitation is that needs without purchasing power may go unmet, and income/wealth can become concentrated.\n**Socialist economy:** collective/public ownership and allocation by a central authority to maximise social welfare. Production, distribution and prices are directed according to assessed needs. Its strength is the priority given to equity; its limitations include restricted consumer choice and weaker incentives that can reduce efficiency.\n**Mixed economy:** public and private ownership coexist. Private firms pursue profit within regulation; public activity pursues social welfare. It seeks growth with social justice, but public-sector inefficiency/corruption and weak coordination can undermine results. India chose this approach rather than an extreme version of either system."},{"id":"ied-review-2-why-india-adopted-planning-and-a-mixed-economy","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why India adopted planning and a mixed economy. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why India adopted planning and a mixed economy\nIndia inherited low productivity, poverty, weak industry and scarce capital. Market forces alone were unlikely to undertake the large, coordinated investment needed in infrastructure and basic industries. Government therefore supplied a big push while retaining private property and democracy.\nEconomic planning means using resources in accordance with national priorities: a central authority specifies targets, policies and programmes for a stated period. A plan explains how limited resources will be used to achieve defined objectives. Goals are essential because resources cannot be allocated purposefully without knowing what is to be achieved.\nThe Planning Commission was established in 1950 under Jawaharlal Nehru; the First Five Year Plan began in 1951. A **perspective plan** sets longer-term aims, commonly over 15–25 years, while each Five Year Plan identifies nearer-term priorities. P.C. Mahalanobis, the architect of Indian planning, is especially associated with the Second Plan’s industrial strategy, the Indian Statistical Institute and Sankhya."},{"id":"ied-review-2-growth-modernisation-self-reliance-self-sufficiency-and-equity","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Growth, modernisation, self-reliance, self-sufficiency and equity. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Growth, modernisation, self-reliance, self-sufficiency and equity\n**Growth:** increase the capacity to produce goods and services. It can come from a larger resource base, more productive capital and supporting services, or better technology. Sustained growth in real GDP indicates an expanding output flow.\n**Modernisation:** adopt new technology and improve social outlook, including gender equality and women’s participation. **Self-reliance:** reduce dependence on foreign financial resources. **Self-sufficiency:** reduce dependence on foreign supplies of essentials. The goal protected economic decisions from political pressure attached to aid or food imports.\n**Equity:** spread benefits across society so everyone can meet basic needs—food, clothing, shelter, education and healthcare. A plan may emphasise one goal more than another, but growth without widespread benefits is not enough. The four common headings are growth, modernisation, self-reliance/self-sufficiency and equity."},{"id":"ied-review-2-growth-versus-development-equal-versus-equitable","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Growth versus development; equal versus equitable. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Growth versus development; equal versus equitable\n**Structural composition** means each sector’s share in GDP. **Structural change** means these shares change over time, normally with agriculture’s share declining and industry/services expanding. India experienced a particularly strong movement towards services. A smaller agricultural GDP share does not mean farming should be neglected.\n**Growth** is a sustained rise in output. **Development** combines growth with equity and structural improvement. **Equal distribution** would give everyone the same income; **equitable distribution** permits differences related to skills and responsibilities while preventing excessive deprivation and ensuring basic opportunities.\n**Inclusive growth** creates opportunities and distributes benefits widely. Modernisation was expected to support jobs through productivity → higher income → greater demand → more employment. Labour-saving technology can weaken that chain, producing jobless growth. Distinguish the intended complementarity from the possible trade-off."},{"id":"ied-review-2-five-land-reforms-and-their-purpose","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Five land reforms and their purpose. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Five land reforms and their purpose\nThe inherited problems were low productivity, low investment, scattered holdings, subsistence farming and exploitation. **Institutional reforms** changed agrarian ownership and relationships; **technical reforms** changed production methods. The basic institutional objective was equity through “land to the tiller”.\n**Abolition of intermediaries:** restore cultivators’ rights, end exploitative rent collection and connect them directly with government. **Ceilings on holdings:** fix a maximum holding and redistribute surplus to small holders/landless workers. **Consolidation:** replace scattered plots with land in one place, reducing cultivation costs.\n**Regulation of rent:** prevent excessive/illegal extraction; the chapter describes rent generally being limited to about one-third of crop value. **Cooperative farming:** pool resources to buy inputs cheaply, sell collectively and strengthen bargaining, without confusing cooperation with compulsory state ownership."},{"id":"ied-review-2-why-land-reforms-achieved-uneven-results","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why land reforms achieved uneven results. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why land reforms achieved uneven results\n**Eviction and evasion:** some landlords evicted tenants or presented themselves as actual cultivators to avoid transferring rights. **Incomplete coverage:** many landless agricultural labourers remained outside the benefits of ownership reforms.\n**Court challenges and delay:** large owners challenged ceiling laws; the delay allowed them to transfer holdings to relatives and evade limits. Implementation also depended on political commitment and local administration. West Bengal and Kerala are highlighted as relatively successful examples.\nThe intended chain was secure rights → stronger incentive to improve land → higher productivity and equity. Weak implementation interrupted that chain. Therefore distinguish the purpose of the reform from its actual reach."},{"id":"ied-review-2-the-green-revolution-input-package","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: The Green Revolution input package. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"The Green Revolution input package\nGreen Revolution refers to a sharp increase in crop output associated with HYV technology, especially from the mid-1960s/1967–68. “Green” refers to crops and “revolution” to the substantial jump in production.\nIts four textbook elements are **HYV seeds**, **chemical fertilisers**, **insecticides/pesticides for crop protection**, and **expanded irrigation**. High-yield seeds require complementary nutrients and reliable water; crop protection helps preserve the potential gain. Credit and extension services make the package accessible.\nThe package, rather than any single input, broke the earlier stagnation. Irrigation projects and plant-protection services supported adoption. A farmer receiving seeds alone may fail to benefit if water, inputs and finance are missing."},{"id":"ied-review-2-the-two-phases-and-the-role-of-government-support","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: The two phases and the role of government support. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"The two phases and the role of government support\n**Mid-1960s to mid-1970s:** gains were concentrated in wheat/rice-growing regions with adequate water. The supplied textbook names Punjab, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu. Adoption was initially easiest for prosperous farmers who could afford expensive complementary inputs.\n**Mid-1970s to mid-1980s:** coverage widened across regions and crops. Low-interest loans and subsidised fertilisers/pesticides helped smaller farmers access the package. Government assistance therefore helped spread benefits beyond the initial adopters.\nThe second phase widened access but did not eliminate every regional and income inequality. In an answer on phases, include both the time periods and the change in geographical/social coverage—not just “first limited, second widespread”."},{"id":"ied-review-2-the-five-gains-of-green-revolution","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: The five gains of Green Revolution. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"The five gains of Green Revolution\n**Spurt in crop productivity:** more output per hectare ended a long period of agricultural stagnation. **Substantial rise in acreage:** shorter crop duration and improved cultivation made multiple cropping more feasible; gross cropped area can rise even without an equal rise in physical land area.\n**Shift from subsistence to commercial farming:** output beyond the farmer’s own needs created a marketable surplus. **Change in farmers’ outlook:** farming became a source of profit and reinvestment, rather than only survival.\n**Self-sufficiency in foodgrains and buffer stocks:** larger supplies reduced import dependence and enabled reserves for lean seasons/emergencies. More food reaching the market can restrain prices and support consumers. These are distinct gains—do not collapse them into “production increased”."},{"id":"ied-review-2-marketable-surplus-fallowing-cropping-and-buffer-stocks","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Marketable surplus, fallowing, cropping and buffer stocks. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Marketable surplus, fallowing, cropping and buffer stocks\n**Marketable surplus** is output above the farmer’s on-farm requirements. Selling it provides cash for producer goods and household consumption. It indicates commercialisation, whereas total production includes what the family keeps.\n**Fallowing** means leaving land uncultivated for a period. **Double/multiple cropping** uses land for more than one crop during a year; it raises gross cropped area and employment opportunities. Do not confuse physical land area with the sum of area cultivated across seasons.\n**Buffer stock** is a reserve held for emergencies and seasonal shortages. The textbook links its introduction to the Fourth Plan. Foodgrain reserves support PDS distribution and respond to crop failure or natural disasters."},{"id":"ied-review-2-four-limitations-and-the-equity-problem","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Four limitations and the equity problem. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Four limitations and the equity problem\n**Limited crops:** the strongest gains were concentrated in wheat and rice, without a comparable breakthrough in pulses and many commercial crops. **Uneven spread:** better-watered and supported states gained more; several eastern/central regions benefited less.\n**Limited farming population:** the high input cost excluded many small and marginal farmers. **Economic divide:** prosperous farmers adopted more easily, so differences between rich and poor could widen despite subsidies. These headings concern crop coverage, geography, access and inequality respectively.\nExcessive chemical and groundwater use also raises sustainability concerns discussed in rural development. Higher overall food output is a major achievement, but it does not prove that equity was achieved. A balanced appraisal gives the gains and limitations separately."},{"id":"ied-review-2-arguments-for-and-against-agricultural-subsidies","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Arguments for and against agricultural subsidies. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Arguments for and against agricultural subsidies\n**A vice—huge fiscal burden:** support becomes costly if continued indiscriminately after a technology is established. **Misappropriated benefits:** input suppliers and prosperous farmers may capture benefits intended for poor cultivators. **Wasteful consumption:** artificially cheap water, electricity and chemicals encourage overuse and environmental harm.\n**A virtue—risk and poverty:** farmers face uncertain harvests and many cannot afford the new input package. Subsidies encourage adoption and prevent technology being accessible only to rich farmers. Removing support can widen inequality.\nThe balanced conclusion is to improve targeting and use, rather than treating every subsidy as either necessary forever or wasteful by definition. Explain the mechanism after each heading: cheap water → little incentive to conserve → excessive extraction."},{"id":"ied-review-2-why-public-sector-leadership-was-necessary","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why public-sector leadership was necessary. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why public-sector leadership was necessary\n**Lack of capital:** the investment needed for basic/heavy industry was beyond the capacity of the few established private industrial houses. **Low inducement to invest:** low purchasing power and small markets reduced expected returns; infrastructure projects also had long gestation periods.\n**Growth with social justice:** private profitability alone would not ensure regional balance or prevent concentration of wealth. Public investment could build capacity in neglected areas and supply essential infrastructure.\n**Commanding heights** are strategic industries that support the rest of the economy—transport, communications, basic industry and defence. State control was intended to secure this foundation. The public sector was the leading investor, while private firms still participated in the mixed economy."},{"id":"ied-review-2-ipr-1956-classification-licensing-and-concessions","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: IPR 1956: classification, licensing and concessions. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"IPR 1956: classification, licensing and concessions\n**Three-fold classification:** the first group was exclusively state-owned; the second allowed both sectors with the state taking the leading role; the third was left to regulated private enterprise. This established the state’s leading role and supported the Second Plan’s industrial strategy.\n**Industrial licensing:** private firms needed a licence to establish or expand production. The state could direct scarce resources and encourage investment in backward regions. **Industrial concessions:** tax holidays, subsidised power and related incentives made investment in these regions more attractive.\nThe intention was coordinated industrialisation and regional equity. The later criticism was that excessive licensing also obstructed entry and competition. Keep the original purpose separate from weaknesses in implementation."},{"id":"ied-review-2-small-industries-three-advantages-and-protection","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Small industries: three advantages and protection. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Small industries: three advantages and protection\nThe Karve Committee (1955) emphasised small-scale industry’s role in rural development. The chapter historically defines small industry through the investment permitted in plant/machinery; classification thresholds change, so the economic rationale matters more than treating an old limit as permanent.\n**Labour-intensive/employment-oriented:** more workers can be employed with limited capital. **Locational flexibility/equality across regions:** small units can operate outside major industrial centres. **Small investment/equity across people:** entry is feasible for smaller entrepreneurs, reducing concentration of wealth.\nProtection included reservation of products, lower excise duties and concessional credit. Weak finance, technology and marketing justified support, though prolonged protection could also reduce competitive pressure. Large industry built the basic industrial base; small industry pursued employment and decentralisation."},{"id":"ied-review-2-import-substitution-versus-export-promotion","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Import substitution versus export promotion. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Import substitution versus export promotion\n**Import substitution** saves foreign exchange by producing domestically what was previously imported. Scarce foreign exchange could then finance essential developmental imports such as machinery. **Export promotion** earns foreign exchange by making domestic products competitive abroad. They address the same scarcity from different sides.\nProtection took two forms. **Tariffs** raise the price of imported goods through taxation. **Quotas** limit the quantity that may enter. Both reduce foreign competition and help domestic industries establish themselves.\nThe infant-industry rationale is that new domestic firms need time to develop. The limitation is that protection without pressure to improve can produce high costs, poor quality and weak international competitiveness. Saving foreign exchange is not the same as building export capacity."},{"id":"ied-review-2-the-seven-features-of-the-1950-1990-strategy","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: The seven features of the 1950–1990 strategy. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"The seven features of the 1950–1990 strategy\n**Mixed economy and Five Year Plans** formed the overall framework. **Public enterprises** had the central industrial role; **private enterprises** played a regulated, secondary role in strategic industrialisation.\n**Import substitution** guided production priorities to achieve self-reliance and conserve foreign exchange. **Protection from foreign competition** operated through duties and import quotas.\n**Large-scale industry** built infrastructure and basic/heavy industry. **Small-scale industry** promoted employment, decentralisation and equity. Explain how each instrument related to a planning goal: for example, regional concessions supported equity, while machinery imports supported productive capacity."},{"id":"ied-review-2-achievements-and-limitations-of-the-planning-strategy","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Achievements and limitations of the planning strategy. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Achievements and limitations of the planning strategy\n**Achievements:** agriculture received a breakthrough through land/technical reforms; industrial output expanded; the industrial base diversified beyond cotton and jute into heavy industry and electronics; steel plants and other large projects created infrastructure; small units supported employment and regional spread.\n**Excessive public-sector dominance:** government entered activities private firms could handle. **Public monopolies and losses:** weak accountability encouraged inefficiency, leakage and corruption. **Licence raj:** established firms could obtain licences to block competitors rather than expand production.\n**Protection without competitiveness:** domestic growth did not always deliver internationally competitive prices/quality. **Inward-looking foreign-exchange policy:** import substitution received more emphasis than earning foreign exchange through exports. Agricultural productivity rose, but adequate non-farm jobs did not emerge to absorb surplus labour. The strategy broke stagnation while leaving important structural problems."},{"id":"ied-review-2-plan-wise-objectives-and-the-annual-plan-breaks","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Plan-wise objectives and the annual-plan breaks. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Plan-wise objectives and the annual-plan breaks\n**First Plan, 1951–56:** agriculture and equitable distribution. **Second, 1956–61:** industrial production and heavy industry. **Third, 1961–66:** foodgrain self-sufficiency, employment and lower inequality. Three Annual Plans followed in 1966–69.\n**Fourth, 1969–74:** growth and price stability. **Fifth, 1974–79:** living standards, especially weaker sections. An Annual Plan followed in 1979–80. **Sixth, 1980–85:** poverty removal, reduced inequality and infrastructure. **Seventh, 1985–90:** jobs and agricultural productivity. Two Annual Plans followed in 1990–92.\n**Eighth, 1992–97:** fuller manpower use, elementary education and infrastructure. **Ninth, 1997–2002:** agriculture/rural development, stable growth and population control. **Tenth, 2002–07:** quality of life, health, education and consumption. **Eleventh, 2007–12:** inclusive growth, education/health quality, a second agricultural push, quality jobs and environment. **Twelfth, 2012–17:** faster, sustainable and more inclusive growth. These are the extension table’s short-period priorities; the four common goals apply across plans."},{"id":"ied-review-2-niti-aayog-five-functions","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: NITI Aayog: five functions. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"NITI Aayog: five functions\nNITI Aayog—the National Institution for Transforming India—was established on 1 January 2015, replacing the Planning Commission. It is the government’s policy think tank, chaired by the Prime Minister.\n**Policy formulation:** develop long-term strategies. **Monitoring and evaluation:** assess implementation and offer expert advice. **Cooperative and competitive federalism:** give Centre/states a common platform and encourage improvement through comparative performance.\n**Technology upgradation and capacity building:** improve institutional and technical capability. **Promoting equity:** support inclusive growth so benefits reach all sections. Cooperative federalism means working together; competitive federalism means learning from comparison and striving to perform better."},{"id":"ied-review-2-why-growth-did-not-automatically-solve-poverty","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why growth did not automatically solve poverty. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why growth did not automatically solve poverty\nAn **interventionist state** regulates markets and directly supports provision so essential goods reach poorer sections. Markets allocate according to purchasing power, which is different from allocating according to need.\nThe textbook’s starvation-with-food-stocks example shows the distribution problem: sufficient food production can coexist with hunger when households lack income or PDS delivery fails. The same distinction applies to income, consumption expenditure, land and industrial wealth: an increase in totals does not establish equitable ownership or access.\nLand reforms improved some cultivators’ rights, but shrinking/fragmented holdings constrained efficiency; industrial assets could remain concentrated in large houses. When interpreting poverty or tax data, distinguish a percentage from an absolute number—a falling share can coexist with a rising count if the population grows enough."},{"id":"ied-review-2-why-higher-investment-need-not-produce-rapid-growth","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why higher investment need not produce rapid growth. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why higher investment need not produce rapid growth\nThe chapter asks why saving and investment could rise while output growth remained slow. **High capital-output ratio:** a larger amount of capital was needed for each extra unit of output. **Inventory rather than fixed-capital formation:** foodgrain stocks are investment in accounting terms but do not create machinery or productive capacity in the same way.\n**Low-productivity investment:** resources placed in traditional activities using backward technology generated relatively low returns. The issue is therefore the composition and efficiency of investment, not only its quantity.\nThis is the planning chapter’s saving–investment paradox. Do not confuse it with Macro’s paradox of thrift, where a collective attempt to save more can reduce demand and income."},{"id":"ied-review-3-why-reforms-became-necessary-in-1991","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why reforms became necessary in 1991. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why reforms became necessary in 1991\nThe earlier mixed-economy strategy built an industrial base, but restrictions, inefficient public enterprises and weak competition increasingly constrained productivity. Government expenditure exceeded revenue, public borrowing rose, and debt servicing absorbed resources. Inflation raised living costs and production costs.\nThe immediate trigger was a **balance-of-payments crisis**: foreign-exchange reserves were insufficient for more than about two weeks of imports, making essential imports and external payments difficult. India approached the World Bank and IMF for assistance of approximately $7 billion. Assistance came with conditions promoting reforms and greater participation in world markets.\nThe **New Economic Policy** sought a more competitive economy through liberalisation, privatisation and globalisation. Distinguish the immediate foreign-exchange crisis from the deeper fiscal and structural problems that made the economy vulnerable."},{"id":"ied-review-3-meaning-and-purpose-of-liberalisation","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Meaning and purpose of liberalisation. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Meaning and purpose of liberalisation\n**Liberalisation** means removing unnecessary government controls and restrictions so enterprises have greater freedom to make economic decisions. Earlier controls included industrial licensing, restrictions on expansion, import licensing, foreign-exchange controls and regulated prices or interest rates.\nThe expectation was that competition, market signals and freedom to invest would improve efficiency, quality and productivity. Reforms covered industry, finance, taxation, foreign exchange, trade and investment. Greater freedom does not mean that safety standards, banking supervision or every form of government intervention disappears.\n**Laissez-faire**, discussed in the box, is the stronger idea of minimal state interference, with the state largely protecting life, property and contracts. Liberalisation in India involves reducing controls within a regulated economy, rather than adopting that extreme position."},{"id":"ied-review-3-five-changes-in-industrial-regulation","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Five changes in industrial regulation. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Five changes in industrial regulation\n**Abolition of industrial licensing:** most industries could begin production without obtaining an industrial licence. The textbook lists alcohol, cigarettes, defence equipment, industrial explosives and dangerous chemicals as historical exceptions. These illustrate retained controls in sensitive activities, rather than a permanent list of today’s licensing rules.\n**Contraction of public-sector reservation:** more activities were opened to private enterprise; the chapter traces reservation from 17 industries to eight and subsequently three in its historical account. **De-reservation of small-scale industries:** many goods earlier reserved for small producers could be produced by larger firms.\n**Freedom to expand capacity:** firms obtained greater freedom to determine their scale of production according to market demand. **Freedom to import capital goods and technology:** easier access to machinery and international technology agreements supported modernisation. Explain the control removed and its intended effect, rather than merely writing “less regulation”."},{"id":"ied-review-3-from-financial-controller-to-facilitator","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: From financial controller to facilitator. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"From financial controller to facilitator\nThe financial sector includes banks, stock exchanges and other institutions that mobilise savings and provide funds. Reforms aimed to shift the RBI’s role from detailed control towards facilitating efficient financial activity while retaining prudential supervision and financial stability.\n**Greater competition:** private Indian banks and foreign banks were allowed greater participation. **Operational freedom:** banks obtained more scope to decide lending and deposit interest rates and open branches subject to applicable conditions. **Access to funds:** financial institutions could raise funds more freely in domestic and overseas markets.\n**Foreign institutional investors:** institutions such as mutual funds, pension funds and merchant bankers gained access to Indian financial markets. Greater foreign participation expanded the potential supply of finance. The chapter’s particular ownership ceilings describe its policy period; the lasting concept is reduced entry restrictions combined with continued regulation."},{"id":"ied-review-3-demonetisation-intended-gains-and-immediate-costs","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Demonetisation: intended gains and immediate costs. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Demonetisation: intended gains and immediate costs\nOn **8 November 2016**, the existing ₹500 and ₹1,000 notes ceased to be legal tender under the announced demonetisation programme. People were given a specified period to deposit or exchange eligible notes, and new notes were introduced. Demonetisation changes the legal-tender status of currency; it is different from inflation reducing currency’s purchasing power.\n**Intended gains:** expose unaccounted money and reduce the shadow economy; widen the tax base and tax revenue; increase bank deposits and funds available for lending; disrupt counterfeit currency and terror financing; encourage digital payments; and promote formal banking and financial inclusion. These are policy objectives and possible channels, not proof that every objective was fully achieved.\n**Immediate costs:** cash shortages disrupted everyday transactions; production and trade suffered, especially in the cash-dependent informal sector; workers lost employment or earnings; long queues imposed time and inconvenience; and weaker consumption and investment slowed activity. An appraisal should explain both the intended mechanism and these adjustment costs."},{"id":"ied-review-3-direct-taxes-indirect-taxes-and-tax-reform","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Direct taxes, indirect taxes and tax reform. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Direct taxes, indirect taxes and tax reform\n**Fiscal policy** concerns government revenue and expenditure. A **direct tax** is paid by the person on whom its legal burden falls, as with income tax; its burden is not normally shifted to another person. An **indirect tax**, such as GST or customs duty, can be passed on through prices.\nTax reforms sought to rationalise rates, simplify procedures and improve compliance. Very high rates and complicated rules can encourage evasion and discourage enterprise. Lower or more reasonable rates, fewer complications and a wider tax base were expected to encourage voluntary payment and improve revenue collection.\nThe objective is not simply “cut every tax”. A sound explanation connects reasonable rates and simpler administration to incentives, compliance, coverage and the government’s ability to finance development."},{"id":"ied-review-3-gst-value-addition-advantages-and-limitations","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: GST: value addition, advantages and limitations. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"GST: value addition, advantages and limitations\n**Goods and Services Tax**, introduced on **1 July 2017**, brought many indirect taxes into a common framework. **Supply-based tax:** liability is linked to the supply of goods and services. **Value addition:** tax is intended to apply to the value added at successive stages. **Input-tax credit:** eligible tax already paid on inputs can be credited against output-tax liability, reducing tax-on-tax or cascading.\n**Advantages:** a simpler and more uniform indirect-tax system; a more integrated national market; greater transparency through recorded transactions; and potential improvement in compliance and revenue, with less scope for unrecorded business. A common framework does not mean one identical rate for every product.\n**Limitations:** some products remain outside GST’s ordinary coverage, including alcoholic liquor for human consumption and specified petroleum products; electricity is also outside GST. Firms can face compliance burdens and uncertainty when procedures or rates change. Learn the mechanism and these strengths and constraints rather than treating a changing rate table as the definition."},{"id":"ied-review-3-devaluation-and-market-based-exchange-rates","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Devaluation and market-based exchange rates. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Devaluation and market-based exchange rates\nIn 1991, the rupee was devalued to address the external-payments crisis. A lower external value makes Indian exports cheaper to foreign buyers and imports more expensive in rupees, potentially improving export competitiveness and restraining imports. The textbook describes the two-step adjustment in July 1991.\nExchange-rate reforms subsequently gave market demand and supply a greater role in determining the rupee’s value. This facilitated international transactions and linked India more closely with world markets. **Devaluation** is an official reduction under a fixed or administered arrangement; **depreciation** is a fall associated with market forces.\nThe effect on the trade balance is not automatic: buyers must respond to price changes, and essential imports may remain necessary. The intended channel is the change in the relative prices of exports and imports."},{"id":"ied-review-3-opening-trade-and-investment","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Opening trade and investment. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Opening trade and investment\n**Removal of quantitative restrictions:** limits on the amount of many imports were reduced or removed. **Reduction of tariffs:** lower import duties reduced protection and exposed domestic producers to competition. **Relaxation of import licensing:** most imports no longer required the earlier licence system, with sensitive health, safety and environmental restrictions retained.\n**Removal or reduction of export duties and restrictions:** these measures aimed to encourage exports and improve international competitiveness. Easier foreign investment and access to imported inputs, machinery and technology were intended to improve production quality and efficiency.\nTariffs change the price of imports; quotas restrict their quantity. For an exam answer, keep that distinction explicit and explain why trade reform can benefit consumers while placing adjustment pressure on protected domestic firms."},{"id":"ied-review-3-privatisation-disinvestment-and-the-public-sector-journey","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Privatisation, disinvestment and the public-sector journey. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Privatisation, disinvestment and the public-sector journey\n**Privatisation** means reducing or withdrawing government ownership or management of an enterprise. It may involve transfer of management, sale of part of the government’s equity, or complete sale to private owners. **Disinvestment** means selling government shareholdings: a minority share sale need not transfer management or make an enterprise fully private.\nPublic enterprises originally helped diversify production, build basic industries and infrastructure, and address activities private capital could not adequately undertake. Over time, some faced inefficiency, losses, excessive interference, leakages, pilferage and corruption. Reforms aimed to reduce this burden and improve accountability and performance.\n**Navratna, Maharatna and Miniratna** status gives selected public enterprises greater managerial and financial autonomy. Autonomy can improve investment decisions and competitiveness while government ownership continues. These categories are therefore not themselves privatisation, and the status of individual firms can change."},{"id":"ied-review-3-benefits-and-social-costs-of-privatisation","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Benefits and social costs of privatisation. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Benefits and social costs of privatisation\n**Efficiency:** owners with a direct financial stake have an incentive to control costs and improve output. **Competition and modernisation:** pressure to survive can encourage better technology and management. **Diversification and reinvestment:** commercially viable firms can develop new products and reinvest profits. **Consumer choice:** competing firms may offer better quality and a wider range of goods.\n**Social-interest concern:** commercial profitability may receive priority over affordable essential services or employment. **Weak purchasing power:** poorer groups may be unable to buy services even when the market supplies them efficiently. Regulation and public support remain important where social benefits exceed private returns.\nThe **Sircilla powerloom case** connects higher electricity charges and production costs to reduced output, lower wages and distress among weavers. Use the chain “input cost → viability → employment and earnings” to explain why a reform’s distributional effects matter, rather than assuming either all private enterprise or all public enterprise is inherently efficient."},{"id":"ied-review-3-globalisation-and-its-five-policy-supports","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Globalisation and its five policy supports. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Globalisation and its five policy supports\n**Globalisation** is the increasing integration and interdependence of economies through flows of goods, services, capital, technology and expertise. Liberalisation and privatisation created conditions for greater integration; globalisation describes the resulting connections with the world economy.\n**Encouragement of foreign investment:** relaxation of equity restrictions and a more facilitative foreign-exchange framework, including FEMA. **Rupee convertibility for current transactions:** easier conversion for trade in goods and services, interest, dividends and remittances; this is distinct from unrestricted capital-account convertibility.\n**Long-term trade policy:** a more predictable outward-looking framework. **Reduction in tariffs:** lower import duties. **Reduction in quantitative restrictions:** removal of many import quantity controls, with the chapter highlighting 2001 in the historical reform sequence. These measures increased competition and international access; they did not abolish every restriction."},{"id":"ied-review-3-outsourcing-why-india-became-a-service-destination","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Outsourcing: why India became a service destination. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Outsourcing: why India became a service destination\n**Outsourcing** occurs when an organisation contracts work to an outside provider instead of performing it internally. International outsourcing places that work in another country. Examples include call centres, record keeping, accountancy, banking support, music and film editing, medical transcription, clinical services and online teaching.\nImproved information and communication technology allows work and results to move rapidly across borders. India’s relatively low labour costs and large pool of skilled, English-speaking workers made it attractive for many service activities. Firms seek cost savings and expertise; Indian workers and firms can gain income, employment and export opportunities.\nThe benefit depends on skills and access to infrastructure, so it may be concentrated in particular workers and cities. Outsourcing is one channel of globalisation, not a synonym for every foreign investment or every export."},{"id":"ied-review-3-wto-role-expected-benefits-and-unequal-access","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: WTO: role, expected benefits and unequal access. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"WTO: role, expected benefits and unequal access\nThe **World Trade Organization** began on **1 January 1995**, succeeding the GATT framework established in 1948. The Marrakesh agreement associated with its creation involved 123 participating countries. Its purpose is a rules-based international trading system.\n**Five roles:** promote freer trade; discourage arbitrary restrictions through common rules; improve competition and market access; provide a framework for trade agreements and their implementation; and expand markets so resources can be used more efficiently. A **bilateral** agreement involves two countries; a **multilateral** agreement involves several.\n**India’s expected gains:** more export opportunities; textile employment and exports following removal of Multi-Fibre Arrangement quotas; and agricultural opportunities if developed countries reduced subsidies and barriers. **Concern:** implementation can be unequal—richer countries may retain support or barriers, and capital often moves more freely than labour. Expected gains should be distinguished from guaranteed gains."},{"id":"ied-review-3-first-generation-and-second-generation-reforms","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: First-generation and second-generation reforms. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"First-generation and second-generation reforms\n**First-generation reforms** mainly changed administrative and executive controls, such as licensing, trade restrictions and investment approvals. These could often be implemented through government policy decisions.\n**Second-generation reforms** seek deeper institutional changes, including legal frameworks and areas such as labour, governance and public services. They may require legislation, coordination and broader political agreement, making implementation more difficult or slower.\nThe distinction explains why announcing market-opening measures is easier than ensuring effective institutions, skills, infrastructure and social protection. Those complementary conditions affect whether the initial reforms deliver broad benefits."},{"id":"ied-review-3-two-different-jobs-of-the-reform-programme","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Two different jobs of the reform programme. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Two different jobs of the reform programme\n**Stabilisation measures** address immediate macroeconomic imbalances: the balance-of-payments crisis, inflation and unsustainable fiscal deficits. They aim to restore the economy’s ability to make payments and maintain stability in the short run.\n**Structural adjustment measures** change how the economy operates over the longer run: improving efficiency, increasing competition, reducing restrictive controls and encouraging investment and modernisation. Industrial, financial and trade reforms belong to this broader restructuring.\nThe two are related but different. Securing foreign exchange can ease an immediate crisis without by itself making firms efficient; improving competition can raise long-term productivity while imposing short-run adjustment costs."},{"id":"ied-review-3-nine-favourable-outcomes-growth-stability-and-choice","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Nine favourable outcomes: growth, stability and choice. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Nine favourable outcomes: growth, stability and choice\n**Vibrant economy:** greater openness and competition encouraged economic activity. **Stimulant to industrial production:** access to capital, technology and markets improved opportunities for expansion and modernisation. **Check on fiscal deficit:** fiscal discipline and reform aimed to reduce the government’s imbalance. **Larger foreign-exchange reserves:** improved external inflows strengthened the payments position.\n**Check on inflation:** stabilisation and improved supplies helped contain price pressures in parts of the reform period. **Consumer sovereignty:** wider choice and competition increased the importance of consumer preferences. **Private and foreign investment:** a more liberal framework encouraged new capital and enterprise.\n**Emerging economic power:** faster growth and international integration increased India’s economic significance. **From monopoly to competition:** reduced protection and entry barriers challenged established producers. These are the chapter’s favourable appraisal headings; performance varied by sector and year, so a rise in investment or reserves alone does not prove that every social objective was met."},{"id":"ied-review-3-fdi-versus-foreign-institutional-investment","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: FDI versus foreign institutional investment. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"FDI versus foreign institutional investment\n**Foreign direct investment (FDI)** involves a lasting business interest and a significant role in ownership or management—for example, establishing a factory or acquiring a controlling business stake. It can bring technology, management and production capacity as well as finance.\n**Foreign institutional investment (FII)** generally involves institutional investment in financial securities such as shares and bonds without taking managerial control of the enterprise. It supplies portfolio finance and can be more readily withdrawn as investors change their preferences.\nBoth are foreign investment, but their relationship with production, control and financial-market volatility differs. Do not identify every purchase of an Indian share by a foreign institution as the establishment of a foreign-controlled business."},{"id":"ied-review-3-why-agriculture-and-industry-did-not-benefit-equally","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why agriculture and industry did not benefit equally. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why agriculture and industry did not benefit equally\n**Neglect of agriculture:** lower public investment in irrigation, power, roads and research constrained productivity; reduced subsidies could raise input costs; lower import duties exposed farmers to foreign competition; and a shift towards export-oriented cash crops could create risks for food production. Agricultural weakness also hurts industry by reducing raw-material supply and rural demand.\n**Industrial slowdown and uneven competition:** cheaper imports could displace domestic production, while smaller firms struggled to upgrade technology. Indian exporters could still face barriers in richer countries, creating an uneven playing field. Infrastructure and finance constraints limited the ability to respond to competition.\n**Unemployment or jobless growth:** capital-intensive technology can expand output without proportionate employment. Workers displaced from protected or public enterprises may lack the skills required in expanding sectors. Explain why higher GDP does not automatically mean enough suitable jobs."},{"id":"ied-review-3-the-remaining-six-criticisms-of-reforms","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: The remaining six criticisms of reforms. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"The remaining six criticisms of reforms\n**Disinvestment:** selling valuable public assets cheaply or using proceeds mainly for routine expenditure can weaken long-term public wealth. **Fiscal policy:** lower customs duties and tax concessions can constrain revenue and funds for social expenditure if wider activity and compliance do not compensate.\n**Lopsided growth:** rapid expansion of services, particularly IT, can coexist with weak agriculture and manufacturing. **Urban concentration:** firms prefer cities with better infrastructure and skilled labour, widening regional and rural–urban gaps. **Economic colonialism:** excessive dependence on foreign firms, technology or markets can reduce domestic economic autonomy.\n**Consumerism:** advertising and demonstration effects may encourage consumption beyond means and weaken saving priorities. The related **dualism** box describes the coexistence of modern, high-income activities and traditional, low-productivity ones. A balanced policy encourages useful investment and competition while supporting infrastructure, domestic capabilities, weaker sections and strategic needs."},{"id":"ied-review-4-human-resources-human-capital-and-formation","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Human resources, human capital and formation. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Human resources, human capital and formation\nPeople become an economic resource through their capacity to work, learn and organise production. **Human capital** is the stock of knowledge, skills, abilities and health embodied in people that increases their productive capacity. **Human capital formation** is the process of adding to this stock through education, training, health and related investments.\nThe stock–flow distinction matters: a person’s existing skills are human capital; acquiring additional skills during a period is human capital formation. Expenditure is an investment when it develops capabilities that generate benefits over time, rather than merely meeting a current need.\nHuman capital formation can raise earnings and national output, but its benefits also include improved judgement, adaptability and participation. The textbook’s definition associated with Meier emphasises developing people’s skills and capabilities for productive activity."},{"id":"ied-review-4-productive-capacity-versus-human-well-being","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Productive capacity versus human well-being. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Productive capacity versus human well-being\n**Human capital** treats education and health as means of raising productivity and income. **Human development** treats people’s well-being, capabilities and freedom as valuable ends in themselves. Education and good health therefore matter even when their immediate contribution to market output is small.\nAn educated, healthy worker may produce more; that is the human-capital argument. A person also deserves opportunities to learn and live a healthy life; that is the human-development argument. The approaches overlap, but the second has a broader purpose.\nFor a distinction question, compare the objective, the role of education and health, and the criterion of success: higher productive capacity and earnings versus a better and more capable human life."},{"id":"ied-review-4-seven-bases-of-distinction","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Seven bases of distinction. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Seven bases of distinction\n**Formation:** physical capital is created using material inputs; human capital develops through education, health and training. **Decision making:** entrepreneurs usually make conscious investment decisions about machinery, whereas human-capital decisions may initially be made by parents, institutions or government on a person’s behalf.\n**Tangibility:** machines and buildings are tangible; skills and knowledge are embodied capabilities. **Separability:** a machine can be sold separately from its owner, but a person’s skills cannot be separated from that person. **Mobility:** both can move, but human movement is also constrained by nationality, culture, family and immigration rules.\n**Depreciation:** physical capital wears out; human capabilities can deteriorate through ageing, poor health or obsolete knowledge, making retraining important. **Benefits:** physical investment mainly generates private returns, while education and health also produce substantial social benefits. Use these as comparison bases, not as a claim that physical investment never benefits society."},{"id":"ied-review-4-education-and-health-as-investments","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Education and health as investments. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Education and health as investments\n**Expenditure on education** develops knowledge and skills, improves employability and productivity, and can generate higher lifetime earnings. Its costs include fees and materials as well as the earnings forgone while studying. Benefits arrive over time, so education is treated as an investment in people.\n**Expenditure on health** improves the ability to work regularly and effectively. The textbook includes **preventive medicine** such as vaccination, **curative medicine** for treatment, **social medicine** through health awareness, and **sanitation** through safe water, hygiene and waste disposal.\nHealthy people lose fewer working days, learn more effectively and can use their skills better. Education and health reinforce each other: knowledge improves health choices, while health improves learning and attendance."},{"id":"ied-review-4-learning-beyond-school","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Learning beyond school. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Learning beyond school\n**On-the-job training** builds skills while a person is employed. It may take place inside the firm under experienced workers or through an external training institution. Firms incur training costs because better skills can improve quality, efficiency and output; arrangements encouraging workers to remain help firms recover that investment.\n**Adult education** gives people opportunities to gain literacy, knowledge and practical capabilities after the usual school age. Government agencies and voluntary organisations can provide such programmes, enabling people excluded from initial schooling to participate more effectively in work and society.\nTraining is especially important when technology changes. It maintains the value of existing human capital and addresses the difference between possessing a qualification and possessing the particular skills a job requires."},{"id":"ied-review-4-why-moving-and-searching-can-build-productive-capacity","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why moving and searching can build productive capacity. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why moving and searching can build productive capacity\n**Migration** may be internal or international. People move to obtain better employment and earnings or to use their skills more effectively. Costs include travel, higher living expenses and the psychological cost of separation from family and familiar surroundings; the decision compares these with expected future benefits.\n**Information** about educational institutions, training, vacancies, wages and working conditions helps people choose where to invest in skills and where to work. Collecting it takes time and money, but can reduce mismatch and improve returns to human capabilities.\nThese sources do not necessarily create a new skill immediately. They improve the acquisition, allocation or use of human capital—an important reason the textbook includes them alongside education, health and training."},{"id":"ied-review-4-three-ways-human-capital-supports-development","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Three ways human capital supports development. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Three ways human capital supports development\n**Change in emotional and material environment:** education broadens aspirations, reasoning and attitudes towards change, while a skilled workforce improves the material conditions of production. People become better able to adopt new methods and pursue improved living standards.\n**Higher productivity of physical capital:** trained and healthy workers use equipment efficiently, reduce waste and develop innovations and inventions. The same machinery can yield different output depending on the capabilities of those using it.\n**Greater participation and equality:** wider access to capabilities enables more people, including disadvantaged groups and women, to contribute to development and share its benefits. The Seventh Five-Year Plan recognised human resources as central to development. The relationship is two-way: human capital promotes growth, and higher income finances further education and health."},{"id":"ied-review-4-why-years-of-schooling-do-not-tell-the-whole-story","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why years of schooling do not tell the whole story. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why years of schooling do not tell the whole story\nIndicators such as enrolment, years of schooling and teacher–pupil ratios measure access or inputs, but do not completely capture learning quality. Similarly, health expenditure does not automatically measure how healthy people are or how effectively healthcare is delivered.\nCountries with similar measured education or health can have different growth rates because infrastructure, institutions, technology and employment opportunities also matter. This does not show that human capital is unimportant; it shows that measurement is incomplete and growth has several causes.\nUse the full chain: investment must improve actual capabilities, those capabilities must be used productively, and complementary conditions must support their use."},{"id":"ied-review-4-five-obstacles-in-india","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Five obstacles in India. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Five obstacles in India\n**Rising population:** resources must serve more people, limiting improvements per person when investment does not keep pace. **Brain drain:** skilled people trained using domestic resources may migrate abroad, reducing the immediate domestic return on that investment.\n**Deficient manpower planning:** education and training may not match the number and type of jobs available, producing skill shortages alongside educated unemployment. **Inadequate on-the-job training:** especially in traditional and small activities, workers may have few opportunities to update practical skills.\n**Low academic standards:** weak teaching, facilities or assessment can produce qualifications without adequate learning. The answer is therefore not merely “spend more”: expand access, improve quality, align training with opportunities and make effective use of existing skills."},{"id":"ied-review-4-literacy-education-and-seven-benefits","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Literacy, education and seven benefits. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Literacy, education and seven benefits\n**Literacy** is the ability to read and write with understanding; **education** is the broader development of knowledge, reasoning, skills and personality. A literate person is not necessarily well educated. Education is commonly organised into primary, secondary and tertiary levels.\n**Responsible citizens:** informed people make better social and civic choices. **Science and technology:** education supports invention and adoption of improved methods. **Efficient use of resources:** knowledge reduces waste and improves decisions. **Wider mental horizon:** it develops reasoning and openness to new ideas.\n**Participation in growth:** skills allow people to enter productive activities. **Higher cultural standards:** education develops understanding of social and cultural life. **Human personality and empowerment:** it builds confidence, independence and agency, particularly for women and disadvantaged groups. These social gains extend beyond the student’s own earnings."},{"id":"ied-review-4-four-reasons-for-public-involvement","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Four reasons for public involvement. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Four reasons for public involvement\n**Large investment requirements:** institutions, teachers and facilities need substantial resources and long-term commitment. **Affordability:** private providers charging to recover costs can exclude poorer families, even when educating them would benefit society.\n**Standards and information:** students and patients cannot always judge quality in advance; regulation and reliable standards help protect them. **Rights and social responsibility:** access to basic education and healthcare should not depend entirely on purchasing power.\nGovernment therefore finances provision, operates institutions, regulates quality and supports access. NCERT supports school curriculum and educational materials, UGC has roles in higher education, and AICTE concerns technical education. ICMR supports medical research. Different bodies serve different functions; do not treat them as interchangeable."},{"id":"ied-review-4-how-to-interpret-expenditure-and-the-6-recommendation","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: How to interpret expenditure and the 6% recommendation. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"How to interpret expenditure and the 6% recommendation\nEducational expenditure can be expressed as a **share of total government expenditure**, showing its budget priority, or as a **share of GDP**, showing public resources committed relative to the economy’s size. These denominators answer different questions.\nThe textbook reports a rise from 7.92% to 16.54% of government expenditure and from 0.64% to 4.47% of GDP between 1952 and 2020. These are historical public-expenditure figures, not today’s rates and not total public-plus-private spending. The Education Commission of 1964–66 recommended expenditure of around **6% of GDP**.\nThe Tapas Majumdar Committee in 1999 estimated substantial additional resources—about ₹1.37 lakh crore over its proposed programme—for universal elementary education. The **Right to Education Act, 2009** provides for free and compulsory education for children aged **6–14**. The chapter also discusses education cess as a financing measure; distinguish historical financing arrangements from the enduring right and spending objective."},{"id":"ied-review-4-elementary-higher-and-state-level-spending","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Elementary, higher and state-level spending. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Elementary, higher and state-level spending\nElementary education receives a large absolute share because it serves many children, while expenditure **per student** is generally higher in tertiary education. A larger total allocation and a larger per-student allocation are not the same thing.\nThe lesson is to expand resources across levels, rather than finance basic education only by weakening higher education. Higher education develops teachers, researchers, professionals and technical capabilities needed throughout the system. States differ greatly in resources, enrolment needs and spending per person; state governments finance much of public education.\nWhen comparing states, specify the year, the denominator and what is included. Higher spending alone does not establish better outcomes unless facilities, teaching quality, attendance and learning also improve."},{"id":"ied-review-4-progress-knowledge-economy-and-nep-2020","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Progress, knowledge economy and NEP 2020. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Progress, knowledge economy and NEP 2020\nIndia has expanded educational institutions, enrolment and literacy since independence. The textbook compares literacy of **18.33% in 1951** with **74.04% in 2011**. These census figures demonstrate long-run progress, while also showing that universal literacy had not yet been achieved.\nA **knowledge economy** relies heavily on the creation, communication and productive use of knowledge. IT, digital networks and access to information can improve productivity, but require both access and quality skills. A large number of graduates is not sufficient if their learning does not match changing work.\nThe **National Education Policy 2020** emphasises broader and multidisciplinary learning. Areas such as artificial intelligence and big data demand combinations of mathematical, scientific, technical and communication skills. The challenge is to connect expanded educational access with strong learning and adaptability."},{"id":"ied-review-4-six-continuing-gaps-and-the-poverty-cycle","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Six continuing gaps and the poverty cycle. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Six continuing gaps and the poverty cycle\n**Large number of illiterates:** progress in the literacy rate still leaves many people without basic capabilities. **Inadequate vocational orientation:** degree-focused education and preference for white-collar work can coexist with shortages of practical skills. **Gender bias:** girls may face early withdrawal, household responsibilities and unequal opportunities.\n**Low rural access:** distance, inadequate facilities and teacher shortages restrict learning. **Expensive private education:** unequal purchasing power creates unequal educational opportunities. **Inadequate public expenditure:** weak funding limits institutions, staff and quality.\nThe education pyramid narrows sharply towards higher levels. Poverty can force children out of school, while poor education limits earnings and reproduces poverty. Formal entitlement under RTE is not enough if documentation barriers, indirect costs, poor facilities and weak teaching prevent meaningful participation. The remedy combines access, retention, quality and practical relevance."},{"id":"ied-review-5-meaning-importance-and-the-rural-development-agenda","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Meaning, importance and the rural development agenda. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Meaning, importance and the rural development agenda\n**Rural development** is a comprehensive action plan to improve rural economic and social life. It integrates productive employment, income, education, health, infrastructure and environmental sustainability. Agriculture is central, but rural development is broader than increasing crop output.\nA large population depends on rural activities, and poverty and low productivity remain concentrated there. Slower agricultural growth and limited non-farm opportunities can widen rural–urban differences even when national GDP rises. Development must improve crop productivity and profitability while creating opportunities outside cultivation.\n**Lingering challenges:** adequate credit and effective agricultural marketing. **Emerging challenges:** diversification of productive activities and sustainable practices such as organic farming. Roads, power, irrigation, research, education, healthcare and skills support all these efforts."},{"id":"ied-review-5-why-farmers-borrow-and-the-three-credit-periods","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why farmers borrow and the three credit periods. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why farmers borrow and the three credit periods\nFarmers incur costs before receiving harvest income. Small holdings and low retained surplus make it difficult to finance seeds, fertilisers, electricity and labour out of savings. Credit bridges the gap between sowing and sale and finances lasting improvements.\n**Short-term credit, roughly 6–12 months:** seasonal inputs and recurring expenses. **Medium-term credit, about 1–5 years:** machinery, fencing and improvement of wells. **Long-term credit, about 5–20 years:** land purchase and permanent improvements. The purpose and repayment period should match the investment’s benefits.\n**Productive credit** supports income-generating activity; **consumption or other non-productive credit** meets household and social expenditure. Borrowing for essential consumption may still be necessary, but repayment is difficult when it does not create additional income."},{"id":"ied-review-5-from-moneylenders-to-social-banking","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: From moneylenders to social banking. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"From moneylenders to social banking\nMoneylenders, traders and commission agents traditionally dominated rural credit. High interest, manipulation of accounts, tied sales and weak bargaining power could leave farmers in a persistent debt trap. Their accessibility did not ensure fair terms.\n**Bank nationalisation in 1969** promoted social banking and wider rural access. The Green Revolution increased the need for credit because purchased inputs and commercial production required more working capital. The aim was to provide timely, affordable institutional finance instead of dependence on exploitative arrangements.\nThe **multi-agency approach** uses government, cooperative institutions, commercial banks, regional rural banks and land-development banks, supported by NABARD and the RBI. These institutions have complementary roles rather than identical functions."},{"id":"ied-review-5-cooperatives-commercial-banks-rrbs-and-land-development-banks","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Cooperatives, commercial banks, RRBs and land-development banks. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Cooperatives, commercial banks, RRBs and land-development banks\n**Cooperative credit societies** pool resources and provide accessible, timely credit. Their objectives include reducing moneylender dependence, spreading finance across regions and supporting weaker or neglected areas; some also provide agricultural advice and related assistance.\n**Commercial banks**, including SBI established in 1955 and banks brought under public ownership in 1969, finance agriculture directly and through cooperative arrangements. **Regional rural banks** focus on rural and relatively backward areas, particularly smaller farmers and weaker sections.\n**Land-development banks** mainly provide longer-term finance for land and permanent agricultural improvements. Availability alone is insufficient: loan timing, terms, accessibility and suitability for small borrowers determine whether credit helps production."},{"id":"ied-review-5-apex-support-and-coordination-of-rural-finance","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Apex support and coordination of rural finance. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Apex support and coordination of rural finance\n**NABARD**, established in **1982**, is the apex institution supporting agricultural and rural development finance. Its main roles are providing or refinancing funds, strengthening and restructuring the credit-delivery system, training and coordinating institutions, and monitoring projects supported through its refinancing.\nThe **RBI’s supporting functions** in the textbook include short-term refinancing; developing cooperative credit; supporting funds for longer-term rural finance; helping state participation in cooperative institutions; training personnel; undertaking rural-credit surveys; encouraging rural branch expansion; and providing remittance facilities and guidance.\nNABARD and the RBI strengthen the system behind the lender. A farmer’s ordinary loan may come from a bank or cooperative even when an apex institution supplies refinancing or institutional support."},{"id":"ied-review-5-how-shgs-reach-borrowers-without-collateral","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: How SHGs reach borrowers without collateral. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"How SHGs reach borrowers without collateral\n**Self-help groups (SHGs)** bring members, often poor rural women, together to save small amounts regularly. Pooled savings and bank linkages provide small loans for household needs and self-employment. **Microcredit** means credit in small amounts suited to borrowers poorly served by conventional banking.\nMembers’ mutual knowledge and group responsibility can substitute for conventional collateral, with comparatively simple formalities and manageable interest. Activities such as livestock rearing, food processing and small enterprises can increase income, confidence and financial independence.\nThe **Kudumbashree example in Kerala** illustrates women’s savings and group-based enterprise. The value is not just the size of a loan: regular saving, collective decision making and access to institutions can strengthen bargaining power and participation."},{"id":"ied-review-5-timely-farm-credit-and-access-to-banking","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Timely farm credit and access to banking. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Timely farm credit and access to banking\nThe **Kisan Credit Card (KCC)** provides a convenient credit arrangement for eligible agricultural needs, with an assessed limit and access when funds are required. It reduces the need to complete a fresh full loan process for every seasonal expense.\n**Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana**, launched in **2014**, expands access to bank accounts and associated financial services. The chapter discusses basic accounts, eligible overdraft and insurance facilities, and direct receipt of wages, pensions or benefits into accounts.\nFinancial inclusion involves usable services, not just opening an account. Affordable borrowing, regular saving, reliable payments and understanding how to use facilities determine whether access improves a household’s position."},{"id":"ied-review-5-achievements-weaknesses-and-the-way-forward","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Achievements, weaknesses and the way forward. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Achievements, weaknesses and the way forward\n**Achievements:** wider organised credit, rural branch expansion and NABARD support reduced some dependence on moneylenders. Credit helped farmers buy modern inputs and move towards commercial production.\n**Weaknesses:** collateral and formalities can exclude poorer borrowers; low saving and weak deposit mobilisation constrain lending; overdue loans weaken institutions; and the social priority of rural lending can weaken under commercial pressure. Distinguish genuine inability to repay after crop failure from wilful default.\nCredit should support viable production, with suitable repayment terms, insurance, information and marketing support. Repeated lending without a realistic income source does not remove poverty; equally, treating every poor borrower as uncreditworthy excludes the people social banking is meant to serve."},{"id":"ied-review-5-marketing-is-a-chain-not-just-a-sale","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Marketing is a chain, not just a sale. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Marketing is a chain, not just a sale\n**Agricultural marketing** includes assembling produce, processing, grading, packaging, storing, transporting, selling and distributing it. A farmer’s final return depends on this entire chain, not only on the price quoted at the farm gate.\nFarmers may face faulty weights, manipulated accounts, excessive commissions, inadequate market information and poor transport or storage. Debt can tie them to a particular trader. Without storage or cash to wait, they may sell immediately after harvest when prices are low.\nThese weaknesses justify intervention: improving infrastructure and fair dealing increases the share of the final price received by producers and reduces waste. Credit and marketing are linked because urgent repayment can force a distress sale."},{"id":"ied-review-5-four-groups-of-government-measures","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Four groups of government measures. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Four groups of government measures\n**Regulated markets:** market committees and rules improve weighing, transparency and trading practices, reducing manipulation. **Physical infrastructure:** roads, rail links, warehouses, cold storage and processing facilities reduce transport costs, spoilage and forced sale.\n**Cooperative marketing:** farmers combine produce and bargain collectively, sharing services and reducing intermediary dependence. Gujarat’s milk cooperatives illustrate its potential; weak finance, coordination and management can limit success.\n**Support-price and distribution measures:** MSP provides a declared support benchmark for specified crops; procurement by agencies such as FCI contributes to buffer stocks; and the PDS distributes essential goods to eligible households through fair-price shops. These are distinct functions—price support, stock holding and consumer distribution—not three names for the same operation."},{"id":"ied-review-5-direct-markets-and-contracts-with-firms","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Direct markets and contracts with firms. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Direct markets and contracts with firms\n**Direct farmer–consumer markets** reduce intermediary stages and can improve the producer’s share while offering consumers fresher produce. Examples include Apni Mandi in Punjab and Haryana, Rythu Bazaars in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, and Uzhavar Sandies in Tamil Nadu.\n**Contract arrangements with firms** can specify price, quality and purchase conditions before production. Firms may provide inputs, technical knowledge or assured markets, reducing some uncertainty for farmers.\nBenefits depend on fair terms and enforcement. A small farmer may have weaker bargaining power than a large buyer, and rejection or quality disputes can create new risks. Explain both the opportunity and the conditions needed for it to work."},{"id":"ied-review-5-two-dimensions-of-diversification","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Two dimensions of diversification. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Two dimensions of diversification\n**Crop diversification** means growing a wider variety of crops rather than relying on one crop or the traditional pattern. It can spread weather and price risks and open opportunities in higher-value crops. Subsistence farmers may hesitate because familiar food crops provide household security.\n**Diversification of productive activity** means moving some labour and resources from cultivation into allied and non-farm activities. Animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services create additional sources of income.\nBenefits include higher and more stable earnings, productive use of surplus or disguised-unemployed labour, balanced rural development and less pressure to migrate to cities. Diversification should create viable opportunities rather than merely move people into another low-income activity."},{"id":"ied-review-5-livestock-and-the-white-revolution","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Livestock and the White Revolution. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Livestock and the White Revolution\n**Animal husbandry** includes rearing livestock for milk, meat, eggs, wool and other products. Mixed crop–livestock farming provides income at different times and can support households in dry regions. Women and landless households often participate, making livestock important for inclusive rural livelihoods.\nConstraints include low animal productivity, inadequate knowledge, poor breeds, insufficient credit and limited veterinary care. Better breeding, feed, disease control, training and market access can raise returns.\n**Operation Flood**, launched in **1970**, organised milk collection, grading, processing and distribution through cooperative networks. Pooling milk and linking rural producers with urban markets improved bargaining and income opportunities. The resulting expansion is associated with the **White Revolution**, with Gujarat’s cooperatives a prominent example."},{"id":"ied-review-5-two-other-sources-of-rural-employment","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Two other sources of rural employment. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Two other sources of rural employment\n**Fisheries:** inland fishing uses rivers, canals, ponds and other inland waters; marine fishing uses seas and oceans. Fishing and related processing and marketing support livelihoods, including women’s employment. Poverty, indebtedness, inadequate technology and finance constrain producers, while overfishing and pollution threaten sustainability.\n**Horticulture** includes fruits, vegetables, flowers, plantation crops, nurseries and related activities. Diverse climatic conditions create opportunities for higher-value production and employment. The chapter’s **Golden Revolution** discussion links research and technology transfer with expansion in fruits, vegetables and honey.\nHorticulture needs irrigation, reliable electricity, cold chains, transport, processing, research and market information because many products perish quickly. Switching acreage can also reduce production of staples such as pulses. Assess profitability alongside nutrition, risk and environmental needs."},{"id":"ied-review-5-tanwa-rural-information-and-sagy","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: TANWA, rural information and SAGY. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"TANWA, rural information and SAGY\n**Tamil Nadu Women in Agriculture (TANWA)** trained women in improved agricultural practices. Better skills raised productivity and encouraged savings, groups and enterprises such as mushroom production and small manufactures. The example links training to both earnings and empowerment.\n**Information technology** can provide weather forecasts, soil advice, prices, employment information and access to government services. Rural information kiosks and the National Virtual Academy for Rural Prosperity illustrate connecting villages with useful knowledge. Information helps only when it is timely, understandable and supported by access to the relevant service.\n**Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY)**, launched in October 2014, encourages MPs to develop model villages through local participation and coordinated improvements in health, nutrition, education and livelihoods. The chapter describes indicative village populations of 3,000–5,000 in plains and 1,000–3,000 in hilly areas, and selection outside the MP’s own or spouse’s village."},{"id":"ied-review-5-meaning-and-five-advantages-of-organic-farming","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Meaning and five advantages of organic farming. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Meaning and five advantages of organic farming\n**Organic farming** relies on ecological processes, organic manure, compost and biological methods to maintain soil health and manage pests, reducing reliance on synthetic chemical inputs. Its aim is sustainable production that protects the resource base for future generations.\n**Reduced dependence on non-renewable inputs:** many synthetic inputs rely on petroleum-based production. **Environmental protection:** less chemical runoff can reduce soil and groundwater pollution. **Soil fertility:** organic matter helps maintain soil structure and biological activity.\n**Food and health appeal:** consumers may value reduced chemical residues and product quality, though organic status alone does not guarantee every nutritional claim. **Suitability for small producers:** locally available inputs and labour-based methods can reduce dependence on purchased chemicals. These benefits depend on appropriate practices, rather than merely stopping fertiliser use."},{"id":"ied-review-5-exports-transition-costs-and-the-cotton-example","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Exports, transition costs and the cotton example. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Exports, transition costs and the cotton example\nIndia’s abundant labour can be an advantage in labour-intensive organic production. Certified products may obtain premium prices and export opportunities. However, certification, consumer trust, market connections and practical knowledge are necessary to realise those gains.\n**Challenges:** yields can fall during transition; lower output can raise cost per unit; products may have a shorter shelf life; certification and marketing can be difficult; and farmers need training and suitable inputs. A sudden universal switch without support can threaten farm incomes and food availability.\nThe Maharashtra organic-cotton case involving Kisan Mehta and Prakruti illustrates farmers experimenting with chemical-free production and international standards. The broader lesson is to combine soil protection with economic viability. The chapter also discusses **PM-PRANAM** as an initiative encouraging alternative fertilisers and balanced fertiliser use; organic farming is part of the wider sustainability agenda."},{"id":"ied-review-6-who-counts-as-a-worker","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Who counts as a worker?. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Who counts as a worker?\nA **worker** contributes to the production of goods and services counted in economic activity. Workers include employees, self-employed people and unpaid helpers in family enterprises. Payment may be in cash or kind, and someone working for only part of the year can still be a worker.\nA farmer, street vendor, teacher and unpaid family-business helper can all be workers. Being a consumer does not itself make a person a worker. Unpaid domestic services within one’s own household are generally excluded from measured economic production, although they are valuable work in everyday life.\nEmployment is studied to understand the quantity and quality of human resources, sectoral contributions, job security, exploitation, child labour and training needs. People work for income and survival, but also for self-worth, participation and contribution to society."},{"id":"ied-review-6-three-employment-statuses","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Three employment statuses. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Three employment statuses\n**Self-employed workers** operate their own enterprises or farms and earn returns from that activity rather than receiving a wage from an employer. **Regular salaried employees** work under a continuing employment arrangement, usually with more predictable pay and conditions.\n**Casual wage labourers** are hired for short periods or particular tasks, often on daily wages, without assured continuing work. Construction labour and seasonal agricultural labour are common examples. Regular status usually offers better security, but the actual benefits depend on the contract and enforcement.\nEmployment status is different from industry. A person in manufacturing could be self-employed, regularly employed or casually hired; do not treat “factory worker” as automatically a secure salaried job."},{"id":"ied-review-6-people-available-people-employed-and-hours-supplied","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: People available, people employed and hours supplied. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"People available, people employed and hours supplied\n**Labour force** consists of employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work and seeking it under the relevant measurement definition. **Workforce** consists only of the employed. People neither employed nor seeking or available for work are outside the labour force.\n**Labour supply** concerns the amount of work offered, measured in worker-hours or worker-days. The same number of people can supply more labour by working longer hours. Thus a change in hours need not mean a change in the number of people in the labour force.\nKeep denominators separate: unemployment is measured relative to the labour force, while worker–population ratio relates workers to the population. A student not seeking work is not automatically unemployed."},{"id":"ied-review-6-worker-population-ratio-and-rural-urban-differences","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Worker–population ratio and rural–urban differences. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Worker–population ratio and rural–urban differences\n**Worker–population ratio (WPR)** is the percentage of the population engaged in work. It indicates participation in measured employment, not the quality, productivity or pay of that work. A higher WPR does not by itself establish greater prosperity.\nThe textbook’s **2022–23** figures are: rural men 54%, urban men 55.6%, all men 54.4%; rural women 30%, urban women 18.7%, all women 27%; and total rural 42.3%, urban 37.7%, all persons 41.1%. These are a dated comparison, not current live statistics.\nRural participation may be higher because poverty pushes people into work earlier and fewer people can remain in education or wait for suitable jobs. Urban opportunities for study and job search can reduce measured participation. Interpret rates together with age, education, income and the character of employment."},{"id":"ied-review-6-why-participation-differs-between-groups","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why participation differs between groups. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why participation differs between groups\nMen’s measured participation is higher than women’s because of unequal educational access, household responsibilities, social restrictions, limited suitable opportunities and under-recognition of women’s productive work. Rural women may participate in family farms or enterprises; such productive assistance should be counted even without a separate wage.\nRural employment is dominated by self-employment because many households cultivate land or run small activities and have limited salaried opportunities. Urban areas offer more regular employment in firms, offices and services. Casual labour remains important where people lack assets, skills or secure contracts.\nWomen can be concentrated in home-based, traditional or low-paid activities because mobility and opportunities are restricted. Do not confuse unpaid family-enterprise work, which is economic activity, with own-household domestic services, which are generally outside the measured production boundary."},{"id":"ied-review-6-industry-groups-and-sectoral-distribution","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Industry groups and sectoral distribution. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Industry groups and sectoral distribution\n**Primary sector:** agriculture and allied activities, mining and quarrying. **Secondary sector:** manufacturing, electricity/gas/water supply and construction. **Tertiary sector:** trade, transport, storage and other services. These groups classify what workers produce, rather than their employment status.\nThe chapter’s **2022–23 national distribution** is about **46.1% primary, 24.9% secondary and 29% tertiary**. Rural employment depends more heavily on agriculture, while urban employment is concentrated in industry and services. Women are particularly concentrated in agricultural and family-based work.\nAgriculture’s large employment share alongside a much smaller output share suggests low average output per worker and pressure on land. Structural development requires productive opportunities outside farming, rather than displacement without alternative livelihoods."},{"id":"ied-review-6-a-slow-shift-and-new-ways-of-working","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: A slow shift and new ways of working. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"A slow shift and new ways of working\nThe textbook compares **1972–73** employment shares of about **74.3% primary, 10.9% secondary and 14.8% tertiary** with the later distribution. The primary share has fallen and the other shares have risen, but agriculture remains the largest source of employment.\nThis is a structural shift, though slower than the transformation of output. A growing services share in GDP need not absorb all workers leaving farming, especially when expanding services require skills that many workers lack.\nHome-based work is not entirely new: weaving and other household production have long existed. Digital technology expanded remote work, particularly during the COVID-19 period. Its opportunities depend on connectivity, skills and the nature of the job."},{"id":"ied-review-6-jobless-growth-and-the-output-employment-gap","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Jobless growth and the output–employment gap. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Jobless growth and the output–employment gap\n**Jobless growth** means output grows without a corresponding expansion in employment. It does not necessarily mean literally zero jobs are created; the concern is that employment growth is inadequate relative to output growth and the number seeking work.\nCapital-intensive technology, automation and efficiency improvements can increase production using relatively fewer workers. Growth concentrated in high-skill activities may not provide suitable opportunities for the wider labour force. Some existing jobs can also disappear during restructuring.\nTo evaluate growth, ask how many jobs it creates, who can access them, and whether they provide productive, secure livelihoods. Labour-intensive activities, skills, infrastructure and support for smaller enterprises help connect output growth with employment."},{"id":"ied-review-6-what-casualisation-means-and-what-the-trend-actually-shows","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: What casualisation means—and what the trend actually shows. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"What casualisation means—and what the trend actually shows\n**Casualisation** is a shift towards casual wage work relative to self-employment or regular salaried work. Workers may face uncertain hours, weak bargaining power, easy dismissal and limited benefits. Rural surplus labour migrating without adequate skills can enter such work; educated workers may also accept insecure contracts when alternatives are scarce.\nThe textbook’s series is not a continuous rise: the casual share increased from **23.2% in 1972–73** to **31.8% in 1993–94**, but was around **30% in 2011–12** and **23.3% in 2022–23**. State the relevant period rather than claiming that casualisation increased in every year.\nThe concept concerns the quality and composition of work. A rise in total employment can coexist with insecurity if new jobs are predominantly casual or poorly protected."},{"id":"ied-review-6-organised-versus-unorganised-employment","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Organised versus unorganised employment. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Organised versus unorganised employment\nThe textbook classifies **all public-sector establishments and private establishments employing ten or more workers** as the formal or organised sector. Smaller private enterprises and many own-account activities belong to the informal or unorganised sector.\nFormal employment is more likely to have written conditions, regular pay, social-security benefits and enforceable protection. Informal workers often face uncertain earnings, weak implementation of safeguards, limited collective bargaining and greater exposure to market fluctuations. This does not mean informal workers have no legal rights.\nThe chapter’s **2011–12** comparison puts roughly **30 million workers, or 6%, in the formal sector**, and **443 million, or 94%, in the informal sector**. These are historical textbook figures. A worker’s actual job protection should still be examined; establishment size alone does not guarantee every benefit."},{"id":"ied-review-6-why-protection-matters","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why protection matters. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Why protection matters\n**Informalisation** describes the predominance or spread of employment outside effectively protected formal arrangements. Workers may be employed yet remain vulnerable to poverty, accidents, illness and loss of work. Social security, skills and enforceable conditions therefore matter alongside the number of jobs.\nThe **Ahmedabad textile-mill case** describes large-scale closures and displacement from previously organised employment. Loss of wages and benefits pushed households towards insecure work and financial distress. The lesson is that industrial restructuring needs support for workers’ transition.\nThe textbook’s **labour-code box** highlights social protection for gig and platform workers, appointment letters and clearly stated terms, health checks for workers over 40, and safeguards for women working at night. These illustrate efforts to extend protection and improve working conditions rather than merely count employment."},{"id":"ied-review-6-who-is-unemployed-and-where-data-come-from","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Who is unemployed and where data come from. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Who is unemployed and where data come from\n**Unemployment** occurs when people willing and able to work at the prevailing terms cannot obtain work. Someone choosing not to seek work is different from someone seeking a job unsuccessfully. An employed person in unsuitable or insufficient work may face underemployment rather than open unemployment.\nThe chapter identifies the **Census**, employment surveys of the national statistical system, including the **Periodic Labour Force Survey**, and **employment-exchange registrations** as sources of information. Registered applicants are only a subset of all unemployed people; survey definitions and reference periods also matter.\nTo interpret a figure, ask whether it measures people, days or hours and what willingness, availability and job-search criteria were used. Do not calculate the unemployment rate by dividing unemployed people by the entire population."},{"id":"ied-review-6-two-important-rural-forms","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Two important rural forms. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Two important rural forms\n**Disguised unemployment:** more people are engaged in an activity than are required, so some can be withdrawn without reducing total output. Their marginal contribution is effectively zero. A joint-family farm may divide a fixed amount of work among many members when alternative jobs are unavailable.\n**Seasonal unemployment:** work is available during particular seasons but not throughout the year. A single-crop area may provide work during sowing and harvesting while leaving workers idle in the off-season. Similar patterns can occur in seasonal processing or construction activities.\nDisguised unemployment concerns excess workers at a given time; seasonal unemployment concerns lack of work during part of the year. Irrigation, multiple cropping, allied activities and non-farm employment can help. Disguised unemployment can also occur outside agriculture when enterprises are overstaffed."},{"id":"ied-review-6-eight-causes-and-their-mechanisms","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Eight causes and their mechanisms. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Eight causes and their mechanisms\n**Slow economic growth:** output and investment may not expand enough to absorb jobseekers. **Rapid population growth:** the labour force can grow faster than employment opportunities. **Seasonal agriculture:** limited irrigation and single cropping leave long off-seasons. **Joint-family system:** workers remain attached to a farm despite insufficient productive work.\n**Decline of cottage and small industries:** loss of traditional livelihoods is not always offset by new opportunities. **Low investment:** inadequate capital formation limits enterprises and job creation. **Capital-intensive technology:** output can rise with relatively little additional labour.\n**Low mobility of labour:** language, family ties, social conditions, information and moving costs restrict movement to available jobs. These causes can reinforce each other, so remedies must address both labour demand and people’s ability to reach and perform the jobs."},{"id":"ied-review-6-direct-and-indirect-employment","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Direct and indirect employment. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Direct and indirect employment\n**Direct employment:** government departments and public enterprises hire workers to provide administration, education, health, transport, infrastructure and other services. These workers are employed by the public institution itself.\n**Indirect employment:** public production, procurement and infrastructure create opportunities elsewhere. A public steel plant supplies inputs to downstream private industries, while roads make new businesses viable. Suppliers, contractors and linked enterprises can employ additional workers.\nGovernment also promotes self-employment, credit, skills and wage-work programmes. Distinguish an immediate public job from a policy that enables another employer or an individual enterprise to create work."},{"id":"ied-review-6-self-employment-rural-enterprises-and-roads","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Self-employment, rural enterprises and roads. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"Self-employment, rural enterprises and roads\n**PMRY (1993):** assisted educated unemployed youth in establishing enterprises through credit and support. **SJSRY (1997):** addressed urban poverty through wage employment and self-employment arrangements. **REGP (1995):** promoted rural industries and artisan employment.\n**PMEGP (2008):** a credit-linked subsidy programme supporting micro-enterprises in manufacturing and services. **SGSY (1999):** promoted rural self-employment through groups, credit and assistance; its approach was subsequently restructured under **NRLM/Aajeevika**, with an emphasis on sustainable livelihoods.\n**PMGSY (December 2000):** all-weather rural road connectivity improves access to markets, services and employment. These programme names belong to different periods; learn each programme’s mechanism and distinguish a historical scheme from its successor. PMEGP should not be described as discontinued simply because a textbook passage groups it with older schemes."},{"id":"ied-review-6-mgnrega-and-the-textbook-s-updated-guarantee-box","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: MGNREGA and the textbook’s updated guarantee box. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"MGNREGA and the textbook’s updated guarantee box\n**MGNREGA, 2005**, historically guaranteed **100 days of wage employment per rural household per financial year** to adult members volunteering for unskilled manual work. It is a household guarantee, not 100 days separately for every adult. Its framework included employment within the prescribed period, unemployment allowance where applicable, and a minimum one-third participation provision for women.\nThe purpose is livelihood security combined with useful rural assets and local work opportunities. It helps households during agricultural slack periods and can strengthen workers’ bargaining power.\nThe chapter’s newer box discusses the **Viksit Bharat—Guarantee for Rozgar and Ajeevika Mission (Gramin) Act, 2025**, which replaces the earlier framework and raises the guarantee to **125 days per rural household**. Keep the date and framework explicit: answer 100 days when asked about MGNREGA’s original provision, and 125 days when asked about the updated Act."},{"id":"ied-review-6-mudra-skills-make-in-india-and-start-up-india","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: MUDRA, skills, Make in India and Start-up India. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"MUDRA, skills, Make in India and Start-up India\n**MUDRA**, launched in 2015, supports credit for eligible small and micro enterprises, enabling self-employment and expansion. **PMKVY** supports industry-relevant skills through the skills-development framework and training partners. Credit and training work best when supported by markets and viable business opportunities.\n**Make in India**, launched on 25 September 2014, seeks to encourage production, investment and manufacturing activity with employment opportunities. **Start-up India** encourages entrepreneurship and enterprise creation, which can generate jobs for founders and others.\n**DAY-NULM** promotes urban poor households’ livelihoods through skills, wage employment and self-employment support. These programmes use different channels—finance, skills, investment and enterprise—so an answer should connect each name to its employment mechanism rather than merely list schemes."},{"id":"ied-review-6-covid-era-support-and-employer-incentives","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: COVID-era support and employer incentives. Give the relevant distinctions, reasons or examples.","solution":"COVID-era support and employer incentives\n**Atmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY, 2020)** used employment-related incentives to encourage new hiring or re-employment during recovery from COVID disruption. **Pradhan Mantri Rojgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY, 2016)** supported eligible employer contributions to encourage formal job creation; the chapter’s account refers to a defined registration and benefit period.\n**Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan**, launched on 20 June 2020, was a **125-day campaign** supporting livelihoods and rural works for returning migrants and affected rural households. Its 125-day campaign duration is different from a household’s annual legal employment guarantee under the later Act.\nThe wider challenge is to create productive work while improving security, matching skills with demand and extending opportunities to women and disadvantaged groups. Employment policy succeeds when people can earn a sustainable livelihood, not simply when a scheme has been announced."},{"id":"macro-review-1-restored-scope-of-macroeconomics-all-six-areas","chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Scope of macroeconomics: all six areas. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Scope of macroeconomics: all six areas\nScope means the field of study. The chapter identifies six leading areas; each needs its own heading and explanation.\n**1 · Estimation of National Income and Related Aggregates:** Macroeconomics begins with national income and measures the economy’s overall economic activity. It estimates related aggregates such as Gross Domestic Product (GDP) and Net Domestic Product (NDP), allowing comparison of output and income over time.\n**2 · Theory of Employment:** It studies employment and unemployment in the economy. Keynesian theory explains why inadequate aggregate demand can leave willing workers unemployed, and identifies remedies for restoring output and employment.\n**3 · Theory of Money:** It studies money and the creation of credit by commercial banks. It also examines the central bank’s role—in India, the RBI—in regulating money supply and credit in the economy.\n**4 · Theory of General Price Level:** It explains movements in the general price level, including inflation and deflation. Inflation is a persistent increase in the general price level; deflation is a persistent decrease. A change in one product’s price is different from a change in the economy-wide price level.\n**5 · Government Budget:** It studies how government receipts and expenditure affect the level of economic activity. Through taxation, public spending and borrowing, budgetary policy influences aggregate demand, employment, growth and economic stability.\n**6 · Exchange Rate and Balance of Payments:** It studies the determination and management of the foreign exchange rate. Balance of payments records a country’s economic transactions with the rest of the world, connecting the domestic economy with international trade, income and financial flows."},{"id":"macro-review-1-restored-significance-of-macroeconomics-all-six-points","chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Significance of macroeconomics: all six points. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Significance of macroeconomics: all six points\nSignificance explains why studying these economy-wide relationships is useful. Keep these six headings separate from the six areas of scope.\n**1 · Description of the Economy:** National-income estimates across sectors reveal the nature and level of economic activity. Unemployment data show the extent of the employment problem and help identify remedies. The government budget shows how the state regulates economic activity. Together these provide a systematic picture of the economy’s performance.\n**2 · Roadmap for Business Decisions:** The description of the economy helps businesses decide whether to expand investment and production. For example, rising national income and consumer expenditure suggest stronger demand; firms may invest more, expanding output and employment. Macroeconomic conditions therefore guide individual business decisions.\n**3 · Policy Formulation:** Macroeconomic data help governments design policies and programmes. For example, information about people below the poverty line helps target Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT): money is transferred directly to beneficiaries’ linked bank accounts. The policy should respond to the scale and nature of the problem identified.\n**4 · Global Economic Issues:** It brings international issues into view, including exchange rates and the movement of Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) between countries. These affect trade, financing, productive capacity and the relationship between the domestic economy and the rest of the world.\n**5 · Environmental Pollution and Sustainable Development:** Macroeconomic analysis helps assess environmental costs and formulate a strategy of sustainable development. Sustainable development meets development needs without depleting the resources available to future generations; output growth must therefore be assessed alongside its environmental consequences.\n**6 · Structural Changes and Growth Path of the Economy:** It helps identify changes in the economy’s structure and trace the nature and pace of growth. Changes in occupational structure—for example, workers moving from agriculture towards industry and services—show how development is changing the economy, beyond an increase in total GDP."},{"id":"macro-review-1-restored-micro-versus-macro-seven-bases-of-distinction","chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Micro versus macro: seven bases of distinction. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Micro versus macro: seven bases of distinction\nBoth study scarcity and choice, but at different levels. An industry aggregates firms, yet studying one industry is still microeconomics.\n**1 · Concept:** Microeconomics studies individuals, households, firms and industries; macroeconomics studies the economy as a whole.\n**2 · Central Issue:** Micro focuses on allocation of resources: consumers maximise satisfaction and producers maximise profit. Macro focuses on overall output and employment, and policies that maximise growth with stability.\n**3 · Economic Variables:** Micro uses variables such as an individual consumer’s demand and a producer’s supply. Macro uses aggregate demand, aggregate supply, national income, economy-wide consumption, investment and employment.\n**4 · Aggregation of Economic Units:** Micro involves limited aggregation, such as all firms in one industry. Macro involves the wider aggregation of economic units across the whole economy.\n**5 · Economic Agents:** Micro emphasises individual agents such as consumers and producers pursuing personal gains. Macro examines institutional decision makers such as the RBI, government and regulators, whose policies affect growth and stability.\n**6 · Method of Study:** Partial equilibrium studies one market assuming other markets unchanged. General equilibrium studies simultaneous equilibrium and interdependence across markets. The textbook associates the former with micro and the latter with macro.\n**7 · Assumptions of Study:** In the textbook’s simplified comparison, micro takes overall output and employment as given while studying allocation and distribution; macro varies overall output and employment while taking distribution as given. These are analytical assumptions, not a claim that macro can never study inequality."},{"id":"macro-review-1-restored-why-the-great-depression-challenged-classical-economics","chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Why the Great Depression challenged classical economics. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Why the Great Depression challenged classical economics\nThe Great Depression of the 1930s explains why macroeconomics emerged as a distinct field, rather than simply applying individual-market reasoning to the whole economy.\n**Classical belief: individual gains and social welfare:** Classical reasoning argued that individuals pursuing self-interest maximise their satisfaction; together this was expected to maximise social welfare and ensure optimal use of scarce resources.\n**Classical belief: automatic full employment:** Competitive markets were expected to move to equilibrium. If labour supply exceeded demand, falling wages were expected to reduce supply and raise demand until full employment returned. Full employment was therefore treated as the normal condition.\n**What the Depression showed:** The textbook’s historical example reports US unemployment rising from about 3% to 25% during 1929–33, alongside a roughly one-third fall in GDP. Persistent unemployment contradicted the expectation of rapid automatic recovery.\n**Low Level Equilibrium Trap:** Low AD → low planned output → low employment → low GDP/income → low AD again. Firms could settle at an equilibrium with widespread unused capacity; market adjustment did not necessarily break the cycle.\n**Keynes’s diagnosis and remedy:** Keynes identified inadequate aggregate demand as the central problem and advocated large-scale government expenditure to raise demand. His 1936 General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money established the modern macroeconomic approach."},{"id":"macro-review-2-restored-fixed-investment-and-inventory-investment-why-each-matters","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Fixed investment and inventory investment: why each matters. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Fixed investment and inventory investment: why each matters\nInvestment adds to capital. Fixed investment concerns productive assets; inventory investment concerns changes in stocks of inputs and output.\n**Fixed Investment:** An increase in producers’ fixed assets, such as plant and machinery, adds productive capacity. For example, increasing a stock from eight to ten machines adds two machines. Higher capacity supports higher output and economic growth.\n**Inventory Investment:** Inventory includes raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods. Inventory investment is closing inventory minus opening inventory; it can be positive or negative. It is a flow even though inventory itself is a stock.\n**Why keep raw-material stocks?:** They ensure uninterrupted supplies of inputs and reduce the need for daily market purchases. This protects production against uncertainty about input prices and availability.\n**Why keep finished-goods stocks?:** They enable producers to meet expected future demand promptly. Desired inventory is planned; undesired inventory accumulates when sales fall short of expectations and can impose losses.\n**Significance of Net Investment:** Net investment adds to the existing capital stock, expands productive capacity and creates employment opportunities. Gross investment also includes replacement of worn-out assets, so a large gross figure does not necessarily imply a large net expansion."},{"id":"macro-review-2-restored-circular-flow-three-phases-assumptions-and-significance","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Circular flow: three phases, assumptions and significance. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Circular flow: three phases, assumptions and significance\nProduction, income generation and expenditure form an unending circular flow. Each phase leads to the next, giving three ways of measuring the same economic activity.\n**1 · Phase of Production:** Producers hire land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship from households and use these with intermediate inputs to add value. Turning wood worth ₹5,000 into chairs worth ₹10,000 creates ₹5,000 of value added.\n**2 · Phase of Income Generation / Distribution:** Value added becomes factor income: rent for land, wages and salaries for labour, interest for capital and profit for entrepreneurship. What firms pay as factor payments, households receive as factor incomes.\n**3 · Phase of Disposition / Expenditure:** Income is spent on final goods: household consumption and producers’ investment. Expenditure creates demand, inducing further production. Thus production generates income, income finances expenditure, and expenditure supports production.\n**Assumptions of the Simple Two-Sector Model:** Only households and producers exist; households spend their entire income, so there is no saving; the economy is closed, so there are no exports or imports; and there is no government. State these when explaining the simplest circular-flow diagram.\n**Triple Identity:** Value of production = income generated = expenditure on final output. Money flows express the value of real flows and move in the opposite direction to goods and factor services.\n**Significance: Estimation of National Income:** Circular flow explains the production, income and expenditure approaches. Measure value added, sum factor incomes, or sum final expenditure; each views the same flow from a different angle.\n**Significance: Intersectoral Interdependence:** Households depend on producers for goods and income; producers depend on households for factor services and demand. The model makes these mutual dependencies visible."},{"id":"macro-review-2-restored-depreciation-replacement-and-the-reserve-fund","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Depreciation, replacement and the reserve fund. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Depreciation, replacement and the reserve fund\nDepreciation is consumption of fixed capital: loss of value while fixed assets are used. Replacement maintains capacity; net investment expands it.\n**Normal Wear and Tear:** Repeated use wears out machines and other fixed assets. A part of gross investment must replace the capital consumed rather than expand the stock.\n**Accidental Damage and Expected Obsolescence:** The chapter includes normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence due to changes in technology or demand. Unexpected catastrophic destruction is a capital loss, distinguished from normal depreciation.\n**Depreciation Reserve Fund:** Producers set aside funds to replace worn-out fixed assets. With a ₹10 lakh machine and an expected ten-year life, ignoring scrap value, an annual provision of ₹1 lakh finances replacement at the end of its life.\n**Why the fund matters:** Without replacement finance, productive assets deteriorate and capacity may fall. Lower investment can reduce output, income and employment, contributing to economic slowdown. Gross investment = net investment + depreciation."},{"id":"macro-review-3-restored-gdp-and-welfare-all-four-limitations","chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: GDP and welfare: all four limitations. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"GDP and welfare: all four limitations\nReal GDP growth can increase the goods and services available to people. However, the textbook gives four separate reasons why GDP is an incomplete index of welfare.\n**1 · Distribution of Income:** If additional income is concentrated among the rich, rising GDP need not improve living standards for most people. An increasing divide between rich and poor can coexist with high aggregate growth.\n**2 · Composition of GDP:** What is produced matters. More defence goods can contribute indirectly through security, but do not directly increase household consumption. A rise in luxuries for the rich while necessities remain scarce may do little for broad welfare.\n**3 · Non-monetary Exchanges / Transactions:** Barter and payments in kind may go unrecorded or be inadequately measured, understating output and making GDP a weaker welfare measure. Production for own consumption is conceptually included where it can be imputed; absence of cash does not automatically make productive activity non-economic.\n**4 · Externalities:** Externalities are benefits or costs imposed on others without a price or compensation. A neighbour enjoys a beautiful garden without paying: positive externality. Factory smoke or polluted water harms others without adequate compensation: negative externality. GDP does not directly adjust for these unpriced welfare effects."},{"id":"macro-review-3-restored-domestic-territory-and-why-product-equals-income","chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Domestic territory and why product equals income. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Domestic territory and why product equals income\nDomestic territory is an economic accounting boundary, not simply the geographical land within political borders.\n**Geographical Territory and Economic Activity:** It includes territory administered by the government within which persons, goods and capital circulate, subject to the accounting conventions used in the chapter.\n**Embassies and Establishments Abroad:** Include the country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad. Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations situated within its geographical borders.\n**Ships and Aircraft:** Include ships and aircraft operated by residents between countries under the domestic-territory convention. Physical movement across a border does not by itself change the operator’s economic territory.\n**Fishing Vessels and Floating Platforms:** Include resident-operated fishing vessels, oil and gas rigs and floating platforms in international waters, and the relevant extraction rights specified in the accounting convention.\n**Domestic Product = Domestic Income:** Production creates value added, and value added is distributed as factor income. Thus the product and income are two views of the same activity, provided the gross/net and price basis are consistent."},{"id":"macro-review-4-restored-value-added-method-all-inclusions-and-exclusions","chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Value-added method: all inclusions and exclusions. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Value-added method: all inclusions and exclusions\nCount value created during the current year once. Both the inclusion decision and the reason are important in theory and classification questions.\n**Include: Commission on Second-hand Goods:** The old good itself was counted when produced, but the broker’s current service is new production and must be counted.\n**Include: Own-account Production:** Goods made by a producing unit for its own use are included. A car producer using a newly produced car to transport employees still creates current output.\n**Include: Goods for Self-consumption:** Impute the value of goods such as wheat produced and consumed by a farming family. Not being sold does not mean that the good was not produced.\n**Include: Imputed Rent:** An owner-occupied house provides housing services just as a rented house does. Its imputed rent is included.\n**Exclude: Resale of Second-hand Goods:** Reselling an existing asset creates no new output of that asset in the current year. Include only any current service involved in the sale.\n**Exclude: Intermediate Goods as a Separate Addition:** Their value is already embodied in final output. Either count final goods or sum value added at each stage, never add both.\n**Exclude: Own-household Domestic Services:** Unpaid domestic services within one’s own household are excluded under the textbook’s measurement convention. Paid domestic services are different.\n**Illegal Activities: Textbook Treatment:** The chapter excludes these from its measured estimates because reliable recorded data are unavailable. Use this stated treatment for its classification questions.\n**Double Counting Example:** Wheat ₹100 → flour ₹160 → bread ₹250. Values added are ₹100, ₹60 and ₹90, totalling ₹250. Adding sales of all three stages gives ₹510 and counts the same intermediate value repeatedly."},{"id":"macro-review-4-restored-income-method-all-inclusions-and-exclusions","chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Income method: all inclusions and exclusions. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Income method: all inclusions and exclusions\nInclude income earned from current productive services. Distinguish a productive reward from a transfer, an asset transaction or a capital gain.\n**Include: Retirement Pension:** An employment-related retirement pension is treated as compensation for past service in the chapter. It differs from a welfare old-age pension, which is a transfer.\n**Include: Current Commissions and Brokerage:** Commission on second-hand goods and brokerage on shares or bonds are rewards for current services. The sale price of the old asset or financial security itself is not factor income.\n**Include: Imputed Rent and Self-consumption:** Owner-occupied housing services and goods produced for own consumption generate current income even when no cash sale occurs.\n**Include: Productive Interest:** Interest paid by producing units on capital borrowed for production is factor income. Interest on consumption loans is excluded in the textbook treatment.\n**Exclude: Transfer Earnings:** Welfare pensions, unemployment allowances, scholarships and pocket money have no corresponding productive service and are not national income.\n**Exclude: Illegal Income, Windfall and Capital Gains:** The chapter excludes unrecorded illegal income; lottery winnings and gains from asset-price changes are not rewards for current production.\n**Exclude: Spending from Accumulated Savings:** The transfer or use of earlier savings does not itself create current factor income. Count any current goods or services purchased through the relevant production accounts instead.\n**Exclude: Indirect Taxes from Factor-cost Income:** Indirect taxes are part of market prices but not rewards paid to factors. Deduct net indirect taxes when converting market-price output to factor-cost income.\n**Avoid Repeating Profit Components:** Corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings together form profit. If total profit is supplied, do not add those components again. Likewise, do not add rent or interest again if operating surplus already includes them."},{"id":"macro-review-4-restored-expenditure-method-the-complete-precautions","chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Expenditure method: the complete precautions. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Expenditure method: the complete precautions\nFinal spending on current domestic output counts. A transaction moving money or ownership is not automatically expenditure on newly produced goods and services.\n**Include: Final Expenditure:** Include private consumption, government final consumption, gross domestic capital formation and net exports. Investment includes fixed capital and change in inventories.\n**Include: Brokerage and Commission:** Current brokerage on financial securities and commissions on second-hand goods purchase new services, even though the underlying asset purchase is excluded.\n**Include: Imputed Expenditure:** Include the imputed value of goods produced for own consumption and owner-occupied housing services.\n**Exclude: Intermediate Expenditure:** Its value is already included in final expenditure. Adding it separately would double count the same output.\n**Exclude: Second-hand Purchases:** These purchase output produced in an earlier period. They do not add current output, apart from current sale-related services.\n**Exclude: Financial-asset Purchases:** Buying shares, debentures and bonds exchanges financial claims. It does not itself purchase a newly produced final good or service.\n**Exclude: Transfer Payments:** Welfare pensions and scholarships transfer purchasing power without corresponding value addition. A later purchase of current output using that money is a separate transaction.\n**Net Exports and Stocks:** Subtract imports to remove foreign output contained in expenditure. Add inventory change only if it is not already included in gross domestic capital formation."},{"id":"macro-review-5-restored-functions-of-money-and-why-each-matters","chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Functions of money and why each matters. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Functions of money and why each matters\nMoney has two primary functions and three secondary functions. Explain the function, illustrate it, and connect it to the economic benefit.\n**Primary 1 · Medium of Exchange:** Money is accepted between sale and purchase, removing the need for a double coincidence of wants. A farmer can sell wheat for money and later buy clothing from someone else. This expands exchange and markets, encourages production for sale rather than only self-consumption, and supports GDP growth.\n**Primary 2 · Measure of Value / Unit of Account:** Prices express different goods, services and assets in a common unit. This permits value comparisons, national-income estimation and price-index construction. A common unit also makes asset markets and investment decisions easier.\n**Secondary 1 · Standard of Deferred Payments:** Future debts, principal and interest can be expressed and settled in money. Repaying a monetary amount is simpler than returning goods of exactly the original quality. This supports financial markets, lending, consumption and investment.\n**Secondary 2 · Store of Value:** Money carries purchasing power into the future without the physical deterioration associated with many stored goods. Convenient saving can finance future investment; inflation nevertheless erodes real purchasing power. Saving supports growth when channelled into productive investment, while a widespread cut in spending can weaken demand.\n**Secondary 3 · Transfer of Value:** Money allows purchasing power to move between people, regions and countries. This supports capital mobility, global financial markets, multinational enterprises and foreign investment, and can help finance development in capital-scarce regions.\n**Static versus Dynamic Functions:** Static functions are these conventional exchange, value and payment functions. Dynamic functions concern money’s role in economic stability and growth: regulating its supply can restrain inflation or support demand during slowdown."},{"id":"macro-review-5-restored-evolution-of-money-stages-gains-and-limitations","chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Evolution of money: stages, gains and limitations. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Evolution of money: stages, gains and limitations\nThe chapter traces a journey from barter to metallic currency, paper money, plastic money and electronic payments. Each stage eases some earlier difficulties while creating new issues.\n**1 · Barter System:** Goods exchange directly for goods, without money prices. Selling and buying must occur together, limiting exchange mainly to mutually matched needs. The chapter contrasts its lack of monetary-price uncertainty with its severe limits on trade.\n**2 · Metallic Currency:** Gold, silver and later alloy coins separate sale from purchase and expand markets. However, large payments are cumbersome, and convenient small denominations can be difficult.\n**3 · Paper Money:** Money supply becomes more elastic and easier to expand than a supply tied to metal. This supports national and international markets and investment. Excessive or insufficient expansion can create inflationary or deflationary pressures.\n**4 · Plastic Money and e-Money:** Cards, electronic transfers, wallets and digital banking make payments convenient and can broaden financial inclusion while reducing dependence on printed currency. Cybercrime, fraud and access problems require attention.\n**Digital Payments, Bitcoin and CBDCs:** A card or bank transfer is a method of accessing/transferring money. Bitcoin is a decentralised digital asset whose value responds to market demand and supply. A Central Bank Digital Currency is issued by the central bank and combines a digital form with state backing. These are different concepts, not interchangeable labels.\n**Changing Role of Money:** Initially a medium of exchange, money also became a unit of value, a store of purchasing power and a standard for future payments. “Money is what money does” expresses this functional approach."},{"id":"macro-review-5-restored-m2-m3-m4-and-liquidity","chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: M2, M3, M4 and liquidity. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"M2, M3, M4 and liquidity\nBroader money measures add less immediately spendable components to the most liquid measure, M1. These are stock measures at a point in time.\n**M1 · The Starting Point:** Currency with the public + demand deposits with commercial banks + other eligible deposits with the RBI. Government and interbank holdings are excluded from money held by the public.\n**M2:** M1 + deposits with Post Office Savings Bank accounts.\n**M3 · Aggregate Monetary Resources:** M1 + net time deposits with commercial banks. The chapter calls M3 aggregate monetary resources.\n**M4:** M3 + total deposits with post offices, excluding National Savings Certificates.\n**Narrow versus Broad Money:** The chapter groups M1/M2 as narrow-money concepts and M3/M4 as broad-money concepts. Specify which measure is being used before comparing money supply.\n**Liquidity:** Liquidity means ease and speed of conversion into cash. Currency is the most liquid asset; demand deposits are readily usable through payment instructions. Time deposits are less immediately accessible, so broader measures include less-liquid components."},{"id":"macro-review-5-restored-who-supplies-money-bank-money-and-high-powered-money","chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Who supplies money? Bank money and high-powered money. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Who supplies money? Bank money and high-powered money\nDistinguish the institutions creating money, the money held by the public, and the monetary base supporting deposit creation.\n**1 · Central Bank:** The RBI is the principal currency-note issuing authority in the textbook’s account. Note issue follows the minimum reserve system described in the chapter.\n**2 · Commercial Banks:** They do not print notes or mint coins, but create demand deposits through lending. These chequeable deposits serve as bank money.\n**3 · Government:** The chapter identifies the Ministry of Finance as issuer of ₹1 notes and coins. Distinguish this issue authority from the RBI’s circulation and distribution functions.\n**High-powered Money / Monetary Base:** Currency with the public + banks’ vault cash + cash reserves held by banks with the RBI. It supports the banking system’s ability to create deposits.\n**Why It Differs from Money Supply:** Currency with the public belongs to both concepts, but banks’ own cash and reserves are not part of money held by the public. Demand deposits belong to money supply and are supported by the monetary base.\n**Ideal Supply of Money:** The chapter describes an ideal supply as one consistent with the supply of goods and services, avoiding both inflationary pressure and deficient-demand conditions."},{"id":"macro-review-5-restored-paper-currency-legal-tender-and-minimum-reserves","chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Paper currency, legal tender and minimum reserves. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Paper currency, legal tender and minimum reserves\nIndia’s textbook monetary system is described as a paper currency standard, even though coins also circulate. Its notes and coins are credit money because face value exceeds material value.\n**Limited and Unlimited Legal Tender:** The chapter contrasts coins, accepted up to the prescribed legal limit, with paper notes as unlimited legal tender. “Unlimited” concerns the value of debt settled under the applicable conditions, not an entitlement to pay with withdrawn or invalid notes.\n**Minimum Reserve System:** The textbook gives a minimum reserve of ₹200 crore in gold and foreign securities, including at least ₹115 crore of gold. This is a minimum backing requirement, not a rule that reserves must equal a fixed percentage of all currency issued.\n**No Conversion into Bullion:** Holding currency does not entitle the holder to demand its face value in gold or silver from the issuing authority. Gold backing and convertibility into gold are different ideas."},{"id":"macro-review-6-restored-central-bank-all-seven-functions-explained","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Central bank: all seven functions explained. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Central bank: all seven functions explained\nThe central bank is the apex institution controlling and regulating the banking and monetary system. Its objective is growth with stability rather than the commercial bank’s private profit.\n**1 · Issuer of Currency:** It has the principal monopoly of note issue, subject to the government’s ₹1-note/coin exception described in Money. Centralised issue creates uniformity, public confidence, a central check on supply and flexibility to respond to inflation or deflation. The earnings from issue accrue to the public authority.\n**2 · Banker to the Government:** As banker, it maintains accounts, accepts deposits, makes payments and transfers funds. As agent, it manages the issue, purchase, sale and redemption of government securities and debt. As financial adviser, it advises on monetary and financial conditions, stability and borrowing.\n**3 · Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role:** It holds commercial banks’ reserve deposits and provides financial accommodation. It supervises compliance with directives and reserve requirements such as CRR and SLR, thereby influencing credit creation.\n**4 · Lender of Last Resort:** A bank unable to obtain emergency finance elsewhere can approach the central bank against eligible security. This protects confidence, helps prevent a liquidity problem spreading and supports monetary-system stability.\n**5 · Custodian of Foreign Exchange:** It holds and manages official foreign-exchange reserves and may buy or sell foreign currency to moderate exchange-rate instability under managed floating.\n**6 · Clearing House Function:** Banks settle mutual claims through accounts with the central bank. For example, offsetting cheques between two banks allows only the net amount to be settled, avoiding unnecessary cash transfers and reducing cash requirements.\n**7 · Control of Credit:** It regulates the cost and availability of bank credit. In the textbook framework, tighter credit restrains inflationary demand and easier credit supports activity during deficient demand. Quantitative tools affect overall credit; qualitative tools target particular uses or sectors."},{"id":"macro-review-6-restored-banks-financial-institutions-and-central-bank-differences","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Banks, financial institutions and central-bank differences. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Banks, financial institutions and central-bank differences\nA financial institution is not automatically a bank. The chapter’s banking test combines acceptance of chequeable deposits with lending or advances.\n**All Financial Institutions Are Not Banks:** The chapter uses LIC as a lender that does not accept chequeable public deposits and ordinary post-office savings as deposit-taking without the paired commercial-lending function. Accepting a deposit or lending alone does not satisfy its two-function definition.\n**Customers and Position:** A commercial bank serves households and businesses; the central bank stands at the apex and mainly serves government and the banking system.\n**Role in Money Supply:** The central bank issues currency; commercial banks create deposit money through loans. Credit creation is not currency printing.\n**Role in Foreign Exchange:** The central bank is custodian of official reserves and manages exchange-rate interventions. Commercial banks deal in foreign exchange as part of customer services and business.\n**Objectives:** Commercial banks seek profit subject to regulation. The central bank aims at monetary stability and growth, supervising the system rather than competing as an ordinary retail bank."},{"id":"macro-review-6-restored-quantitative-credit-controls-rates-and-open-market-operations","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Quantitative credit controls: rates and open-market operations. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Quantitative credit controls: rates and open-market operations\nQuantitative controls influence overall credit rather than one selected activity. For each instrument, explain the action, the effect on bank funds or borrowing cost, and the resulting effect on spending.\n**Bank Rate:** The textbook describes this as the rate for central-bank lending to commercial banks, distinguished from secured short-term repo transactions. Raising it tends to raise borrowing costs and restrain credit; lowering it tends to encourage credit.\n**Open Market Operations:** The central bank buys or sells government securities in the open market. Purchases inject funds and support bank lending; sales absorb funds and reduce lending capacity. Remember the action from the central bank’s viewpoint: buying adds liquidity, selling withdraws it.\n**Repo Rate:** Repo is lending against securities under an agreement to reverse the transaction at a specified date and price. A higher repo rate makes central-bank funds costlier and can raise bank lending rates, reducing demand for credit. Lowering it works in the opposite direction.\n**Reverse Repo Rate:** This is the rate associated with banks placing funds with the central bank through reverse-repurchase arrangements. A higher return can encourage banks to park more funds rather than lend; lowering it can encourage lending. The effect depends on banks’ alternative uses for funds.\n**Cost versus Availability:** Rates mainly influence the cost and attractiveness of funds. Open-market operations and reserve requirements influence available liquidity. Both channels ultimately affect credit, investment, consumption and aggregate demand."},{"id":"macro-review-6-restored-crr-slr-and-all-three-selective-controls","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: CRR, SLR and all three selective controls. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"CRR, SLR and all three selective controls\nReserve requirements influence lending capacity; selective controls influence which uses receive credit. State both the direction of the policy and its mechanism.\n**Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR):** The prescribed share of bank deposit liabilities held as cash reserves with the RBI. Raising CRR leaves a smaller share available to support lending and reduces the simple credit multiplier; lowering it expands potential credit creation.\n**Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR):** The prescribed share of relevant liabilities held by the bank in eligible liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved unencumbered securities. A higher SLR restrains funds available for other lending; a lower SLR can release funds. CRR is held with the RBI; SLR assets are maintained by the bank.\n**1 · Margin Requirement:** Margin is collateral value minus the loan granted. With collateral worth ₹1 crore and a loan of ₹80 lakh, the margin is ₹20 lakh or 20%. Raising the margin reduces the maximum loan for given collateral, particularly restraining speculative borrowing; lowering it permits more credit.\n**2 · Rationing of Credit:** Fix quotas or limits on credit for selected business activities. Tighter quotas restrict lending to those uses; relaxation increases availability. This is selective credit control.\n**3 · Moral Suasion:** The central bank uses advice, persuasion and pressure to obtain banks’ cooperation. It may urge restraint during inflation and more liberal lending during deficient demand. The chapter notes that it can affect both overall and selected credit, though usually classified as qualitative."},{"id":"macro-review-6-restored-when-monetary-tools-work-all-success-conditions","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: When monetary tools work: all success conditions. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"When monetary tools work: all success conditions\nChanging a policy instrument does not guarantee a proportionate change in lending or spending. The chapter separates four conditions for repo-rate policy and two for open-market operations.\n**Repo 1 · Dependence on the Central Bank:** Banks with ample funds of their own depend less on central-bank loans, so a change in the repo rate may have a weaker influence.\n**Repo 2 · Sensitivity of Demand for Funds:** Banks may not respond strongly to small rate changes when business expectations or credit demand are weak. A cheaper source of funds does not by itself create willing borrowers.\n**Repo 3 · Structure of Interest Rates:** If non-bank financial institutions do not adjust their rates correspondingly, borrowers can switch sources and weaken policy transmission.\n**Repo 4 · Overall Supply of Funds:** When non-bank sources are popular and readily available, regulating the price of bank funds has less influence on total financing.\n**OMO 1 · Existence of a Securities Market:** Open-market purchases and sales require a well-organised, functioning market for government securities. Without willing counterparties, operations have limited reach.\n**OMO 2 · Excess Reserves with Banks:** Large excess reserves can weaken the intended relationship between liquidity withdrawal and lending. Banks can absorb a drain without proportionately reducing loans, while injections may remain idle when demand for credit is low."},{"id":"macro-review-7-restored-ad-and-as-assumptions-components-and-schedules","chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: AD and AS: assumptions, components and schedules. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"AD and AS: assumptions, components and schedules\nIn this course, AD is planned expenditure measured against income, and AS is planned output. The diagram’s axes and assumptions determine how the curves are interpreted.\n**AD Means Planned Expenditure:** AD is the expenditure buyers intend to incur on domestically produced goods and services during an accounting year. It is not the sum of physical quantities of different goods, nor necessarily actual expenditure.\n**AD at Zero Income:** Some consumption is necessary even when current income is zero. It is financed from past saving or borrowing. This autonomous expenditure makes the AD curve start above the origin.\n**Two-, Three- and Four-Sector Components:** In a two-sector closed economy AD = C + I. Add government for AD = C + I + G. In an open economy AD = C + I + G + (X − M). Exports add demand for domestic output; imports remove spending on foreign output.\n**AD Schedule and Curve:** The schedule lists planned expenditure at different income levels. With income on the horizontal axis, consumption and AD rise as income rises. This upward-sloping income–expenditure curve differs from an AD curve drawn against the general price level.\n**AS, Income and the 45° Line:** Planned production creates income, so AS is represented by Y. With the same scale on both axes, the 45° line represents equality between output/income and expenditure.\n**Model Conditions:** Technology and relevant prices are treated as constant; spare capacity allows output to respond to demand before full employment. At full employment, real output cannot keep rising merely because planned spending rises."},{"id":"macro-review-7-restored-consumption-function-every-diagram-observation","chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Consumption function: every diagram observation. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Consumption function: every diagram observation\nThe consumption function relates household consumption to income: C = C̄ + bY, where C̄ is autonomous consumption and b is MPC.\n**Autonomous Consumption:** C̄ is consumption when Y = 0. The curve starts above the origin on the consumption axis, and saving at zero income is −C̄.\n**Induced Consumption:** bY is consumption attributable to income. Higher income raises consumption; the amount consumed out of an additional unit of income is MPC.\n**Keynes’s Psychological Law:** As income rises, consumption rises by less than the increase in income, leaving part for saving. In the standard linear model, 0 < MPC < 1 and the consumption line is flatter than the 45° reference line. Constant MPC means equal increments of income produce equal increments of consumption.\n**Break-even Point:** Where the consumption line crosses C = Y, all income is consumed and saving is zero. Below that income, C > Y and households dissave; above it, C < Y and saving is positive.\n**Slope versus Average:** The consumption-line slope is MPC = ΔC/ΔY. APC = C/Y is the slope of a ray from the origin to a point on the line, not the slope of the consumption line itself.\n**Why APC Falls in the Linear Model:** APC = C̄/Y + MPC. As income rises, the autonomous-consumption term becomes smaller relative to income, so APC falls towards MPC when C̄ is positive."},{"id":"macro-review-7-restored-deriving-saving-and-consumption-curves-from-one-another","chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Deriving saving and consumption curves from one another. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Deriving saving and consumption curves from one another\nConsumption and saving exhaust income: Y = C + S. Use this identity to derive either function and check their common break-even income.\n**Consumption to Saving:** From C = C̄ + bY, S = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y. The saving intercept is the negative of autonomous consumption and the saving slope is MPS = 1 − MPC.\n**Saving to Consumption:** From S = a + mY, C = Y − S = −a + (1 − m)Y. A negative saving intercept produces a positive consumption intercept.\n**Locate the Two Intercepts:** At Y = 0, consumption is C̄ and saving is −C̄. Mark these on their respective vertical axes; do not start both curves at the origin.\n**Locate the Shared Break-even Income:** Set S = 0 or C = Y. Both give Y = C̄/(1 − b). The saving line crosses the income axis directly below the consumption line’s intersection with the 45° line.\n**Complete the Curves:** Draw consumption with slope b and saving with slope 1 − b. The slopes add to one; below break-even income saving is negative, and above it saving is positive.\n**Undefined Ratios at Zero Income:** APC and APS divide by Y and are undefined when income is zero. MPC and MPS concern changes and can remain well defined; do not report an ordinary finite APC or APS at zero income."},{"id":"macro-review-8-restored-equilibrium-conditions-stock-signals-and-the-two-approaches","chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Equilibrium: conditions, stock signals and the two approaches. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Equilibrium: conditions, stock signals and the two approaches\nEquilibrium requires intended output to equal intended expenditure. The producer’s desired inventory may be positive; equilibrium means no unintended change in it, not zero stocks.\n**AD = AS Approach:** At equilibrium, Y = C + I in the two-sector model. Intended purchases match intended production, actual stocks equal desired stocks, and producers have no reason to change output.\n**S = I Approach:** Since Y = C + S and AD = C + I, equating Y and AD gives C + S = C + I, hence planned S = planned I. Saving is a leakage and investment an injection; equilibrium requires their equality.\n**When AS > AD:** Actual stocks exceed desired stocks. Firms cut planned production and employment. Falling income reduces saving until planned S equals planned I.\n**When AD > AS:** Stocks fall below the desired level, and some demand goes unfulfilled. With spare capacity, firms expand production and employment; income and saving rise towards equilibrium.\n**Equilibrium Is Not Necessarily Full Employment:** Spending plans can match production plans while workers remain involuntarily unemployed. Full-employment equilibrium additionally requires fuller utilisation of available resources.\n**Ex-post Identity Is Different:** Actual investment includes unplanned inventory changes, so actual saving and actual investment are equal in accounting. This identity holds even when planned saving and investment differ."},{"id":"macro-review-9-restored-deficient-demand-all-six-causes","chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Deficient demand: all six causes. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Deficient demand: all six causes\nDeficient demand means planned AD is below the level required for full employment. Start from AD = C + I + G + (X − M); taxation influences disposable income and consumption.\n**1 · Reduction in Private Consumption Expenditure:** A lower propensity to consume, or higher propensity to save, reduces C at a given income and shifts AD down. In mature economies, consumption may grow more slowly once many wants are satisfied.\n**2 · Reduction in Private Investment Expenditure:** Poor or bearish business expectations reduce expected returns and investment. Lower I directly reduces planned expenditure, with further effects through the multiplier.\n**3 · Reduction in Government Expenditure:** Budget constraints or spending cuts can reduce government consumption or investment. Both lower AD.\n**4 · Decline in Exports:** Lower foreign purchases mean less expenditure on domestically produced goods and services, reducing AD and domestic income.\n**5 · Rise in Imports:** When buyers switch towards cheaper foreign products, more spending goes to foreign output. Imports enter with a negative sign, reducing demand for domestic output, other things equal.\n**6 · Increase in Tax Rates:** Higher taxes leave households with less disposable income. Consumption can fall even if their propensity to consume is unchanged, pulling AD down."},{"id":"macro-review-9-restored-deficient-demand-all-four-consequences","chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Deficient demand: all four consequences. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Deficient demand: all four consequences\nDemand below the full-employment requirement leaves capacity unused. The textbook groups the effects into four situations, with a connected downward spiral.\n**1 · Underemployment Equilibrium:** AD and AS can intersect at output below potential GDP. Firms have no incentive to produce more when planned purchases are insufficient, despite idle resources and involuntary unemployment.\n**2 · Deflationary Gap:** The gap is the shortfall of planned AD from required AD at full-employment output. Weak demand hurts inducement to invest; low investment reduces output and income, which weakens demand again: the low-level equilibrium trap.\n**3 · Undesired Stocks:** Sales fall short of production plans, so unwanted inventories accumulate. Producers may cut prices to clear stocks and reduce future output and employment.\n**4 · Loss of Profits:** Unsold output and price reductions reduce profitability. Bearish expectations can cause another cut in investment, reinforcing weak income and demand.\n**Overall Effects:** Output falls, involuntary unemployment rises and prices face downward pressure. The basic fixed-price income model emphasises falling output; the chapter’s broader discussion also describes deflationary price pressure and contraction in the business cycle."},{"id":"macro-review-9-restored-excess-demand-all-six-causes","chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Excess demand: all six causes. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Excess demand: all six causes\nExcess demand occurs when planned spending exceeds the AD consistent with full-employment output. The causes reverse the directions of the deficient-demand causes.\n**1 · Increase in Private Consumption Expenditure:** A higher propensity to consume or lower propensity to save raises C at a given income. The demonstration effect—adopting the spending patterns of richer groups—can also increase consumption.\n**2 · Increase in Private Investment Expenditure:** Bullish business expectations and expected high returns raise investment, increasing AD directly and through induced consumption.\n**3 · Increase in Government Expenditure:** Expansion of development and welfare commitments raises government consumption or investment, adding to AD.\n**4 · Increase in Exports:** More foreign demand for domestic output, potentially encouraged by relatively lower domestic prices, raises AD.\n**5 · Decrease in Imports:** Higher foreign prices can shift purchases towards domestic output. A lower M increases net exports and therefore AD, other things equal.\n**6 · Cut in Tax Rates:** Lower taxes leave more disposable income, enabling higher consumption and planned expenditure."},{"id":"macro-review-9-restored-excess-demand-all-four-consequences","chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Excess demand: all four consequences. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Excess demand: all four consequences\nAt full employment, with technology and productive capacity given, extra nominal demand cannot create an unlimited increase in real output.\n**1 · Inflationary Gap:** Planned AD exceeds the full-employment requirement. Pressure on fully used resources raises costs and the general price level. Measure the gap vertically at full-employment income.\n**2 · Static GDP:** Real GDP remains at the full-employment level because there is no spare capacity. Nominal GDP can rise as prices rise. Always specify real GDP when saying output remains constant.\n**3 · Wage–Price Spiral:** Higher demand raises pressure on wages and other costs; higher prices raise living costs and wage claims, which can raise costs and prices again. Persistent escalation threatens economic stability.\n**4 · Loss of Profit from Unfulfilled Demand:** The textbook’s point concerns potential profit forgone because producers cannot expand supplies to satisfy additional demand. It is distinct from claiming that every firm’s actual money profit must fall during inflation.\n**Overall Effects:** Real output and employment remain at capacity in this model, while prices rise. Compare this with deficient demand, which can reduce output and employment."},{"id":"macro-review-10-restored-revenue-deficit-and-fiscal-deficit-all-implications","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Revenue deficit and fiscal deficit: all implications. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Revenue deficit and fiscal deficit: all implications\nDeficit definitions measure different gaps. Their consequences depend on the method of financing, the use of funds and the economy’s available capacity.\n**Revenue Deficit 1 · Pressure on Welfare Spending:** A shortfall of revenue receipts against current expenditure can force cuts to subsidies or welfare programmes, potentially reducing social welfare.\n**Revenue Deficit 2 · Higher Liabilities:** Borrowing from the public, central bank or abroad can finance the shortfall but increases liabilities and future debt-service obligations.\n**Revenue Deficit 3 · Disinvestment Pressure:** Selling public shareholdings can raise funds, but reduces public ownership. The textbook also notes possible increased foreign control when buyers are foreign firms.\n**Fiscal Deficit 1 · Inflationary Spiral:** Central-bank financing can expand money and nominal spending. If output cannot respond because capacity is fully used, prices rise. Inflation is therefore a conditional consequence, not an automatic result of every deficit.\n**Fiscal Deficit 2 · National Debt:** Repeated borrowing increases debt and interest obligations. More resources spent servicing past debt can limit funds for development.\n**Fiscal Deficit 3 · Vicious Circle of High Deficit and Low Growth:** Debt service and fiscal pressures may constrain productive spending; higher taxes can weaken private demand and investment. Weak growth then limits revenue, keeping the deficit high. Productive public investment can instead support growth, so composition matters.\n**Fiscal Deficit 4 · Crowding-out:** Heavy public borrowing can reduce funds available to private investors and put upward pressure on financing costs, discouraging private investment.\n**Fiscal Deficit 5 · Erosion of Government Credibility:** Unsustainable borrowing can weaken confidence and creditworthiness, discourage investors and increase financing difficulty. A deficit used responsibly for productive capacity has different implications from persistent financing of unproductive spending.\n**Primary Deficit:** Primary deficit removes interest on past debt from fiscal deficit. Zero primary deficit means the remaining borrowing requirement equals interest payments; a positive primary deficit indicates an additional gap even before those interest payments."},{"id":"macro-review-10-restored-balanced-surplus-and-deficit-budgets-complete-appraisal","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Balanced, surplus and deficit budgets: complete appraisal. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Balanced, surplus and deficit budgets: complete appraisal\nA budget’s appropriateness depends on economic conditions. The textbook gives separate merits and demerits for balanced, surplus and deficit budgets.\n**Balanced Budget · Meaning and Merits:** Receipts equal expenditure. Its stated merits are restraint of wasteful expenditure and a signal of financial stability and fiscal discipline.\n**Balanced Budget · Demerits:** It does not necessarily remedy unemployment caused by inadequate AD. In developing economies, insisting on balance can prevent the large initial public-investment push needed for growth.\n**Surplus Budget · Meaning and Merits:** Receipts exceed expenditure. Greater revenue collection and/or lower spending can reduce AD, making a surplus useful against an inflationary gap.\n**Surplus Budget · Demerits:** It can worsen depression by weakening AD further. Persistently suppressing spending can reduce output and employment and contribute to a low-level equilibrium trap.\n**Deficit Budget · Meaning and Merits:** Expenditure exceeds receipts before the financing needed to close the gap. Keynes favoured its use during depression: public spending raises AD directly and can induce further consumption and investment.\n**Deficit Budget · Demerits:** At full employment, additional spending can enlarge excess demand without increasing real output, intensifying inflation and possibly a wage–price spiral. Debt sustainability and the use of borrowed funds also matter."},{"id":"macro-review-10-restored-public-expenditure-all-four-importance-points","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Public expenditure: all four importance points. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Public expenditure: all four importance points\nPublic expenditure has growth and welfare roles. Explain what the government spends on and the channel through which it affects the economy.\n**1 · Accelerates GDP Growth:** Investment in public enterprises, capital grants for equipment, input subsidies and farm procurement support production and investment. Infrastructure also adds productive capacity.\n**2 · Promotes Equality:** Welfare pensions and access to food, education and healthcare improve the position of poorer households, reducing inequalities in opportunities and living standards.\n**3 · Restores Economic Stability:** During recession, public consumption and investment raise AD. This can interrupt the cycle of weak demand, weak output and weak income.\n**4 · Creates an Investment-friendly Environment:** Roads, dams, bridges and reliable transport reduce private production costs and improve access to markets. This encourages private investment rather than replacing every private activity."},{"id":"macro-review-11-restored-fixed-exchange-rate-all-five-merits","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Fixed exchange rate: all five merits. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Fixed exchange rate: all five merits\nA fixed rate is officially set and defended. The chapter’s arguments concern the benefits of greater exchange-rate predictability, subject to successful maintenance of the rate.\n**1 · Market Stability and Mobility of Capital:** Reducing exchange-rate fluctuations lowers one source of uncertainty for international investors and can encourage cross-border capital movement.\n**2 · Growth of International Trade:** Exporters and importers can plan contracts with more predictable domestic-currency returns and costs, supporting international trade.\n**3 · Stable Macroeconomic Policies:** The textbook argues that a stable exchange rate removes frequent exchange-rate changes as a source of policy disturbance. Maintaining the peg still places constraints on policy; it does not give unlimited monetary independence.\n**4 · Devaluation as a Tool to Expand Foreign Markets:** An official downward adjustment in the domestic currency’s value can make exports cheaper to foreigners and improve producers’ international competitiveness, depending on demand responses.\n**5 · A Check on Inflation:** High domestic inflation makes exports less competitive at a fixed rate, weakening receipts and reserves. Pressure to preserve the rate can encourage policies restraining inflation."},{"id":"macro-review-11-restored-fixed-exchange-rate-all-five-demerits","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Fixed exchange rate: all five demerits. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Fixed exchange rate: all five demerits\nThe same commitment that provides predictability requires defence and can prevent the official rate from adjusting to market conditions.\n**1 · Reserves of Forex:** The authority needs foreign-exchange reserves to meet excess demand for foreign currency and defend the official rate. Sustained pressure can exhaust reserves.\n**2 · Inefficient Allocation of Resources:** An official rate differing from the market-clearing rate distorts the relative prices guiding production, imports and exports.\n**3 · Small Size of Forex Market:** The chapter argues that a tightly fixed rate limits foreign exchange as an independently traded commodity, restricting the scope of currency-market activity.\n**4 · Discourages Venture Capital:** In the chapter’s usage, this means funds placed in foreign-currency trading to profit from exchange movements. A tightly fixed rate limits these opportunities; this is different from ordinary start-up venture finance.\n**5 · Divergence from Equilibrium:** The official rate can remain above or below the equilibrium rate. Persistent excess demand or supply then requires intervention and can impair efficient production and trade."},{"id":"macro-review-11-restored-flexible-exchange-rate-all-five-merits","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Flexible exchange rate: all five merits. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Flexible exchange rate: all five merits\nA flexible rate moves with demand and supply. Its advantages arise from allowing prices to adjust rather than defending a prescribed value.\n**1 · Large Forex Reserves Not Required for Rate Defence:** No official peg needs continuous defence. Market adjustment can equilibrate currency demand and supply, although countries may still hold reserves for other reasons.\n**2 · Efficient Allocation of Resources:** Market-clearing exchange rates transmit scarcity and demand signals, helping guide trade, production and investment across countries.\n**3 · Large Size of Forex Market:** Currency itself becomes a traded asset, expanding financial-market activity and related business opportunities.\n**4 · International Mobility of Liquidity:** Less foreign exchange needs to remain committed to defending a fixed rate, potentially freeing funds for international transactions and investment.\n**5 · Encourages Venture Capital in Currency Trading:** Exchange-rate movements create opportunities for investors willing to bear currency risk. This is the chapter’s currency-market meaning of venture capital."},{"id":"macro-review-11-restored-flexible-exchange-rate-all-five-demerits","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Flexible exchange rate: all five demerits. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Flexible exchange rate: all five demerits\nMarket adjustment creates uncertainty and can transmit foreign disturbances into the domestic economy.\n**1 · Marginalisation of Weak Currencies:** Low demand for a weak currency can lead to steep depreciation. Imports and foreign-currency obligations become more expensive in domestic money, creating pressure for smaller or vulnerable economies.\n**2 · Uncertainty of the Market:** Frequent exchange-rate changes make future export receipts, import costs and investment returns harder to predict.\n**3 · Difficulty Maintaining Stable Monetary Policy:** Exchange movements affect inflation, capital flows and interest-rate decisions. Responding to them can complicate a stable policy path.\n**4 · Difficulty with Bilateral Trade Agreements:** Changing currency values make it harder to predict the value of payments under long-term trade arrangements. Hedging can reduce, but not eliminate, this problem.\n**5 · External Shocks:** Foreign-currency appreciation can raise the domestic-currency cost of imports even when their foreign price is unchanged. A stronger dollar, for example, increases the rupee bill for a fixed dollar payment."},{"id":"macro-review-11-restored-demand-for-foreign-exchange-all-seven-components","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Demand for foreign exchange: all seven components. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Demand for foreign exchange: all seven components\nForeign exchange is demanded to make payments abroad or acquire foreign-currency assets. Distinguish a change in the exchange rate from other factors shifting demand.\n**1 · Repayment of International Loans:** Foreign-currency loan principal must be repaid in the agreed currency, creating demand for foreign exchange.\n**2 · Investment in the Rest of the World:** Residents buying foreign securities, businesses or other foreign assets need the relevant currency.\n**3 · Imports:** Payments for imported goods and services create demand for foreign exchange.\n**4 · Direct Purchases Abroad:** Foreign travel, study and medical treatment require payments abroad. The chapter includes tourists’ direct purchases here.\n**5 · Grants and Donations:** Unilateral payments sent abroad also require foreign exchange, even though no goods or productive service is received in return.\n**6 · Payment of Incomes:** Factor incomes such as wages, rent, interest and profits paid to non-residents create an outward flow.\n**7 · Speculative Trading:** People may acquire foreign currency expecting its value to rise and hoping to profit from a later sale.\n**Why Demand Slopes Downward:** With ₹ per dollar on the vertical axis, a lower dollar price makes foreign goods and investment cheaper in rupees, increasing demand for dollars, other things equal. A price change causes movement along the curve; other determinants shift it."},{"id":"macro-review-11-restored-supply-of-foreign-exchange-all-seven-sources","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Supply of foreign exchange: all seven sources. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Supply of foreign exchange: all seven sources\nThe supply of foreign exchange comes from receipts from abroad and conversion of foreign currency into domestic currency.\n**1 · Exports:** Foreign buyers pay for domestic goods and services, generating foreign-exchange receipts.\n**2 · Investment from the Rest of the World:** Inward FDI and portfolio investment bring foreign funds into the economy.\n**3 · Direct Purchases by Non-residents:** Foreign tourists and other non-residents purchasing domestically contribute foreign-exchange receipts.\n**4 · Loans from the Rest of the World:** Borrowing abroad brings in foreign exchange now, while creating future repayment obligations.\n**5 · Grants and Donations from Abroad:** Foreign assistance, gifts and donations create receipts without a corresponding repayment or current productive service.\n**6 · Income Receipts:** Factor incomes earned from abroad—including wages, interest, rent and profits—bring in foreign exchange.\n**7 · Remittances by Non-residents:** Money sent by non-residents to domestic recipients is a source of foreign exchange. A gift remittance is a transfer, not automatically factor income.\n**Why Supply Slopes Upward:** A higher ₹-per-dollar rate gives more rupees per dollar. It can make domestic goods cheaper for foreign buyers and improve the attractiveness of domestic purchases or investments, increasing foreign-exchange supply, other things equal."},{"id":"macro-review-12-restored-balance-of-payments-all-five-significance-points","chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Balance of payments: all five significance points. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Balance of payments: all five significance points\nBoP data reveal the economy’s external position and inform decisions. A single flow should be interpreted with its purpose and the wider account.\n**1 · Financial Status of the Domestic Economy:** The accounts show external receipts, payments and financing needs. Heavy reliance on borrowing can indicate vulnerability or dependence, though borrowing may also finance productive investment.\n**2 · Net Factor Income from Abroad:** Factor-income receipts and payments provide information needed for NFIA, the bridge from domestic income to national income.\n**3 · Net Exports as a Component of AD:** Exports minus imports form part of aggregate demand. A rise in X − M increases demand for domestic output and can raise output and employment where spare capacity exists.\n**4 · Market Potential:** Foreign investment flows can provide evidence about investors’ expectations of market opportunities and returns. Their size is one indicator, not a complete measure, of market potential.\n**5 · Monetary and Fiscal Policies:** External inflows and outflows influence currency demand, reserves and financial conditions, which the RBI considers in monetary policy. Investment flows and the tax environment can also inform fiscal-policy decisions."},{"id":"macro-review-12-restored-bop-equilibrium-surplus-deficit-and-reserve-movements","chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: BoP equilibrium, surplus, deficit and reserve movements. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"BoP equilibrium, surplus, deficit and reserve movements\nThe chapter separates ordinary current/capital flows from the official-reserves account. Accounting balance and equilibrium of autonomous transactions are different concepts.\n**Equilibrium:** Ignoring errors and omissions, current-account balance + capital-account balance = zero with no reserve movement. A current-account deficit can be offset by an equal capital-account surplus.\n**Surplus:** Receipts on the relevant non-reserve transactions exceed payments. The authority can acquire foreign assets, increasing official reserves.\n**Deficit:** Payments exceed receipts. A drawdown of reserves or other accommodating finance covers the shortfall.\n**Reserve Entry versus Reserve Stock:** Buying a foreign reserve asset is a use of foreign exchange, while selling a reserve asset provides foreign exchange. An increase in the reserve stock therefore does not mean a credit inflow; follow the sign convention in the question.\n**Accounting Always Balances:** Once counterpart entries and financing are included, total accounts balance. This does not imply the country has no deficit, external debt or financing problem. For example, a current deficit of ₹100 with ordinary capital inflow of ₹60 leaves ₹40 to be financed through reserve use or other accommodation."},{"id":"macro-review-10-restored-non-tax-revenue-all-seven-sources","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Non-tax revenue: all seven sources. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Non-tax revenue: all seven sources\nNon-tax receipts arise from sources other than taxes and are part of revenue receipts when they neither create liabilities nor reduce government assets.\n**1 · Fees:** Payments for specified administrative or judicial services, such as passports, registration and court services. The chapter distinguishes these from prices charged for ordinary commercial services.\n**2 · Fines:** Economic penalties for breaking laws. Their primary purpose is enforcement and deterrence rather than raising revenue.\n**3 · Escheat:** Property without a legal heir or valid claimant can pass to the state, generating income for the government under the chapter’s classification.\n**4 · Special Assessment:** Owners may pay when public development raises their property value, helping recover part of the cost of roads, drainage or similar improvements.\n**5 · Income from Public Enterprises:** Profits and dividends from government-owned enterprises provide revenue. Selling government shares is different: it reduces an asset and is a capital receipt.\n**6 · Spectrum Receipts:** The chapter classifies income from allocating telecom spectrum as a non-tax receipt.\n**7 · Grants and Donations:** Assistance and voluntary donations received by government, including during natural calamities, are revenue receipts when they carry no repayment liability."},{"id":"macro-review-10-restored-plan-and-non-plan-expenditure-the-historical-distinction","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Plan and non-plan expenditure: the historical distinction. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Plan and non-plan expenditure: the historical distinction\nThe textbook also explains this older budget classification. It is separate from the revenue/capital classification; learn what each term meant rather than treating them as synonyms.\n**Plan Expenditure:** Expenditure linked to specified development plans and programmes, including central assistance to states. It could include revenue spending, such as assistance, and capital spending, such as roads, bridges and hospitals.\n**Non-plan Expenditure:** Expenditure outside the plan category, including routine government functioning, law and order, defence and subsidies in the chapter’s account.\n**Two Different Classification Bases:** Plan/non-plan asks about a spending programme’s relationship to the planning framework. Revenue/capital asks whether it creates assets or reduces liabilities. Plan expenditure is not automatically capital expenditure."},{"id":"macro-review-12-restored-bop-disequilibrium-all-economic-political-and-social-causes","chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: BoP disequilibrium: all economic, political and social causes. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"BoP disequilibrium: all economic, political and social causes\nDisequilibrium can result from changes in trade or capital flows. A factor can improve or worsen the position depending on the direction of the change.\n**Economic 1 · Development Expenditure:** Large development programmes can require substantial imports of equipment and inputs, increasing foreign payments and potentially creating a deficit.\n**Economic 2 · Business Cycles:** Depression abroad may reduce demand for exports; expansion abroad can raise it. The effect depends on the countries affected and the response of imports and other flows.\n**Economic 3 · Domestic Inflation:** Higher domestic prices can make imports relatively attractive and exports less competitive, worsening the external balance.\n**Economic 4 · Import Substitutes:** Domestic alternatives reduce import requirements and can improve the balance, provided their production is viable.\n**Economic 5 · Changing Cost Structure:** Technological or managerial improvements alter relative competitiveness. Favourable domestic cost changes can increase exports; improvements by trading partners can have the opposite effect.\n**Political 1 · Instability:** Uncertainty can discourage direct and portfolio investment, reducing capital inflows or causing withdrawals.\n**Political 2 · Government Policy:** Large cuts in import duties can encourage imports and widen a current-account deficit, other things equal. Other policy changes can alter trade and investment incentives.\n**Social 1 · Tastes and Preferences:** Changing consumer preferences can raise exports when foreign demand favours domestic products, or raise imports when residents prefer foreign goods.\n**Social 2 · Cross-border Prejudices:** Political or social hostility can divert trade towards costlier import sources or less profitable export markets, weakening the external position."},{"id":"macro-review-2-restored-four-sectors-and-the-flows-connecting-them","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":6,"tag":"Full-topic review","q":"Explain: Four sectors and the flows connecting them. Use the relevant headings and reasons.","solution":"Four sectors and the flows connecting them\nEach sector depends on other sectors. Real flows move goods and services; money flows record the corresponding payments and receipts.\n**Household Sector:** Households consume goods and services and own factors of production. They supply land, labour, capital and entrepreneurial services to producers and receive factor incomes.\n**Producer Sector:** Firms hire or purchase factor services and combine them with intermediate inputs to produce goods and services. They depend on households for inputs and demand.\n**Government Sector:** Government provides welfare, administration, law and order and defence, and also acts as a producer. It depends on households and firms for tax and non-tax revenue; they depend on it for public services.\n**External Sector:** The rest of the world connects through exports, imports and cross-border capital flows. A closed economy excludes this sector; an open economy includes it.\n**Real Flows:** Factor services move from households to firms; goods and services move from firms to households. These are actual resource and output flows.\n**Money Flows:** Factor payments move from firms to households; consumption expenditure moves from households to firms. They travel opposite to the corresponding real flows and keep income circulating."}]};
